var tafsirData_2 = {
  "1": "“Alif-Lãm-Mĩm.”\n\nThis is the first verse of the second chapter of the Quran, and it consists of three Arabic letters written together. Some chapters of the Quran open with such letters, and they are known in Arabic as the disconnected letters. At first reading, they may seem strange, because they carry no obvious meaning like ordinary words do. Yet their presence is intentional and has been a subject of careful discussion among scholars throughout Islamic history.\n\nOne of the most important things to understand is that the precise meaning of these letters is known to Allah alone. The Prophet and his Companions did not give a single clear explanation that resolves every detail, and the reliable scholars of the past were careful not to claim a definite meaning they could not prove. Instead, they pointed to several possible purposes. Some explained that the letters are a challenge to the Arabs, who were masters of the Arabic language. The Quran, which is made up of the very letters the Arabs used every day, defies them to produce anything like it, and this shows that the Quran is not from any human being but is a miracle from Allah. The letters therefore stand as a kind of announcement: the speech that follows is built from the same letters you speak, yet no one can match it.\n\nOther explanations focus on how these letters draw attention. Because they are unusual, they immediately capture the ears and hearts of the listeners, waking them up so that they pay full attention to what follows. Some scholars also read meanings into the individual letters, understanding each letter as referring to a name of Allah or to a period of time, but such readings are speculative and are not agreed upon. The respectful position is to acknowledge that their full meaning rests with Allah while still recognising their role as a remarkable opening to the Quran.\n\nRight after this mysterious opening, the chapter presents the Quran itself, so the contrast between the strange letters and the clear message that follows is part of the effect. The reader meets something beyond human explanation, and then is told that the Book before him has no doubt about it. In this way, the very first verse of al-Baqarah prepares the heart to receive the guidance that is about to be declared, and it does so without pretending to solve every puzzle, because part of faith is accepting what Allah has kept to Himself.",
  "2": "“This is the Book! There is no doubt about it—a guide for those mindful ˹of Allah˺,”\n\nAfter the mysterious letters of the opening verse, the Quran immediately turns to speak of itself. “This is the Book!” points directly at the Quran, and the exclamation gives the statement a strong, confident tone. The Arabic wording can also carry the sense of “that is the Book,” which many scholars explain as a reference to a book whose greatness was already known and expected, the Quran that was promised to come. Either way, the verse makes a bold claim: what the reader holds is truly the Book from Allah.\n\nThe next phrase, “There is no doubt about it,” removes any hesitation about whether this Book is genuine. The Arabic word for doubt here means uncertainty or suspicion, and the verse declares that none of that belongs in the matter of the Quran. This is not a command to stop doubting through blind stubbornness; it is a statement of fact based on the Quran’s own nature. For those who examine it honestly, its guidance, its consistency, and its message testify that it is from Allah. The verse therefore settles the matter for the sincere seeker, even though those who refuse to look may continue to waver.\n\nThen the verse tells the reader what the Book actually does: it is “a guide for those mindful ˹of Allah˺.” The word for guide points to guidance that leads people out of confusion and into the truth. Importantly, the guidance is described as reaching “those mindful of Allah.” The Arabic term is often translated as God-consciousness or mindfulness of Allah, and it refers to a deep awareness of Allah that protects a person and shapes his choices. So the Quran guides the person whose heart is open and sincere. This does not mean the Quran is useless to others; rather, it means that guidance takes effect when a person is genuinely seeking Allah and willing to follow. A person who is arrogant and closed-minded may hear the same words and remain unchanged, while a humble, mindful person is moved to the right path. This is why the qualities of such mindful believers are described in detail in the very next verses.",
  "3": "“who believe in the unseen, establish prayer, and donate from what We have provided for them,”\n\nThis verse begins to list the qualities of the people who benefit from the guidance of the Quran, those described in the previous verse as “mindful ˹of Allah˺.” The first quality mentioned is that they “believe in the unseen.” The word that is transliterated as al-ghayb means that which is hidden and cannot be seen with the eyes. This includes belief in Allah, in the angels, in the books revealed by Allah, in the messengers, in the Last Day, and in everything the Quran tells about the world beyond our senses. Believing in the unseen is not the same as believing in anything without reason; it is accepting what Allah has truthfully informed us about, because we trust His message even when our eyes cannot verify it. This is the foundation of faith, and it distinguishes the believer from the person who trusts only what he can touch and see.\n\nThe second quality is that they “establish prayer.” The Arabic wording carries the idea of praying in a complete, careful, and continuous way, not merely going through the motions. Establishing prayer means performing it on time, with proper concentration and humility, and letting it order the life of the believer. In the Quran, prayer is often linked with remembrance of Allah and with keeping a person away from evil and indecency. So this phrase points to a prayer that is real and maintained, not neglected or rushed.\n\nThe third quality is that they “donate from what We have provided for them.” The word for donate points to spending and giving for the sake of Allah, and the phrase “from what We have provided” reminds the believer that all wealth is a gift from Allah. Because the wealth is from Allah, the believer does not cling to it selfishly; he gives to the poor, the needy, and good causes. The verse places belief in the unseen, prayer, and giving together because they cover the whole of faith and action: belief in the heart, worship of the body, and generosity with wealth. When these three work together, the person is truly on the path of guidance that the Quran came to show.",
  "4": "“and who believe in what has been revealed to you ˹O Prophet˺ and what was revealed before you, and have sure faith in the Hereafter.”\n\nThis verse continues the description of the mindful believers that began in the previous verse. Here the Quran says that they believe in “what has been revealed to you ˹O Prophet˺,” which is the Quran given to Muhammad, peace be upon him. It then adds that they also believe in “what was revealed before you,” meaning the earlier scriptures sent to the prophets who came before him, such as the Torah given to Moses and the Gospel given to Jesus. This shows that true belief in the Quran does not cut the believer off from the earlier prophets; rather, it confirms them. The Quran itself states that it confirms the scriptures that came before it. At the same time, believing in the earlier books means believing in what Allah genuinely revealed in them, not in the changes that later crept into their records.\n\nThen the verse completes the picture by saying that these believers “have sure faith in the Hereafter.” The word that is transliterated as al-akhirah means the last life, the life that comes after this world and after death. The phrase “sure faith” points to firm certainty, not just a vague hope. Believing with certainty in the Hereafter means believing in the resurrection, the final judgment, and the reward and punishment that follow. This certainty shapes how a person lives, because someone who is certain that he will give an account for his deeds is careful about what he does.\n\nTaken together, the previous verse and this one give a full picture of the believer’s heart. The believer has faith in the unseen, prayer that is established, generosity with wealth, belief in the Quran and the earlier revelations, and certainty in the last life. These are not separate compartments; they support one another. Believing in the Quran confirms the earlier prophets, and certainty in the Hereafter gives meaning to prayer and giving. This is why the very next verse is able to declare that such people are truly guided and will be successful. The description is now complete, and the Quran can sum it up in a single encouraging statement.",
  "5": "“It is they who are ˹truly˺ guided by their Lord, and it is they who will be successful.”\n\nAfter describing the believers in detail across the previous two verses, the Quran now gathers everything into a conclusion. “It is they who are ˹truly˺ guided by their Lord.” The wording places emphasis on “they,” drawing attention to this particular group, the mindful believers whose qualities were just listed. Their guidance is not something they produced on their own; it is “by their Lord.” This is an important point, because it keeps the believer humble. Guidance is a gift from Allah. A person may seek the truth sincerely, but the opening of the heart to accept it and the strength to stay on it come from Allah. So the verse returns all credit to the Lord who guided them.\n\nThen the verse adds, “and it is they who will be successful.” The Arabic word behind “successful” points to real and lasting achievement, the success that comes when a person reaches what his soul was created for and is saved from what harms it. The repetition of “they” in both halves of the verse is deliberate. It separates this group from others who might claim to be guided or successful without deserving it. Here the Quran gives certainty: the believers described above are the truly guided ones, and they are the truly successful ones.\n\nIt is worth noticing the structure of the whole opening section. The Quran said that the Book is a guide for the mindful. Then it listed what mindfulness actually looks like: faith in the unseen, established prayer, giving from wealth, belief in all the revelations, and certainty in the Hereafter. Now it closes by promising that this kind of faith leads to guidance and success. The section forms one connected argument from beginning to end. This also makes the contrast that follows much more powerful, because immediately after presenting the believers, the Quran turns to describe those who reject faith and whose end is very different. The success of the believer is therefore set against the failure of the one who turns away, and the reader is invited to see clearly which path he is on.",
  "6": "“As for those who persist in disbelief, it is the same whether you warn them or not—they will never believe.”\n\nAfter the believers were described and promised guidance and success, the Quran now turns to a different group. The phrase “As for those who persist in disbelief” points to people who have chosen disbelief and stayed firmly in it. The word behind “persist” suggests that they are settled and determined in their rejection, not simply unaware or still searching. This is an important detail, because it means the verse is not describing every person who has not yet accepted faith. It is describing those whose hearts have become hardened through their own choice to reject what they know to be true.\n\nFor such people, the verse says, “it is the same whether you warn them or not—they will never believe.” This addresses the Prophet directly. It is as if the Quran is comforting him and telling him not to blame himself or to be troubled when some people do not respond. A person who is locked in disbelief is not going to be moved by warning, no matter how clear or sincere the warning is. The Prophet’s duty was to deliver the message clearly, not to guarantee that every listener would accept it. The Quran makes this point in many places, telling him that he is only a warner and that guidance belongs to Allah.\n\nThe word “never” may seem harsh, but it must be understood in context. It does not mean that no disbeliever can ever change, because the Quran itself tells of people who accepted faith even after years of rejection. Rather, it describes the state of those who persist in disbelief until the very end, those who have sealed themselves off from the truth. Their disbelief is not a simple lack of information; it is a deliberate turning away. This is why the next verse explains what has happened to such people. Their hearts and their hearing and their sight have been affected, so that the truth no longer reaches them. The believer who recites this is reminded to be grateful for the guidance he received, because it came only through the mercy and will of Allah.",
  "7": "“Allah has sealed their hearts and their hearing, and their sight is covered. They will suffer a tremendous punishment.”\n\nThis verse explains what has happened to the persistent disbelievers described in the previous verse. “Allah has sealed their hearts and their hearing.” The image of sealing is powerful. A seal closes something off so that nothing can enter. Scholars explain that this sealing is the result of the people’s own repeated rejection of the truth. Because they kept turning away, their hearts gradually became closed to guidance, and this closing is attributed to Allah because it is part of His just dealing with them. It is not that Allah forced them into disbelief against their will; rather, their own stubbornness led them to a point where the truth no longer found an entry. The Quran is clear that Allah does not wrong anyone, and that people are only punished for what their own hands have earned.\n\nThen the verse adds that “their sight is covered.” The word for covered suggests a veil that hangs over the eyes, so that even when they see the signs of Allah, they do not truly perceive them. This is a deeper blindness than simply having eyes that do not work. Such people can see the physical world, but they cannot see the meaning and the warning behind it. Their hearing is also mentioned, because hearing is how the Quran’s message reaches them. When heart, hearing, and sight are all blocked, a person is cut off from the truth in every direction. This is a description of a spiritual condition, not a claim that their ears and eyes literally stopped working.\n\nThe verse ends with the warning that such people “will suffer a tremendous punishment.” The punishment is described as tremendous to show how serious the outcome is. The whole verse therefore paints a clear and sobering picture. What begins as a person’s own choice to reject the truth can grow into a condition where the person is no longer able to receive it, and the end of that road is a severe punishment. At the same time, this verse is often read as a warning to believers as well, urging them never to play with disbelief, because what starts as a small turning away can become a sealed heart. The seriousness of the description is meant to move hearts toward sincerity and away from the fate it describes.",
  "8": "“And there are some who say, “We believe in Allah and the Last Day,” yet they are not ˹true˺ believers.”\n\nWith this verse the Quran introduces a third group, after having described the believers and then the disbelievers. The new group is introduced with the words “And there are some who say.” The word “some” shows that this is a limited number of people within the wider society. These people make a claim with their tongues, declaring that “We believe in Allah and the Last Day,” yet the verse immediately adds that “they are not ˹true˺ believers.” The contradiction between what they say and what they are is the whole point of the description. Their claim comes only from their mouths, while their hearts do not share it.\n\nThese people are the hypocrites. The Arabic word for a hypocrite, transliterated as munafiq, describes someone who shows belief outwardly while hiding disbelief within. Hypocrisy of this kind is considered a very serious matter in the Quran, often ranked as more dangerous than open disbelief, because it spreads corruption from inside the community of believers. These people live among the believers, appear to be part of them, and yet their loyalty belongs elsewhere. The Quran exposes them so that the believers can recognise their behaviour and guard against it.\n\nIt is significant that they say they believe in “Allah and the Last Day,” because those are the very beliefs that were praised in the earlier verses describing the believers. By putting these words in the mouths of the hypocrites, the Quran shows that making a claim to faith is easy, but true faith is a matter of the heart and is proved by deeds. This also connects the verse to what came before: the believers genuinely believed in Allah and the Hereafter, while the hypocrites merely said they did. The next several verses will go on to expose the inner reality of the hypocrites, showing how they deceive others, how they make excuses for their corruption, and how they mock the believers. In this way, the Quran patiently lifts the veil from their true condition so that no sincere person is fooled.",
  "9": "“They seek to deceive Allah and the believers, yet they only deceive themselves, but they fail to perceive it.”\n\nThis verse exposes the inner state of the hypocrites who were introduced in the previous verse. “They seek to deceive Allah and the believers.” The word for deceive points to an attempt to trick or outwit someone. The hypocrites imagine that they can hide their disbelief from Allah and from the believers, presenting themselves as faithful while secretly being the opposite. The verse mentions deception of Allah and deception of the believers together, because the hypocrites try to appear sincere before the believers and imagine they can hide anything from Allah. Of course, Allah cannot truly be deceived, because nothing is hidden from Him, so the attempt is foolish from the start.\n\nThe next phrase gives the surprising truth: “yet they only deceive themselves.” This is one of the most striking ideas in this passage. By trying to deceive others, the hypocrites are actually harming only themselves. The harm they think they are causing to the believers is really falling back on their own heads, because their lies, their false witness, and their cunning all add to their own sin and bring them closer to punishment. Meanwhile, the believers are not harmed by their deception, because the believers place their trust in Allah and are protected by Him. So the deception, which was meant to be a weapon against others, turns out to be a wound against the deceivers themselves.\n\nThe verse then explains why the hypocrites continue in this self-harming behaviour: “but they fail to perceive it.” They do not realise that they are only deceiving themselves. If they truly understood the situation, they would see that their cunning gains them nothing and harms them greatly. The idea that a person can harm himself while imagining he is harming others is a theme that runs throughout the Quran. Someone who fights against the truth is ultimately fighting against his own soul. This verse, therefore, is not just a description of a group from the past; it is a warning to anyone who is tempted to live a double life, presenting faith outwardly while hiding corruption within. Such a person harms no one more than himself, even if he never realises it.",
  "10": "“There is sickness in their hearts, and Allah ˹only˺ lets their sickness increase. They will suffer a painful punishment for their lies.”\n\nThis verse speaks of a sickness that lies in the hearts of the hypocrites. The word for sickness is used here as a powerful image. Just as a body can become ill and lose its health, so the heart can become ill and lose its soundness. In this context, the sickness refers to the corruption and doubt within them, the disbelief, envy, and confusion that have damaged their inner selves. Because their hearts are sick, they do not see clearly and they make the wrong choices, as the surrounding verses show.\n\nThe verse then adds that “Allah ˹only˺ lets their sickness increase.” This increase is a consequence of their own actions. Every time the hypocrites hear the truth and turn away, every time they lie and mock, the sickness in their hearts grows worse. Scholars explain this as a form of punishment that comes gradually. Because they choose not to use the light of guidance they have received, the light is taken away further, and their hearts become darker. This is not Allah forcing evil upon them; it is the natural result of repeatedly rejecting good. The Quran compares the heart to a vessel: if it is filled with corruption, there is little room left for faith.\n\nThe verse ends with the warning that the hypocrites “will suffer a painful punishment for their lies.” The punishment is described as painful to stress its severity. It is tied specifically to their lies, because lying is one of the defining habits of hypocrisy. They lie about their faith, they lie to the believers, and they hide what is truly in their hearts. The Quran frequently connects hypocrisy with false speech and with breaking promises, so naming their punishment as a reward for their lies fits the whole picture. Reading this verse together with the previous ones, the reader sees a clear chain: the heart becomes sick through rejection, the sickness grows through continued turning away, and the end is a painful punishment. The believer is thus warned to guard his heart against even the small seeds of hypocrisy, such as insincerity and false speech.",
  "11": "“When they are told, “Do not spread corruption in the land,” they reply, “We are only peace-makers!””\n\nThis verse gives a concrete example of the hypocrites’ behaviour. “When they are told, ‘Do not spread corruption in the land.’” The word for corruption, transliterated as fasad, refers to ruin, disorder, and harm. It can include everything that breaks down the well-being of a society, whether it is injustice, dishonesty, division, or disobedience to Allah. The verse shows that the believers, and those who carry the truth, warn the hypocrites not to engage in such corruption. This warning itself is a sign that the message of the Quran is meant to build peace and order, not to destroy them.\n\nThe reply of the hypocrites is given next: “they reply, ‘We are only peace-makers!’” The word they use points to setting things right, repairing, and bringing about harmony. So the hypocrites deny that they are causing any harm and instead claim that they are the ones who improve things and make peace. The contradiction is obvious to any honest observer. People who mock the truth, who lie about their faith, and who spread doubt among the believers are not peace-makers at all; their actions bring division and harm. Yet they dress their corruption in fine words, and this is a key feature of hypocrisy: the ability to make evil look like good.\n\nThis is a very common human pattern, which is why the verse is so important. People rarely describe themselves as corruptors; almost everyone believes that their own actions are reasonable, even when those actions cause harm. The hypocrites’ claim that they are peace-makers shows that a person can be deeply involved in corruption while sincerely believing, or pretending, that he is doing good. The next verse will answer their claim directly, declaring that it is they who are truly the corruptors. For the reader, this passage carries a clear lesson: it is not enough to claim that one is a reformer or a peace-maker; one’s actions must actually match that claim, and the person who is warned against harm should examine his own deeds rather than simply defending himself.",
  "12": "“Indeed, it is they who are the corruptors, but they fail to perceive it.”\n\nThis verse answers the claim of the hypocrites in the previous verse, where they called themselves peace-makers. The Quran now states the truth plainly with the word “Indeed,” which gives the statement emphasis and certainty. “It is they who are the corruptors.” The wording places the emphasis on “they,” directly correcting the false claim. The people who think they are improving things, or who pretend to be reformers, are in fact the very ones spreading corruption. Their lying, their mocking of the truth, their sowing of doubt, and their double-faced dealings all work to damage the society from within.\n\nThen the verse adds the same striking note seen earlier: “but they fail to perceive it.” This shows that the hypocrites are not only corrupt; they are also blind to their own corruption. They do not see the harm they cause, or they refuse to admit it. This blindness is itself part of their punishment, because a person who cannot recognise his own wrongdoing is far from being able to correct it. The Quran repeatedly returns to this idea that the heart, once sick, loses the ability to see clearly. What a person does affects not only his actions but also his understanding, so that wrongdoing can make a person unable to see his own faults.\n\nThe pairing of these two verses is deliberate. Verse eleven showed the hypocrites claiming to be peace-makers, and verse twelve declares that they are truly the corruptors. This contrast is a lesson in how the Quran judges matters. It does not accept people’s own descriptions of themselves; it looks at what they actually do and what their words and deeds really produce. A person may call himself a reformer, but if his actions bring harm and division, then his true title is corruptor, whatever he claims. For the reader, this teaches careful honesty about one’s own conduct. It is a reminder to measure actions by their real effects and by the standard of the Quran, rather than by the labels people use to describe themselves. The verse stands as a warning that self-deception is one of the greatest dangers, because the person who is deceived cannot begin to mend his ways until he first sees the truth.",
  "13": "“And when they are told, “Believe as others believe,” they reply, “Will we believe as the fools believe?” Indeed, it is they who are fools, but they do not know.”\n\nThis verse continues to expose the behaviour of the hypocrites with another example. “And when they are told, ‘Believe as others believe.’” This is a command for them to believe sincerely as the believers have believed, to enter into faith with their hearts and not just with their tongues. The phrase “as others believe” refers to the genuine believers who have accepted the truth. The hypocrites are being invited to share in the same sincere faith, but their response reveals their true state.\n\nThey reply, “Will we believe as the fools believe?” They use the word “fools” to mock the believers, calling them foolish for giving up their old ways and following the Prophet. In their arrogance, they imagine that the believers are simple-minded and that they themselves are too clever to accept such a thing. This is a classic mark of hypocrisy and arrogance: looking down on those who sincerely seek the truth and mocking what one does not understand. The Quran then answers their mockery directly with the word “Indeed,” giving the reply emphasis: “it is they who are fools.” The very people who call the believers fools are the ones who are truly foolish, because they reject guidance and harm their own souls while imagining they are wise.\n\nThe verse closes with the phrase “but they do not know,” showing that the hypocrites are unaware of their own foolishness. This is part of the tragedy of their condition. A person who knows he is foolish might correct himself, but one who is foolish and does not know it remains trapped in his error. Throughout this passage, the Quran uses this kind of reversal to show how deception works. The hypocrites call themselves reformers when they are corruptors, and they call the believers fools when they are the real fools. In both cases, their words are exactly opposite to the truth, and their blindness keeps them from seeing it. For the reader, this is a reminder not to be impressed by proud words or to look down on sincere believers, and a warning that true wisdom lies in accepting guidance rather than in mocking those who follow it.",
  "14": "“When they meet the believers they say, ‘We believe.’”\n\nThis verse exposes how the hypocrites speak in two completely different ways, depending on who they are with. When they are in the company of the believers, they claim to believe, putting on the appearance of sincere Muslims and repeating the words of faith. This is the kind of double speech that the previous verses have been describing. The hypocrites present one face to the believers and keep another hidden. The verse uses the word “meet” to picture them running into the believers, and at that moment their tongues immediately claim belief, whether their hearts share it or not.\n\nThe verse then turns to what happens “when alone with their evil associates.” The word for “evil associates” points to the leaders and companions who share their hypocrisy, those who are called devils among humans and jinn in other verses of the Quran. When the hypocrites are alone with these people, they drop the mask and say, “We are definitely with you; we were only mocking.” This is a confession of the truth that they hide in public. They tell their evil companions that they were never really on the side of the believers, and that their profession of faith was only a joke and a form of mockery.\n\nThe phrase “we were only mocking” is especially important, because it shows how far their corruption has gone. It is one thing to hide one’s faith; it is another thing entirely to make a mockery of faith itself and of those who sincerely believe. The believers who take their religion seriously are being treated as the butt of a joke by people who claim to be among them. This is why the next verse will respond with a warning that their mockery will be turned back against them. For the reader, the verse serves as a clear description of hypocrisy: speech that changes with the company, a tongue that claims faith while the heart mocks it, and a double life built on deception.",
  "15": "“Allah will throw their mockery back at them.”\n\nThis verse answers the mockery of the hypocrites that was described in the previous verse. There they said that they were only mocking when they claimed to believe. Now the Quran declares that their mockery will be thrown back at them. This is a just and fitting response: those who make a joke of faith and of the believers will find that their joke turns against them. In the Hereafter, their punishment will be their portion, and the very thing they treated lightly will become the source of their ruin. Their laughter will turn into the reality they tried to ignore.\n\nThe rest of the verse adds that Allah leaves them “to continue wandering blindly in their defiance.” The word for “wandering blindly” paints a picture of people staggering about in darkness without a clear path, moving forward yet going nowhere useful because they have no light to guide them. This is a precise image of the hypocrite’s life. They may be clever, active, and confident, but because their hearts are full of deceit, they are really stumbling through life without guidance. Their defiance keeps them moving, but it is movement in the wrong direction.\n\nThe idea that Allah leaves them to wander is not a statement that Allah forces them into error against their will. It is a description of a just consequence. Because they have chosen deception and mocked the truth, the light of guidance does not reach their hearts, and they are left to the results of their own choice. This is consistent with the Quran’s constant teaching that Allah does not wrong anyone and that guidance and misguidance follow a person’s own sincere search or rejection. The image of wandering blindly also connects to the earlier descriptions of the sick heart that cannot see clearly. For the believer, the verse is both a warning against taking faith lightly and a comfort that Allah sees all mockery and will deal with it justly.",
  "16": "“They are the ones who trade guidance for misguidance.”\n\nThis verse describes the hypocrites using the language of trade and commerce, which is a powerful way to show how foolish their choice is. The word “trade” turns their behaviour into a bargain. They have given up something valuable and taken something worthless in exchange. What they gave up is guidance, the true path that leads to Allah and to success in the Hereafter. What they took instead is misguidance, the confusion and loss that come from rejecting the truth. No sensible merchant would make such a deal, yet this is exactly what the hypocrites have done with the most precious thing they possess.\n\nThe verse continues, “But this trade is profitless.” A trader hopes to gain from his business, but here there is nothing to gain. The deal brings no benefit at all, only loss. In fact, it brings loss upon loss, because they have lost guidance and gained error. The verse then adds that “they are not ˹rightly˺ guided.” This final phrase completes the picture. They are not among those who are guided, and their own choice has placed them outside the path that leads to success.\n\nThe image of trade appears elsewhere in the Quran, and it is always striking. A person is warned not to sell the Hereafter for the life of this world, and not to trade faith for disbelief. Here the same idea is applied to the hypocrites, who have effectively sold guidance for misguidance. The lesson is clear and can be understood by anyone, even without deep religious training. When a person gives up what is genuinely valuable for what is worthless, he has made a bad bargain. The Quran wants every reader to see that rejecting truth and living a double life is the worst business deal a human being can make. It looks clever for a while, but in the end it yields nothing but loss.",
  "17": "“Their example is that of someone who kindles a fire.”\n\nThis verse begins a powerful parable about the condition of the hypocrites. The word “example” introduces a comparison meant to make an invisible truth visible. The image is of a person who lights a fire, seeking warmth and light. When the fire “lights up all around them,” the person can see and feels safe. But then the Quran says that “Allah takes away their light, leaving them in complete darkness—unable to see.” The light that briefly gave them vision is suddenly removed, and they are left in darkness that is worse than the darkness they started with, because for a moment they had light and then lost it.\n\nScholars explain this parable in several connected ways. The fire can represent whatever the hypocrites rely on in place of true faith, such as their worldly gains, their clever schemes, or their false confidence. These things may appear to give them light and comfort for a time, but they do not last, and when they are taken away, the hypocrites are left in total confusion. Another reading connects the light with the faith and guidance they rejected. They tasted something of the truth and then turned from it, so the light was withdrawn from them. Either way, the parable shows that whatever they built their hopes on cannot save them.\n\nThe phrase “leaving them in complete darkness—unable to see” describes their true end. Unlike a person who was always in darkness, they experienced light and lost it, and so their blindness is of their own making. The parable is deeply fitting because it shows the temporary nature of the hypocrite’s apparent success. Their schemes may flourish for a while, just as a fire burns brightly for a while, but the Quran warns that this will not last. For the reader, the example encourages honest self-examination: what is the fire that one relies on, and will it continue to give light when the darkness of the Hereafter arrives?",
  "18": "“They are ˹wilfully˺ deaf, dumb, and blind.”\n\nThis verse sums up the spiritual condition of the hypocrites with three powerful words. “Deaf” means they do not truly hear the truth when it reaches them, even though their ears work. “Dumb” means they do not speak the truth or use their tongues to bear true witness, even though they can talk. “Blind” means they do not see the signs of Allah, even though their eyes are open. The word “wilfully” in the chapter’s wording is crucial, because it shows that this deafness, dumbness, and blindness is chosen. It is not something forced upon them; it is the result of their own persistent refusal to hear, speak, and see the truth.\n\nThe verse then says, “so they will never return ˹to the Right Path˺.” The word “return” is significant, because it suggests they once had the opportunity to come back to the truth, and the door was open to them. But because they have locked themselves in their own wilful blindness, they will not return. This is not a denial of Allah’s mercy, which is vast and can reach anyone who sincerely seeks it. Rather, it describes the state of people who have so hardened themselves that they no longer seek the way back. They have closed the door from their own side.\n\nThis verse should be read together with the earlier description of the disbelievers, whose hearts, hearing, and sight were sealed. The three senses of hearing, sight, and speech cover the ways a person receives and shares the truth. When all three are blocked or misused, the person is effectively cut off from guidance. The Quran uses this image often to describe both open disbelievers and hypocrites. For the reader, the verse carries a strong warning: the faculties of hearing, sight, and speech are gifts, and a person who refuses to use them for the truth can lose their benefit. It also reminds the believer to be grateful for the ability to hear the Quran, see its signs, and speak the truth.",
  "19": "“Or ˹those caught in˺ a rainstorm from the sky with darkness, thunder, and lightning.”\n\nThis verse offers a second parable for the condition of the disbelievers, using a completely different image from the fire in the earlier verse. The word “Or” connects this new example to the previous one, presenting an alternative picture of the same reality. Here the image is a violent rainstorm, bringing darkness, thunder, and lightning. A person caught in such a storm is trapped and frightened. The darkness hides the way, the thunder terrifies, and the lightning flashes dangerously. It is an image of confusion and fear, which is exactly how the Quran describes the inner state of those who reject the truth.\n\nThe verse then gives a concrete detail: “They press their fingers into their ears at the sound of every thunder-clap for fear of death.” This is a vivid and human picture. Terrified by the roaring thunder, people cover their ears, as if that will protect them from what is coming. Scholars explain this as a picture of the disbelievers trying to block out the truth of the Quran. Just as the person presses fingers into ears to avoid the sound of thunder, the disbeliever tries to shut out the warning that reaches him, fearing what it might mean for him. But covering the ears does not stop the storm, and it does not stop the truth.\n\nThe verse ends with the sobering statement, “And Allah encompasses the disbelievers ˹by His might˺.” No matter how much they try to shield themselves, they cannot escape Allah. The word “encompasses” pictures Allah surrounding them on every side. Their attempt to hide from the storm is useless, because the One who controls the storm and everything else is already around them. This is a powerful reminder that no one can outrun the truth or escape the reality of Allah’s knowledge and power. The verse combines a vivid scene with a deep truth: those who flee from Allah by shutting their ears are, in the end, completely within His grasp.",
  "20": "“It is as if the lightning were about to snatch away their sight.”\n\nThis verse continues the storm parable from the previous verse, focusing now on the lightning. The image is of people in a thunderstorm whose eyes are nearly blinded by the flashes of lightning. The Quran says the lightning is “about to snatch away their sight,” showing how intense and frightening the light is to them. Then it gives a striking picture of their movement: “Whenever lightning strikes, they walk in its light, but when darkness covers them, they stand still.” In the brief moments of lightning they can see a little and move forward, but as soon as darkness returns, they freeze, unable to take another step. This is a perfect image of the disbeliever’s unstable condition. They get glimpses of the truth, and in those moments they seem to make progress, but the truth does not stay with them, so they fall back into confusion and stop.\n\nThe verse then adds, “Had Allah willed, He could have taken away their hearing and sight.” This shows that their situation is entirely under Allah’s control. If Allah had wished, their hearing and sight, meaning their ability to perceive and respond to truth, could have been removed entirely. But He did not remove them completely; they are left in a state of instability, moving and stopping, seeing and losing sight. This in-between state is part of the trial, and it shows both Allah’s justice and His restraint.\n\nThe verse closes with the firm declaration, “Surely Allah is Most Capable of everything.” Nothing is beyond His power, whether giving guidance, taking it away, punishing, or forgiving. This statement stands as a reminder that the whole scene, the storm, the lightning, the darkness, and the fate of the people in it, is under the command of Allah. For the reader, the parable teaches that a faith that only appears in moments of convenience is not a reliable guide. The true believer walks steadily in the light of guidance, while the one who relies on occasional flashes is forever stopping in the dark.",
  "21": "“O humanity! Worship your Lord, Who created you and those before you.”\n\nWith this verse, the Quran begins a new address that reaches out to all of humanity, not only to a particular people. The call is to worship Allah, and the reason given is deeply personal: He is the One “Who created you and those before you.” Worship is not presented as a random rule; it is tied to the very fact of creation. The One who brought every human being into existence deserves to be worshipped, because the created owe their very existence to their Creator. This appeal is meant to reach the heart of every person, regardless of when or where they live.\n\nThe verse explains the purpose of this worship with the words “so that you may become mindful ˹of Him˺.” The word for mindfulness, often translated as God-consciousness, points to a deep awareness of Allah that shapes a person’s choices and behaviour. Worship is not an end in itself that leaves the heart empty; its purpose is to produce this protective awareness of Allah. A person who truly worships Allah develops a sense of His presence, and that sense keeps him from evil and leads him toward good. This is why worship is prescribed: not to burden people, but to build in them a living relationship with their Lord.\n\nThe phrase “and those before you” places the reader in a long chain of creation. Every generation that came before was also created by Allah, so the call to worship is not new to this community alone. This connects the verse to the earlier parts of the chapter, which described the believers, the disbelievers, and the hypocrites, and now turns to a general invitation to all people. The argument is simple and powerful: you did not create yourself, and those who came before you did not create themselves, so the One who created you all is the only One worthy of your worship. This appeal to the universal fact of creation forms the foundation of the call to faith.",
  "22": "“˹He is the One˺ Who has made the earth a place of settlement for you and the sky a canopy.”\n\nThis verse continues the argument for worshipping Allah by pointing to the blessings around us. The earth has been made a “place of settlement,” meaning a stable and suitable home where human beings can live and build their lives. The sky is described as a “canopy,” a protective covering spread above the earth. These are not small matters; they are the basic conditions that make life possible. The verse reminds the reader to look at the world and see in it the care of the One who arranged everything for human benefit.\n\nThe verse then mentions more blessings: Allah “sends down rain from the sky, causing fruits to grow as a provision for you.” The rain and the fruits that grow from it are gifts that sustain life. The word “provision” points to everything that people need to live. By mentioning creation, the sky, the earth, rain, and food, the verse gathers together the necessities of human existence and shows that they all come from Allah. The message is that the very things a person relies on every day are signs pointing back to their Giver.\n\nThe verse ends with the command, “So do not knowingly set up equals to Allah ˹in worship˺.” This is the practical conclusion of all the blessings. After seeing that Allah alone created, settled, sheltered, and provided, a person should not turn to other things as partners in worship. The word “knowingly” is significant. It warns against making this mistake while knowing better. To worship idols, people, or created things alongside Allah is not only wrong; it is ungrateful, because it directs gratitude and worship away from the true Giver to those who never provided anything. The verse therefore connects the physical blessings that everyone enjoys to the spiritual duty of worshipping Allah alone. Looking at the world with understanding leads directly to monotheism.",
  "23": "“And if you are in doubt about what We have revealed to Our servant.”\n\nThis verse presents a bold challenge to anyone who doubts that the Quran is from Allah. It addresses those who are in doubt about “what We have revealed to Our servant,” meaning the Quran that was sent down to the Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him. The verse does not simply ask people to accept the Quran on faith alone; it invites them to test its claim. If a person truly doubts that this Book is from Allah, then let him try to produce something like it.\n\nThe exact challenge is to “produce a sûrah like it and call your helpers other than Allah, if what you say is true.” A sûrah is a chapter of the Quran, and the challenge is to bring even a single chapter that matches the Quran’s quality. The phrase “call your helpers other than Allah” tells the doubters to gather every resource they can, every writer, poet, and ally, and use them all in the attempt. The condition “if what you say is true” points out that if they really believed the Quran was human-made, they should be able to match it, since the Quran is written in their own language and uses their own letters and words.\n\nThis challenge is one of the great proofs of the Quran’s divine origin. The Quran was revealed in Arabic to a people famous for their mastery of language and poetry, yet they could not meet this challenge despite trying for years. Throughout history, no one has produced anything that truly equals the Quran. The challenge appears several times in the Quran, sometimes asking for a chapter, sometimes for ten chapters, and sometimes for the whole Book, always with the same result. For the sincere seeker, this verse is an invitation to examine the Quran honestly. For the one in doubt, it is a direct test: if you truly doubt, then try to match it. The inability to do so is itself a sign of its truth.",
  "24": "“But if you are unable to do so—and you will never be able to do so.”\n\nThis verse follows the challenge of the previous verse with a firm conclusion. The Quran declares that the doubters “are unable to do so,” and then adds the emphatic statement that “you will never be able to do so.” This is not a boast; it is a statement of certainty. No human effort, then or at any time, will ever produce something that truly matches the Quran. The inability to answer the challenge is itself evidence that the Book is from Allah, because the language, letters, and style used in the Quran were all available to the people being challenged, yet they still could not imitate it.\n\nThe verse then warns, “then fear the Fire fuelled with people and stones, which is prepared for the disbelievers.” Since the doubters cannot meet the challenge and the Quran is proven true, the reasonable response is to take its warnings seriously. The Fire is described with striking details: it is “fuelled with people and stones.” This means that the people who reject the truth become fuel for the Fire, and stones are mentioned as part of its fuel as well. Scholars note that in the worship of idols, stones and carved images were objects of devotion, so the mention of stones carries a strong meaning for a people who worshipped idols made of stone. Those very stones, and the people who worshipped them, are what the Fire is prepared with.\n\nThe phrase “prepared for the disbelievers” makes clear who the Fire awaits. This is not meant to frighten people into blind fear, but to warn them with honesty so they may turn to the truth before it is too late. The whole passage, from the challenge to produce a sûrah to the warning of the Fire, forms one connected argument: the Quran is true because it cannot be matched, so the wise response is to accept its guidance and fear the punishment it warns about.",
  "25": "“Give good news ˹O Prophet˺ to those who believe and do good.”\n\nThis verse turns from warnings to glad tidings. The Prophet is told to “give good news” to a particular group: those “who believe and do good.” Notice that belief is joined with action. Faith alone is not enough; it must be accompanied by good deeds. The pairing of belief and good works runs throughout the Quran, and it is placed at the very heart of this promise. The good news is meant for people whose faith is real and whose deeds match it.\n\nThe promise is described in beautiful detail: “they will have Gardens under which rivers flow.” This is a picture of Paradise, a place of lush gardens watered by rivers running beneath them. It is a place of ease, beauty, and comfort, the opposite of the Fire described in the previous verse. The verse then describes a remarkable experience of the people of Paradise: “Whenever provided with fruit, they will say, ‘This is what we were given before,’ for they will be served fruit that looks similar ˹but tastes different˺.” In Paradise, the fruit resembles what they enjoyed in this world, so they recognize it, but it is far better. This detail shows that the joy of Paradise is not entirely strange; it builds on the good things of this life, purified and perfected.\n\nThe verse also promises that the believers “will have pure spouses, and they will be there forever.” The mention of pure spouses points to the companionship of Paradise, free from the flaws and troubles of this world. And the phrase “they will be there forever” gives the deepest comfort of all: this joy is eternal, with no end and no fear of losing it. The verse stands as a gentle contrast to the warnings that came before. Those who reject the truth face a Fire prepared for them, while those who believe and do good are promised everlasting Gardens. The good news is a genuine invitation to respond to the Quran’s call with faith and righteous action.",
  "26": "“Surely Allah does not shy away from using the parable of a mosquito.”\n\nThis verse responds to those who mocked the Quran for using humble or simple examples. The previous verses used parables like fire and rainstorm, and now the Quran points out that Allah does not hesitate to use even the parable of a mosquito “or what is even smaller.” The disbelievers had mocked such examples, finding it beneath them. The Quran answers that Allah does not shy away from them, because a small example can carry a great truth. There is nothing shameful in teaching through simple images.\n\nThe verse then describes two different responses to these parables. “As for the believers, they know that it is the truth from their Lord.” The believers accept the parable with trust, recognizing it as guidance from Allah. “And as for the disbelievers, they argue, ‘What does Allah mean by such a parable?’” The disbelievers respond with pride and rejection, questioning why Allah would use such a thing. The difference between the two groups is not in the parable itself, but in the heart that receives it. The same example produces faith in one person and mockery in another.\n\nThe verse then makes an important point about how parables work: “Through this ˹test˺, He leaves many to stray, and guides many. And He leaves none to stray except the rebellious.” A single parable serves as a test. For the humble believer, it is a means of guidance. For the arrogant rejecter, it becomes a cause of straying. This is not because the parable itself is confusing, but because the rebellious heart refuses to accept it. The word “rebellious” describes those who persistently oppose the truth. The verse teaches that guidance is not mechanical; the same message can guide or misguide depending on the state of the heart. This is a profound and challenging truth, and it leads directly into the description of the rebellious that follows in the next verse.",
  "27": "“those who violate Allah’s covenant after it has been affirmed.”\n\nThis verse continues the sentence that began in the previous verse, describing who the rebellious are. The first quality is that they “violate Allah’s covenant after it has been affirmed.” Every human being has a covenant with Allah, the commitment to acknowledge Him as Lord and to obey Him. Scholars explain this covenant in different ways: it is the natural disposition that Allah placed in every person, and it is also the covenant taken through the messengers and scriptures. The rebellious violate this agreement, breaking the commitment after it was established. This makes their rebellion a deliberate breach of trust, not an act of ignorance.\n\nThe second quality is that they “break whatever ˹ties˺ Allah has ordered to be maintained.” Allah has commanded that certain bonds be kept strong, such as the ties of kinship, the bonds between believers, and the ties of honesty and justice. The rebellious cut these bonds, harming families, communities, and their own souls. Third, they “spread corruption in the land.” Wherever they go, their actions bring disorder and harm rather than peace and good. Taken together, these three qualities give a clear picture of the rebellious person: one who breaks his commitment to Allah, severs the bonds Allah commanded, and spreads corruption on earth.\n\nThe verse ends with the verdict, “It is they who are the ˹true˺ losers.” Though such people may appear successful in this world, the Quran calls them the real losers, because they have lost what truly matters: their covenant with Allah, their relationships, their good character, and ultimately their own salvation. The word “true” before losers emphasizes that their loss is genuine and certain, even if they do not see it. This verse connects directly back to the previous discussion of parables. The parable guides those who honor their covenant and corrupts none but the rebellious. The description of the rebellious here explains why such hearts fail to benefit from guidance.",
  "28": "“How can you deny Allah?”\n\nThis verse opens with a striking question designed to wake the reader up. “How can you deny Allah?” The question is addressed to human beings who reject their Lord, and it is meant to make them think. The rest of the verse gives the reason why denial makes no sense: “You were lifeless and He gave you life, then He will cause you to die and again bring you to life, and then to Him you will ˹all˺ be returned.” The argument is built on the simple fact of human existence. Every person was once nothing, without life, and it was Allah who brought them into being. How can someone who owes his very life to Allah deny the One who gave it?\n\nThe verse then moves from the past to the future. Allah “will cause you to die and again bring you to life.” Death is not the end; it is another stage on a journey that leads back to Allah. The resurrection is described as certain, and the final return to Allah is presented as the destination of every human being. The argument is both logical and deeply personal. The same power that brought a person from nothingness into life is more than able to bring that person back after death. If the first creation is a fact, then the second creation is not hard to imagine for the One who did the first.\n\nThe phrase “and then to Him you will ˹all˺ be returned” carries both a warning and a comfort. It is a warning because everyone will face what they have done, and it is a comfort because justice will finally be established. The question at the beginning, “How can you deny Allah?”, is answered by the whole verse: denial is unreasonable because life, death, and resurrection all point to Allah. This verse connects to the earlier call to worship the Lord who created people and those before them. The appeal to creation and the return to Allah form the twin foundations of faith in the Quran.",
  "29": "“He is the One Who created everything in the earth for you.”\n\nThis verse adds another layer to the argument for Allah’s power and generosity. Allah created everything on the earth for the benefit of humanity. The word “for you” shows that all of creation is placed at the service of human beings, to sustain them and meet their needs. This is a picture of divine care: the whole earth, with all its resources, was made as a provision for people. This idea appears elsewhere in the Quran, which teaches that Allah has subjected the heavens and the earth to human use.\n\nThe verse then describes the creation of the heavens: “Then He turned towards the heaven, forming it into seven heavens.” The Quran speaks of the heavens being shaped into seven heavens, a detail that points to the ordered structure of the universe. Scholars note that this is among the matters whose full reality only Allah knows, but the Quran states it as a truth to be accepted. The word “then” suggests a sequence in creation, though the precise nature of that sequence is understood to be part of Allah’s plan.\n\nThe verse ends with the declaration, “And He has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of all things.” This completes the picture of Allah as Creator. He not only creates the earth and the heavens; He also knows everything about them and everything in them. Nothing escapes His knowledge. The verse therefore moves from the visible world, the earth and the sky, to the invisible reality of Allah’s knowledge. For the reader, the message is that the One who created everything and knows everything is fully deserving of worship, and nothing in creation can be a partner to Him. The verse supports the call to worship Allah alone that runs through this entire section of the chapter.",
  "30": "“˹Remember˺ when your Lord said to the angels, ‘I am going to place a successive ˹human˺ authority on earth.’”\n\nThis verse begins the story of the creation of Adam, the first human being. The word “Remember” calls the reader to reflect on an event from the past. Allah told the angels that He was going to place a “successive ˹human˺ authority on earth,” meaning a being that would succeed one another across generations and act as Allah’s representative or vicegerent on earth. This is the human being, created to worship Allah and to act responsibly in the world.\n\nThe angels responded with a question: “Will You place in it someone who will spread corruption there and shed blood while we glorify Your praises and proclaim Your holiness?” The angels, in their great obedience, wondered why Allah would place a being that might cause corruption and bloodshed, when they themselves were constantly engaged in glorifying and declaring the holiness of Allah. Their question was not a rebellion or an objection born of arrogance; it was a seeking of understanding, a genuine expression of wonder. The Quran records it so that readers can learn from the dialogue.\n\nAllah responded, “I know what you do not know.” This short answer contains a deep truth. The angels did not know the full wisdom behind creating human beings, and Allah did, so He did not fully explain it to them at that moment. Later verses reveal part of that wisdom, as Adam is taught the names of things and the angels cannot match his knowledge. The verse teaches that Allah’s wisdom is greater than any creature can grasp. What may seem strange to the limited mind is carried out according to a perfect plan. The creation of humanity as a responsible authority on earth was not a mistake; it was a deliberate act filled with meaning that unfolds in the verses that follow.",
  "31": "“He taught Adam the names of all things.”\n\nThis verse moves the story of Adam forward. After announcing the creation of a human authority on earth, Allah “taught Adam the names of all things.” Scholars explain this as teaching Adam the names and realities of created things, giving him knowledge and the ability to name objects, animals, and concepts. This knowledge was a great honor, and it set human beings apart. The word “names” points not just to sounds but to the ability to know and distinguish things, to understand and communicate about creation.\n\nThe verse continues, “then He presented them to the angels and said, ‘Tell Me the names of these, if what you say is true?’” Allah presented these named things to the angels and challenged them to name them. The phrase “if what you say is true” refers back to the angels’ earlier question in the previous verse, where they wondered about placing a human authority on earth. Now Allah gives them a chance to demonstrate their own understanding. If they were more deserving of this authority, they should be able to name the things presented to them.\n\nThis verse sets up the test that follows. The angels, for all their obedience and worship, were given only what Allah taught them, and they could not name these things on their own. Adam, however, had been specially taught. The verse therefore reveals one of the great purposes of human creation: human beings were given knowledge and the capacity for understanding. This knowledge is part of what made humanity fit to carry responsibility on earth. The verse also shows that Allah deals with His creation through wisdom and proof, teaching through dialogue and testing rather than forcing. What follows in the next verses shows how the angels respond and how Adam demonstrates the knowledge he was given.",
  "32": "“They replied, ‘Glory be to You! We have no knowledge except what You have taught us.’”\n\nThis verse records the reply of the angels when they were challenged to name the things presented to them. They answered with humility and sincerity: “Glory be to You! We have no knowledge except what You have taught us.” The phrase “Glory be to You” is a declaration of Allah’s perfection and a recognition that He is far above any defect. The angels immediately acknowledged their own limitation. They did not pretend to know or make excuses; they simply confessed that whatever knowledge they had came from Allah, and that they could not go beyond what they had been taught.\n\nTheir reply is a beautiful model of humility. Instead of being offended by the challenge, they accepted it gracefully and turned to praising Allah. This stands in contrast to the arrogance that would later be shown by Iblis in the following verses. The angels’ response also contains a deep lesson about knowledge: all true knowledge comes from Allah. Human beings, and even angels, only know what Allah teaches them. This is why true learning is always connected to humility and gratitude toward the Giver of knowledge.\n\nThe angels then said, “You are truly the All-Knowing, All-Wise.” They affirmed two of Allah’s qualities. He is All-Knowing, because nothing escapes His knowledge, and All-Wise, because everything He does is done with perfect wisdom, even when creatures cannot fully grasp it. This reply answers the previous verse’s challenge while also accepting whatever Allah decided. The angels did not question the wisdom of placing a human authority on earth after seeing that Adam had been given knowledge they did not have. Their humble reply prepared the way for the next verse, where Adam is called upon to show his knowledge and demonstrate the very reason for his honored position.",
  "33": "“Allah said, ‘O Adam! Inform them of their names.’”\n\nThis verse completes the scene that began two verses earlier. After the angels admitted they had no knowledge except what Allah had taught them, Allah turned to Adam and told him to “Inform them of their names.” Adam, who had been taught the names of all things, then did as he was commanded. The verse shows Adam using the knowledge that had been given to him, and by doing so, he demonstrated the value of human creation and the wisdom behind placing humans as an authority on earth.\n\nAfter Adam informed the angels, Allah said, “Did I not tell you that I know the secrets of the heavens and the earth, and I know what you reveal and what you conceal?” This statement confirms that Allah knew all along how the event would unfold. The phrase “the secrets of the heavens and the earth” points to Allah’s knowledge of everything hidden, while “what you reveal and what you conceal” refers to the angels’ spoken question and whatever was in their hearts. Allah’s knowledge covers both the outward and the inward.\n\nThe story as a whole carries several lessons. It shows the dignity of human beings, who were given knowledge and the capacity to bear responsibility. It shows the humility of the angels, who accepted their limits and praised Allah. It shows the perfect knowledge and wisdom of Allah, who created humanity with a purpose that was not immediately obvious. And it sets the stage for the next event, when the angels are commanded to prostrate before Adam. The verse is part of the Quran’s explanation of why human beings were created and why they were honored. It also prepares the reader for the trial that follows with Iblis. The story also shows that knowledge is a gift from Allah and a source of honour when used rightly. Adam was given knowledge that the angels did not have, and that knowledge demonstrated the wisdom of human creation. At the same time, knowledge brings responsibility, because what is learned is meant to be used in obedience to Allah. The verse therefore connects the dignity of humanity, the humility of the angels, and the perfect knowledge of Allah, teaching the reader to value knowledge and to use it in serving the truth rather than in pride.",
  "34": "“And ˹remember˺ when We said to the angels, ‘Prostrate before Adam.’”\n\nThis verse takes the story of Adam to its next turning point. After Adam was taught the names of all things and showed his knowledge before the angels, Allah commanded the angels to “Prostrate before Adam.” This was a command of respect and honour, not an act of worship, because worship is due to Allah alone. The angels obeyed: “so they all did.” Their obedience was immediate and complete, showing both their devotion to Allah and their acceptance of the honour given to Adam.\n\nThe verse then records an exception: “but not Iblîs, who refused and acted arrogantly, becoming unfaithful.” Iblis is the name given in the Quran to the one who refused. Scholars explain that Iblis was among the jinn, a being created from fire, and he had been given a place among the angels. When the command to prostrate came, he refused. The Quran describes his refusal as rooted in arrogance, because he considered himself better than Adam and thought himself above the command. The word “becoming unfaithful” describes the result: by his refusal and pride, he turned away from obedience to Allah and became a disbeliever.\n\nThe story of Iblis is mentioned in several places in the Quran, and it always carries a warning. Pride is the root of his downfall. He judged by his own standards instead of submitting to Allah’s command. The verse therefore teaches that arrogance can lead even a being who was elevated to a position of honour to ruin. For Adam, the command to prostrate honoured the human being as a creature of knowledge and responsibility. For the reader, the lesson is clear: true honour lies in obedience to Allah, and the greatest danger is the pride that makes a person refuse to submit.",
  "35": "“We cautioned, ‘O Adam! Live with your wife in Paradise and eat as freely as you please.’”\n\nThis verse describes the command given to Adam and his wife after they were placed in Paradise. The word “cautioned” introduces a gentle warning along with the permission. Adam is told to “Live with your wife in Paradise and eat as freely as you please.” They were given generous freedom to enjoy the blessings of Paradise, eating from what they wished. This shows the abundance and ease that surrounded them, and it sets the background for the one restriction that follows.\n\nThe command continues: “but do not approach this tree, or else you will be wrongdoers.” One tree was singled out, and they were told not to approach it. The verse uses the words “do not approach,” which is even more careful than “do not eat,” because it warns them to stay away entirely so that they would not fall into the sin. The consequence is described as becoming “wrongdoers,” meaning that by disobeying they would wrong themselves. This is an important detail: the harm of disobedience falls first on the one who disobeys.\n\nThe presence of a single prohibition alongside wide permission shows the nature of the test. Adam and his wife were free to enjoy almost everything, yet they were asked to avoid one thing. This made their obedience meaningful, because they had a real choice. The verse teaches that a person can be surrounded by blessings and still fail by reaching for what was forbidden. It also shows that Allah’s commands are given with fairness; the test was small and clear. The story prepares the reader for the next verse, where Satan deceives them and they fall into the very thing they were warned against. The test in Paradise also reveals something about how Allah guides His servants. He did not forbid everything; He allowed Adam and his wife to enjoy the bounties of Paradise freely. This shows that Islam is not a religion of harsh denial but of balance, in which lawful enjoyment is blessed while disobedience to clear commands is warned against. The single prohibition made the trial meaningful and demonstrated that true obedience is the free choice to respect Allah’s limits, even when a person is surrounded by ease and plenty.",
  "36": "“But Satan deceived them—leading to their fall from the ˹blissful˺ state they were in.”\n\nThis verse records the tragic result of the temptation. “But Satan deceived them.” The word for Satan refers to the one who refused to prostrate, and it describes how he led Adam and his wife astray. The Quran elsewhere tells how he whispered to them, tempting them to approach the forbidden tree. The deception worked, and they fell from their “˹blissful˺ state,” losing the ease and innocence they had enjoyed. Their disobedience brought about the change the warning had spoken of.\n\nThe verse then gives Allah’s command: “Descend from the heavens ˹to the earth˺ as enemies to each other.” Adam and his wife, and through them their descendants, were sent down to the earth. The phrase “as enemies to each other” is understood by scholars to describe the enmity between human beings and Satan, who now openly sought to mislead them. It may also refer to the hostility that enters human relationships when evil enters the world. Either way, the descent to earth marked the beginning of human life in this world.\n\nThe verse ends with a reassuring note: “You will find in the earth a residence and provision for your appointed stay.” Even in this new situation, Allah did not abandon them. The earth was to be their dwelling place, and provision was to be found in it, all for an “appointed stay,” meaning a fixed period that would end with death and the return to Allah. The verse balances the consequence of sin with continued mercy. Adam and his wife left Paradise, but they were not left without care, and the door of returning to Allah remained open, as the next verse shows. The fall was real, but it was not the end of the story.",
  "37": "“Then Adam was inspired with words ˹of prayer˺ by his Lord.”\n\nThis verse shows the mercy of Allah after the fall. “Then Adam was inspired with words ˹of prayer˺ by his Lord.” Scholars explain that Adam was taught words to say in repentance, words through which he acknowledged his wrong and sought forgiveness. The Quran elsewhere records such a prayer, where Adam and his wife say that they have wronged themselves and that if Allah does not forgive them, they will be among the losers. Here the Quran summarizes this by saying that Adam was inspired with words of prayer.\n\nBecause of these words, the verse says, “so He accepted his repentance.” Allah accepted Adam’s turning back. This shows that repentance is met with mercy. When a person sincerely acknowledges his mistake, seeks forgiveness, and turns back to Allah, Allah accepts it. The verse therefore stands as one of the greatest sources of hope in the Quran. The very first human act of disobedience was met with a path of return.\n\nThe verse ends with the declaration, “Surely He is the Accepter of Repentance, Most Merciful.” These two names, the Accepter of Repentance and the Most Merciful, become a recurring refrain in this chapter. They remind the reader that Allah’s mercy is open to those who turn to Him. The story of Adam is not only history; it is a pattern. Every human being can fall into error, and every human being can return to Allah through sincere repentance. The verse also connects to the earlier account of the fall, showing that sin does not close the door of mercy. What matters is what a person does after the mistake: whether he turns back to his Lord or turns further away. The Quran here teaches a pattern that every believer can follow. When a person slips, the path back is not despair but repentance. Adam did not blame others or refuse to accept his mistake; he turned to his Lord in humility, and his Lord accepted him. This is why the verse is such a comfort, because it shows that Allah’s mercy reaches even the first sin and that the way back is always open to those who sincerely seek it.",
  "38": "“We said, ‘Descend all of you!’”\n\nThis verse repeats the command to descend to the earth, and it addresses all who were involved. “We said, ‘Descend all of you!’” The word “all” makes clear that no one remained in the previous state. Adam, his wife, and their adversary all went down to the earth. This descent marked the beginning of human life on earth, the place of testing where people would live and then die, before being raised again. It is the setting for everything that follows in the Quran’s guidance.\n\nThe verse then gives the most important promise in this passage: “Then when guidance comes to you from Me, whoever follows it, there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve.” Allah did not send human beings to earth and leave them without a way back. He promised that guidance would come, and that whoever follows it would be safe. The promise uses two reassuring words: “no fear” and “nor will they grieve.” Scholars explain that fear looks toward the future, while grief looks toward the past, so this promise covers both directions of a person’s worry. The one who follows Allah’s guidance is protected from the fear of what is to come and the grief over what has passed.\n\nThis verse explains why human life on earth has meaning. It is a journey in which Allah has provided guidance as a lamp. The one who follows it walks in safety, and the one who rejects it faces the consequences. The verse also looks forward to the sending of messengers and books, the means through which this guidance would come. For the reader, the promise is deeply personal: guidance is available, and following it removes the deepest fears. This is the hope that stands at the centre of the Quran’s message, and it appears again and again, including in the very next verse which warns of the opposite path.",
  "39": "“But those who disbelieve and deny Our signs will be the residents of the Fire.”\n\nThis verse sets out the other side of the promise that was given in the previous verse. There, those who follow guidance were promised no fear and no grief. Here, “those who disbelieve and deny Our signs” are told what awaits them. The verse pairs disbelief with denial of Allah’s signs, because the two belong together. Disbelief is the refusal of faith, and denying the signs is the refusal to accept the evidence and messages that Allah has sent, whether through creation, scriptures, or messengers.\n\nTheir end is stated plainly: “will be the residents of the Fire.” The word “residents” pictures people who live in a place as their permanent home, and the verse confirms this by adding, “They will be there forever.” There is no temporary stay, no way out. The description of the Fire as a place of residence, and the explicit statement of eternity, removes any false hope that the punishment might come to an end. The verse is a warning stated without ambiguity.\n\nPlaced immediately after the promise of guidance, this verse completes the two possible outcomes of human life. Allah presented the way of guidance and the way of denial, and each leads to its own end. The contrast is sharp and is meant to move the reader toward the path of faith. It is important to note the verse does not speak of believers being in the Fire forever; it speaks specifically of those who disbelieve and deny the signs. This is a central teaching of the Quran, and it gives the warning its meaning. The promise of safety and the warning of the Fire stand together, inviting every reader to choose the path that leads to peace. The verse also reinforces the justice of Allah. The people who follow guidance are promised peace, and the people who deny the signs are warned of the Fire. This is not arbitrary; it is the natural consequence of each person’s own choice. Denying the signs after they have been made clear is a deliberate act, and the Quran states plainly that its end is the Fire. The clarity of this warning is itself a mercy, because it gives people the information they need to turn to the right path before it is too late.",
  "40": "“O children of Israel! Remember My favours upon you.”\n\nWith this verse, the Quran begins a long address to the children of Israel, the descendants of the prophets Jacob, Joseph, and the others among them. The call is to “Remember My favours upon you.” The word “remember” is a call to bring to mind and keep in heart all the blessings Allah had given them. Throughout history, the children of Israel had received many favours: rescue from Egypt, food from the heavens, water from a rock, prophets among them, and divine scriptures. The verse asks them to recall these favours so that gratitude would lead them to obedience.\n\nThe verse then makes a covenant: “Fulfil your covenant and I will fulfil Mine.” Allah had made a covenant with the children of Israel, and they were asked to fulfil their part of it. Their part involved belief, obedience, and worship of Allah alone. Allah’s part involved His protection, mercy, and reward. This mutual commitment is the framework of the whole address. If they fulfilled their side, Allah would fulfil His. The verse places responsibility on them and opens a door of hope through faithfulness.\n\nThe verse ends with the command, “and stand in awe of Me ˹alone˺.” The word for awe carries the sense of mindfulness and reverence, a deep awareness of Allah that shapes behaviour. It is directed to Allah alone, reminding them that their awe and fear belong to no one else. This command ties the whole verse together: gratitude for favours, faithfulness to the covenant, and reverence for Allah. For the reader, the verse shows that divine favour is meant to produce gratitude and obedience, and it establishes the pattern of the entire address to the children of Israel, which continues in the following verses with further reminders and commands.",
  "41": "“Believe in My revelations which confirm your Scriptures.”\n\nThis verse calls the children of Israel to believe in the new revelation, the Quran, which confirms what they already had. The Quran does not cancel or contradict the earlier scriptures in matters of the fundamentals of faith; it confirms them and calls people back to their original truth. By saying “My revelations which confirm your Scriptures,” the verse establishes that the Quran and the earlier books share the same source, the one Allah, and that belief in the Quran is not a departure from their own heritage but a fulfilment of it.\n\nThe verse then warns, “Do not be the first to deny them or trade them for a fleeting gain.” The children of Israel were among the first to be given the news of the coming final messenger, and they were being warned not to be the first to reject him when he came. The phrase “trade them for a fleeting gain” points to the danger of exchanging the truth for worldly benefits, such as positions, wealth, or approval. The Quran warns in several places against selling the signs of Allah for a small price. To do so is to lose the lasting value of truth for something temporary.\n\nThe verse ends with the command, “And be mindful of Me.” Mindfulness of Allah, often translated as God-consciousness, means keeping Allah in view in all one’s affairs. The three commands in this verse work together: believe in the Quran that confirms your scriptures, do not be the first to reject it, and do not sell the truth for worldly gain. All of this is gathered under the umbrella of being mindful of Allah. The verse is a direct appeal to the children of Israel to accept the truth that was revealed to the final messenger, and it shows that their own scriptures had prepared them to recognize it.",
  "42": "“Do not mix truth with falsehood or hide the truth knowingly.”\n\nThis verse gives a clear moral command to the children of Israel. “Do not mix truth with falsehood.” This warns against blending what is true with what is false in such a way that the two become confused. It can mean mixing false ideas and claims into the scriptures, and it can also mean, more broadly, presenting falsehood in the dress of truth so that people cannot distinguish between them. The command protects the purity of truth and warns against the corruption that comes from tampering with it.\n\nThe verse then adds, “or hide the truth knowingly.” Hiding the truth is a separate and serious sin. This refers to concealing what a person knows to be true, such as the descriptions of the coming messenger that were found in their scriptures. The verse uses the word “knowingly,” which makes the sin clear: these people knew the truth and chose to hide it. Hiding the truth out of envy, pride, or worldly interest is a betrayal, because it deprives others of guidance they deserve to receive.\n\nThe two commands in this verse address the two main ways people corrupt the truth. The first is adding falsehood to it, mixing lies with it. The second is removing parts of it, hiding what is true. Both destroy the purity of the message, and both are condemned. The verse is part of the Quran’s address to the people of scripture, warning them about the misuse of knowledge. It also carries a universal lesson for every person who holds knowledge: the truth must be preserved from distortion and shared openly. Knowledge is a trust, and hiding or corrupting it is a violation of that trust. This command connects naturally to the reminder of favours and covenants that surrounds it in the address to the children of Israel.",
  "43": "“Establish prayer, pay alms-tax, and bow down with those who bow down.”\n\nThis verse commands three acts of worship that are central to Islam. “Establish prayer” means to perform prayer in a complete and continuous manner, with proper times, conditions, and concentration. Prayer is the regular connection between the servant and Allah, and establishing it means giving it its full due rather than neglecting or rushing it. The command appears throughout the Quran and is one of the pillars of the faith.\n\nThe second command is to “pay alms-tax.” This refers to the charity that is due from a person’s wealth, a set portion given to the poor and needy. Where the word for prayer points to the worship of the body, alms-tax points to the worship of wealth. The two are often mentioned together in the Quran, because a person’s faith is shown both in devotion to Allah and in care for others. Giving alms purifies wealth and helps build a just and compassionate society.\n\nThe third command is to “bow down with those who bow down.” This calls them to join the community of believers in prayer, performing the bowing that is part of it alongside others. The phrase emphasizes unity and congregation, the idea of worshipping together rather than in isolation. It also calls the children of Israel to join the early Muslims in the worship of the one true Allah. The three commands together paint a picture of complete devotion: prayer to Allah, charity to people, and unity with the community. This verse is part of the address to the children of Israel, but its commands are universal, forming the very heart of Islamic practice. It connects to the surrounding verses that call them to faith, mindfulness, and gratitude for Allah’s favours. The three commands in this verse also show the complete shape of devotion. Prayer connects the servant to Allah, alms connects the servant to the community, and bowing with others joins the servant to the body of believers. None of these stands alone. A faith that prays but neglects the needy, or that gives charity but never worships, is incomplete. By bringing all three together, the verse teaches that worship, charity, and community are inseparable parts of true religion. This is why the Quran repeats this trio throughout, always calling the believer to a faith that is lived in all its dimensions.",
  "44": "“Do you preach righteousness and fail to practice it yourselves?”\n\nThis verse confronts a painful contradiction. “Do you preach righteousness and fail to practice it yourselves?” The people being addressed were teaching others to do right while not doing right themselves. The Quran points out this inconsistency sharply, asking whether it makes sense to tell others to be righteous while neglecting one’s own conduct. The question is meant to wake them up and make them examine themselves.\n\nThe verse adds, “although you read the Scripture?” This detail makes the contradiction even more striking. They were people who read the scripture, who knew its commands, and yet they did not practice what they preached. Reading the scripture should have reminded them of their duties and held them accountable. Instead, they used their knowledge to instruct others while ignoring it in their own lives. The Quran is pointing out that knowledge without practice is of little benefit and that those who know are even more responsible.\n\nThe verse ends with the question, “Do you not understand?” This question calls them to reason and reflect. Anyone who thinks clearly can see that preaching what one does not practice is hypocrisy and self-contradiction. The verse therefore condemns the gap between words and deeds. This is a lesson that applies to every believer, not only to the children of Israel. It warns against being people who say but do not do, who teach righteousness while living without it. True righteousness is not only spoken; it is lived. The verse is connected to the surrounding commands to establish prayer and pay alms, showing that genuine worship must be accompanied by sincere practice in one’s own life. The warning is not addressed only to scholars of the past. It reaches every person who speaks about religion, because those who call others to goodness bear a greater responsibility to live it themselves. A society that sees its teachers and leaders practice what they preach is far more likely to be guided, while one that sees a gap between words and deeds grows confused and skeptical. The verse therefore protects both the integrity of the one who teaches and the trust of those who listen.",
  "45": "“And seek help through patience and prayer.”\n\nThis verse gives practical guidance for facing the difficulties of life. “And seek help through patience and prayer.” When a person faces trials, hardships, or the struggle to obey Allah, he is told to seek help through patience and through prayer. Patience is the inner strength that allows a person to endure without giving up, and prayer is the direct connection to Allah that brings comfort and strength. Together they form a powerful means of facing hardship. This is why the Prophet, peace be upon him, would turn to prayer during times of difficulty, and why the Quran repeats this guidance in a later verse addressed to the believers.\n\nThe verse then says, “Indeed, it is a burden except for the humble.” The word “it” refers to prayer, and the verse acknowledges that prayer can feel heavy and difficult for some people. It is a burden for those who pray without the right spirit, those who go through the motions without humility. But for the humble, those who recognize their need for Allah and approach Him with sincerity, prayer is not a burden; it is a source of ease. The difference is in the heart, not in the prayer itself.\n\nThe next verse will explain who the humble are, but the point here is already clear. Prayer and patience are the believer’s tools for seeking help. When a person is patient, he does not despair. When a person prays, he does not rely on his own strength alone. The verse teaches that these two are connected: patience keeps a person steady, and prayer keeps him connected to Allah. For the reader facing difficulty, this is a practical command: do not face hardship alone, but turn to patience and prayer, and do so with a humble heart that truly relies on Allah.",
  "46": "“those who are certain that they will meet their Lord and to Him they will return.”\n\nThis verse explains who the humble people are that were mentioned in the previous verse. The description is given in terms of their certainty about the Hereafter: they are “those who are certain that they will meet their Lord and to Him they will return.” The word “certain” points to firm, settled belief, not vague hope. These humble believers have no doubt that they will stand before their Lord and that their journey will end with a return to Him. This certainty shapes everything about their lives, including how they pray and how they face hardship.\n\nBecause they are certain they will meet their Lord, they take their worship seriously and do not feel prayer is a burden. The certainty of the return gives meaning to their patience, because they know that whatever they endure in this world is temporary and will be met with justice in the next. A person who truly believes he will return to Allah lives with an awareness that his actions matter and that he will give an account for them. This is what makes prayer easy for the humble: they are not praying into emptiness; they are speaking to the Lord they are certain to meet.\n\nThe connection between this verse and the previous one is direct. The humble are those who combine patience and prayer with a living certainty of the Hereafter. The Quran frequently links ease of worship with belief in the meeting with Allah. When faith in the return is strong, obedience becomes lighter and trials become bearable. The verse therefore teaches that belief in the Hereafter is not an abstract idea but a practical force that changes how a person lives, prays, and endures. It is this certainty that transforms the burden of worship into a source of help.",
  "47": "“O Children of Israel! Remember ˹all˺ the favours I granted you.”\n\nThis verse renews the address to the children of Israel with a call to remember. “O Children of Israel! Remember ˹all˺ the favours I granted you.” The verse uses the same call that appeared earlier in the chapter, reminding them of the many blessings Allah had bestowed upon them. The word “all” emphasizes the abundance and variety of these favours. They were not few or ordinary; they were numerous and extraordinary, given throughout their history.\n\nThe verse then adds, “and how I honoured you above the others.” This refers to the special status Allah gave to the children of Israel at that time, choosing them above the nations of their era. They were chosen to receive prophets, scriptures, and divine guidance. Scholars explain that this honour was for that particular time and purpose, not a permanent privilege, and the Quran is reminding them of it to stir gratitude and responsibility. The honour they received was meant to lead them to obedience, not to pride.\n\nThe call to remember favours serves a clear purpose. Remembering what Allah has done for a person naturally leads to gratitude, and gratitude leads to obedience. The verse is asking the children of Israel to recall their history so that they would recognize who their true Lord was and respond to Him properly. This verse is part of a series of reminders that run through the chapter, each calling them back to faith and gratitude. For the reader, the verse teaches a general principle: recalling Allah’s blessings is a powerful way to strengthen faith and motivate obedience. It also shows that divine favour carries with it a duty to respond with thankfulness and faithfulness. Gratitude is one of the central themes of this entire chapter. By asking them to remember, the Quran is training their hearts to acknowledge that everything they had came from Allah. When a people forget the favours they have received, they become ungrateful and disobedient; when they remember, they are filled with thankfulness and a sense of duty. The verse therefore is not simply recalling the past; it is building the attitude that makes obedience natural.",
  "48": "“Guard yourselves against the Day on which no soul will be of help to another.”\n\nThis verse warns about the Day of Judgment in vivid terms. “Guard yourselves against the Day on which no soul will be of help to another.” On that day, every person will face the consequences of his own deeds, and no one will be able to help another. The ties that seem so strong in this world will not avail anyone on that day. Parents cannot save their children, friends cannot save each other, and no amount of wealth or status will be of any use. Each soul will stand alone before its Lord.\n\nThe verse then lists what will not happen: “No intercession will be accepted, no ransom taken, and no help will be given.” The people being addressed in the chapter had various false hopes, such as relying on their prophets, their ancestry, or their claim of being Allah’s chosen people. The verse removes all such hopes. No intercession will be accepted on behalf of the disbeliever, no ransom, however great, will be accepted to buy freedom, and no help will come from any side. The warning is complete and leaves no loophole.\n\nThe purpose of this warning is not to despair people but to wake them up. If no one can help on that day, then the only reasonable course is to take care of oneself now, before the day comes, by believing and doing good. This is what “guard yourselves” means: to protect oneself from the punishment by righteous action. The verse connects to the surrounding address to the children of Israel, who needed to be reminded that their status, ancestry, and claims would not protect them. It also carries a universal lesson: every person is responsible for his own deeds, and the time to prepare for the Day of Judgment is now, in this life, through faith and good works.",
  "49": "“˹Remember˺ how We delivered you from the people of Pharaoh.”\n\nThis verse begins to list the historical favours that the children of Israel were asked to remember. The first and greatest of these was deliverance from the people of Pharaoh, the ruler of Egypt who had enslaved them. The verse says Allah delivered them “from the people of Pharaoh, who afflicted you with dreadful torment.” This torment is then described: “slaughtering your sons and keeping your women.” Pharaoh’s cruelty included killing the newborn sons of the Israelites out of fear and keeping the women and girls alive. This was a policy of oppression designed to weaken and humiliate the people.\n\nThe verse ends by calling this whole experience “a severe test from your Lord.” The suffering itself was a test, testing their patience, faith, and trust in Allah. The children of Israel were being refined through hardship, and their deliverance from such a tyrant was a favour so great that it should have produced lasting gratitude and faithfulness. The story of Moses, the Exodus, and the rescue from Pharaoh is one of the most repeated narratives in the Quran, and it is always told to teach lessons of faith, patience, and trust in Allah.\n\nThis verse is the first of several historical reminders that the Quran gives to the children of Israel in this chapter. Each reminder points back to a favour from Allah and calls them to respond with gratitude and obedience. The mention of Pharaoh’s slaughter of sons also connects to the earlier themes of the chapter, where oppression and tyranny are contrasted with the justice and mercy of Allah. For the reader, the verse teaches that deliverance from oppression is a profound blessing, and it reminds people who have been saved from hardship to remember and be thankful to the One who saved them.",
  "50": "“And ˹remember˺ when We parted the sea, rescued you, and drowned Pharaoh’s people before your very eyes.”\n\nThis verse continues the account of the favours given to the children of Israel. “And ˹remember˺ when We parted the sea.” When the Israelites were fleeing from Pharaoh and his army, with the sea before them and their enemies behind them, Allah parted the sea so that they could cross on dry land. This was a miracle of immense power and mercy. The verse says Allah “rescued you,” confirming that the parting of the sea was a deliverance.\n\nThe same verse records the fate of their enemies: “and drowned Pharaoh’s people before your very eyes.” As the children of Israel crossed safely, the waters returned and drowned the pursuing army of Pharaoh. The phrase “before your very eyes” emphasizes that they witnessed this with their own sight. They saw their oppressors destroyed, and they saw their own salvation. This was a sign they could never forget, and it is recorded in the Quran as a proof of Allah’s power and justice.\n\nThe verse is one of the many signs that the Quran recalls to the children of Israel. The parting of the sea is mentioned in several places in the Quran, each time serving as evidence of Allah’s might and of His care for those who believe. For the Israelites, this event should have produced lasting faith, gratitude, and trust. For the reader, the verse teaches that Allah is able to save His servants from the most hopeless situations and to deal justly with oppressors. No obstacle, not even a sea, can stand against the command of Allah when He wills to rescue His people. This favour is recorded not merely as history but as a lasting sign. The Quran returns to it time and again, because it demonstrates in one event two of Allah’s most important attributes: His power to save and His justice against oppressors. For the believer, the parting of the sea is a reminder that no human difficulty is truly impossible for Allah to remove, and that patience and trust are never wasted, even when the way forward seems completely blocked. What looked like a dead end became the very path of deliverance.",
  "51": "“And ˹remember˺ when We appointed forty nights for Moses.”\n\nThis verse turns to another event in the history of the children of Israel. “And ˹remember˺ when We appointed forty nights for Moses.” This refers to the time when Moses was called to meet his Lord for forty nights, a period during which he received the scripture. Scholars explain that Moses went up to the mountain to be alone with Allah for this appointed time. During his absence, something went wrong among his people.\n\nThe verse records what happened: “then you worshipped the calf in his absence, acting wrongfully.” While Moses was away, his people took a calf made of gold and worshipped it, falling into idolatry. The verse calls this action wrongful, and it is a striking detail in the Quran’s narrative. The people who had been rescued from Egypt, who had witnessed the parting of the sea, and who had received so many favours, fell into worshipping a calf they had made with their own hands. This shows how quickly people can turn from the truth when their prophet is absent.\n\nThe event is a powerful lesson. The worship of the calf is mentioned several times in the Quran as an example of ingratitude and weakness. Despite the miracles they had seen, some of the people still fell into idolatry. The verse also sets up the next verse, where Allah forgave them despite this terrible act. For the reader, the story teaches the danger of turning away from Allah and His prophets, and the ease with which people can slip back into wrong belief when they are not firmly grounded. It also shows that even great signs do not guarantee faith in every heart. The story also illustrates a deep truth about human nature: worship is not always firm in the heart even when miracles are seen. The same people who crossed the parted sea turned to a calf within a short time. This shows that faith must be built on more than spectacular signs; it must be built on sincere hearts, constant remembrance, and the teaching of prophets. The verse therefore points to why messengers, books, and reminders are needed, because people forget, stray, and need to be called back.",
  "52": "“Even then We ˹still˺ forgave you so perhaps you would be grateful.”\n\nThis verse reveals the extent of Allah’s mercy toward the children of Israel. “Even then We ˹still˺ forgave you.” The words “even then” point back to the terrible act described in the previous verse, the worship of the calf while Moses was away. Despite such a grave sin, Allah forgave them. This forgiveness was an extraordinary mercy, and it is recorded in the Quran so that its depth can be appreciated. The people who had worshipped the calf were given another chance.\n\nThe verse explains the purpose of this forgiveness: “so perhaps you would be grateful.” The forgiveness was not only to spare them from punishment; it was meant to awaken gratitude in their hearts. Gratitude would lead them to obedience and to faithfulness in the future. The verse shows that divine mercy is often given with a purpose, to draw the heart of the servant back to Allah and to remind them of His goodness.\n\nThe forgiveness after the worship of the calf is one of the clearest examples in the Quran of Allah’s mercy toward those who repent and return. It is mentioned in the same passage that describes the path of repentance, and it stands as a hope for all who have fallen into sin. For the reader, the verse teaches that no sin is beyond Allah’s forgiveness for the one who turns back sincerely. It also shows that forgiveness carries a responsibility: those who are forgiven are meant to become grateful, and gratitude is shown through obedience. The mercy of Allah and the duty of thankfulness belong together. The forgiveness mentioned here came after sincere repentance, and it teaches that Allah does not treat a returning servant as though the past never mattered. Rather, He accepts the one who turns back and wipes away the sin. This is a message of immense hope, because it shows that the door of mercy remains open even after serious sin. At the same time, the purpose of being forgiven was to produce gratitude, and gratitude in turn was meant to keep them from repeating the same mistake. Forgiveness is a new beginning, and a new beginning carries a responsibility.",
  "53": "“And ˹remember˺ when We gave Moses the Scripture—the standard ˹to distinguish between right and wrong˺.”\n\nThis verse completes the series of favours with the gift of the scripture. “And ˹remember˺ when We gave Moses the Scripture.” The scripture given to Moses is the Torah, and it was a great blessing for the children of Israel, providing them with divine law and guidance. The verse describes it as “the standard ˹to distinguish between right and wrong˺.” The word for this standard, often transliterated as furqan, points to something that separates and distinguishes, a criterion by which truth is told from falsehood and right from wrong.\n\nThe purpose of this gift is given at the end of the verse: “that perhaps you would be ˹rightly˺ guided.” The scripture was given so that the people could be guided to the truth and live according to it. A standard that distinguishes right from wrong is only useful if people actually use it. The gift of the scripture came with the expectation that they would follow its guidance and be led to the right path.\n\nThe whole series of reminders that began a few verses earlier now comes to a point. The children of Israel were given deliverance from Pharaoh, the parting of the sea, provision in the wilderness, forgiveness after the worship of the calf, and the scripture as a standard of guidance. All of these favours were meant to produce gratitude and obedience. By listing them, the Quran is calling them to remember how much Allah had given them and how much they owed in return. For the reader, the verse teaches the value of the scripture as a gift from Allah and the importance of using it as the standard for distinguishing truth from falsehood. Guidance is available, but it must be followed.",
  "54": "“And ˹remember˺ when Moses said to his people, ‘O my people! Surely you have wronged yourselves by worshipping the calf.’”\n\nThis verse records how Moses addressed his people after he returned and found them worshipping the calf. He did not begin with blame that crushed them; he first told them the truth about what they had done. “Surely you have wronged yourselves by worshipping the calf.” The wrong they committed was not done against Allah in the sense that it could harm Him; it was done against themselves, because by worshipping the calf they destroyed their own souls and brought punishment upon themselves. This way of speaking runs throughout the Quran: sin is called a wronging of oneself, because the sinner is the one who loses.\n\nMoses then gave them the path back: “so turn in repentance to your Creator and execute ˹the calf-worshippers among˺ yourselves.” The phrase “execute the calf-worshippers among yourselves” has been understood by scholars as a command that the people, after repenting, were to punish those among them who had taken the calf as a god, in order to purify the community. This was a severe but necessary step for that time and situation, carried out in the manner Allah commanded. The verse then explains why this was required: “That is best for you in the sight of your Creator.” The harsh but just measure was better for them than leaving the corruption in their midst.\n\nThe verse ends with the good news of mercy: “Then He accepted your repentance. Surely He is the Accepter of Repentance, Most Merciful.” Once they turned back and carried out what was commanded, Allah accepted their repentance. The two names at the end, the Accepter of Repentance and the Most Merciful, echo what was said after Adam’s repentance earlier in the chapter. This repetition is deliberate; it teaches that Allah’s mercy follows the sincere repentance of His servants, whether in the time of Adam or in the time of Moses. The door of return is always open for those who turn back to their Creator with sincere hearts.",
  "55": "“And ˹remember˺ when you said, ‘O Moses! We will never believe you until we see Allah with our own eyes.’”\n\nThis verse records another moment of insolence and weakness from the children of Israel. They said to Moses, “We will never believe you until we see Allah with our own eyes.” They were not satisfied with the miracles and signs they had already witnessed; they demanded to see Allah directly. This demand was both unreasonable and arrogant, because seeing Allah in this life is not possible for human beings, and they were testing the limits of what faith should accept. Their demand came from a hardened heart that kept finding new excuses not to believe.\n\nThe consequence was immediate and severe: “so a thunderbolt struck you while you were looking on.” The verse does not describe the full punishment in detail, but it tells the reader that a thunderbolt struck them while they were watching, and the next verse reveals that this meant death. The demand to see Allah was met with a punishment that showed them, in the harshest way, the greatness of the One they had challenged. Their request was not granted in the manner they imagined; instead, they were struck down.\n\nThe story carries an important lesson about how people approach Allah. Faith does not mean refusing to believe until every demand is met; it means trusting what Allah and His messengers have shown. The children of Israel had seen miracles that should have filled their hearts with certainty, yet they still asked for more. This is a warning against the arrogant demand for ever more signs, and a reminder that belief should come from the heart and from the evidence Allah has already provided. For the reader, the verse teaches gratitude for the signs one has and humility in approaching Allah, rather than testing Him with impossible demands.",
  "56": "“Then We brought you back to life after your death.”\n\nThis verse records the mercy that followed the punishment described in the previous verse. After the thunderbolt struck the children of Israel and they died, Allah “brought you back to life after your death.” The event is a clear proof of Allah’s power over life and death. Those who had been struck down were raised again, which showed that Allah alone gives life and takes it, and that He is able to bring the dead back whenever He wills. For the people of Israel, this was both a lesson and a second chance.\n\nThe verse gives the purpose of this resurrection: “so that perhaps you would be grateful.” Being brought back to life was such an extraordinary favour that it should have filled them with gratitude and humility. Anyone who has been given back something he had lost feels thankfulness, and being given back life itself is the greatest gift of all. The verse shows that Allah’s mercy repeatedly gives His servants new chances, and that each new chance is meant to produce gratitude and obedience.\n\nThis event is part of the long series of favours that the Quran recalls to the children of Israel. The same people who demanded to see Allah were shown, through their death and resurrection, how little they controlled and how much they depended on Him. For the reader, the verse teaches that life is a gift from Allah, that death is in His hand, and that resurrection is not beyond His power. It also teaches gratitude: a person who truly understands that his life is a gift will use it in obedience to the Giver. The mention of life after death here also points forward to the final resurrection, when all people will be raised to meet their Lord.",
  "57": "“And ˹remember when˺ We shaded you with clouds and sent down to you manna and quails.”\n\nThis verse continues the account of Allah’s favours to the children of Israel during their journey in the wilderness. “We shaded you with clouds” refers to the shelter Allah provided for them in the harsh desert, a shade from the burning heat. This was a constant care that accompanied them. The verse then mentions their food: “and sent down to you manna and quails.” Manna was a sweet provision that came down from above, and quails were birds they could eat. Together, they formed a complete food supply in a place where food was scarce. Allah provided for their needs without their effort.\n\nThe command that accompanied this provision was to “Eat from the good things We have provided for you.” The food was not only sufficient; it was good and pure. They were simply asked to eat it with gratitude. The verse then makes an important point about their later behaviour: “The evildoers ˹certainly˺ did not wrong Us, but wronged themselves.” When the people complained, disobeyed, or rejected this provision, they did not harm Allah, because Allah has no need of their gratitude or obedience. The harm fell on themselves, because by their ingratitude and disobedience they lost the favour and brought punishment upon their own souls.\n\nThis verse is a reminder that provision, shelter, and food are all signs of Allah’s care, and that they should be met with gratitude. The theme of wronging oneself through ingratitude runs throughout the Quran. When a person refuses the good gifts of Allah or uses them in disobedience, the loss is entirely his own. For the reader, the verse teaches appreciation for the daily blessings that are easy to take for granted, such as food, shelter, and protection. It also teaches that gratitude is not a small matter; it is the difference between receiving more favour and losing what one has.",
  "58": "“And ˹remember˺ when We said, ‘Enter this city and eat freely from wherever you please.’”\n\nThis verse moves to another event in the history of the children of Israel. “And ˹remember˺ when We said, ‘Enter this city.’” The city referred to here is understood by scholars as a town they were commanded to enter after their journey in the wilderness. The instruction was to enter it and to “eat freely from wherever you please,” meaning they were given the freedom to enjoy its provisions. The command was simple and generous, yet the way they entered was to be special.\n\nThe verse continues: “enter the gate with humility, saying, ‘Absolve us.’” They were commanded to enter through the gate in a humble manner and to speak a word of supplication, asking for forgiveness and relief from their sins. The word “Absolve us” in the chapter’s wording carries the sense of asking Allah to lighten and forgive. The manner of entry, with humility and a prayer of forgiveness, was as important as the entry itself, because it was an act of obedience and remembrance.\n\nThe promise that followed was tied to this obedience: “We will forgive your sins and multiply the reward for the good-doers.” If they entered as commanded, Allah would forgive their sins, and those who did good would receive multiplied reward. The verse shows the connection between humble obedience and divine mercy. A simple act, entering a gate humbly and saying a word of repentance, was the key to forgiveness and reward. This is why the next verse records the tragedy that followed: the people changed the words they were commanded to say, and so they lost the blessing. For the reader, the verse teaches that humility and sincere prayer before Allah open the door to forgiveness, and that small acts of obedience done properly carry great value in the sight of Allah.",
  "59": "“But the wrongdoers changed the words they were commanded to say.”\n\nThis verse records the disobedience that followed the command in the previous verse. The children of Israel were told to enter the city gate with humility and to say the word of repentance that was commanded. Instead, “the wrongdoers changed the words they were commanded to say.” Scholars relate that they altered the command, twisting the word of submission into a word of mockery and entering the gate in an arrogant manner rather than humbly. Their disobedience was not a small slip; it was a deliberate changing of what Allah had ordered, showing the rebelliousness that had taken root among them.\n\nThe consequence is stated plainly: “So We sent down a punishment from the heavens upon them for their rebelliousness.” Because they refused to obey and changed what they were told, a punishment came down upon them from the heavens. The verse makes clear that this punishment was the result of their own action. The word “rebelliousness” describes the spirit behind their disobedience. They did not simply forget; they openly turned away from the command and even mocked it.\n\nThis verse is a powerful lesson about the seriousness of tampering with divine commands. The children of Israel were given a simple instruction, yet they changed it. This shows how even simple obedience can be lost when the heart is proud and rebellious. The Quran uses this example to warn its readers against taking Allah’s commands lightly or altering them to suit their desires. Disobedience that begins with changing a few words can lead to punishment from the heavens. For the reader, the verse teaches the importance of obeying Allah’s commands as they are given, with humility, rather than reshaping them to fit one’s own wishes. It also shows that punishment follows rebellion as a natural and just consequence.",
  "60": "“And ˹remember˺ when Moses prayed for water for his people.”\n\nThis verse records a scene of mercy and provision in the wilderness. “And ˹remember˺ when Moses prayed for water for his people.” The children of Israel were in a dry land and needed water, and Moses turned to his Lord in prayer on their behalf. This is a beautiful picture of the prophet as a servant who calls upon Allah for his people. When they needed water, Moses did not rely on his own power; he prayed, and Allah responded.\n\nThe response came in the form of a command: “We said, ‘Strike the rock with your staff.’” Moses struck the rock as he was told, and “then twelve springs gushed out.” The water came forth in twelve springs, and the verse adds that “each tribe knew its drinking place.” The children of Israel were divided into twelve tribes, and each was given its own spring. This arrangement was both practical and a sign of Allah’s perfect care; each tribe had clear access to water and knew where to drink. The provision was organized, not chaotic.\n\nThe verse then records a further instruction: “Eat and drink of Allah’s provisions, and do not go about spreading corruption in the land.” Along with the gift came a command. They were to enjoy the provision but not to use it as a cause of corruption, rivalry, or injustice. The gift of water was a favour, and it was to be received with gratitude and order. This verse is part of the long reminder to the children of Israel of the favours Allah had shown them. For the reader, it teaches that Allah provides for His servants in the most difficult circumstances, that prayer opens the door to provision, and that every blessing carries the duty of gratitude and good conduct.",
  "61": "“And ˹remember˺ when you said, ‘O Moses! We cannot endure the same meal ˹every day˺.’”\n\nThis verse records a new complaint from the children of Israel despite the many favours they had received. They said to Moses, “We cannot endure the same meal ˹every day˺.” They had been given manna and quails, pure food from Allah, yet they grew tired of it and asked for something else. Their request was not merely for variety; it was a sign of ingratitude toward the provision that had saved them in the wilderness. They said, “So ˹just˺ call upon your Lord on our behalf, He will bring forth for us some of what the earth produces of herbs, cucumbers, garlic, lentils, and onions.” They asked for the vegetables and crops of the earth.\n\nMoses responded with a question that pointed out their foolishness: “Do you exchange what is better for what is worse?” The manna and quails were a pure and blessed provision, while what they asked for was ordinary produce that could be found in any village. To trade the better for the worse was a sign of how far their hearts had drifted. Moses then told them, “˹You can˺ go down to any village and you will find what you have asked for.” If what they wanted was so easily available, they did not need to complain; they could simply go and find it.\n\nThe verse then records the tragic consequences of their continued disobedience: “They were stricken with disgrace and misery, and they invited the displeasure of Allah for rejecting Allah’s signs and unjustly killing the prophets. This is ˹a fair reward˺ for their disobedience and violations.” Their history included rejection of signs and the killing of prophets, and the verse calls their punishment a fair reward for what they had done. For the reader, the verse teaches the danger of ingratitude and of becoming bored with Allah’s blessings. It also shows that a people who continually reject signs and harm the messengers of Allah bring disgrace and punishment upon themselves.",
  "62": "“Indeed, the believers, Jews, Christians, and Sabians—whoever ˹truly˺ believes in Allah and the Last Day and does good.”\n\nThis verse makes an important statement about the scope of divine reward. It names four groups: “the believers, Jews, Christians, and Sabians.” The Sabians are a group mentioned in the Quran whose exact identity is debated, but they are treated here as a community that claimed to follow a revealed religion. The verse then lays down the condition that applies to all of them: “whoever ˹truly˺ believes in Allah and the Last Day and does good will have their reward with their Lord.”\n\nThe meaning of this verse has been a subject of careful discussion. The verse appears to be speaking about the people of these communities who, at the time before the coming of the final messenger, sincerely believed in Allah and the Last Day and did good deeds. Scholars explain that after the coming of the Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him, the criterion of true belief became believing in him as well, because the Quran was sent as the final guidance. This is supported by other verses of the Quran that call people to believe in the final messenger. The verse should therefore not be read as saying that every person who merely belongs to one of these named communities will be saved regardless of what they believe or do. The condition is clear: belief in Allah, belief in the Last Day, and doing good.\n\nThe verse ends with the reassuring words that such people “will have their reward with their Lord. And there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve.” This is the same promise given earlier to those who follow guidance: no fear and no grief. The verse teaches that true salvation depends not on labels alone but on genuine belief and righteous action. It also shows the fairness of Allah, who judges people according to the truth they received and the sincerity of their hearts. For the reader, the verse is a call to genuine faith and good deeds rather than relying on a mere name or community.",
  "63": "“And ˹remember˺ when We took a covenant from you and raised the mountain above you.”\n\nThis verse returns to the theme of covenants. “And ˹remember˺ when We took a covenant from you and raised the mountain above you.” Scholars explain that when Allah made a covenant with the children of Israel to follow the scripture, the mountain was raised above them as a dramatic sign, as if to say that they should accept the covenant seriously or the mountain would fall upon them. This was a powerful way of impressing upon them the weight of the commitment they were making. It was both a warning and a demonstration of Allah’s power.\n\nThe command that accompanied the covenant was to “Hold firmly to that ˹Scripture˺ which We have given you and observe its teachings.” They were to grasp the scripture firmly, meaning to accept it wholeheartedly, and to observe its teachings in their daily lives. The purpose was stated at the end of the verse: “so perhaps you will become mindful ˹of Allah˺.” The covenant, the scripture, and the observing of its teachings were all meant to produce mindfulness of Allah, a deep awareness that would shape their conduct.\n\nThe verse is part of the Quran’s repeated reminder to the children of Israel of their obligations. They had been given a great trust, the scripture, and they had made a covenant to uphold it. The raising of the mountain was a sign of the seriousness of that trust. For the reader, the verse teaches that receiving guidance carries responsibility. A person who is given the scripture, the knowledge of right and wrong, is expected to hold firmly to it and observe its teachings. It also shows that Allah’s signs and covenants are meant to produce mindfulness, not merely to be observed as events of the past.",
  "64": "“Yet you turned away afterwards.”\n\nThis verse records the failure that followed the covenant. “Yet you turned away afterwards.” Despite the covenant, despite the raising of the mountain, and despite the commands they had received, the children of Israel turned away. The word “afterwards” points out that their turning came after they had been given every reason to remain faithful. They had witnessed the signs and accepted the covenant, and then they broke it. This made their failure all the more serious.\n\nThe verse then shows the mercy that saved them: “Had it not been for Allah’s grace and mercy upon you, you would have certainly been of the losers.” Their turning away deserved punishment, and only the grace and mercy of Allah prevented them from being ruined. The verse uses the word “losers” to describe what they would have become, connecting to the earlier use of this word in the chapter. It was Allah’s mercy, not their own merit, that saved them from destruction.\n\nThis verse carries an important balance. It does not hide their disobedience; it records it honestly. But it also highlights the mercy of Allah, which repeatedly spared them despite their failure. The verse teaches that divine mercy is real and that it can save a people even after they have turned away. At the same time, it warns that mercy is not an excuse for continued disobedience. The pattern is consistent with the rest of the chapter: favours are given, people fail, and mercy intervenes. For the reader, the verse is a reminder both of human weakness and of the vastness of Allah’s grace, and it encourages sincere turning back to Allah rather than continued turning away. The verse teaches that even those who have been given great privileges are not safe from loss if they abandon their covenant. The grace of Allah saved them, but that grace was not a blank promise; it was a mercy that came after they had failed, calling them back to their duty. This is why the Quran tells the story: to show both the seriousness of breaking a covenant and the reality of divine mercy, so that readers are neither arrogant in their privileges nor despairing of Allah’s forgiveness.",
  "65": "“You are already aware of those of you who broke the Sabbath.”\n\nThis verse moves to another lesson from the history of the children of Israel. “You are already aware of those of you who broke the Sabbath.” The Sabbath was the day of rest that was made sacred for the children of Israel, and they were commanded to stop their work and worldly activity on that day. A group of them, however, broke this command. Scholars relate that they devised a way to catch fish on the Sabbath while pretending to keep the letter of the command, and in doing so they violated its spirit. The verse says the children of Israel were already aware of this event, meaning it was a well-known lesson among them.\n\nThe punishment is described in a few striking words: “We said to them, ‘Be disgraced apes!’” They were transformed into apes as a punishment, disgraced and humiliated. This transformation is understood by scholars as a real and visible punishment that befell them, a sign of how far their disobedience had led them. The mention of apes is meant to show their loss of dignity and the shame of their action. Their hearts had become so corrupted that they were lowered in the most degrading way.\n\nThe verse is a powerful warning about playing games with Allah’s commands. The people who broke the Sabbath thought they were clever in finding a way around the law, but Allah saw through their trickery and punished them. This is one of the clearest examples in the Quran of the danger of taking divine commands lightly and looking for loopholes. For the reader, the verse teaches that Allah’s commands are to be obeyed sincerely, not outwitted, and that trying to bend the law while pretending to obey it is a form of corruption that brings disgrace. It also shows that the children of Israel, despite all their privileges, were not above punishment.",
  "66": "“So We made their fate an example to present and future generations.”\n\nThis verse explains the purpose behind the punishment of the Sabbath-breakers described in the previous verse. “So We made their fate an example to present and future generations.” The transformation and punishment they suffered was not kept secret; it was made into a lesson that would be remembered. The people at that time saw it, and those who came after learned about it. The fate of the Sabbath-breakers became a standing warning against playing with Allah’s commands.\n\nThe verse also says their fate was made “a lesson to the God-fearing.” The people most able to benefit from the lesson were those who feared Allah, those whose hearts were open and sincere. A warning only helps those who take it seriously. The arrogant and rebellious looked at the punishment and learned nothing, while the God-fearing understood the message and guarded against the same mistake. This shows that the same event can teach different people different things, depending on the state of their hearts.\n\nThis verse is connected to the broader purpose of the Quran’s stories. The Quran does not tell the history of past communities merely to record facts; it tells it so that readers can learn and avoid the same errors. The fate of the Sabbath-breakers stands as an example for all generations, including the readers of the Quran today. For the believer, the lesson is to take Allah’s commands seriously and to let the mistakes of the past guide present conduct. The verse also teaches that warnings are effective for those who fear Allah, which is a reason to cultivate that fear in one’s own heart. The Quran frequently mentions past nations and their punishments for exactly this purpose, so that later peoples might avoid the same fate. When a lesson is recorded and remembered, it becomes a mercy for those who come after, because they can learn from the mistakes of others without having to suffer them. This is why Allah preserved these events in the Quran: not to burden readers with history, but to give them clear guidance and warning. The fate of the Sabbath-breakers therefore lives on as a sign, and the most successful reader is the one who takes the warning to heart.",
  "67": "“And ˹remember˺ when Moses said to his people, ‘Allah commands you to sacrifice a cow.’”\n\nThis verse begins the well-known story of the cow, the longest single narrative in this chapter and the reason the chapter is named al-Baqarah, the Cow. Moses told his people, “Allah commands you to sacrifice a cow.” The command was part of solving a mysterious murder that is described later in the passage. Instead of obeying readily, the people questioned Moses and made the matter difficult for themselves.\n\nTheir first response showed their suspicion and mockery: “They replied, ‘Are you mocking us?’” The children of Israel doubted whether Moses was serious, accusing him of making a joke of them. Moses answered with complete seriousness and integrity: “I seek refuge in Allah from acting foolishly!” The phrase “I seek refuge in Allah” is a way of asking Allah for protection, and it shows that Moses would never treat Allah’s command as a joke. Mocking and foolishness were the opposite of what a prophet does.\n\nThe exchange reveals an important difference. Moses was a sincere prophet who delivered exactly what Allah commanded, while his people responded with doubt, delay, and unnecessary questions. Their repeated questioning would make the command harder for them, as the following verses show. For the reader, the story teaches the value of immediate obedience to clear commands. When Allah commands something, the believer should respond with readiness, not with suspicion and endless questions. The story also shows how the people’s own stubbornness turned a simple command into a difficult test, a lesson about the consequences of resisting Allah’s instructions. The command to sacrifice a cow may seem strange to a modern reader, and this is precisely why the story is worth studying. The point of the narrative is not the cow itself but the attitude of the people who received the command. Their doubt and suspicion revealed a heart that did not truly trust its prophet or its Lord. Even when a command is simple, a rebellious heart finds ways to resist it, while a sincere heart finds ways to obey. The story therefore stands as a mirror for the reader, asking whether one responds to Allah’s commands with readiness or with suspicion.",
  "68": "“They said, ‘Call upon your Lord to clarify for us what type ˹of cow˺ it should be!’”\n\nThis verse continues the story of the cow, showing how the children of Israel kept adding to their own difficulty. Instead of obeying the simple command to sacrifice a cow, they asked for more details. They said, “Call upon your Lord to clarify for us what type ˹of cow˺ it should be!” Rather than finding any cow and sacrificing it, which was all that was asked, they wanted to know the exact type. Their questions were not born of sincere desire to obey; they were a form of delay and evasion.\n\nMoses conveyed the answer from Allah: “He replied, ‘Allah says, “The cow should neither be old nor young but in between. So do as you are commanded!”’” The cow was to be neither old nor young, but in between. And the command ended with a direct call: “So do as you are commanded!” This reminder shows that the purpose was obedience, not endless questioning. The answer narrowed the choice, but they still had room to obey.\n\nThe verse teaches a clear lesson about the attitude of the children of Israel. Every time they were given a command, they tried to add conditions, ask for explanations, and delay. This made the task harder for themselves. The command was simple, but they complicated it. For the reader, the story is a warning against a questioning, evasive attitude toward Allah’s commands. When the command is clear, the response should be obedience. Unnecessary questions, when they come from a desire to avoid obeying, only bring difficulty. The story also shows the patience of Moses, who kept conveying Allah’s answers even as his people continued to delay. The children of Israel had every reason to trust their prophet. They had seen him bring them signs from Allah, yet they still questioned and delayed. Their questions were not about matters they could not understand; they were about a simple command they did not want to obey. This is the difference between a sincere question and an excuse. A sincere question seeks clarity in order to obey better, while an excuse seeks delay in order to avoid obeying. Allah responded to their questions with patience, but the command always pointed back to the same thing: obedience.",
  "69": "“They said, ‘Call upon your Lord to specify for us its colour.’”\n\nThis verse shows the children of Israel continuing to make the command more difficult for themselves. After being told the type of cow, they asked for another detail: “Call upon your Lord to specify for us its colour.” They were not content with the answer they had received; they wanted more specification. This was another attempt to delay and complicate what should have been a simple act of obedience.\n\nMoses again conveyed the answer from Allah: “He replied, ‘Allah says, “It should be a bright yellow cow—pleasant to see.”’” The cow was to be bright yellow and pleasant to see, a description that made it distinctive and easy to find. The verse uses the detail of colour to show how patient Allah was in answering their questions, and how precise the answers were. Each answer removed one excuse for not obeying.\n\nThe story is told in the Quran to teach the consequences of unnecessary questioning. The children of Israel had been told simply to sacrifice a cow, and they could have obeyed at once. Instead, they kept asking for details, and each answer made the task more specific and harder to fulfil. This is a powerful warning against the kind of questioning that comes from a heart unwilling to obey. When a person delays obedience by demanding more and more clarification, he often makes the matter harder for himself. The verse also shows the mercy and patience of Allah, who answered their questions rather than punishing their delay, and the constancy of Moses, who faithfully relayed every answer from his Lord. Each answer from Allah removed another excuse, leaving the children of Israel with no reason to delay. Yet the pattern continued, and this itself is a lesson. When a person is determined not to obey, no amount of clarification will satisfy him, because the real obstacle is not ignorance but unwillingness. Allah’s answers were precise and gracious, showing that He guides those who seek guidance, but the people kept finding new questions because their hearts were not ready to submit. The verse calls the reader to examine his own attitude toward clear commands.",
  "70": "“Again they said, ‘Call upon your Lord so that He may make clear to us which cow.’”\n\nThis verse records yet another question from the children of Israel, showing how far they had gone in delaying the command. “Again they said, ‘Call upon your Lord so that He may make clear to us which cow.’” They had been told the type and the colour, and now they wanted to know exactly which cow, because, as they said, “all cows look the same to us.” Their excuse was that the cows looked alike, but this was really another way of avoiding the command.\n\nThey added a hopeful phrase: “Then, Allah willing, we will be guided ˹to the right one˺.” By saying “Allah willing,” they expressed that they would be guided after receiving the answer. But the repeated questions had already shown their reluctance. Their words of submission rang hollow against their actions of delay. They kept making the matter more complicated instead of simply obeying.\n\nThe lesson of this story is becoming clearer with each verse. The children of Israel were given a simple command, and their endless questions were a form of resistance. Each question brought a precise answer, yet they still hesitated. This shows that the real problem was not a lack of information but a lack of willingness to obey. For the reader, the story warns against using questions as an excuse to delay obedience. Sincere questions that help a person understand and obey are good, but questions that arise from evasion and reluctance only create difficulty. The verse also highlights the patience of Allah in dealing with His servants and the way He gives clear guidance to those who seek it, even when they are slow to respond. The phrase \"Allah willing\" in their speech shows that even their polite words could not hide their reluctance. A person can say all the right things while delaying all the right actions. The children of Israel spoke of being guided while doing everything to avoid obeying. This reminds the reader that words of submission are only meaningful when they are followed by deeds of obedience. The story repeatedly contrasts what they said with what they did, teaching that true faith is shown in action, not only in speech.",
  "71": "“He replied, ‘Allah says, “It should have been used neither to till the soil nor water the fields.”’”\n\nThis verse records the final answer to the questions of the children of Israel. The cow they were to sacrifice should have been used “neither to till the soil nor water the fields,” meaning it should be a cow that had not been worked in farming, one that was “wholesome and without blemish.” By this point, the specifications had become so many and so specific that finding the exact cow was very difficult. The children of Israel had turned a simple command into an almost impossible task through their own endless questioning.\n\nWhen they heard the final specification, “They said, ‘Now you have come with the truth.’” They admitted that Moses had now given them the full truth of what was required. Their admission came after they had already made the matter hard for themselves. Yet even after acknowledging the truth, the verse records, “Yet they still slaughtered it hesitantly!” They finally carried out the command, but with hesitation and reluctance, rather than with the eager obedience they should have shown from the beginning.\n\nThe whole story of the cow is a striking lesson about the consequences of delay and unnecessary questioning. The children of Israel could have sacrificed any cow and fulfilled the command with ease. Instead, their repeated questions made the command so specific and difficult that they nearly failed to obey. The verse shows that their admission of the truth did not erase the cost of their hesitation. For the reader, the story teaches the value of immediate, sincere obedience to Allah’s commands. It warns against the kind of questioning that delays and complicates, and it reminds the believer that obedience is best offered willingly and promptly, before unnecessary difficulty is created.",
  "72": "“˹This is˺ when a man was killed and you disputed who the killer was.”\n\nThis verse reveals the purpose behind the command to sacrifice the cow, connecting the story to a specific event. “˹This is˺ when a man was killed and you disputed who the killer was.” A murder had taken place among the children of Israel, and they could not agree on who the killer was. The dispute threatened to cause division and injustice, because without proof no one could be held responsible. The verse then says, “but Allah revealed what you concealed.” Allah was about to expose what they had hidden, showing that no secret could remain hidden from Him.\n\nScholars explain that the command to sacrifice the cow was given in connection with this murder, as a means through which Allah would reveal the identity of the killer. The way this happened is described in the next verse. The connection shows that the long story of the cow was not an isolated test but was tied to a real situation in which the children of Israel needed guidance and justice. Allah used their obedience to the command as a way to uncover the truth.\n\nThe verse teaches that Allah knows all secrets, including the hidden facts of human disputes and crimes. When people try to conceal the truth, Allah is fully aware of what they hide. The story also shows that Allah guides His servants toward justice even through seemingly strange commands. For the reader, the verse is a reminder that nothing can be hidden from Allah, and that disputes and wrongs will eventually be uncovered by His wisdom. It also sets the stage for the next verse, where the striking of the dead body with a piece of the cow brings the truth to light. The mention of the murder and the dispute also shows how Allah deals with human affairs. He is not distant from the conflicts and crimes of people; He is aware of them and can bring hidden truths to light. For the children of Israel, the inability to resolve the murder was a crisis that threatened justice, and Allah guided them through it. This gives the reader confidence that even in the most confused and hidden of human disputes, Allah sees the truth and can reveal it in His time and His way.",
  "73": "“So We instructed, ‘Strike the dead body with a piece of the cow.’”\n\nThis verse completes the story of the cow and reveals its purpose. After the cow was sacrificed, Allah instructed the children of Israel to “Strike the dead body with a piece of the cow.” Scholars explain that when they did so, the slain man was brought back to life by the power of Allah and identified his killer, or that the truth of the murder was revealed through this sign. The exact manner is not detailed in the Quran, but the point is clear: through this command and their obedience, the hidden truth was uncovered.\n\nThe verse then draws the great lesson from the event: “This is how ˹easily˺ Allah brings the dead to life.” The striking of the dead body with a piece of the cow, and the revealing of the truth that followed, is presented as a demonstration of Allah’s power over death and life. If Allah can bring life in this remarkable way as a sign, then the final resurrection is entirely within His power. The verse continues, “showing you His signs so that you may understand.” The entire event was a sign meant to lead them to understanding and to strengthen their faith.\n\nThe story of the cow, which began several verses earlier, now finds its conclusion and its meaning. What seemed like a strange and difficult command was, in fact, a means of revealing truth and demonstrating divine power. For the reader, the story teaches that Allah’s commands are always wise, even when their purpose is not immediately clear, and that obedience opens the way for truth and justice. It also reinforces one of the central messages of the chapter: Allah is able to bring the dead back to life, and the signs He shows are meant to guide people to understanding and faith.",
  "74": "“Even then your hearts became hardened like a rock or even harder.”\n\nThis verse makes a striking comparison about the hearts of the children of Israel. Despite all the signs they had seen, their hearts became hardened, and the Quran compares them to rock, or even harder. The image is meant to show how far they had gone from softness and faith. A rock is hard and does not easily change, and their hearts had become the same toward the truth. What should have softened them, the miracles and reminders, only seemed to leave them unchanged.\n\nThe verse then makes a point about rocks themselves: “for some rocks gush rivers; others split, spilling water; while others are humbled in awe of Allah.” The comparison is remarkable because it shows that even rocks can respond to Allah in ways that these hearts did not. Some rocks burst with water, others crack open, and others fall in humility before Allah. In this way, the very rocks show more responsiveness to their Creator than these hardened hearts showed to His guidance. The imagery is meant to shame the hard-heartedness that refuses to soften.\n\nThe verse ends with the sobering statement, “And Allah is never unaware of what you do.” Nothing about their condition or their deeds escaped Allah. The verse connects their hardened hearts to their actions and warns that Allah sees it all. For the reader, the verse teaches the danger of letting the heart harden through repeated rejection of the truth. The heart is meant to be soft and responsive, able to be moved by remembrance of Allah. When a person repeatedly turns away, the heart can become like stone. The verse is a call to protect the softness of the heart and to respond to Allah’s signs before it grows hard.",
  "75": "“Do you ˹believers still˺ expect them to be true to you?”\n\nThis verse asks the believers a pointed question. “Do you ˹believers still˺ expect them to be true to you?” The question concerns a group of the People of the Book, and it asks whether it is reasonable to expect their loyalty and faithfulness. The verse then gives the reason for doubt: “though a group of them would hear the word of Allah then knowingly corrupt it after understanding it.” These people would hear the word of Allah, understand it, and then deliberately change and corrupt it. If a people treat even the word of Allah this way, how can they be trusted with the believers?\n\nThe verse highlights a particular kind of corruption. The corruption described is not done in ignorance; it is done “after understanding it,” meaning they knew what the word meant and still changed it. This makes the corruption a deliberate act rather than an honest mistake. The Quran accuses some among the People of the Book of this kind of tampering with divine revelation, and it warns the believers not to place naive trust in them.\n\nThis verse should be read with care. It is not attacking all People of the Book as individuals; it is describing the behaviour of a group among them, as the phrase \"a group of them\" makes clear. The point is that some had shown themselves untrustworthy with the very word of Allah. The verse teaches the believers to be discerning and cautious, to judge people by their actions, and to recognize that those who corrupt the truth will not be reliable allies. It also serves as a general warning against placing trust in those who have shown they will twist the truth for their own ends. The question is directed to the believers at the time of the Prophet, who had hoped that the People of the Book might accept the message and be reliable allies. The verse tempers that hope with realism drawn from experience. When a people have shown that they will corrupt even the word of Allah, expecting unwavering honesty from them is unwise. This does not justify treating all of them unfairly, but it does call for discernment. Trust must be earned, and those who twist the truth for their own ends have given clear evidence of where their loyalty lies.",
  "76": "“When they meet the believers they say, ‘We believe.’”\n\nThis verse exposes the double-faced behaviour of some of the People of the Book, in language that echoes the earlier description of the hypocrites. “When they meet the believers they say, ‘We believe.’” In front of the believers, they claimed to accept the new faith. “But in private they say ˹to each other˺, ‘Will you disclose to the believers the knowledge Allah has revealed to you, so that they may use it against you before your Lord? Do you not understand?’” When alone, they questioned one another about disclosing knowledge to the believers, fearing that the believers would use it against them.\n\nTheir concern reveals their dishonesty. They knew that what they were hiding could be used against them, and they worried that the believers, with the knowledge that was being shared, would be able to argue with them before their Lord. The private exchange shows that their public profession of belief was not sincere. Like the hypocrites described earlier in the chapter, they spoke one way in public and another in private. Their fear of being exposed drove their scheming.\n\nThe verse teaches that sincerity and honesty are the foundation of trust. Those who act in a double-faced manner, claiming faith in public while concealing opposition in private, are not to be relied upon. The Quran records their private words so that the believers would understand the reality of the situation. For the reader, the verse is a lesson in discernment and a warning against assuming that every public profession of faith is matched by the heart. It also shows that Allah knows what people reveal and what they keep hidden, a truth that the next verse states directly. This verse mirrors the earlier description of the hypocrites almost exactly, and the parallel is deliberate. The Quran uses the same image of meeting the believers in public and speaking differently in private to show that double-faced behaviour is a recurring danger, whether it appears among people who claim to be Muslims or among people of other communities. In both cases, the root is the same: insincerity and a heart that is not truly with the believers. The lesson for the reader is to look past public words and judge people by their consistent, honest conduct.",
  "77": "“Do they not know that Allah is aware of what they conceal and what they reveal?”\n\nThis verse asks a pointed question about the foolishness of those who scheme in private. “Do they not know that Allah is aware of what they conceal and what they reveal?” The people who whispered together in private, planning how to hide knowledge and deceive the believers, seemed to forget that Allah sees and knows everything. Their secrecy was useless, because nothing is hidden from Allah. The concealment that they thought would protect them was completely exposed to the One whose knowledge covers everything.\n\nThe pairing of \"conceal\" and \"reveal\" covers the two sides of human behaviour. What a person hides and what a person makes public are both known to Allah. No secret planning, no whispered conversation, and no hidden intention escapes His awareness. The verse therefore destroys the illusion of privacy when it comes to Allah. People may hide things from other people, but they cannot hide anything from Allah.\n\nThis verse connects directly to the previous one, where the People of the Book questioned one another about concealing knowledge in private. The Quran answers their concern by pointing to Allah’s perfect knowledge. If they truly believed that Allah knows everything, they would not imagine that their scheming could succeed. For the reader, the verse is a powerful reminder that every thought, word, and action is before Allah. It encourages sincerity and honesty, because pretense is pointless before the One who sees all. It also brings comfort to the believers, who know that the schemes of their enemies are not hidden from Allah, who will deal with them justly. This truth applies to every believer as well. Since Allah is aware of everything a person conceals and reveals, there is no such thing as a truly private sin or a successfully hidden scheme. This awareness should make a person careful and sincere, because acting well in public while corrupting in secret is exposed before Allah even if no human ever knows. It also brings comfort: the believer does not need to expose every enemy’s plot, because Allah already knows it and will deal with it in His justice.",
  "78": "“And among them are the illiterate who know nothing about the Scripture except lies.”\n\nThis verse describes another group among the People of the Book. “And among them are the illiterate who know nothing about the Scripture except lies.” These were people who did not read or know the scripture themselves, and so they only knew whatever tales and claims were told to them, much of which was false. Their ignorance made them easy to mislead, because they could not check what they were told against the actual scripture. They followed reports without verifying them.\n\nThe verse adds that “they ˹wishfully˺ speculate.” Because they had no real knowledge, they relied on speculation, wishes, and guesswork about religious matters. They built their beliefs not on solid ground but on assumptions and desires. This combination, ignorance and wishful speculation, left them without genuine understanding of their own faith and made them vulnerable to those who would mislead them.\n\nThe verse teaches the importance of knowledge in religion. Belief should be built on sound understanding, not on hearsay and guesswork. Those who do not learn the truth for themselves are at the mercy of whoever tells them what to believe. The verse also warns against two things at once: being content with ignorance and replacing knowledge with speculation. For the reader, the lesson is to seek genuine understanding of the scripture and faith, to verify what is learned, and to avoid the danger of building belief on unfounded wishes. The verse is part of the Quran’s wider call to knowledge, reflection, and sound understanding as foundations of true faith. The verse also points to a duty for those who have knowledge. When people remain ignorant, they can be misled by anyone who speaks confidently, whether truthful or not. This is why those who understand the scripture are responsible for sharing the truth clearly and correcting falsehood. A community that abandons the teaching of its own scripture leaves its members to follow speculation and rumour. The verse therefore calls both for seeking knowledge and for spreading it, so that people are not left at the mercy of lies and wishful thinking.",
  "79": "“So woe to those who distort the Scripture with their own hands.”\n\nThis verse delivers a severe warning. \"So woe to those who distort the Scripture with their own hands then say, 'This is from Allah'—seeking a fleeting gain!\" The word \"woe\" is an expression of ruin and punishment. The warning is against those who write something with their own hands, altering the scripture, and then falsely claim that it is from Allah. Their motive is described as \"seeking a fleeting gain,\" meaning they did this for worldly benefit, wealth, status, or approval. To change divine revelation for a temporary worldly reward is one of the gravest crimes against the truth.\n\nThe verse then repeats the warning with even greater intensity: \"So woe to them for what their hands have written, and woe to them for what they have earned.\" The repetition emphasizes the seriousness of the matter. They are condemned for what they have written, because they wrote falsehood and attributed it to Allah, and they are condemned for what they have earned, because the worldly gain they received was bought with corruption of the truth. Both the act and its reward are cursed.\n\nThis verse teaches the sanctity of divine revelation. The words of Allah are not to be altered, distorted, or tampered with for any worldly benefit. Those who do so bring destruction upon themselves. The warning is a reminder to all who handle religious texts and teachings to be scrupulously honest, never to add falsehood to the truth or to attribute to Allah what is not from Him. It also shows that seeking fleeting worldly gain at the expense of the truth is a disastrous bargain. For the reader, the verse upholds the purity of the scripture and warns against all forms of corruption in conveying the word of Allah.",
  "80": "“˹Some of˺ the Jews claim, ‘The Fire will not touch us except for a number of days.’”\n\nThis verse records a claim made by some of the Jews. They claimed, \"The Fire will not touch us except for a number of days.\" This reflected a belief among them that any punishment they might face in the Hereafter would be brief, because they considered themselves special, as children of a chosen people. They imagined that their status would protect them from lasting punishment, and that their time in the Fire, if any, would be short and temporary.\n\nThe verse then tells the Prophet how to respond: \"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ 'Have you taken a pledge from Allah— for Allah never breaks His word—or are you ˹just˺ saying about Allah what you do not know?'\" The response exposes the emptiness of their claim with a simple question. If they truly had such a guarantee, they would have a pledge from Allah, and Allah never breaks His word. But they had no such pledge; they were merely saying about Allah something they did not know. Their claim was based on wishful thinking, not on any divine promise.\n\nThe verse teaches that claims about religion must be grounded in what Allah has actually revealed, not in assumptions of privilege or false hope. No community is exempt from the consequences of its deeds simply because of its ancestry or status. The Quran repeatedly corrects the idea that being part of a chosen people automatically secures salvation. The next verses make this clear by stating the general rule: those who do evil will face the Fire, and those who believe and do good will enter Paradise. For the reader, the verse is a warning against claiming privileges in the sight of Allah without evidence, and a call to rely on genuine faith and good deeds rather than on imagined status.",
  "81": "“But no! Those who commit evil and are engrossed in sin will be the residents of the Fire.”\n\nThis verse firmly corrects the false claim made in the previous verse. The people had claimed that the Fire would not touch them except for a few days. The Quran answers with \"But no!\" rejecting that claim completely. It then states the general rule: \"Those who commit evil and are engrossed in sin will be the residents of the Fire. They will be there forever.\" The rule is clear and applies to everyone, with no exception based on ancestry, community, or privilege. Whoever commits evil and is deeply absorbed in sin will reside in the Fire forever.\n\nThe phrase \"engrossed in sin\" is important. It describes people who are not merely guilty of an occasional mistake, but who are thoroughly wrapped up in sin, persisting in it without turning back. Their evil is not a passing slip; it is a settled state. For such people, the Fire is their permanent residence. The verse removes any basis for the false hope that one can persist in evil and still escape lasting punishment.\n\nThis verse teaches that salvation is tied to deeds, not to identity. No one is saved simply by belonging to a certain group or being descended from certain people. What matters is what a person believes and does. The verse is addressed to the Jews who made the claim, but its principle is universal. It applies to every person in every community. For the reader, the verse is a call to take sin seriously and to turn away from it, and it reinforces the central message of the chapter that a person is judged by his own actions, for good or for evil. The verse also corrects a mistaken idea that some might hold about the People of the Book, namely that they were somehow exempt from the consequences of evil. The Quran states plainly that the rule applies to them like everyone else. There is no privilege that cancels the effects of wrongdoing. This is part of the Quran’s repeated teaching that Allah judges fairly, without favouritism. It also serves as a reminder to believers not to assume that belonging to the right community makes one safe; what saves is sincere faith and righteous action, sustained to the end of life.",
  "82": "“And those who believe and do good will be the residents of Paradise.”\n\nThis verse states the positive counterpart to the warning in the previous verse. After saying that those engrossed in evil will reside in the Fire, the Quran now says, \"And those who believe and do good will be the residents of Paradise. They will be there forever.\" The two verses form a complete picture of divine justice: evil leads to the Fire, and faith combined with good deeds leads to Paradise.\n\nThe pairing of belief and good deeds appears constantly in the Quran. Faith is not meant to remain only in the heart; it is meant to show itself in action. The verse places \"believe\" and \"do good\" together, teaching that neither alone is enough. A faith that produces no good deeds is incomplete, and good deeds without sincere faith do not have their true worth. The combination of the two is what leads to Paradise.\n\nThe verse ends with \"They will be there forever.\" Just as the residents of the Fire are there forever, the residents of Paradise are there forever. The eternity of the reward matches the eternity of the punishment, and both are stated plainly. For the reader, the verse is a promise and an encouragement. It shows that the path to Paradise is open to anyone who believes sincerely and does good, regardless of background or ancestry. It also connects to the wider theme of the chapter, which repeatedly returns to the two ends of human life, the Fire and the Garden, and calls every reader to choose the path of faith and righteous action. This verse follows the warning about evil with a promise of hope, showing that the door of Paradise is open to all who meet its conditions. It is important that the verse does not restrict the promise to one nation or community. It speaks generally of those who believe and do good, wherever they come from. This universality is a central theme of the Quran: salvation is not inherited but earned through faith and deeds. The verse thus gives every reader, whatever their background, a clear path to follow and a genuine hope to hold.",
  "83": "“And ˹remember˺ when We took a covenant from the children of Israel.”\n\nThis verse recalls a covenant taken from the children of Israel that contained basic moral duties. The covenant included the command to \"Worship none but Allah; be kind to parents, relatives, orphans and the needy; speak kindly to people; establish prayer; and pay alms-tax.\" These commands cover the essence of religion: exclusive worship of Allah, kindness to family and the vulnerable, good speech, prayer, and charity. They are the foundations of both faith and good conduct toward others.\n\nThe verse then records the failure of the children of Israel: \"But you ˹Israelites˺ turned away—except for a few of you—and were indifferent.\" Despite this clear covenant, they turned away from it, with only a few remaining faithful. The word \"indifferent\" describes the attitude of those who turned away; they treated these commands lightly and turned aside from them. The covenant was clear, but they abandoned it.\n\nThis verse is part of the Quran’s extended reminder to the children of Israel of their obligations and failures. It also serves a wider purpose for the reader: it lists the core moral duties that Allah requires of every believer. Worship of Allah alone, kindness to parents and relatives, care for orphans and the needy, good speech, prayer, and charity are timeless commands. The verse teaches that these duties were not new; they were part of the covenant given to earlier communities, and they remain central to true religion. It also warns against the carelessness that leads people to neglect clear duties, and it encourages gratitude for guidance by actually following it. The covenant recorded here is so central that it sums up much of what religion requires. Worship of Allah alone is the first duty, and it is followed by duties toward people: kindness to parents, relatives, orphans, and the needy, good speech, prayer, and charity. This shows that Islam, from the very beginning of revelation, has always combined the rights of Allah with the rights of people. The failure of the children of Israel to keep this covenant is recorded as a warning, but the commands themselves remain binding and beautiful, giving the reader a clear summary of what it means to live a life pleasing to Allah.",
  "84": "“And ˹remember˺ when We took your covenant that you would neither shed each other’s blood nor expel each other from their homes.”\n\nThis verse recalls another covenant taken from the children of Israel, this time concerning their treatment of one another. They covenanted that they would \"neither shed each other's blood nor expel each other from their homes.\" This was a promise to live together in peace, respecting the lives and homes of one another. The shedding of blood refers to killing, and expulsion refers to driving people out of their homes, and both were forbidden by this covenant.\n\nThe verse then says, \"you gave your pledge and bore witness.\" They accepted this covenant and witnessed it. They committed themselves to it knowingly. The verse sets up the next one, which will expose how they broke this very covenant. They pledged not to shed blood or expel one another, and then they did exactly those things.\n\nThis covenant and the next verse are directed at the Jewish community of Medina at the time of the Prophet, who were being reminded of their own history and obligations. The Quran uses this example to show that breaking covenants brings disgrace. For the reader, the verse teaches the importance of keeping promises, especially promises that protect life and community. The sanctity of human life and the right of people to live in their homes are basic principles that Allah commanded from early times. When a people violate these, they bring harm upon themselves. The verse also connects to the wider theme of the chapter, which stresses honesty, justice, and the keeping of trusts. The mention of not expelling people from their homes highlights the value Islam places on security and community. A society cannot be just or stable if its members kill one another or drive one another from their homes. These protections were commanded to earlier communities as well, showing that they are timeless principles of human decency. By recalling this covenant, the Quran reminds both its first readers and later generations that peace, the sanctity of life, and the right to one’s home are fundamental, and that breaking these promises carries serious consequences.",
  "85": "“But here you are, killing each other and expelling some of your people from their homes.”\n\nThis verse exposes the contradiction between the covenant the children of Israel made and how they actually behaved. They had pledged not to shed each other's blood or expel one another from their homes, yet \"here you are, killing each other and expelling some of your people from their homes.\" The verse confronts them directly with their own actions, showing that their covenant was broken. They also aided one another \"in sin and aggression,\" helping each other in wrongdoing rather than stopping it.\n\nThe verse then points out another contradiction: \"and when those ˹expelled˺ come to you as captives, you still ransom them—though expelling them was unlawful for you.\" They would ransom their people when they became captives, which was a good act, yet they had been the ones to expel them in the first place. They paid to free captives while themselves driving people from their homes. This made their actions inconsistent and hypocritical. The Quran then asks, \"Do you believe in some of the Scripture and reject the rest?\" They picked and chose which commands to follow, obeying some while ignoring others.\n\nThe verse warns that such selective belief brings punishment: \"Is there any reward for those who do so among you other than disgrace in this worldly life and being subjected to the harshest punishment on the Day of Judgment?\" Those who believe in part of the scripture and reject the rest are warned of disgrace in this world and severe punishment in the Hereafter. The verse ends, \"For Allah is never unaware of what you do.\" For the reader, the lesson is clear: faith is not a matter of picking convenient commands while ignoring inconvenient ones. True belief means accepting the scripture fully and acting consistently with it.",
  "86": "“These are the ones who trade the Hereafter for the life of this world.”\n\nThis verse sums up the condition of those who broke their covenant and acted inconsistently. \"These are the ones who trade the Hereafter for the life of this world.\" The image is of a trade or exchange, and it is a bad one. They gave up the lasting value of the Hereafter in exchange for the temporary benefits of this world. Their worldly gains, such as the ransom they paid and the status they sought, could not compare to what they lost in the Hereafter. They preferred immediate worldly advantage over eternal reward.\n\nThe verse then states their end: \"So their punishment will not be reduced, nor will they be helped.\" Their punishment is certain and complete. It will not be lightened, and no one will be able to help them out of it. The trade they made brought them only loss. The worldly benefits they chose could not save them, and no ally could come to their aid.\n\nThe image of trading the Hereafter for this world appears several times in the Quran, and it is always a warning against shortsightedness. A person who chooses the temporary over the eternal, immediate pleasure over lasting reward, makes a poor bargain. The verse teaches that this world and the Hereafter are not equals to be traded; the Hereafter is far greater and more lasting. For the reader, the verse encourages keeping the Hereafter in view when making choices, so that one does not sacrifice eternal good for fleeting worldly gain. It also comforts the oppressed by assuring them that those who break their word and oppress others will face a punishment that no one can reduce or avert. The warning is especially sharp because the choice was clear. They could see that the Hereafter is eternal while this world is fleeting, yet they still chose the world. This shows how powerful greed and habit can be in pulling a person away from what he knows to be right. The verse encourages the reader to weigh choices carefully and to remember that no worldly gain, however large, can compare to the value of salvation. It also reassures the just that those who exploit others for worldly ends will not escape punishment and will receive no help on the Day of Judgment.",
  "87": "“Indeed, We gave Moses the Book and sent after him successive messengers.”\n\nThis verse reviews part of the history of the messengers sent to the children of Israel. \"Indeed, We gave Moses the Book and sent after him successive messengers.\" Moses was given the scripture, and after him a succession of prophets was sent to guide the people. The verse then mentions Jesus: \"And We gave Jesus, son of Mary, clear proofs and supported him with the holy spirit.\" Jesus, the son of Mary, was given clear proofs and was supported with the holy spirit, which scholars understand as the angel Gabriel. Jesus performed miracles by Allah’s permission, confirming his message.\n\nThe verse then asks a pointed question: \"Why is it that every time a messenger comes to you ˹Israelites˺ with something you do not like, you become arrogant, rejecting some and killing others?\" The question exposes the pattern of the children of Israel. Whenever a messenger came with a message they did not like, they responded with arrogance, rejecting some messengers and even killing others. Their treatment of the messengers was not based on truth but on their own desires. If a messenger agreed with them, they accepted him; if the message challenged them, they turned away or worse.\n\nThe verse teaches that the messengers came with truth and clear proofs, yet they were met with arrogance and violence. The children of Israel were given many prophets, yet they mistreated them. This is a reminder of the patience of Allah and the injustice of rejecting the truth. For the reader, the verse warns against the arrogance that rejects guidance because it challenges one’s desires. It also shows that Allah sent messengers to guide people, and that the proper response to a truthful messenger is acceptance and humility, not pride and rejection.",
  "88": "“They say, ‘Our hearts are unreceptive!’”\n\nThis verse records another claim made by some of the Jews. \"They say, 'Our hearts are unreceptive!'\" This was their response when asked to believe. They claimed that their hearts were wrapped, covered, or sealed, as if they were unable to receive the new message. The Quran elsewhere records a similar statement, where they say their hearts are covered. By saying this, they were excusing their rejection of the truth, as if it were impossible for them to accept it.\n\nThe verse answers their claim: \"In fact, Allah has condemned them for their disbelief.\" The truth is that their hearts were not unreceptive by Allah’s doing in the sense that they had no choice; rather, their own disbelief and rejection had brought Allah’s condemnation upon them. The verse then adds, \"They have but little faith.\" Whatever faith they had was small and did not benefit them. Their claim of unreceptive hearts was really an admission of their own rejection, and that rejection was itself the cause of their ruin.\n\nThe verse teaches that people sometimes use excuses to avoid the truth, but Allah knows the real state of their hearts. Claiming that one cannot believe is not a valid excuse when the truth has been made clear. The heart can become covered through a person’s own repeated rejection, and this is the result of one’s own choice. For the reader, the verse warns against making excuses for rejecting truth and reminds that Allah’s judgement is based on the reality of belief and disbelief, not on the excuses people give. It also encourages protecting the heart from the kind of repeated rejection that can seal it against guidance. The claim that the heart is unreceptive can also be turned into a lesson for believers. A person can say, like these people, that he cannot change, that his heart is hard, or that belief is beyond him. But the heart is not fixed by fate; it is shaped by a person’s own choices. Repeated rejection hardens it, while sincere seeking softens it and draws it toward Allah. The verse therefore encourages the reader to keep the heart soft, to respond to the truth, and never to use the excuse of a hard heart to justify giving up on faith, because the hardening itself is the result of choices a person can still change.",
  "89": "“Although they used to pray for victory ˹by means of the Prophet˺ over the polytheists.”\n\nThis verse points out a striking contradiction in the behaviour of some of the Jews. \"Although they used to pray for victory ˹by means of the Prophet˺ over the polytheists.\" Before the Prophet Muhammad came, some of the Jews of Arabia used to pray for victory by means of him, expecting that a final prophet would come who would help them triumph over their enemies. They awaited his coming and looked forward to it.\n\nYet when he actually came, they rejected him. The verse says, \"when there came to them a Book from Allah which they recognized, confirming the Scripture they had ˹in their hands˺, they rejected it.\" The Book they received, the Quran, confirmed the scripture they had, and they recognized it as the truth, yet they rejected it. Their recognition of the truth did not prevent their rejection, because their hearts had turned away, often out of envy and worldly concern.\n\nThe verse ends, \"So may Allah's condemnation be upon the disbelievers.\" Their rejection, after recognizing the truth, made them disbelievers deserving of condemnation. The verse teaches that knowing the truth is not enough; what matters is accepting it. These people awaited a prophet and recognized him when he came, yet rejected him because the truth did not serve their desires. For the reader, the verse warns against the danger of letting envy, pride, or worldly interest stand between oneself and the truth. It also shows that a person can be very close to guidance and still turn away from it, which is a sobering lesson in sincerity. There is a deep irony in this verse that the reader should notice. These people prayed for the coming of a prophet, expected his arrival, and even knew him when he came, yet they rejected him because he did not fit their assumptions. This shows that waiting for the truth and recognizing it are not the same as accepting it. A person can be very close to the truth and still turn away if the truth demands something he is unwilling to give. The verse warns against pride and envy and teaches that genuine seekers accept the truth wherever it leads them.",
  "90": "“Miserable is the price they have sold their souls for.”\n\nThis verse delivers a harsh judgement on those who rejected the truth. \"Miserable is the price they have sold their souls for.\" They exchanged their own souls, their true selves and their eternal salvation, for something miserable. The verse explains what they did: \"denying Allah's revelation and resenting Allah for granting His grace to whoever He wills of His servants!\" They denied what Allah had revealed, and they resented that Allah chose to give His grace and prophethood to the one He willed among His servants. Their envy of the Prophet and of the honour given to him led them to reject the truth.\n\nThe verse continues, \"They have earned wrath upon wrath.\" Their punishment is doubled, wrath added to wrath, because of their denial and their resentment. The verse ends, \"And such disbelievers will suffer a humiliating punishment.\" Their end is a punishment that humiliates, matching the pride and envy that drove their rejection.\n\nThis verse teaches a profound lesson about envy and resentment. The refusal to accept a truth, simply because one is jealous of the one who brought it, is a destructive attitude. The Jews rejected the truth because they resented that prophethood went to someone they did not expect. Their envy led them to harm their own souls. For the reader, the verse warns against letting envy of others close one’s heart to the truth. It also teaches that Allah grants His grace and chooses His messengers as He wills, and that resenting His choice harms only the one who resents. True wisdom is to accept Allah’s decisions with humility and to seek the truth wherever it is found. The phrase \"wrath upon wrath\" shows that punishment can be compounded by a person’s own ongoing rejection. Every time they denied the truth or resented the blessing given to others, they added to their own guilt. This is a sobering thought: a person’s reaction to divine favour, whether with gratitude or with envy, shapes his own fate. The verse therefore teaches the reader to examine his heart for envy, which can blind a person to clear truth, and to replace resentment of Allah’s choices with acceptance and gratitude.",
  "91": "“When it is said to them: ‘Believe in what Allah has revealed,’ they reply, ‘We only believe in what was sent down to us.’”\n\nThis verse records another evasion by the people of the Book. When they were told, \"Believe in what Allah has revealed,\" they replied, \"We only believe in what was sent down to us.\" They claimed that they would only accept the revelation they had already received, the scripture given to them, and would not accept anything new. This sounded like faithfulness to their own scripture, but the Quran exposes its emptiness.\n\nThe verse explains their mistake: \"and they deny what came afterwards, though it is the truth confirming their own Scriptures!\" The revelation they denied, the Quran, was the truth and it confirmed their own scriptures. It did not contradict what they had; it confirmed it. By rejecting it, they were not being faithful to their own scripture; they were denying the very truth their scripture pointed to. Their claim of loyalty was really an excuse for rejecting the new messenger.\n\nThe verse then tells the Prophet to ask them a question that exposes the dishonesty of their position: \"Ask ˹them, O Prophet˺, 'Why then did you kill Allah's prophets before, if you are ˹truly˺ believers?'\" If they truly believed in what was sent down to them, they would not have killed the prophets who came with messages from Allah. Their history of killing prophets showed that their claim to believe in revelation was not sincere. For the reader, the verse teaches that loyalty to the truth cannot be selective. Those who claim to follow revelation must accept the full truth that Allah reveals, including new guidance, and must not use their existing beliefs as an excuse to reject what confirms them. This verse teaches the importance of consistency between what one claims and what one does. The people claimed to believe only in their own scripture, yet their history showed that they had rejected and even killed the prophets who came with revelation. This contradiction exposed the emptiness of their claim. The reader is reminded that a person’s actions reveal the reality of his belief more than his words do, and that true loyalty to revelation is shown by accepting the messengers whom Allah sends and following the truth they bring.",
  "92": "“Indeed, Moses came to you with clear proofs.”\n\nThis verse continues the exposure of the people of the Book’s inconsistency. \"Indeed, Moses came to you with clear proofs.\" Moses brought clear proofs and signs from Allah, yet his own people did not remain faithful to him. The verse then recalls their failure: \"then you worshipped the calf in his absence, acting wrongfully.\" While Moses was away, they worshipped the calf, an act of clear wrongdoing. Their rejection of the truth was not new; it had a long history among them.\n\nThe verse is addressed to the descendants of those who worshipped the calf, reminding them that their ancestors had rejected the clear proofs of Moses. This is used as an argument against their claim that they only believed in what was sent down to them. Their own history showed that they had not always been faithful to what was sent down to them; they had worshipped a calf despite Moses’ clear proofs.\n\nThe verse teaches that a claim to loyalty to revelation is not proven by words alone but by consistent obedience. The ancestors of these people claimed to follow Moses, yet they worshipped a calf in his absence. This shows the gap between claiming faith and living it. For the reader, the verse is a reminder that the history of a community can expose the weakness of its present claims. It also reinforces the importance of remaining faithful to guidance, especially when a prophet or teacher is absent, and warns against the ease with which people can fall into wrong belief when they are not firmly grounded. The verse also carries a message about faithfulness to absent guidance. The people were tested most severely when Moses was away, and they failed. This shows how important it is to be firm in one’s own faith rather than relying only on the presence of a leader. When a teacher or prophet is absent, the strength of the heart is truly tested. The reader is encouraged to build faith that does not depend on circumstances, so that obedience continues whether one is being watched and guided directly or not.",
  "93": "“And when We took your covenant and raised the mountain above you.”\n\nThis verse returns to the covenant taken from the children of Israel. \"And when We took your covenant and raised the mountain above you ˹saying˺, 'Hold firmly to that ˹Scripture˺ which We have given you and obey.\"\" As before, the mountain was raised above them as a dramatic sign of the seriousness of the covenant, and they were commanded to hold firmly to the scripture and obey. This is the same covenant mentioned earlier in the chapter, and it is recalled again here to show their ongoing failure.\n\nTheir response is recorded with terrible irony: \"they answered, 'We hear and disobey.'\" Instead of \"We hear and obey,\" which would have been the proper response of submission, they said \"We hear and disobey.\" Their words openly contradicted the command. They heard what was asked of them and responded with disobedience. The verse then says, \"The love of the calf was rooted in their hearts because of their disbelief.\" Even after all this, the love of the calf remained deep in their hearts, a sign of their persistent idolatry and disbelief.\n\nThe verse ends with a rebuke: \"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ 'How evil is what your ˹so-called˺ belief prompts you to do, if you ˹actually˺ believe ˹in the Torah˺!'\" If they truly believed in the Torah, how evil was what their belief prompted them to do, from disobeying the covenant to clinging to the love of the calf. Their claimed belief did not produce obedience. For the reader, the verse teaches that true faith produces obedience. The response \"We hear and disobey\" is the opposite of the proper response of the believer, which is to hear and obey. It also shows that a faith that does not translate into action is worthless, and that love of anything that competes with Allah is a form of disbelief that must be uprooted from the heart.",
  "94": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ ‘If the ˹eternal˺ Home of the Hereafter with Allah is exclusively for you ˹Israelites˺ out of all humanity.’”\n\nThis verse challenges a claim made by some of the Jews. They claimed that the Home of the Hereafter, meaning Paradise, was exclusively for them out of all humanity. The verse tells the Prophet to test this claim by saying, \"then wish for death if what you say is true!\" The argument is simple and powerful. If Paradise is truly reserved for them alone and they are certain of it, then they should desire death, because death would bring them immediately to their reward. Their eagerness for Paradise would naturally make them wish for the moment that leads to it.\n\nThe word \"then\" in the challenge connects their claim to a test of their sincerity. A person who is certain of a great reward ahead of him does not fear the step that brings him to it. If the Jews really believed that Paradise was theirs alone, they would welcome death. Their unwillingness to wish for death would reveal that their claim was empty. The verse therefore uses their own certainty, or lack of it, as evidence against them.\n\nThis method of argument is a lesson in how to respond to false claims. Instead of arguing endlessly, the Quran presents a test that exposes the emptiness of the claim. The next verse will record their response, or rather their failure to respond, showing that they could not wish for death because they knew what they had done. For the reader, the verse teaches that claims of special favour with Allah must be proven by sincerity and by readiness to meet the consequences of those claims. It also shows that confidence in one’s own privileged status, without faith and deeds, is no more than an empty word.",
  "95": "“But they will never wish for that because of what their hands have done.”\n\nThis verse states directly that the people who claimed Paradise was exclusively theirs would never wish for death. The reason is given: \"because of what their hands have done.\" They knew what they had done, the sins, the rejection of messengers, the corruption of scripture, and so they did not want to face the consequences. Their fear of death exposed the falseness of their claim. If they really believed they were destined for Paradise, they would have desired death, but they feared what awaited them.\n\nThe phrase \"what their hands have done\" is a common expression in the Quran for the deeds a person has earned. The hands are mentioned because they are the instrument of much of what people do. Their accumulated sins made them dread the meeting with their Lord. Their own actions contradicted their proud claim of exclusive salvation. They could not wish for death because they were not confident in their reward.\n\nThe verse then adds, \"And Allah has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of the wrongdoers.\" Allah knows exactly what they have done and what is in their hearts. Nothing is hidden from Him. The verse therefore stands as both a statement of their true condition and a reminder of divine justice. For the reader, the verse teaches that words of privilege mean nothing when deeds contradict them. A person’s confidence in the Hereafter should be built on faith and good deeds, not on a claim of special status. It also teaches that fear of meeting one’s Lord can be a sign of unaddressed wrongdoing, and that the remedy is sincere repentance before it is too late. The deeper lesson is about the reality of self-knowledge. People often claim more than they can stand behind, but deep inside they know their own record. The claim of exclusive Paradise made by these people could not survive contact with their own conscience, because they knew their deeds did not match their words. This is why the verse says they would never wish for death; their inner awareness of their own wrongdoing prevented them. The reader is reminded that a person’s own deeds will eventually speak the truth about his claims, both in this world and in the Hereafter.",
  "96": "“You will surely find them clinging to life more eagerly than any other people.”\n\nThis verse reinforces the point made in the previous verses about the love of this world. \"You will surely find them clinging to life more eagerly than any other people, even more than polytheists.\" The people who claimed exclusive salvation were among those most attached to this life, even more than the polytheists who did not believe in the Hereafter at all. This is remarkable, because one would expect that those certain of Paradise would be less attached to this world. Instead, their great love of life betrayed their lack of real confidence in what they claimed.\n\nThe verse gives a vivid picture: \"Each one of them wishes to live a thousand years.\" A thousand years is a very long life, and the verse uses it to show how desperately they clung to this world. They wished for an almost endless life on earth, not for the Hereafter they claimed to be theirs. This love of this world contradicted their claim that Paradise was reserved for them. If they were truly certain of Paradise, they would not be so desperate to delay their arrival.\n\nThe verse then states the futility of their wish: \"But even if they were to live that long, it would not save them from the punishment.\" However long they lived, it would not protect them from the punishment that awaited them. Length of life cannot save anyone from the consequences of their deeds. The verse ends, \"And Allah is All-Seeing of what they do.\" Allah sees everything they do, and no amount of clinging to life can hide their deeds from Him. For the reader, the verse teaches the emptiness of loving this world above the Hereafter and warns against being so attached to life that one forgets what comes after it.",
  "97": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ ‘Whoever is an enemy of Gabriel should know that he revealed this ˹Quran˺ to your heart by Allah’s Will.’”\n\nThis verse responds to a claim made by some of the Jews who said they were enemies of the angel Gabriel, or who objected to the revelation because Gabriel brought it. The verse tells the Prophet to say, \"Whoever is an enemy of Gabriel should know that he revealed this ˹Quran˺ to your heart by Allah's Will.\" Gabriel is the angel who brought the revelation down to the heart of the Prophet. The mention of the heart shows that the Quran reached the very core of the Prophet, not merely his ears. And it was \"by Allah's Will,\" meaning that Gabriel did not act on his own; he was the messenger of Allah, carrying out Allah’s command.\n\nThe verse then describes the Quran: \"confirming what came before it—a guide and good news for the believers.\" The Quran confirms the earlier scriptures, and it serves as guidance and good news for the believers. By presenting these qualities, the verse answers those who were enemies of Gabriel. How could they be enemies of the angel who brought such a Book, a Book that confirms the truth and guides to it? Their enmity was not against Gabriel alone but against the message itself.\n\nThis verse teaches that being an enemy of a messenger of Allah is to be an enemy of the message and of the One who sent it. The angel Gabriel is honored in Islam as the one who conveyed the revelation. The verse also shows that the Quran is a confirmed truth, a guide, and good news. For the reader, the verse encourages recognizing the honour of the revelation and the angels who serve Allah, and it warns against enmity toward the messengers of Allah, which is ultimately enmity toward Allah Himself. The next verse develops this point further.",
  "98": "“Whoever is an enemy of Allah, His angels, His messengers, Gabriel, and Michael.”\n\nThis verse extends the point made in the previous verse. \"Whoever is an enemy of Allah, His angels, His messengers, Gabriel, and Michael.\" The verse lists Allah, His angels, His messengers, and two specific angels, Gabriel and Michael. These are among the most honored of Allah’s creation. To be an enemy of any of these is a serious matter, because it is ultimately enmity toward Allah and His truth. The verse mentions Gabriel and Michael by name because some of the People of the Book claimed enmity toward them or toward the angels who brought guidance.\n\nThe verse then gives the consequence: \"then ˹let them know that˺ Allah is certainly the enemy of the disbelievers.\" Those who are enemies of Allah, His angels, and His messengers are counted among the disbelievers, and Allah is the enemy of the disbelievers. This means that such people will face Allah’s opposition and punishment. To declare enmity toward the messengers of Allah is to place oneself on the side of disbelief and to earn the enmity of Allah.\n\nThis verse teaches the oneness of the message. All the angels and messengers serve the same truth, and to be an enemy of one is to be an enemy of all and of Allah Himself. There is no separating Gabriel from Michael, or the messengers from one another, in terms of their faithfulness to Allah. For the reader, the verse encourages respect and love for the angels and messengers of Allah as part of faith, and it warns against the arrogance that makes a person an enemy of divine truth. It also connects to the Quran’s teaching that the believers do not make distinctions between the messengers, accepting all of them. The verse also teaches a positive duty that comes with faith. Just as enmity toward the angels and messengers is serious, so too is love for them part of true belief. The believers are commanded to believe in all the messengers and to honour them, without separating one from another. This is one of the pillars of Islamic faith, and it shows the unity of the divine message across time. By mentioning Gabriel and Michael by name, the verse demonstrates that even the angels who carry out specific tasks are part of the same divine plan, and the believer honours them all.",
  "99": "“Indeed, We have sent down to you ˹O Prophet˺ clear revelations.”\n\nThis verse affirms the clear nature of what was revealed to the Prophet. \"Indeed, We have sent down to you ˹O Prophet˺ clear revelations.\" The word \"clear\" describes revelations that are plain, evident, and free from confusion. The Quran is not a book of riddles meant to hide the truth; it is a clear guidance that makes the truth known. This clarity is a mercy, because it leaves no excuse for those who claim they could not understand the message.\n\nThe verse then states, \"˹But˺ none will deny them except the rebellious.\" Only the rebellious deny these clear revelations. The word \"rebellious\" describes those who stubbornly oppose the truth even after it has been made clear. Their denial is not born of confusion or lack of evidence; it comes from their own rebellion and arrogance. When the truth is clear, denial is a choice, and it is a choice made by the rebellious.\n\nThis verse teaches two things at once. First, it affirms that the Quran is clear and its guidance is plain for those who seek it. Second, it explains why some people still reject it: their hearts are rebellious and they refuse to accept what is evident. The verse therefore places responsibility on the deniers, because the clarity of the revelation leaves them without excuse. For the reader, the verse encourages appreciating the clarity of the Quran and seeking its guidance sincerely. It also serves as a warning against letting rebellion and pride close one’s heart to clear truth, because denial of what is clear comes from the heart, not from the message itself. The clarity of the revelation is itself part of Allah’s mercy, because it removes confusion and excuses. When a person can see the truth plainly, the responsibility for accepting or rejecting it falls on him. This is why the verse is both reassuring and sobering. It reassures the sincere seeker that the way is clear, and it warns the stubborn that they will not be able to claim they never understood. The rebellious are those who, seeing the clear light, still refuse to walk in it.",
  "100": "“Why is it that every time they make a covenant, a group of them casts it aside?”\n\nThis verse asks a pointed question about the behaviour of some of the People of the Book. \"Why is it that every time they make a covenant, a group of them casts it aside?\" The question draws attention to a repeated pattern. Whenever they made a covenant, whether with Allah or with others, a group among them would break it and throw it away. This was not a single failure but a habit. Their agreements meant little to them because they did not take seriously the commitments they made.\n\nThe verse then states, \"In fact, most of them do not believe.\" The constant breaking of covenants is linked to a lack of genuine belief. When true faith is present, a person honours his promises and takes his commitments seriously. But most of them, as the verse says, did not truly believe, and so they had no firm foundation for keeping their word. Their dishonesty in covenants was a sign of the weakness of their faith.\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of keeping promises as a sign of faith. In Islam, fulfilling covenants and keeping one’s word is a fundamental duty, and breaking a promise is a serious fault. The verse shows that the failure of the People of the Book to keep their covenants was a reflection of their lack of true belief. For the reader, the verse encourages honesty and faithfulness in all agreements, because a person of faith honours his commitments. It also warns against the habit of treating promises lightly, which can become a sign of weak or absent faith. Keeping one’s word is both a moral duty and a proof of sincere belief. Keeping promises is one of the clearest signs of moral character and of faith. A person who breaks his covenant shows that his word means little and that his reliability cannot be trusted. In Islam, fulfilling promises is a serious duty, and breaking them is linked to hypocrisy. The verse therefore not only criticizes the People of the Book for their past behaviour but also teaches the reader a lasting principle: honour your commitments, because faithfulness in small and large agreements is a reflection of true belief and a foundation of a just society.",
  "101": "“Now, when a messenger from Allah has come to them—confirming their own Scriptures.”\n\nThis verse describes the response of some of the People of the Book to the coming of the Prophet Muhammad. \"Now, when a messenger from Allah has come to them—confirming their own Scriptures.\" The messenger came with a message that confirmed the scriptures they already had. Their own books had spoken of his coming, and the Quran confirmed what was true in them. This should have made them among the first to accept him, since he was confirming the very truth they claimed to follow.\n\nInstead, the verse says, \"some of the People of the Book cast the Book of Allah behind their backs as if they did not know.\" They turned away from the Book of Allah, casting it behind their backs, as if they had no knowledge of it. The image of throwing something behind one’s back is powerful; it shows deliberate rejection and disregard. They behaved as if they did not know what their own scriptures said, even though they did. Their rejection was a conscious turning away from the truth.\n\nThis verse exposes the dishonesty of rejecting a messenger who confirms one’s own scripture. If their scriptures pointed to his coming, then accepting him would be consistent with their faith, while rejecting him contradicted it. By casting the Book behind their backs, they showed that their real concern was not truth but their own interests and envy. For the reader, the verse teaches the importance of accepting the truth wherever it is found, even when it means accepting a new messenger. It also warns against turning away from what one knows to be true, and against letting pride or worldly interest stand between oneself and the guidance of Allah. The image of casting the Book behind one’s back is a warning to all who have been given knowledge. It is possible to know the truth and still turn away from it, treating it as though it had no value. The People of the Book knew their scriptures pointed to the coming messenger, yet they ignored that knowledge when it did not suit them. The verse therefore calls the reader to examine how he treats the knowledge he has, and to be honest in acting on the truth rather than setting it aside when it is inconvenient.",
  "102": "“They ˹instead˺ followed the magic promoted by the devils during the reign of Solomon.”\n\nThis verse is among the more complex verses of the chapter, and it deals with magic and a false claim about the prophet Solomon. \"They ˹instead˺ followed the magic promoted by the devils during the reign of Solomon.\" Some of the People of the Book claimed that Solomon was a magician and that he ruled by magic. The Quran rejects this at once: \"Never did Solomon disbelieve, rather the devils disbelieved.\" Solomon was a prophet of Allah, innocent of such things. It was the devils who disbelieved and who taught magic to the people.\n\nThe verse then mentions two angels: \"along with what had been revealed to the two angels, Hârût and Mârût, in Babylon.\" Scholars explain that these two angels were sent as a test, and they taught some people a form of sorcery, but always with a warning: \"The two angels never taught anyone without saying, 'We are only a test ˹for you˺, so do not abandon ˹your˺ faith.'\" They warned that what they were teaching was a trial and that the people should not abandon faith because of it. This shows that even the knowledge of magic was presented as a test of faith.\n\nThe verse then describes the harm of magic: \"Yet people learned ˹magic˺ that caused a rift ˹even˺ between husband and wife; although their magic could not harm anyone except by Allah's Will.\" Magic could cause harm, but only by Allah’s permission; it was not an independent power. The verse ends with a warning: \"They learned what harmed them and did not benefit them—although they already knew that whoever buys into magic would have no share in the Hereafter. Miserable indeed was the price for which they sold their souls, if only they knew!\" Those who turned to magic traded away their share in the Hereafter. The verse teaches that magic is forbidden and harmful, that no power operates independently of Allah, and that abandoning faith for magic is a miserable bargain.",
  "103": "“If only they were faithful and mindful ˹of Allah˺.”\n\nThis verse follows the discussion of magic with a statement of what would have been better. \"If only they were faithful and mindful ˹of Allah˺.\" The people who turned to magic would have been far better off if they had remained faithful to Allah and mindful of Him. Instead of seeking power through forbidden means, they should have trusted in Allah and kept their faith pure. Their departure from faith, whether by practicing magic or abandoning the truth, brought them only harm.\n\nThe verse continues, \"there would have been a better reward from Allah, if only they knew!\" The reward for faith and mindfulness is better than anything they sought through magic. They sought worldly power, influence, or control, but the reward of Allah for the faithful is far greater and more lasting. The phrase \"if only they knew\" expresses regret, showing that if they truly understood the value of what they were giving up, they would not have exchanged it for magic.\n\nThis verse teaches that faith and mindfulness are the surest path to true reward. The pursuit of forbidden means, such as magic, offers nothing that compares to the reward of Allah. The verse also shows that people often harm themselves because they do not fully grasp the value of faith. If they knew the reward that awaits the faithful, they would never trade it away. For the reader, the verse encourages remaining faithful and mindful of Allah, trusting that His reward is far better than anything the world or forbidden means can offer. It also warns against being so focused on worldly gain that one forgets the far greater value of the Hereafter. The verse also carries a general lesson about where true value lies. People are often attracted to the appearance of power and control, whether through magic, wealth, or influence, but these are poor substitutes for the reward of Allah. Faith and mindfulness bring peace, protection, and a reward that never ends. The phrase \"if only they knew\" is a reminder that human beings often do not grasp the true worth of things until it is too late. The wise person, however, seeks the lasting reward of Allah and refuses to exchange it for anything less.",
  "104": "“O believers! Do not say, ‘Râ’ina.’”\n\nThis verse gives the believers guidance about their speech toward the Prophet. \"O believers! Do not say, 'Râ'ina.'\" The word \"Râ'ina\" was a word that, in Arabic, could carry the meaning of \"hear us\" or \"tend to us,\" but it also had a mocking and offensive sense, because it resembled a word of insult in another language. Some enemies of the believers used this word to mock the Prophet, twisting it into an insult while appearing to speak respectfully. The verse tells the believers not to use this word, so that the mockers would not find a way to twist their speech.\n\nThe verse tells them what to say instead: \"But say, 'Unẓurna,' [Tend to us!] and listen ˹attentively˺.\" They were to use a different word that carried the same meaning of \"attend to us\" or \"wait for us\" but could not be twisted into an insult. The verse also tells them to \"listen attentively,\" reminding them to pay attention to what the Prophet said. This guidance protected both the believers and the Prophet from mockery.\n\nThe verse ends, \"And the disbelievers will suffer a painful punishment.\" Those who used their speech to mock and insult would face punishment. This verse teaches the importance of guarding one’s speech and choosing words carefully. The believers were asked to change a word, not because the word itself was always wrong, but because it could be misused. This shows sensitivity and wisdom in how Muslims should speak and conduct themselves. For the reader, the verse teaches that speech should be chosen with care, that believers should avoid giving their enemies any opportunity to mock the truth, and that those who use words to attack faith will be held accountable. The guidance in this verse about choosing words carefully is a lasting principle of Islamic manners. A believer is asked to be mindful of how his speech may be used and interpreted, especially in the presence of opponents and mockers. Choosing the right word is not a small matter; it can protect the reputation of the community and prevent the mocking of sacred things. The verse also shows the love and care of Allah for His Prophet and for the believers, guiding them in the details of their conduct so that they are not harmed by the enmity of others.",
  "105": "“The disbelievers from the People of the Book and the polytheists would not want you to receive any blessing from your Lord.”\n\nThis verse exposes the envy of those who opposed the believers. \"The disbelievers from the People of the Book and the polytheists would not want you to receive any blessing from your Lord.\" The disbelievers, whether they were people of scripture who rejected the message or polytheists who worshipped others alongside Allah, did not want the believers to receive any good. They envied the blessing that Allah had given to the Muslims. This envy is the same envy that led the People of the Book to reject the Prophet, resenting that Allah had chosen him.\n\nThe verse then affirms the sovereignty of Allah in granting mercy: \"but Allah selects whoever He wills for His mercy.\" The mercy of Allah is not given according to the wishes of people, but according to Allah’s own wisdom and choice. Allah selects for His mercy whomever He wills. No amount of envy or opposition can change this. The believers were blessed, and the disbelievers could not prevent it.\n\nThe verse ends, \"And Allah is the Lord of infinite bounty.\" Allah’s generosity has no limit. His bounty is vast and never runs out. This statement is a source of comfort and confidence. Whatever the disbelievers wished for the believers, Allah’s bounty was far greater, and He could grant it freely. For the reader, the verse teaches not to be troubled by the envy of others. Envy cannot stop the mercy of Allah. It also warns against envy, which is a destructive quality that led people to reject truth and harm themselves. True trust is placed in Allah, who is the Lord of infinite bounty and gives to whom He wills. Envy is a destructive emotion that the Quran repeatedly warns against. It leads a person to wish harm upon others and to reject the truth simply because it came to someone else. The disbelievers’ envy of the believers’ blessing is a clear example. The verse teaches the believer not to be disturbed by the envy of others, because the mercy of Allah is not in the hands of envious people. It also warns the reader against letting envy grow in his own heart, since envy harms the one who feels it more than the one envied.",
  "106": "“If We ever abrogate a verse or cause it to be forgotten.”\n\nThis verse addresses a question that arose about the Quran itself. \"If We ever abrogate a verse or cause it to be forgotten.\" Abrogation means that a ruling or verse revealed earlier is replaced by a later one, and it is part of how divine law was revealed gradually. The verse mentions two possibilities: a verse being abrogated, or a verse being made to be forgotten. In both cases, Allah replaces it with something.\n\nThe verse continues, \"We replace it with a better or similar one.\" Whatever replaces an abrogated verse is either better or at least similar. This shows that the changes in revelation were not arbitrary; they were part of a wise plan. A later ruling might be better suited to the circumstances, or it might be similar to what came before. In all cases, the replacement comes from Allah, who knows what is best.\n\nThe verse ends with a question: \"Do you not know that Allah is Most Capable of everything?\" Allah is fully capable of revealing, abrogating, and replacing verses. Nothing is beyond His power. This question responds to those who doubted or objected to the idea of abrogation, as some of the People of the Book used it as an objection to the Quran. The verse teaches that abrogation is part of Allah’s wisdom and is not a weakness or contradiction in revelation. It shows that Allah guides His servants in stages, replacing rulings as needed while preserving the essence of the truth. For the reader, the verse affirms trust in Allah’s wisdom and power, and teaches that divine guidance is given according to Allah’s perfect plan. The idea of abrogation can seem difficult, but the verse explains it in simple terms. Allah revealed the Quran over time, and some rulings were revealed to suit particular circumstances before being replaced by later guidance. This is a sign of wisdom, not a contradiction. Allah knows what His servants need at each stage, and He guides them accordingly. The verse therefore invites the reader to trust Allah’s plan, recognising that the One who reveals the guidance is fully aware of what is best and is capable of bringing it about.",
  "107": "“Do you not know that the kingdom of the heavens and the earth belongs ˹only˺ to Allah?”\n\nThis verse reminds the reader of the sovereignty of Allah. \"Do you not know that the kingdom of the heavens and the earth belongs ˹only˺ to Allah?\" The word \"kingdom\" refers to full ownership and authority. Everything in the heavens and the earth belongs to Allah alone. He is the sole owner and ruler of all that exists. This is a fact that every believer should know and keep in mind, because it shapes how one understands power, blessing, and dependence.\n\nThe verse then states, \"and you have no guardian or helper besides Allah.\" Since all power belongs to Allah, no one else can truly guard or help. The word \"guardian\" points to one who protects and looks after, and \"helper\" points to one who comes to one’s aid. Neither exists besides Allah in any real sense. All protection and help ultimately come from Him. Anyone or anything else is simply a means, and Allah is the true source.\n\nThis verse teaches complete reliance on Allah. When a person truly understands that the kingdom belongs to Allah alone and that there is no guardian or helper besides Him, he places his trust in Allah rather than in created beings. This does not mean neglecting the means of life; it means recognizing that all power ultimately rests with Allah. For the reader, the verse encourages trust and dependence on Allah in all matters. It also answers those who might rely on false guardians or helpers, reminding them that none besides Allah can truly protect or assist. The verse is a foundation of sincere faith and reliance on Allah. This truth has practical consequences for daily life. If the kingdom belongs to Allah alone, then a believer does not despair when human support fails, because true help comes from Allah. Neither does he become arrogant when he has power, because that power is only a trust from Allah. Reliance on Allah gives stability in both success and hardship, because the believer knows that every good and every protection ultimately comes from the One who owns all things. This is the peace that comes from placing one’s trust in Allah rather than in created beings.",
  "108": "“Or do you ˹believers˺ intend to ask of your Messenger as Moses was asked before?”\n\nThis verse warns the believers against a certain kind of questioning. \"Or do you ˹believers˺ intend to ask of your Messenger as Moses was asked before?\" The reference is to the children of Israel, who asked Moses for extraordinary things, such as demanding to see Allah, rather than accepting what he brought with humility. The verse warns the believers not to repeat this pattern by burdening their Messenger with unnecessary or impossible requests. Their predecessors had asked in this way and brought hardship upon themselves.\n\nThe verse then states, \"But whoever trades belief for disbelief has truly strayed from the Right Way.\" Those who exchanged belief for disbelief, whether by rejecting the Messenger or by demanding what they should not, strayed far from the right path. The word \"trades\" shows that their change was a choice, an exchange of something good for something bad. The result of such a trade is straying, losing the right way.\n\nThis verse teaches the proper attitude toward the Messenger and the revelation. The believers are to accept what is brought to them with trust and obedience, not to test or burden the Messenger with demands that their predecessors had made. The verse also warns against the destructive habit of trading belief for disbelief, whether through excessive demands, doubt, or rejection. For the reader, the verse encourages humility in approaching divine guidance and warns against repeating the mistakes of the past. It teaches that true faith is shown by acceptance and obedience, not by testing the limits of the message. The verse also distinguishes between sincere questions, which seek understanding, and demanding questions, which challenge authority. The children of Israel had asked in a demanding way, testing their prophet and making unreasonable requests. The believers are warned not to follow this path. It is good to seek knowledge and ask for clarification, but it is wrong to test the Messenger or to demand signs beyond what is needed. The proper attitude is humble acceptance combined with sincere seeking of understanding.",
  "109": "“Many among the People of the Book wish they could turn you ˹believers˺ back to disbelief.”\n\nThis verse reveals the hostile wishes of some among the People of the Book. \"Many among the People of the Book wish they could turn you ˹believers˺ back to disbelief because of their envy.\" The motive is named clearly: envy. They wished to turn the believers away from their faith, driven by resentment that the blessing of guidance had come to the Muslims. Their envy had led them to desire the very opposite of good for the believers.\n\nThe verse notes that this envy persists \"after the truth has been made clear to them.\" They knew the truth, yet their envy continued. Their wish to turn the believers back to disbelief was not based on ignorance but on resentment. The verse then gives the believers guidance: \"Pardon and bear with them until Allah delivers His decision.\" The believers were told to pardon and to be patient, not to respond to envy with hatred or retaliation, but to wait for Allah’s decision.\n\nThe verse ends, \"Surely Allah is Most Capable of everything.\" Allah is fully able to handle the matter, to protect the believers, and to deal justly with those who envy them. This is a call to leave the outcome to Allah. For the reader, the verse teaches patience in the face of envy and hostility. Envious people may wish harm, but the believer is told to pardon and bear with them while trusting in Allah. The verse also warns against envy, which is a destructive emotion that led people to reject truth and wish harm on the believers. True reliance on Allah brings peace even in the face of hostility. The command to pardon and bear with the hostile, while waiting for Allah’s decision, shows the patience expected of the believers. Even when others wish them harm out of envy, the believers were told not to respond with hatred or vengeance but to leave the matter to Allah. This guidance reflects the strength of true faith, which can endure hostility without losing its composure or its trust in Allah. The verse also reassures the believers that Allah is fully capable of protecting them and of dealing justly with those who oppose the truth.",
  "110": "“Establish prayer, and pay alms-tax.”\n\nThis verse returns to two core acts of worship. \"Establish prayer, and pay alms-tax.\" Prayer is the worship of the body and the soul, connecting the believer to Allah in regular devotion. Alms-tax is the worship through wealth, giving a portion to those in need. The two are repeatedly joined in the Quran because together they cover the believer’s duty to Allah and to fellow human beings. Establishing prayer and paying alms are signs of sincere faith.\n\nThe verse then makes a profound promise: \"Whatever good you send forth for yourselves, you will ˹certainly˺ find ˹its reward˺ with Allah.\" The phrase \"send forth\" is important; it refers to good deeds that a person does in this life, which will be found later, in the Hereafter. Nothing good is lost. Whatever a person sends ahead, whether prayer, charity, or any righteous deed, will be found with Allah as a reward. This promise removes any doubt that good deeds are wasted.\n\nThe verse ends, \"Surely Allah is All-Seeing of what you do.\" Allah sees everything, so nothing is overlooked, whether good or bad. This both encourages good deeds, because their reward is certain, and warns against negligence, because nothing is hidden. For the reader, the verse teaches the importance of prayer and charity, and the certainty of reward for good deeds. It encourages sending good ahead to the Hereafter, where it will be found with Allah. It also reminds the believer that Allah’s sight covers all actions, so deeds are done sincerely and in His presence. The verse’s promise that good deeds will be found with Allah gives meaning and hope to every act of worship. Nothing sincere is wasted, no matter how small. Prayer and charity, performed with faith, are stored as rewards that the believer will meet in the Hereafter. This encourages consistency in good deeds, because the reward is certain. It also reminds the believer that Allah’s sight is over all, so deeds are done with sincerity and care, knowing that the One who sees them will reward them.",
  "111": "“The Jews and Christians each claim that none will enter Paradise except those of their own faith.”\n\nThis verse records the claims of two communities. \"The Jews and Christians each claim that none will enter Paradise except those of their own faith.\" Each community claimed that Paradise was reserved exclusively for its own members. The Jews said that only Jews would enter Paradise, and the Christians said that only Christians would enter. These claims contradicted each other, yet both were made with confidence. The Quran describes these claims as \"their desires,\" meaning they were wishful thinking rather than truth. Each community wanted to believe that it alone was saved.\n\nThe verse then tells the Prophet how to respond: \"Reply, ˹O Prophet,˺ 'Show ˹me˺ your proof if what you say is true.'\" If they truly had such a claim, they should be able to support it with proof. A claim as serious as this, deciding who enters Paradise, must be based on evidence from Allah, not merely on wishes. Since they had no such proof, their claims were empty. The Quran challenges them to produce evidence they could not provide.\n\nThis verse teaches that salvation is not a matter of group pride or wishful thinking. No community has a monopoly on Paradise. What matters is truth and deeds, not the label one carries. The challenge to produce proof shows that claims about the Hereafter must be grounded in revelation and reality. For the reader, the verse warns against the arrogance of assuming one’s own community is exclusively saved, and it encourages judging oneself by the standard of truth and good deeds. The next verse states the actual criterion for salvation, making clear that it is not mere membership in a group. The verse also reveals how religious pride can blind people to the truth. Each community was so convinced of its own exclusive claim that it could not see the falseness of rejecting the other. This is a warning against group arrogance in any form. True religion humbles a person and connects him to Allah, while pride in one’s group separates people and leads them to make claims they cannot support. The challenge to produce proof calls every community back to evidence and truth rather than to inherited prejudice.",
  "112": "“But no! Whoever submits themselves to Allah and does good will have their reward with their Lord.”\n\nThis verse corrects the false claims of the previous verse. \"But no! Whoever submits themselves to Allah and does good will have their reward with their Lord.\" The word \"no\" rejects the idea that Paradise belongs exclusively to one community. The real criterion is stated clearly: submission to Allah and doing good. \"Submits\" refers to a person who surrenders to Allah in faith and obedience, worshipping Him alone. \"Does good\" refers to righteous action. Both together form the path to reward.\n\nThe verse then gives the promise: \"And there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve.\" This is the same promise that appears several times in the Quran for those who believe and do good. The one who submits to Allah and does good has no reason to fear the future or grieve over the past. Their reward with their Lord is secure.\n\nThis verse teaches the universality of salvation in Islam. It is not based on nationality, ancestry, or community, but on sincere submission to Allah and good deeds. Anyone, from any background, who meets these conditions has a reward with their Lord. This was a profound message in a time when communities claimed exclusive salvation. For the reader, the verse encourages focusing on what truly matters: sincere faith in Allah and righteous action. It also corrects the pride of those who rely on their group identity, reminding them that the standard of Allah is faith and deeds. The promise of no fear and no grief brings comfort to all who sincerely submit to Allah. This verse is one of the clearest statements in the Quran about the path to salvation, and it is addressed to people of all backgrounds. It does not require a person to belong to any particular nation or to inherit faith from his ancestors. It requires submission to Allah and good deeds. This makes the way open to everyone. The verse therefore brings both a warning and a hope: a warning that no one is saved by mere group membership, and a hope that anyone who sincerely submits to Allah and does good will have a reward with their Lord, with no fear and no grief.",
  "113": "“The Jews say, ‘The Christians have nothing to stand on’ and the Christians say, ‘The Jews have nothing to stand on.’”\n\nThis verse records the mutual accusations between the Jews and the Christians. \"The Jews say, 'The Christians have nothing to stand on' and the Christians say, 'The Jews have nothing to stand on.'\" Each community dismissed the religion of the other, claiming the other had no valid basis. This mutual rejection showed how far they had fallen into rivalry and pride. Their claims contradicted each other, and both could not be true.\n\nThe verse notes the irony: \"although both recite the Scriptures.\" Both communities claimed to follow scriptures, yet each said the other had nothing to stand on. Their possession of scriptures did not prevent them from rejecting one another. Then the verse adds, \"And those ˹pagans˺ who have no knowledge say the same ˹about people of faith˺.\" The pagans, who had no knowledge, made similar claims against people of faith. Both the people of scripture and the pagans were making unfounded claims about religion.\n\nThe verse ends, \"Surely Allah will judge between them on the Day of Judgment regarding their dispute.\" On the Day of Judgment, Allah will settle their disputes with perfect justice. He alone knows the truth of their conflicting claims. For the reader, the verse teaches the danger of religious rivalry and pride. Claiming that one’s own group is right and all others are wrong, without proof, leads to conflict and falsehood. The verse also teaches that final judgement belongs to Allah alone, not to human claimants. It encourages humility in religious matters and reliance on Allah’s just decision rather than on mutual accusation and pride. The verse also teaches the value of humility in religious matters. The Jews and Christians were each so sure of their own position that they dismissed the other entirely, and the pagans, with no knowledge, made similar claims. The Quran points out the absurdity of these confident claims by noting that both communities recited scriptures while rejecting each other. The final judgement is left to Allah, who knows the full truth of every dispute. For the reader, this is a call to hold religious beliefs with humility, to seek truth, and to recognise that human disputes will ultimately be settled by Allah’s perfect justice.",
  "114": "“Who does more wrong than those who prevent Allah’s Name from being mentioned in His places of worship and strive to destroy them?”\n\nThis verse condemns the worst kind of wrongdoing against houses of worship. \"Who does more wrong than those who prevent Allah's Name from being mentioned in His places of worship and strive to destroy them?\" The question makes clear that there is no one who does more wrong than the person who stops the worship of Allah in His mosques and places of prayer, and who works to destroy them. To block the remembrance of Allah from His houses and to tear them down is a grave injustice that goes beyond ordinary wrongdoing.\n\nThe verse then states the consequence: \"Such people have no right to enter these places except with fear.\" Those who have committed such aggression have no rightful place entering the mosques, except in a state of fear, meaning they should not feel safe or welcome there because of what they have done. Their aggression has cut them off from the sanctity of the place. The verse continues, \"For them there is disgrace in this world, and they will suffer a tremendous punishment in the Hereafter.\" Their punishment is double: disgrace in this life and a tremendous punishment in the next.\n\nScholars explain that this verse was revealed in connection with the disbelievers of Quraysh who prevented the Prophet and the believers from worshipping at the Sacred Mosque, and who sought to destroy or hinder the message. The principle, however, is general and applies to all who oppose the worship of Allah. For the reader, the verse teaches the great respect due to places of worship and the seriousness of obstructing the remembrance of Allah. It also brings comfort that those who oppress the people of faith will face disgrace and punishment, while the worship of Allah can never be truly destroyed.",
  "115": "“To Allah belong the east and the west.”\n\nThis verse affirms the all-encompassing ownership of Allah. \"To Allah belong the east and the west.\" The east and the west refer to the whole of the earth, every direction in which one can turn. All of it belongs to Allah. The verse then draws a remarkable conclusion: \"so wherever you turn you are facing ˹towards˺ Allah.\" No matter which direction a person faces, Allah is there. This means that no direction is outside the reach of Allah, and His presence encompasses all directions.\n\nThe verse continues, \"Surely Allah is All-Encompassing, All-Knowing.\" Allah encompasses everything in His knowledge and power. Nothing is beyond His reach or hidden from His sight. The verse therefore teaches that Allah is not limited to any direction or place; He is the Creator and Owner of all directions and places.\n\nScholars explain that this verse was revealed in connection with a situation where Muslims were unsure about the direction of prayer, and it reassured them that Allah encompasses everything and knows all. It does not cancel the command to face the qiblah, the direction of the Sacred Mosque, which is established elsewhere in the chapter; rather, it reminds the reader that while believers are commanded to face a specific direction in prayer, Allah Himself is not confined to any direction. For the reader, the verse teaches the greatness of Allah, whose presence encompasses all of creation. It also reassures the believer that wherever one turns to Allah in sincere supplication, Allah hears and knows, because He is All-Encompassing and All-Knowing. This truth should not be misunderstood. The Quran does command the believers to face a specific direction when they pray, the Sacred Mosque in Mecca, and that command is confirmed in this very chapter. The point of this verse is deeper: it reminds the reader that while people are commanded to face one direction in prayer, Allah is not confined to any place or direction. His presence and knowledge encompass all of creation. This combines the discipline of facing the qiblah in worship with the awareness that Allah is above and beyond all directions.",
  "116": "“They say, ‘Allah has offspring.’”\n\nThis verse responds to a false claim about Allah. \"They say, 'Allah has offspring.'\" Some people claimed that Allah had children or offspring, whether the Christians who claimed Jesus was the son of Allah, the pagans who claimed angels were daughters of Allah, or others who made similar claims. The Quran answers this claim with the words \"Glory be to Him!\" which declares Allah’s perfection and distance from anything unbecoming. Allah is far above having offspring, because He has no equal and nothing can be born of Him or be His equal.\n\nThe verse then states the truth: \"In fact, to Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth—all are subject to His Will.\" Everything in the heavens and the earth belongs to Allah and is subject to His will. Nothing in creation is His equal or His child; everything is His servant and His creation. The claim that Allah has offspring is a confusion between Creator and creation. Allah alone is the Creator, and everything else is His creation, existing only by His will.\n\nThis verse teaches the oneness of Allah and corrects false beliefs about Him. The Quran repeatedly rejects the idea that Allah has offspring, whether from the Christians, the pagans, or others. To attribute offspring to Allah is to misunderstand His nature completely, because He is unique and has no equal. For the reader, the verse affirms pure monotheism, the belief that Allah is one, with no partner, no equal, and no offspring. Everything in creation belongs to Him and is subject to His will, and He alone is worthy of worship. The verse protects the believer from false beliefs by grounding faith in the truth of Allah’s uniqueness. The phrase \"all are subject to His Will\" is central to understanding the verse. Everything in creation is a servant of Allah, existing only by His will and fulfilling His command. This is the opposite of having offspring, because offspring are equals and partners, whereas everything in creation is subordinate to Allah. The verse therefore protects the pure oneness of Allah and rejects any belief that gives Allah a child or a partner. This correction of false belief about Allah is a constant theme in the Quran, because proper worship depends on a correct understanding of who Allah is.",
  "117": "“˹He is˺ the Originator of the heavens and the earth!”\n\nThis verse describes Allah as the Originator of the heavens and the earth. The word \"Originator\" points to the One who creates something from nothing, without any previous example or model. Allah originated the heavens and the earth, bringing them into existence by His will. This title establishes Allah’s absolute power as the Creator who needs nothing and no example to create.\n\nThe verse then gives a powerful description of how Allah creates: \"When He decrees a matter, He simply tells it, 'Be!' And it is!\" This is one of the most striking phrases in the Quran. When Allah wills something to happen, He simply commands it to be, and it comes into existence. There is no delay, no difficulty, and no need for a cause. His word is enough. The command \"Be!\" is immediate and effective. This shows the infinite power of Allah, for whom nothing is hard or impossible.\n\nThis verse teaches that creation is entirely in the hands of Allah. He originated everything, and He brings things into being simply by His command. This truth has many consequences. It removes all doubt about Allah’s power, because the One who can create the heavens and the earth with a word can do anything. It also reminds the reader that Allah is not like His creation, which needs tools and causes, but is the Creator who acts by His own will. For the reader, the verse strengthens faith in Allah’s power and encourages trust in Him, knowing that what Allah wills, He brings about, and nothing can stand in the way of His command. This understanding of creation has great consequences for faith. If Allah creates by a simple word, then nothing is too hard for Him, whether bringing the dead back to life, rewarding the righteous, or punishing the wrongdoers. The same power that originated the heavens and the earth will raise the dead on the Day of Judgment. This is why the Quran connects the power of creation with the reality of resurrection. The One who said \"Be!\" to the heavens and the earth can surely say \"Be!\" to the resurrection of all people. Faith in Allah’s creative power is therefore the foundation of faith in the Hereafter.",
  "118": "“Those who have no knowledge say, ‘If only Allah would speak to us or a sign would come to us!’”\n\nThis verse records a demand made by people without knowledge. \"Those who have no knowledge say, 'If only Allah would speak to us or a sign would come to us!'\" They wished that Allah would speak to them directly, or that some spectacular sign would come to them, so that they could believe. They demanded an extraordinary proof before they would accept the message. This was not a sincere seeking of guidance but an excuse for not accepting what was already clear.\n\nThe verse responds to this demand: \"The same was said by those who came before. Their hearts are all alike.\" The people before them had made the same demands, asking for direct speech from Allah or for signs, and their hearts were like the hearts of these people. This shows that such demands are not new and are not sincere. The same excuse keeps being repeated by people who do not want to believe. Their hearts are alike in their stubbornness.\n\nThe verse then states, \"Indeed, We have made the signs clear for people of sure faith.\" The signs have already been made clear, but they benefit \"people of sure faith,\" those whose hearts are sincere and open. The signs are clear, but those who demand ever more signs without sincerity will not be satisfied. For the reader, the verse warns against demanding spectacular proofs while ignoring the clear signs that already exist. True guidance comes to those who seek it sincerely, not to those who keep moving the goalposts. It also teaches that the demand for more signs, when the truth is already clear, comes from a hardened heart rather than from a sincere search for truth.",
  "119": "“We have surely sent you with the truth ˹O Prophet˺ as a deliverer of good news and a warner.”\n\nThis verse describes the mission of the Prophet. \"We have surely sent you with the truth ˹O Prophet˺ as a deliverer of good news and a warner.\" The Prophet was sent with the truth, meaning the message of the Quran and true guidance. His role was twofold: to deliver good news and to warn. He gave good news to those who believed and did good, and he warned those who rejected the truth. This is the mission of all prophets and messengers of Allah.\n\nThe verse then tells the Prophet, \"And you will not be accountable for the residents of the Hellfire.\" The Prophet was not responsible for whether people believed; his duty was to deliver the message. Those who rejected the truth and ended in the Hellfire were accountable for themselves. The verse relieves the Prophet of the burden of other people’s choices, and it also sets a pattern for every caller to the truth. A person conveys the message sincerely, but the acceptance or rejection is a matter of each person’s own choice and responsibility.\n\nThis verse teaches the limits of a messenger’s responsibility. The Prophet did not control people’s hearts; he delivered the truth and warned clearly. This brings both comfort and clarity. It comforts the one who conveys the truth, because he is not held responsible for the response of others. It also clarifies that every person is responsible for his own response to the truth. For the reader, the verse encourages conveying the truth with sincerity and leaving the outcome to Allah. It also reminds the believer that guidance is from Allah, and that a person’s duty is to deliver the message and to live by it.",
  "120": "“Never will the Jews or Christians be pleased with you, until you follow their faith.”\n\nThis verse states a difficult truth for the Prophet and the believers. \"Never will the Jews or Christians be pleased with you, until you follow their faith.\" The disbelievers among the People of the Book would not be satisfied with the Muslims as long as they remained on their own faith. Their displeasure would only end if the Muslims abandoned their faith and followed theirs. This reveals the true motive behind much of their opposition: they were not seeking harmony but wanted the believers to give up their religion.\n\nThe verse then tells the Prophet how to respond: \"Say, 'Allah's guidance is the only ˹true˺ guidance.'\" The response to those who want the believers to change their faith is that Allah’s guidance is the only true guidance. The believers follow Allah’s guidance, not the desires of people. No matter how much the disbelievers press, the believers remain firm on the truth that came from Allah.\n\nThe verse then adds a serious warning: \"And if you were to follow their desires after ˹all˺ the knowledge that has come to you, there would be none to protect or help you against Allah.\" If the Prophet, or the believers, were to follow the desires of the People of the Book after receiving clear knowledge from Allah, then no one could protect them from Allah’s punishment. This is a strong warning against abandoning the truth for the sake of pleasing others. For the reader, the verse teaches firmness in faith and the courage not to compromise the truth to gain the approval of others. It also warns that pleasing people at the expense of the truth has no protector against Allah. The verse also teaches the importance of not seeking the approval of others at the expense of the truth. People will often be displeased with those who follow the guidance of Allah, and no amount of compromise will satisfy them if they want the believers to abandon their faith. The believers are therefore called to a higher loyalty: loyalty to Allah’s guidance. This gives the believer strength and independence of spirit, because he does not depend on the approval of people for his confidence. His guidance comes from Allah, and that is enough.",
  "121": "“Those We have given the Book follow it as it should be followed.”\n\nThis verse describes those who truly benefit from the Book. \"Those We have given the Book follow it as it should be followed.\" The people described here follow the scripture in the right way, with sincerity, understanding, and obedience. They do not twist it or pick and choose, but they follow it as it deserves to be followed. This is the proper attitude toward the scripture of Allah. The verse then says, \"It is they who ˹truly˺ believe in it.\" Those who follow the Book properly are the ones who truly believe in it, because their belief is shown in their actions.\n\nThe verse then contrasts them with others: \"As for those who reject it, it is they who are the losers.\" Those who reject the Book, refusing to follow it, are the losers. Their rejection brings loss, because they turn away from the guidance that would save them. The contrast between those who follow the Book properly and those who reject it is complete. One group believes and benefits; the other rejects and loses.\n\nThis verse teaches that the value of the Book is realized through following it properly. Merely possessing a scripture, or claiming to believe in it, is not enough. What matters is following it as it should be followed, with sincerity and obedience. This applies to all scriptures and to the Quran itself. For the reader, the verse is a call to follow the Quran properly, to believe in it truly, and to put it into practice. It also warns against rejection, which leads to loss. The measure of true belief is how a person follows the Book of Allah in his life. This verse also answers a question that some might ask: how is the Book to be followed properly? The verse points to sincerity and completeness. To follow the Book as it should be followed is to accept its truth, to act on its commands, to refrain from what it forbids, and to believe in it with the heart. It is not to distort it or to pick and choose convenient parts. Those who follow the Book this way are the true believers, and those who reject it are the losers. This principle applies to the Quran above all, calling every reader to receive it with sincerity and put it into practice.",
  "122": "“O Children of Israel! Remember My favours upon you.”\n\nThis verse repeats a call that has appeared earlier in the chapter. \"O Children of Israel! Remember My favours upon you.\" The repetition shows the importance of the message. The children of Israel were being asked, once again, to recall the favours Allah had shown them. Remembering favours is a way of building gratitude, and gratitude is a way of building obedience. The verse then adds, \"and how I honoured you above the others.\" This refers to the special status Allah gave them at that time, choosing them to receive prophets, scriptures, and guidance above the nations of their era.\n\nThe repetition of this call at different points in the chapter serves a purpose. It keeps bringing the children of Israel back to the reality of what Allah had done for them, so that they would recognize their duty in return. The favours were meant to lead to faith and obedience, not to pride or arrogance. The honour they received was a responsibility as much as a privilege.\n\nThis verse teaches that divine favours should be remembered and met with gratitude. When a person or a community forgets the favours of Allah, they become ungrateful and disobedient. When they remember, their hearts are filled with thankfulness. For the reader, the verse encourages reflecting on the blessings of Allah in one’s own life and responding with gratitude and obedience. It also serves as a reminder that privilege and honour come with responsibility, and that those who are given much are expected to give much in return. There is wisdom in the repetition of this reminder. Repetition helps something settle firmly in the heart, and the Quran wants this gratitude to be deeply rooted. The children of Israel were not being reminded once and then forgotten; they were being called again and again to the favours of Allah, so that thankfulness would become a settled habit. The same lesson applies to every believer. Because people easily forget, reminders are needed. A person should regularly reflect on the favours of Allah in his own life, which keeps the heart soft, grateful, and obedient, rather than ungrateful and heedless.",
  "123": "“And guard yourselves against the Day when no soul will be of any help to another.”\n\nThis verse repeats a warning that appeared earlier in the chapter, reinforcing its importance. \"And guard yourselves against the Day when no soul will be of any help to another.\" The Day of Judgment is a day of total personal responsibility. No soul will be able to help another on that day. Every person will stand alone before Allah, facing the consequences of his own deeds. The ties of family, friendship, and community will not save anyone on that day.\n\nThe verse then lists what will not avail: \"No ransom will be taken, no intercession accepted, and no help will be given.\" No ransom, however large, will be accepted to free a person from his sins. No intercession will be accepted for those who have rejected the truth. No help will come from any side. The verse removes every false hope, leaving only the reality of each person’s deeds.\n\nThe repetition of this warning shows how important it is. The children of Israel were being reminded, as were the believers, that no status, ancestry, or claim would protect anyone on that day. The only thing that will avail is faith and righteous deeds done in this life. For the reader, the verse is a call to take the Hereafter seriously and to prepare for it now. It teaches that every person is responsible for himself, that no one can save another from the consequences of his deeds, and that the time to act is before the Day arrives, through sincere faith and good works. This repetition also serves to connect different parts of the chapter and to keep the Hereafter constantly before the reader. The Quran does not mention the Day of Judgment only once and then move on; it returns to it again and again, because it is the reality that gives meaning to everything else. The warning that no soul will help another and that no ransom or intercession will be accepted removes every false reliance. This is meant to drive the reader back to the only thing that works: sincere faith and righteous deeds done in this life, before the Day arrives when no help can be found.",
  "124": "“˹Remember˺ when Abraham was tested by his Lord with ˹certain˺ commandments, which he fulfilled.”\n\nThis verse begins the story of the prophet Abraham, one of the greatest and most honoured of the prophets. \"˹Remember˺ when Abraham was tested by his Lord with ˹certain˺ commandments, which he fulfilled.\" Allah tested Abraham with certain commands, and Abraham fulfilled them all. The word \"tested\" shows that Abraham’s faith was tried through commands and trials, and he passed every test. His obedience was complete. This is why Allah honoured him so greatly.\n\nBecause Abraham fulfilled the commandments, \"Allah said, 'I will certainly make you into a role model for the people.'\" Abraham was to be made an example, a leader, and a guide for people. This is a great honour, and it was earned through his perfect obedience. Abraham then asked, \"What about my offspring?\" He wanted to know whether this honour would also extend to his descendants. \"Allah replied, 'My covenant is not extended to the wrongdoers.'\" The answer is that the covenant, the leadership and guidance, is not given to wrongdoers, even if they are descendants of Abraham.\n\nThis verse teaches several important lessons. It shows that leadership in religion is earned through obedience and righteousness, not inherited through ancestry. Abraham’s descendants were not guaranteed the covenant if they were wrongdoers. The honour of being a role model goes to those who fulfil the commands of Allah. For the reader, the verse encourages striving for righteousness rather than relying on ancestry or lineage. It also introduces the story of Abraham and Ishmael building the Sacred House, which continues in the following verses, showing how the covenant of guidance and leadership was to continue through those who submit to Allah and do right. The response to Abraham’s question is also a lesson in how divine favour is given. Abraham asked whether the covenant would extend to his offspring, and the answer was that it would not be given to wrongdoers. This means that no one inherits the honour of guidance simply by being the child of a great prophet. Each person’s relationship with Allah is based on his own deeds. This is a powerful correction to the idea that family lineage guarantees salvation. Even the descendants of Abraham had to earn their standing through obedience, and wrongdoers among them were excluded from the covenant. The believer is therefore encouraged to build his own relationship with Allah through righteousness rather than relying on the merits of his ancestors.",
  "125": "“And ˹remember˺ when We made the Sacred House a centre and a sanctuary for the people.”\n\nThis verse speaks of the Sacred House, the Kaaba in Mecca. \"And ˹remember˺ when We made the Sacred House a centre and a sanctuary for the people.\" The Sacred House was made a \"centre,\" a gathering place for people, and a \"sanctuary,\" a place of safety where people could come for worship and protection. This shows the special status of the Kaaba as the focal point of worship and as a place of peace and security.\n\nThe verse then mentions the standing-place of Abraham: \"˹You may˺ take the standing-place of Abraham as a site of prayer.\" The standing-place of Abraham refers to the place where Abraham stood when he built the House, and the believers are told to use it as a site of prayer. This connects the Kaaba directly to Abraham and to the worship of Allah. The verse continues, \"And We entrusted Abraham and Ishmael to purify My House for those who circle it, who meditate in it, and who bow and prostrate themselves ˹in prayer˺.\" Abraham and Ishmael were entrusted to purify the House, meaning to keep it clean and dedicated to the worship of Allah alone, for those who perform the circumambulation, who meditate, and who pray.\n\nThis verse teaches the central place of the Kaaba in worship and its connection to the prophets Abraham and Ishmael. The Sacred House was established as a place of gathering, safety, and pure worship of Allah alone. The purification of the House was for the sake of those who worship there. For the reader, the verse encourages understanding the importance of the Sacred Mosque and the unity of worship that it represents. It also shows that the worship of Allah alone, without any partners, is the purpose for which the Sacred House was purified and established.",
  "126": "“And ˹remember˺ when Abraham said, ‘My Lord, make this city ˹of Mecca˺ secure.’”\n\nThis verse records a prayer of the prophet Abraham. \"And ˹remember˺ when Abraham said, 'My Lord, make this city ˹of Mecca˺ secure and provide fruits to its people.'\" Abraham prayed that the city of Mecca, where he had settled his family and where the Sacred House stood, would be made secure and that its people would be provided with fruits and sustenance. This prayer shows Abraham’s concern for the well-being of the city and its people, and it reflects the trust that true believers place in Allah for both security and provision.\n\nAbraham added a condition to his prayer: \"those among them who believe in Allah and the Last Day.\" He asked for these blessings for those who believe in Allah and the Last Day. Allah responded, \"As for those who disbelieve, I will let them enjoy themselves for a little while, then I will condemn them to the torment of the Fire. What an evil destination!\" The disbelievers would enjoy the worldly blessings for a short time, but their end would be the torment of the Fire, which is an evil destination.\n\nThis verse teaches that the blessings of this world, such as security and provision, are given to people regardless of their faith, but they are only a temporary enjoyment. The lasting outcome depends on one’s belief and deeds. The prayer of Abraham shows the heart of a prophet who cared for his community and asked for their well-being. For the reader, the verse encourages praying for security and provision and understanding that worldly blessings are temporary, while the true and lasting reward is with Allah for those who believe and do right. The prayer of Abraham also reveals an important distinction between the blessings of this world and the reward of the Hereafter. In this world, Allah provides for both believers and disbelievers, giving them security, food, and enjoyment for a time. This is part of Allah’s justice and mercy, as people are given a chance in this life. But the final outcome is not based on worldly enjoyment; it is based on faith and deeds. The disbelievers who enjoy this world for a while will face the Fire in the Hereafter. This teaches the reader not to be deceived by the temporary blessings of the world and to focus on what brings lasting success with Allah.",
  "127": "“And ˹remember˺ when Abraham raised the foundation of the House with Ishmael.”\n\nThis verse describes Abraham and Ishmael building the Sacred House. \"And ˹remember˺ when Abraham raised the foundation of the House with Ishmael.\" Abraham and his son Ishmael worked together to raise the foundation of the Kaaba, rebuilding it for the worship of Allah. This was an act of devotion carried out by father and son together. As they built, they prayed, and their prayer is recorded in the verse: \"Our Lord! Accept ˹this˺ from us. You are indeed the All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\"\n\nTheir prayer shows the humility and sincerity of the prophets. Even while performing a great act of worship, building the very House of Allah, they asked Allah to accept it from them. They did not assume that their deeds would automatically be accepted; they prayed for acceptance, acknowledging that acceptance is in Allah’s hand. They called upon Allah as the All-Hearing, who hears every prayer, and the All-Knowing, who knows the sincerity of every deed.\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of sincerity and the prayer for acceptance in worship. The prophets themselves, the best of creation, asked Allah to accept their deeds. This shows that a believer should never be proud of his deeds but should always pray for their acceptance. The verse also shows the value of working together in devotion, as Abraham and Ishmael did, and the deep faith that accompanies acts of worship. For the reader, the verse encourages performing deeds sincerely and humbly asking Allah to accept them, while recognizing that Allah is All-Hearing and All-Knowing, aware of every effort and every intention. This verse is also a reminder of the honour of those who built the Sacred House. Abraham and Ishmael were prophets, yet they did not build the House proudly or arrogantly; they built it with prayer and humility, asking Allah to accept their work. This is the model of true worship, in which even the greatest deeds are accompanied by humility and a sense of dependence on Allah. The reader is encouraged to follow this model in all acts of worship, doing good sincerely and praying for its acceptance, while never feeling that one’s deeds are so great that they need no divine acceptance.",
  "128": "“Our Lord! Make us both ˹fully˺ submit to You.”\n\nThis verse continues the prayer of Abraham and Ishmael as they built the Sacred House. \"Our Lord! Make us both ˹fully˺ submit to You and from our descendants a nation that will submit to you.\" They asked Allah to make them fully submit, meaning to make them true Muslims who surrender completely to Allah. They also asked that from their descendants a nation be raised that would submit to Allah. Their concern extended beyond themselves to their children and descendants, hoping that true submission to Allah would continue through the generations.\n\nThey continued, \"Show us our rituals, and turn to us in grace.\" They asked Allah to teach them the rituals and rites of worship, so that they could perform them correctly, and to turn to them in grace and mercy. They wanted to worship Allah in the way He had prescribed. Their prayer ends, \"You are truly the Accepter of Repentance, Most Merciful.\" They called upon Allah by His names, the Accepter of Repentance and the Most Merciful.\n\nThis verse teaches the desire of the prophets for true worship and for the guidance of their descendants. Abraham and Ishmael did not only pray for themselves; they prayed for a nation of people who would submit to Allah. This shows a selfless concern for the guidance of future generations. The verse also teaches the importance of asking Allah to teach us how to worship Him properly and to turn to us in mercy. For the reader, the verse encourages sincere submission to Allah, concern for the guidance of one’s family and community, and the prayer for Allah’s mercy and guidance in performing true worship. The prayer also shows the connection between worship and the desire to teach others. Abraham and Ishmael did not only want to submit themselves; they wanted a nation of people who would submit to Allah. Their concern for guidance extended to future generations. This teaches the believer to care about the faith of others, especially one’s own family and community, and to pray for their guidance. The prayer also includes a request to be shown the rituals of worship, showing that worshipping Allah properly is a blessing to be sought, not something to be taken for granted.",
  "129": "“Our Lord! Raise from among them a messenger.”\n\nThis verse is the final part of the prayer of Abraham and Ishmael. \"Our Lord! Raise from among them a messenger who will recite to them Your revelations, teach them the Book and wisdom, and purify them.\" Abraham and Ishmael prayed that a messenger be raised from among their descendants, one who would recite the revelations of Allah, teach the Book and wisdom, and purify the people. This prayer was answered by Allah when He sent the Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him, who was from the descendants of Abraham through Ishmael.\n\nThe verse describes the mission of this messenger in detail. He would \"recite to them Your revelations,\" meaning he would convey the Quran. He would \"teach them the Book and wisdom,\" meaning he would teach them the scripture and the understanding of it. And he would \"purify them,\" meaning he would cleanse their hearts and souls from false belief and bad character. These are the tasks of the final messenger, and they were prayed for long before his coming.\n\nThe verse ends, \"Indeed, You ˹alone˺ are the Almighty, All-Wise.\" Abraham and Ishmael called upon Allah as the Almighty and All-Wise, recognizing His power and wisdom in fulfilling their prayer. This verse teaches the connection between the prophets, showing that the prayer of Abraham was fulfilled in the coming of the Prophet Muhammad. It also shows the purpose of sending a messenger: to convey revelation, to teach, and to purify. For the reader, the verse encourages appreciating the Prophet and the guidance he brought, and it shows that true prophethood is a mercy from Allah, fulfilling the prayers of the earlier prophets. This verse is a powerful reminder that the sending of the Prophet Muhammad was the fulfilment of a prayer made long ago. The Prophet himself is described in the Quran as the answer to the prayer of Abraham. This shows the continuity of the message of monotheism from Abraham to the final messenger. It also shows the purpose of prophethood, which is to recite Allah’s revelations, to teach the Book and wisdom, and to purify the people. For the reader, this verse deepens appreciation of the Prophet and the revelation he brought, and it connects the Quran and the final messenger to the prayers and hopes of the earlier prophets.",
  "130": "“And who would reject the faith of Abraham except a fool!”\n\nThis verse defends the faith of Abraham. \"And who would reject the faith of Abraham except a fool!\" Abraham’s faith was pure monotheism, the worship of Allah alone. Only a fool would reject such a faith, because it is the truth that all the prophets taught. The word \"fool\" here points to someone who lacks understanding and is deprived of sound reason. To turn away from the faith of Abraham, the faith of pure monotheism, is the mark of a fool.\n\nThe verse then states, \"We certainly chose him in this life, and in the Hereafter he will surely be among the righteous.\" Allah chose Abraham in this life, honouring him with prophethood and guidance, and in the Hereafter he will be among the righteous. Abraham was chosen by Allah and will be among the people of righteousness in the Hereafter. This shows the high rank of Abraham and the honour Allah gave him.\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of the faith of Abraham, which is the same faith of pure monotheism that the Quran calls people to. To reject it is foolishness, because it is the true religion of all the prophets. The verse also shows the special honour of Abraham, who was chosen by Allah and will be among the righteous in the Hereafter. For the reader, the verse encourages embracing the pure monotheistic faith of Abraham and following his example of submission to Allah. It also warns against rejecting this faith out of foolishness or arrogance, reminding that true success lies in the faith that Abraham followed. The honour of Abraham is a reminder of what true greatness consists of. Abraham was not honoured for wealth or worldly power but for his faith and obedience. He submitted fully to Allah and was chosen by Him. This teaches the reader that the highest rank in the sight of Allah is the rank of faith and righteousness, not the rank of worldly position. Rejecting the faith of Abraham is therefore a rejection of the very path that leads to honour and righteousness. The verse encourages the reader to follow the way of Abraham, the way of pure monotheism and submission, and to recognise it as the path of true wisdom.",
  "131": "“When his Lord ordered him, ‘Submit ˹to My Will˺.’”\n\nThis verse records the command given to Abraham and his response. \"When his Lord ordered him, 'Submit ˹to My Will˺.'\" Allah commanded Abraham to submit to His will, to surrender completely to Allah. This is the call of all the prophets and the essence of Islam, which means submission to Allah. The command to submit was a test of Abraham’s faith, and he responded with complete obedience.\n\nAbraham’s response is recorded in a few beautiful words: \"he responded, 'I submit to the Lord of all worlds.'\" He did not hesitate or argue; he declared his submission to the Lord of all worlds. By saying \"the Lord of all worlds,\" Abraham recognized the greatness and universality of Allah, who is the Lord of everything in existence. His submission was not partial or reluctant; it was complete and wholehearted.\n\nThis verse teaches the meaning of true faith: complete submission to Allah. Abraham was not called to follow a complicated doctrine but to submit to his Lord, and he did so immediately. The word \"Islam\" itself comes from this idea of submission, and Abraham is described in the Quran as a Muslim, one who submits to Allah. For the reader, the verse encourages the same attitude of submission that Abraham showed, surrendering one’s will to Allah wholeheartedly. It also teaches that the Lord being worshipped is the Lord of all worlds, and that true submission is directed to Him alone. The simplicity of this verse is part of its power. The command was short, and the response was short. Abraham was not asked to understand everything before submitting; he was asked to submit, and he did. This is a model of faith. True submission means surrendering one’s will to Allah, trusting Him even before understanding every detail of His plan. The reader is encouraged to develop this same readiness to submit to Allah’s commands, responding to guidance with obedience and trust rather than hesitation. Abraham’s example shows that submission is both the beginning and the core of faith.",
  "132": "“This was the advice of Abraham—as well as Jacob—to his children.”\n\nThis verse shows that the message of submission to Allah was passed down through the generations of the prophets. \"This was the advice of Abraham—as well as Jacob—to his children.\" Abraham advised his children to submit to Allah, and Jacob did the same for his children. The prophets passed on this message to their descendants, making it the inheritance of faith. The verse then records the advice: \"Indeed, Allah has chosen for you this faith; so do not die except in ˹a state of full˺ submission.\"\n\nThe advice contains two parts. First, \"Allah has chosen for you this faith.\" The faith of submission to Allah is a great blessing, chosen by Allah for His prophets and their descendants. Second, \"so do not die except in ˹a state of full˺ submission.\" They were urged to remain in a state of submission to Allah until the very end of their lives. A person should live in submission to Allah and die in submission to Allah, never leaving this faith.\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of the faith of submission being passed down from one generation to the next. The prophets did not keep their faith to themselves; they advised their children and urged them to remain on it. The advice also teaches the value of perseverance in faith, staying committed to submission to Allah until death. For the reader, the verse encourages holding firmly to the faith of Islam and advising one’s children and family to do the same. It also teaches that a person’s life should be lived and ended in a state of submission to Allah, the faith that Allah chose for His servants. The advice to \"not die except in a state of full submission\" is especially profound. A person does not always control the moment of death, but a person can control the state in which he lives, remaining faithful to Allah so that death finds him in submission. This requires steadfastness throughout life, not only at the end. The advice of the prophets to their children therefore points to the importance of perseverance and constancy in faith. The reader is encouraged to hold firmly to submission to Allah through all the stages of life, so that one’s final moment is a moment of faith and obedience.",
  "133": "“Or did you witness when death came to Jacob?”\n\nThis verse asks a question about the time when Jacob was near death. \"Or did you witness when death came to Jacob?\" The question is directed to the Jews who made claims about their forefathers. They did not witness the scene, so they should not attribute beliefs to Jacob that he did not hold. The verse then describes the scene: \"He asked his children, 'Who will you worship after my passing?'\" As death approached, Jacob asked his children the most important question of all: whom would they worship after he was gone. This shows his deep concern for the faith of his children.\n\nTheir answer is recorded: \"They replied, 'We will ˹continue to˺ worship your God, the God of your forefathers— Abraham, Ishmael, and Isaac—the One God. And to Him we ˹all˺ submit.\"\" The children of Jacob promised to worship the One God, the God of their forefathers Abraham, Ishmael, and Isaac, and to submit to Him. They affirmed their commitment to pure monotheism, the worship of the One God, and their submission to Him.\n\nThis verse is a powerful testimony that the prophets and their children were all followers of the One God, worshipping Allah alone. Jacob did not worship multiple gods, and he did not teach his children to do so. The question of whom to worship was at the heart of his final advice. For the reader, the verse teaches the priority of monotheism, the worship of Allah alone, which was the religion of all the prophets and their families. It also encourages passing on this faith to one’s children and confirming it before the end of one’s life, as Jacob did, and it corrects any false claim that the prophets were not followers of the One God.",
  "134": "“That was a community that had already gone before.”\n\nThis verse corrects a misunderstanding about the earlier prophets and their communities. \"That was a community that had already gone before.\" The prophets Abraham, Jacob, and their descendants, and the communities that lived in their time, have already passed away. They are gone, and their time has ended. The verse then states the principle of personal responsibility: \"For them is what they earned and for you is what you have earned.\" Each person is accountable for his own deeds. The earlier community is responsible for what it did, and the present people are responsible for what they do. No one can take another’s deeds or benefit from them unfairly.\n\nThe verse ends with the emphatic statement, \"And you will not be accountable for what they have done.\" The present generation will not be asked about the deeds of the earlier community. This directly addresses the Jews who might have thought that being descendants of Abraham and Jacob automatically made them successful or exempt. The verse teaches that no one inherits righteousness or is saved by the merits of his ancestors. Each person stands before Allah on the basis of his own faith and deeds.\n\nThis verse is a fundamental teaching of the Quran: every soul is responsible for itself. This principle gives life meaning, because it means that each person’s efforts matter and that no one can be saved simply by belonging to a certain family or community. It also brings justice, because everyone is judged by what he himself has earned. For the reader, the verse encourages building one’s own faith and deeds rather than relying on the merits of ancestors or community, and it reassures that Allah’s judgement is perfectly fair, treating each person according to his own record.",
  "135": "“The Jews and Christians each say, ‘Follow our faith to be ˹rightly˺ guided.’”\n\nThis verse records the competing claims of the Jews and Christians. \"The Jews and Christians each say, 'Follow our faith to be ˹rightly˺ guided.'\" Each community called upon people to follow its own faith in order to be guided. The Jews invited people to their faith, and the Christians invited people to theirs. These claims contradicted each other, since both could not be the only path to guidance. Each community believed it alone had the truth.\n\nThe verse then tells the Prophet how to respond: \"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ 'No! We follow the faith of Abraham, the upright—who was not a polytheist.'\" The response is a clear rejection of following either community’s call out of mere loyalty. Instead, the Muslims follow the faith of Abraham, described as \"the upright,\" the one who was devoted to Allah alone and \"was not a polytheist.\" Abraham’s faith was pure monotheism, the worship of Allah alone, without any partner. This was the faith that both the Jews and Christians claimed to follow, yet they had added things and divided into factions. The Muslims return to the original, pure faith of Abraham.\n\nThis verse teaches that true guidance is found in following the pure monotheistic faith of Abraham, not in following the rival claims of divided communities. The mention of Abraham being \"not a polytheist\" is important, because it defines his faith: the worship of Allah alone. The reader is encouraged to follow this pure faith, free from polytheism and from the divisions of sectarian rivalry. The verse also shows the unity of the message of the prophets, which is summed up in the pure monotheism of Abraham, the upright one. This verse also teaches that truth is not determined by the size or age of a community. Both the Jews and the Christians were numerous and established, yet the Quran calls the believers to follow the faith of Abraham, the upright one, rather than the claims of either community. This shows that what matters is truth and purity of faith, not numbers or tradition. The reader is encouraged to follow what is true and pure, as the faith of Abraham was, and not to be swayed by the claims of communities that have departed from that original purity.",
  "136": "“Say, ˹O believers,˺ ‘We believe in Allah and what has been revealed to us.’”\n\nThis verse states the belief of the believers in clear terms. \"Say, ˹O believers,˺ 'We believe in Allah and what has been revealed to us; and what was revealed to Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob, and his descendants; and what was given to Moses, Jesus, and other prophets from their Lord.\"\" The believers declare their faith in Allah and in all the revelations He sent. This includes the Quran revealed to them, and it also includes the revelations given to the earlier prophets, from Abraham and his descendants to Moses, Jesus, and all the other prophets. The believers accept all of them.\n\nThe verse then states, \"We make no distinction between any of them.\" The believers do not accept some prophets and reject others; they believe in all of them as true messengers of Allah. This is a key difference between the Muslims and the communities that rejected some of the prophets. The Muslims honour all the prophets and believe in all the divine books. The verse ends, \"And to Allah we all submit.\" The believers submit fully to Allah, and this submission is the essence of their faith.\n\nThis verse teaches the unity of the prophetic message and the duty to believe in all the prophets. True faith is not selective, accepting some messengers and rejecting others, but complete belief in all the messengers of Allah. The verse also shows the connection between Islam and the earlier prophets, presenting Islam as the fulfilment and confirmation of their message. For the reader, the verse encourages a comprehensive faith that honours all the prophets and all the divine books, and that centres on submission to Allah alone. It also corrects the idea that Muslims reject the earlier prophets, making clear that they believe in all of them.",
  "137": "“So if they believe in what you believe, then they will indeed be ˹rightly˺ guided.”\n\nThis verse responds to the People of the Book. \"So if they believe in what you believe, then they will indeed be ˹rightly˺ guided.\" If the People of the Book were to believe in the same truth that the believers hold, belief in Allah and in all the prophets without distinction, then they would be rightly guided. Their guidance would come from accepting this comprehensive faith. The verse then states, \"But if they turn away, they are simply opposed ˹to the truth˺.\" If they refuse and turn away, they are merely opposing the truth, and their opposition does not harm the truth itself.\n\nThe verse then gives the believers a reassurance: \"But Allah will spare you their evil.\" If the People of the Book turn away and oppose the believers, Allah will protect the believers from their evil. Their opposition will not succeed against the believers, because Allah guards His believing servants. The verse ends, \"For He is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\" Allah hears all words and knows all deeds, including the opposition and schemes of the enemies of truth.\n\nThis verse teaches that true guidance comes through accepting the truth in its fullness, while turning away from it is opposition that harms only the one who opposes. The believers are also taught to trust in Allah for protection. When people oppose the truth, the believers should not fear, because Allah will spare them the evil of their enemies. For the reader, the verse encourages complete acceptance of the truth and trust in Allah’s protection. It also teaches that the outcome of the struggle between truth and falsehood is in Allah’s hands, and that the believers are to remain firm while leaving the result to Allah.",
  "138": "“This is the ˹natural˺ Way of Allah.”\n\nThis verse describes the faith of Islam as the natural way. \"This is the ˹natural˺ Way of Allah.\" The faith of submission to Allah is the natural way that Allah has created in human beings, the way that fits human nature. The word used here points to the natural colouring or way that Allah has set for His servants. True faith is not something alien to human beings; it is the way that matches the nature with which Allah created people.\n\nThe verse then asks, \"And who is better than Allah in ordaining a way?\" No one can ordain a better way than Allah. The way of Allah, the natural way of monotheism and submission, is the best way there is. No human system or way can compare to the way that Allah has ordained. This establishes the superiority of Allah’s guidance over all other ways.\n\nThe verse ends, \"And we worship ˹none but˺ Him.\" The conclusion of following this natural way is the worship of Allah alone. The believers worship none but Allah, directing their devotion and obedience to Him alone. This is the natural way and the pure worship that Allah deserves.\n\nThis verse teaches that Islam is the natural religion, the way that fits human nature as Allah created it. This is why people who think honestly are drawn to it, because it matches their deepest nature. The verse also teaches the superiority of Allah’s way and the oneness of worship. For the reader, the verse encourages embracing the natural way of Islam, recognising that it is the way Allah ordained for His servants, and directing worship to Allah alone. It also gives confidence that the way of Allah is the best and most natural way for human beings. The idea of the natural way has a deep connection to human nature. Allah created human beings with an inborn inclination to recognize and worship their Creator, and the faith of submission to Allah is the way that fulfils this natural inclination. When a person turns to Islam sincerely, he is not doing something foreign to himself; he is returning to the nature on which Allah created him. This is why the Quran calls the way of Allah the natural way. It also explains why true faith brings peace: it is in harmony with what the human soul was made for.",
  "139": "“Say, ‘Would you dispute with us about Allah?’”\n\nThis verse addresses those who argue with the believers about religion. \"Say, 'Would you dispute with us about Allah, while He is our Lord and your Lord?\"\" The question exposes the futility of their dispute. Both the believers and the people of the Book share the same Lord, Allah. If He is the Lord of both, then there is no reason to dispute about Him or about the fundamentals of faith that all the prophets taught. Their dispute is based not on truth but on stubbornness and sectarian rivalry.\n\nThe verse then states the principle of personal accountability: \"We are accountable for our deeds and you for yours.\" Each group is responsible for its own deeds. The believers will be held accountable for what they do, and the others for what they do. No one can be held accountable for another’s deeds. This is the same principle of personal responsibility that the chapter has stressed before.\n\nThe verse ends, \"And we are devoted to Him ˹alone˺.\" The believers are devoted to Allah alone, directing their worship and obedience exclusively to Him. This devotion is the heart of their faith. The verse therefore responds to dispute by pointing to the common Lord, the principle of personal responsibility, and the pure devotion of the believers. For the reader, the verse teaches that arguments about religion should be based on truth, not on rivalry, and that the believers’ position is devotion to Allah alone. It also encourages humility, recognising that each person is accountable for his own deeds before the one Lord. This verse also teaches a valuable lesson about how to respond to religious argument. Instead of entering into endless disputes that only increase division, the believers point to what unites and to the principle of personal responsibility. They affirm that their devotion is to Allah alone. This approach is both dignified and truthful. It reminds the reader that the most important thing is not winning an argument but being true to Allah and to one’s own faith, while leaving the judgement of every person to Allah, who knows all hearts.",
  "140": "“Or do you claim that Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob, and his descendants were all Jews or Christians?”\n\nThis verse challenges a false claim about the prophets. \"Or do you claim that Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob, and his descendants were all Jews or Christians?\" The Jews and Christians were making claims about these prophets, as if the prophets had followed their respective religions. But Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, and Jacob lived before the coming of the religions called Judaism and Christianity in their later forms, and they were followers of pure monotheism, not of the later Jewish and Christian communities. The claim that they were Jews or Christians is historically false.\n\nThe verse then asks a pointed question: \"Say, 'Who is more knowledgeable: you or Allah?'\" This question exposes the arrogance of the claim. Allah is more knowledgeable than anyone, and Allah has revealed the truth about these prophets in the Quran. Their claim contradicts what Allah has revealed. The verse then asks, \"Who does more wrong than those who hide the testimony they received from Allah?\" Hiding the testimony of Allah, the truth they knew, is the worst kind of wrongdoing.\n\nThe verse ends, \"And Allah is never unaware of what you do.\" Allah knows all their deeds, including their false claims and their hiding of the truth. For the reader, the verse corrects the false idea that the prophets were followers of the later Jewish or Christian religions. They were followers of pure monotheism, the worship of Allah alone. The verse also warns against hiding the truth and making false claims about religion, and it teaches that Allah’s knowledge is complete and nothing escapes Him. The question \"Who is more knowledgeable: you or Allah?\" is a powerful way of settling a dispute. It reminds people that their opinions cannot overrule what Allah has revealed. If Allah has revealed that Abraham and the other prophets were followers of pure monotheism, then no human claim to the contrary can change that truth. The verse teaches humility before the knowledge of Allah, and it warns against the arrogance of asserting one’s own view against clear divine revelation. True believers accept the knowledge of Allah over their own assumptions and traditions.",
  "141": "“That was a community that had already gone before.”\n\nThis verse repeats the principle stated earlier in the chapter, reinforcing its importance. \"That was a community that had already gone before.\" The prophets and their communities have passed away. The verse then restates the principle of personal responsibility: \"For them is what they earned and for you is what you have earned. And you will not be accountable for what they have done.\" Each community is responsible for its own deeds, and no one is accountable for the deeds of another.\n\nThe repetition of this verse at this point in the chapter is deliberate. It comes after the discussion of the claims of the Jews and Christians about the prophets. The verse is a reminder that the prophets were not Jews or Christians in the later sense, and that the present communities cannot claim the prophets as members of their own religion. Each generation is responsible for its own beliefs and deeds, not for the deeds of the earlier communities.\n\nThis verse teaches the same lesson as before: personal responsibility and the rejection of false claims based on ancestry. The repetition drives the point home, because it is a message the reader needs to hear clearly. No one can claim the merits of the prophets for himself simply by asserting a connection. Each person is judged by his own faith and deeds. For the reader, the verse encourages building one’s own record of faith and righteousness rather than relying on the merits or identity of earlier communities, and it reminds that Allah’s judgement is just and personal. The repetition of this verse also serves a practical purpose in argument. When the Jews or Christians tried to claim the prophets for their own communities, the Quran responded by pointing out that those communities had passed away and that no one today can claim their deeds or inherit their status. This deflates the false pride that comes from appealing to great ancestors while doing little oneself. The reader is reminded that the prophets’ greatness belonged to them through their own faith and obedience, and that each person must earn his own standing with Allah.",
  "142": "“The foolish among the people will ask, ‘Why did they turn away from the direction of prayer they used to face?’”\n\nThis verse introduces the subject of the change of the direction of prayer, a significant event in early Islam. \"The foolish among the people will ask, 'Why did they turn away from the direction of prayer they used to face?'\" At first, the Muslims had prayed facing Jerusalem. Then Allah commanded them to face the Sacred Mosque in Mecca. This change caused some people, described as the foolish, to question it. They asked why the Muslims had turned away from their former direction of prayer. The question was a way of doubting and mocking the revelation.\n\nThe verse tells the Prophet how to respond: \"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ 'The east and west belong ˹only˺ to Allah. He guides whoever He wills to the Straight Path.'\" The response is that the east and the west, all directions, belong to Allah. The direction of prayer is not an absolute matter in itself; it is a command from Allah. Allah guides whoever He wills to the Straight Path, and the believers follow Allah’s command regardless of direction. The change of direction was a test of obedience and of trust in Allah’s guidance.\n\nThis verse teaches that the command of Allah is to be followed even when people question it. The change of the qiblah was not an arbitrary matter; it was a command from Allah, and the believers obeyed. The verse also teaches that Allah owns all directions and guides whom He wills. For the reader, the verse encourages obedience to Allah’s commands even when they are questioned by others, and it teaches not to be shaken by the doubts of the foolish. The next verses explain the wisdom behind the change of the direction of prayer.",
  "143": "“And so We have made you ˹believers˺ an upright community.”\n\nThis verse explains the wisdom behind the change of the direction of prayer. \"And so We have made you ˹believers˺ an upright community so that you may be witnesses over humanity and that the Messenger may be a witness over you.\" The Muslims were made an upright, balanced community, chosen to be witnesses over humanity, testifying to the truth and being examples for others. The Messenger is a witness over the believers, testifying to their deeds. This gives the community a special role and a great responsibility.\n\nThe verse then refers to the former direction of prayer: \"We assigned your former direction of prayer only to distinguish those who would remain faithful to the Messenger from those who would lose faith.\" The former qiblah, Jerusalem, was assigned as a test. When the command came to face the Sacred Mosque, it distinguished those who would remain faithful to the Messenger from those who would turn away. Those who obeyed the change showed their obedience to Allah and His Messenger, while those who doubted or refused showed their weak faith.\n\nThe verse then says, \"It was certainly a difficult test except for those ˹rightly˺ guided by Allah.\" The change was a difficult test, but it was easy for those whom Allah guided. Their hearts were firm, so they obeyed. The verse then gives a reassurance: \"And Allah would never discount your ˹previous acts of˺ faith. Surely Allah is Ever Gracious and Most Merciful to humanity.\" The prayers offered toward the former direction were not wasted; Allah would not discount the previous acts of faith. Allah is gracious and merciful.\n\nThis verse teaches the wisdom behind divine tests and the importance of obedience. The change of the qiblah was a test of loyalty to the Messenger, and it separated the sincere from the weak. It also teaches that Allah does not waste the good deeds of His servants. For the reader, the verse encourages firm obedience, an understanding of the role of the Muslim community as witnesses to humanity, and trust that Allah values every sincere act of faith.",
  "144": "“Indeed, We see you ˹O Prophet˺ turning your face towards heaven.”\n\nThis verse reveals the circumstances behind the change of the direction of prayer. \"Indeed, We see you ˹O Prophet˺ turning your face towards heaven.\" The Prophet, peace be upon him, wished for the direction of prayer to be changed to the Kaaba in Mecca, and he would turn his face toward heaven, hoping for a command from Allah. This verse shows that Allah saw his desire and responded to it. It also shows the closeness of the Prophet to Allah, who saw his longing.\n\nThe verse continues, \"Now We will make you turn towards a direction ˹of prayer˺ that will please you. So turn your face towards the Sacred Mosque ˹in Mecca˺—wherever you are, turn your faces towards it.\" Allah granted the Prophet’s wish and commanded that the Muslims face the Sacred Mosque in Mecca in prayer, wherever they are. This was the new direction of prayer, the qiblah, that would please the Prophet. The command is repeated for emphasis: wherever the believers are, they should turn their faces toward the Sacred Mosque.\n\nThe verse then states, \"Those who were given the Scripture certainly know this to be the truth from their Lord.\" The People of the Book knew that the change of the direction of prayer was the truth from Allah, because their scriptures indicated the Kaaba as the qiblah of Abraham. The verse ends, \"And Allah is never unaware of what they do.\" Allah knows the deeds of all people.\n\nThis verse teaches that Allah responds to the sincere wishes of His servants and guides them. It also establishes the direction of prayer toward the Sacred Mosque as a command for all believers. For the reader, the verse encourages prayer toward the qiblah, understanding that this was a divine command that pleased the Prophet, and it teaches trust in Allah’s guidance in matters of worship.",
  "145": "“Even if you were to bring every proof to the People of the Book.”\n\nThis verse acknowledges the stubbornness of the People of the Book regarding the direction of prayer. \"Even if you were to bring every proof to the People of the Book, they would not accept your direction ˹of prayer˺.\" No matter how much evidence the Prophet brought, they would not accept the new direction of prayer, the qiblah of the Muslims. Their rejection was not based on a lack of proof but on stubbornness and opposition. The verse then adds, \"nor would you accept theirs; nor would any of them accept the direction ˹of prayer˺ of another.\" Each community was attached to its own direction, and none would accept another’s. The Jews and Christians themselves did not agree on a single direction among themselves.\n\nThe verse then warns the Prophet: \"And if you were to follow their desires after ˹all˺ the knowledge that has come to you, then you would certainly be one of the wrongdoers.\" If the Prophet were to abandon the truth and follow the desires of the People of the Book after receiving clear knowledge from Allah, he would be among the wrongdoers. This is a strong warning against compromising the truth to please others.\n\nThis verse teaches that people will not always accept the truth, no matter how much proof is brought, because their rejection comes from stubbornness. The believers are called to remain firm on the truth that Allah has revealed and not to compromise it to please those who oppose it. For the reader, the verse encourages steadfastness in the truth, an understanding that no proof can convince the obstinate, and a warning against abandoning Allah’s guidance for the desires of others. This verse also teaches a practical truth about people: no amount of evidence will convince someone who is determined not to believe. The Prophet was told that even if he brought every proof, the People of the Book would not accept his direction of prayer. This spares the caller to the truth from despair when people refuse to accept clear proof. A person is not failing if stubborn people reject the truth; the truth has been delivered clearly, and their rejection is their own responsibility. The believer is called to be clear and sincere in conveying the truth, and to leave the outcome to Allah.",
  "146": "“Those We have given the Scripture recognize this ˹Prophet˺ as they recognize their own children.”\n\nThis verse testifies that the People of the Book recognized the Prophet. \"Those We have given the Scripture recognize this ˹Prophet˺ as they recognize their own children.\" The scholars of the People of the Book knew the Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him, and recognized him as the messenger foretold in their scriptures, just as clearly as a parent recognizes his own child. Their recognition was based on the descriptions of the final messenger in their books, which they knew well. This recognition was clear and certain.\n\nThe verse then exposes a tragedy: \"Yet a group of them hides the truth knowingly.\" Although they recognized the truth about the Prophet, a group of them hid it deliberately. They concealed the truth that they knew, out of envy, worldly interest, or pride. Their hiding of the truth was a knowing act; they were not ignorant but chose to conceal what they knew to be true.\n\nThis verse teaches that recognition of the truth is not the same as accepting it. These people recognized the Prophet as clearly as they recognized their own children, yet they hid the truth. This is a striking example of how envy and worldly interest can lead people to conceal the truth they know. For the reader, the verse encourages sincerity in accepting the truth once it is known, and it warns against hiding the truth for any worldly reason. It also confirms the truth of the Prophet’s message, which was recognized by those who knew the earlier scriptures. This verse is a strong confirmation of the truth of the Prophet’s mission. The fact that the scholars of the People of the Book recognized him as clearly as they recognized their own children shows that his coming was truly foretold and that the signs of his prophethood were clear in the scriptures. Their recognition is therefore a proof for the truth of Islam. Yet their concealment of that recognition shows how powerful envy and worldly interest can be. The reader is encouraged to accept the truth with sincerity and never to conceal what he knows to be true for the sake of worldly gain.",
  "147": "“˹This is˺ the truth from your Lord.”\n\nThis short verse makes a profound declaration. \"˹This is˺ the truth from your Lord.\" The Quran, and the guidance it contains, is the truth from Allah. This includes the commands about the direction of prayer and all the teachings of the Quran. The verse affirms the source of the guidance: it comes from Allah, the Lord of the Prophet and of all creation. Because it is from Allah, it is true and cannot be false.\n\nThe verse then gives a command: \"so do not ever be one of those who doubt.\" The Prophet is told not to be among those who doubt, because the truth from his Lord is clear and certain. This command also extends to the believers, who are to have firm certainty in the truth of Allah’s guidance. Doubt has no place when the truth is clear and comes from Allah.\n\nThis verse teaches the certainty of the Quran and its guidance. It is the truth from Allah, and there is no doubt about it. The command not to be among those who doubt encourages firm faith and certainty in the message of the Quran. For the reader, the verse strengthens confidence in the truth of the Quran and its guidance, and it warns against doubt. It teaches that when the truth is clear, it should be accepted with certainty, not questioned or doubted. The verse is short, but it carries a deep and firm message about the certainty of divine truth. This verse also carries a lesson about the danger of doubt. Doubt can creep into a person’s heart when the truth seems difficult or when others oppose it. The Quran commands firm certainty in what has come from Allah, because the truth from Allah is clear and needs no wavering. A person who is certain of the truth is steadfast and not shaken by opposition. The verse therefore encourages building one’s faith on certainty, confirming in the heart that the guidance of the Quran is the truth from Allah, so that no doubt can find a place.",
  "148": "“Everyone turns to their own direction ˹of prayer˺.”\n\nThis verse acknowledges that different communities face different directions in prayer. \"Everyone turns to their own direction ˹of prayer˺.\" Each community has its own qiblah, its own direction that it faces in worship. This was true of the Jews, the Christians, and the Muslims, each with their own direction. The differences in direction were not to be a cause of endless conflict.\n\nThe verse then gives a positive command: \"So compete with one another in doing good.\" Instead of disputing about directions and divisions, the people should compete in doing good. The competition that matters is not about whose direction is correct in a boastful way, but about who does more good deeds. The focus is shifted from rivalry to righteousness.\n\nThe verse continues, \"Wherever you are, Allah will bring you all together ˹for judgment˺.\" Allah will gather all people together for judgement on the Day of Judgment, wherever they are. The differences and directions of this world will be settled by Allah. The verse ends, \"Surely Allah is Most Capable of everything.\" Allah is able to gather everyone and to judge between them.\n\nThis verse teaches that while communities differ in directions and practices, the true competition is in doing good. Differences should not lead to enmity but to a focus on righteousness. For the reader, the verse encourages competing in good deeds rather than in disputes, and it reminds that Allah will gather everyone for judgement. The direction of prayer is a matter of obedience to Allah’s command, but the essential concern is doing good. The verse also teaches trust in Allah’s power to bring all people together and to judge justly. This verse also teaches that diversity of practice does not have to lead to division. Different communities faced different directions, but the believers were called to focus on what truly matters: competing in good. This is a profound lesson for unity. Instead of letting differences become a cause of conflict, people should channel their energies into doing good, which is what Allah will judge. The gathering of all people before Allah for judgement puts every difference in perspective, reminding the believer that the final settlement belongs to Allah and that good deeds are the true competition.",
  "149": "“Wherever you are ˹O Prophet˺, turn your face towards the Sacred Mosque.”\n\nThis verse repeats the command about the direction of prayer. \"Wherever you are ˹O Prophet˺, turn your face towards the Sacred Mosque.\" The Prophet is commanded to face the Sacred Mosque in prayer, wherever he may be. The repetition of this command emphasizes its importance and universality. The direction of prayer toward the Kaaba is not limited to one place; it applies wherever the believer is.\n\nThe verse then states, \"This is certainly the truth from your Lord.\" The command to face the Sacred Mosque is the truth from Allah. It is not an arbitrary human choice but a divine command. This reinforces the certainty of the guidance. The verse ends, \"And Allah is never unaware of what you ˹all˺ do.\" Allah knows all the deeds of people, including their prayers and their obedience.\n\nThis verse teaches the universal application of facing the Sacred Mosque in prayer. Wherever a believer is in the world, he turns toward the Kaaba in his prayers, uniting the Muslims of the world in a single direction of worship. This is a sign of unity among the believers. For the reader, the verse reinforces the command of the qiblah and its certainty as the truth from Allah. It also reminds the believer that Allah is aware of all deeds, which encourages sincerity in worship and in all actions. The repetition of the command also reflects the importance of the qiblah in unifying the Muslims. No matter how far apart the believers are, they all face the same direction in prayer, the Sacred Mosque, and this unites them in worship despite their distance. It is a visible sign of their unity in faith and their devotion to Allah. The verse also reminds the believer that Allah is aware of every act of worship, which encourages sincerity. Facing the qiblah is therefore both an act of obedience and a symbol of the unity of the Muslim community.",
  "150": "“Wherever you are ˹O Prophet˺, turn your face towards the Sacred Mosque.”\n\nThis verse repeats the command to face the Sacred Mosque, and it extends it to the believers. \"Wherever you are ˹O Prophet˺, turn your face towards the Sacred Mosque. And wherever you ˹believers˺ are, face towards it.\" The command is addressed to the Prophet and to all the believers. The repetition stresses the importance of the direction of prayer and its universal application. All believers, wherever they are, are to face the Kaaba in prayer.\n\nThe verse then gives a purpose for this command: \"so that people will have no argument against you, except the wrongdoers among them.\" By obeying the command of the qiblah, the believers would remove the arguments of those who might criticize them, except for the wrongdoers who would argue regardless. The change to the Sacred Mosque, the qiblah of Abraham, would align the Muslims with the original faith of Abraham and remove a basis for objection. Only the wrongdoers would continue to object.\n\nThe verse then says, \"Do not fear them; fear Me.\" The believers are told not to fear the people who object but to fear Allah alone. Their obedience is to Allah, not to the approval of people. The verse continues, \"so that I may ˹continue to˺ perfect My favour upon you and so you may be ˹rightly˺ guided.\" By obeying, the believers would receive the completion of Allah’s favour and guidance.\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of obeying Allah’s command and not fearing the objections of people. The believers are called to fear Allah alone and to follow His guidance. For the reader, the verse reinforces the command of the qiblah and teaches courage in obedience, trusting that obeying Allah brings His favour and guidance even when people object. The verse’s call to fear Allah rather than people is a foundation of true courage. When the believers obey Allah’s commands, they should not be turned back by the criticism of people, because their primary concern is the pleasure of Allah. Fearing Allah and seeking His favour gives a person the strength to do what is right even when others object. The completion of Allah’s favour and the attainment of guidance are the rewards of such obedience, and they are far greater than the approval of people who oppose the truth.",
  "151": "“Since We have sent you a messenger from among yourselves.”\n\nThis verse explains a great favour that Allah has done for the believers. \"Since We have sent you a messenger from among yourselves.\" Allah sent a messenger to them from among their own people, a human messenger who spoke their language and understood their lives. This was a great mercy, because a messenger from among them could be understood, related to, and followed easily. The verse then describes the mission of this messenger: \"reciting to you Our revelations, purifying you, teaching you the Book and wisdom, and teaching you what you never knew.\"\n\nThe messenger recites the revelations of Allah, purifies the people, cleanses their hearts and souls from false belief and bad character, teaches them the Book, the Quran, and wisdom, the understanding of it, and teaches them what they never knew before, the knowledge that comes from divine revelation. This description covers the full purpose of the prophetic mission: conveying revelation, purifying the soul, and teaching knowledge and wisdom.\n\nThis verse teaches the great favour of Allah in sending a messenger from among the people themselves. It also explains the role of the Prophet, which was not only to deliver a message but to purify and teach. For the reader, the verse encourages gratitude for the blessing of the Prophet and the guidance he brought. It also teaches that true knowledge comes through the revelation that the Prophet conveyed, which purified the people and taught them what they could not have known on their own. The verse prepares the way for the command that follows in the next verse. This verse also highlights the perfection of Allah’s favour in sending the messenger. The messenger not only conveyed the revelations but purified the people and taught them the Book and wisdom. This means that guidance is not left to human guesswork; it comes through revelation and teaching. The favour of Allah in sending a messenger from among the people themselves made it possible for them to receive, understand, and live by divine guidance. The reader is encouraged to appreciate the Prophet and the knowledge he brought, and to recognize that the Quran and the teachings of the Prophet are the means through which Allah guided humanity.",
  "152": "“remember Me; I will remember you.”\n\nThis verse is one of the most profound and beloved verses of the Quran. \"remember Me; I will remember you.\" Allah commands His servants to remember Him, and He promises to remember them in return. Remembering Allah includes mentioning Him with the tongue, reflecting on Him with the heart, and obeying Him with the limbs. When a person remembers Allah, Allah remembers him with mercy, reward, and honour. This mutual remembrance shows the closeness between Allah and His servants.\n\nThe verse continues, \"And thank Me, and never be ungrateful.\" The servant is commanded to thank Allah and to avoid ingratitude. Thankfulness to Allah is shown through using His blessings in obedience and acknowledging that all good comes from Him. Ingratitude, the opposite of thankfulness, is to deny or misuse the blessings of Allah. The verse therefore calls for two responses to Allah’s favour: remembrance and gratitude.\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of remembering and thanking Allah. Remembrance keeps the heart connected to Allah, and gratitude acknowledges His blessings. The promise that Allah will remember those who remember Him is a source of great hope and encouragement. For the reader, the verse encourages making the remembrance of Allah a constant habit, through prayer, supplication, and reflection, and cultivating gratitude in the heart. It also teaches that Allah’s remembrance of His servants is a mercy and honour beyond anything the servant deserves. This verse directly follows the description of the Prophet’s mission, showing that the purpose of guidance is to lead people to remember and thank Allah. The command to remember Allah is a command to keep Allah in the heart and on the tongue at all times. This is not limited to formal worship; it includes the constant awareness of Allah in every aspect of life. The promise that Allah will remember His servant is a sign of great honour, because it means that the servant is not forgotten by his Lord. This verse is a source of immense hope and comfort. The reader is encouraged to make remembrance and gratitude the habits of daily life, trusting that Allah, who remembers those who remember Him, will grant them mercy, honour, and reward.",
  "153": "“O believers! Seek comfort in patience and prayer.”\n\nThis verse gives the believers practical guidance for facing difficulties. \"O believers! Seek comfort in patience and prayer.\" When they face hardship, they are told to seek comfort and help through patience and prayer. This is the same guidance that appeared earlier in the chapter. Patience gives the believer inner strength to endure, and prayer connects him to Allah for help and comfort. Together, they provide the support a believer needs in times of trial.\n\nThe verse then gives a promise: \"Allah is truly with those who are patient.\" This is a powerful reassurance. Allah is with those who are patient, supporting them, helping them, and granting them strength. The presence of Allah with the patient is the greatest comfort there is. Knowing that Allah is with a person in his trials gives him courage and hope.\n\nThis verse teaches the believer how to face hardship. Rather than despairing or relying only on human effort, the believer turns to patience and prayer. Patience keeps him steady, and prayer keeps him connected to Allah. The promise that Allah is with the patient gives the trial meaning and hope. For the reader, the verse encourages seeking help through patience and prayer in all difficulties, and trusting that Allah is with those who remain patient. It is a source of strength and comfort for everyone who faces hardship while trying to remain faithful to Allah. This verse also teaches that the believer is not left alone in his trials. Allah is with those who are patient, and this companionship is the greatest source of strength. Patience is not a passive giving up; it is a firm endurance with trust in Allah. Prayer is the means by which the believer draws near to Allah and receives His help. The combination of patience and prayer is the way the believer navigates hardship, and the promise of Allah’s presence with the patient gives the trial meaning. The reader is encouraged to turn to these two tools whenever difficulties arise.",
  "154": "“Never say that those martyred in the cause of Allah are dead.”\n\nThis verse corrects a misunderstanding about those who die in the cause of Allah. \"Never say that those martyred in the cause of Allah are dead—in fact, they are alive!\" The martyrs are not to be considered dead, because they are truly alive. Their life after death is a life that is real, though different from life in this world. They are alive with Allah, honoured and blessed. The verse uses the strongest language to remove the idea that their death was a complete end.\n\nThe verse adds, \"But you do not perceive it.\" The believers cannot perceive the life of the martyrs, because it belongs to a reality beyond this world. The martyrs enjoy a life that is hidden from human sight. This statement encourages faith, because the believer accepts what Allah has revealed even when he cannot see it. The reality of the martyrs’ life is known to Allah and revealed to the believers through the Quran.\n\nScholars explain that the martyrs are alive in a special sense, receiving provision and honour with their Lord, as the Quran states in another chapter. This verse was revealed in connection with the battle of Badr, where some Muslims were killed, and it was revealed to comfort the believers and to honour the martyrs. For the reader, the verse teaches that those who die for the cause of Allah are not lost; they are alive and honoured. It also encourages sacrifice and courage in the cause of truth, knowing that such sacrifice is not wasted. The verse brings comfort to the families of martyrs and strengthens faith in the Hereafter. This verse also carries an important lesson about how death is understood in Islam. Death is not the end of a person’s existence but a transition to another life, and for those who die in the cause of Allah, that life is one of honour and nearness to Allah. The verse therefore changes how the believer views death in the path of Allah: it is not a loss to be mourned as if it were the final end, but a move to a higher life. This understanding gives courage and peace, because the believer knows that sacrificing one’s life in the way of Allah is not in vain and that the martyr is not truly dead but alive with his Lord.",
  "155": "“We will certainly test you with a touch of fear and famine.”\n\nThis verse tells the believers that they will be tested. \"We will certainly test you with a touch of fear and famine and loss of property, life, and crops.\" The tests include fear, hunger, and the loss of wealth, life, and harvests. These are among the most difficult trials that people can face. The verse states that such tests are certain, which means that believers should expect hardship as part of life. The trials are described as a \"touch,\" suggesting that Allah tests within a measure, not beyond what a person can bear.\n\nThe verse then gives good news: \"Give good news to those who patiently endure.\" The good news is for those who face these tests with patience, without despairing and without abandoning faith. They endure the trials while remaining obedient to Allah. The good news that follows is their reward and their success. This shows that trials are not meaningless; they are a means of testing faith and of raising those who endure.\n\nThis verse teaches that trials are part of the believer’s life and should be expected. Allah tests people to see who remains patient and faithful. The good news is reserved for the patient, who endure hardship with trust in Allah. For the reader, the verse prepares for difficulty and encourages patience. It also gives meaning to suffering, because it shows that those who endure with faith will receive good news and reward. The next verses describe who these patient people are and what they say when faced with disaster. The verse also teaches that trials are a universal part of the human experience, not something that only the wicked face. Even the believers are tested, and this testing is part of Allah’s wisdom. It separates the patient and sincere from the weak, and it raises the ranks of those who endure. The mention of property, life, and crops covers the main sources of human security, showing that no area of life is entirely safe from trial. This prepares the believer to face difficulties with faith rather than surprise, and to remember that every trial is a means of drawing closer to Allah and earning His reward.",
  "156": "“who, when faced with a disaster, say, ‘Surely to Allah we belong and to Him we will ˹all˺ return.’”\n\nThis verse describes the patient people who were promised good news in the previous verse. They are \"who, when faced with a disaster, say, 'Surely to Allah we belong and to Him we will ˹all˺ return.'\" When a disaster strikes them, whether the loss of property, life, or crops, they respond with this statement of faith. They recognize that everything belongs to Allah, and that they, and all that they have, came from Allah and will return to Him. This statement, known as the istirja, is a declaration of patience and trust.\n\nThe first part, \"Surely to Allah we belong,\" acknowledges that all things are Allah’s property. What is lost was never truly ours; it was on loan from Allah. The second part, \"and to Him we will ˹all˺ return,\" acknowledges that every person will return to Allah. This perspective softens the pain of loss, because the believer understands that nothing is lost forever and that the true return is to Allah. This statement turns a moment of grief into a moment of faith.\n\nThis verse teaches the believer how to respond to disaster. Instead of despairing or questioning Allah, the believer affirms that everything belongs to Allah and that the final return is to Him. This gives comfort and strength in the hardest moments. For the reader, the verse encourages adopting this statement of patience in times of loss. It also teaches a deep truth: because everything belongs to Allah and we return to Him, no loss in this world is final, and every trial is bearable when met with faith and trust in Allah. This response also reflects a deep truth about ownership. People often forget that what they have, whether wealth, health, or loved ones, is only given to them by Allah, and that He may take it back at any time. When the believer remembers that everything belongs to Allah, loss becomes easier to bear, because what is taken was never truly owned but only entrusted. The statement also looks toward the Hereafter, where the believer will return to Allah and be reunited with what was lost in a better form. This is why the words of patience bring such comfort: they place a painful moment within the larger and hopeful reality of Allah and the return to Him.",
  "157": "“They are the ones who will receive Allah’s blessings and mercy.”\n\nThis verse states the reward of the patient people described in the previous verses. \"They are the ones who will receive Allah's blessings and mercy.\" Those who respond to disaster with patience and with the words \"to Allah we belong and to Him we return\" will receive blessings and mercy from Allah. The word \"blessings\" points to peace and safety, and \"mercy\" points to Allah’s compassion and forgiveness. These are the gifts that Allah grants to the patient.\n\nThe verse then adds, \"And it is they who are ˹rightly˺ guided.\" The patient are also among the rightly guided, because their patience and faith have kept them on the right path. They are guided by Allah because they trust in Him in both ease and hardship. Their guidance is a reward in itself, because it means they walk in the way that pleases Allah.\n\nThis verse teaches that patience is not in vain; it brings great reward. Those who endure trials with faith receive Allah’s blessings, mercy, and guidance. This is the good news promised in the earlier verse. For the reader, the verse encourages patience in times of difficulty, because it is a means of receiving Allah’s blessings and mercy. It also shows that those who face hardship with faith are guided and blessed. The three verses together, the testing, the statement of patience, and the reward, form a complete teaching about how the believer should face difficulty and what awaits him for doing so faithfully. The reward of blessings and mercy is mentioned together because together they cover both peace and compassion. Blessings bring calm and safety to the heart, while mercy covers and forgives. This double reward shows how generous Allah is to the patient. The mention of guidance at the end shows that patience is not merely enduring suffering but walking on the right path through it. A person who is patient with faith is not lost in his hardship; he is guided by Allah through it. This gives the reader confidence that hardship, faced with patience, does not lead away from Allah but deeper into His blessings, mercy, and guidance.",
  "158": "“Indeed, ˹the hills of˺ Ṣafa and Marwah are among the symbols of Allah.”\n\nThis verse refers to two hills near the Sacred Mosque in Mecca, Safa and Marwah. \"Indeed, ˹the hills of˺ Ṣafa and Marwah are among the symbols of Allah.\" The two hills are called symbols of Allah, meaning signs that remind people of Allah and of the rites of pilgrimage. They are connected to the story of Hajar, the mother of Ismail, who walked between these hills seeking water for her son, and this walk became part of the pilgrimage rites.\n\nThe verse then explains the rite: \"So whoever performs the pilgrimage or minor pilgrimage, let them walk between ˹the two hills˺.\" Those who perform the Hajj, the major pilgrimage, or the Umrah, the minor pilgrimage, are commanded to walk between the two hills as part of the rites. The verse then says, \"And whoever does good willingly, Allah is truly Appreciative, All-Knowing.\" Whoever does good willingly and sincerely will be rewarded, because Allah appreciates good deeds and knows all that people do.\n\nScholars explain that this verse was revealed to remove any hesitation some believers had about performing this rite, because the two hills were connected to practices from the time of ignorance. The verse makes clear that walking between Safa and Marwah is a rite commanded by Allah, free from any association with false worship. For the reader, the verse teaches the importance of the rites of pilgrimage and obedience to Allah’s commands. It also teaches that Allah appreciates those who do good willingly, and that sincere acts of worship are known to Allah and rewarded. This verse also teaches that a believer should obey Allah’s commands even when they may seem connected to old practices or when there is hesitation. The Companions had some hesitation about walking between Safa and Marwah because the practice was connected to the time before Islam, and this verse removed that hesitation by declaring the two hills to be symbols of Allah. This shows the wisdom of clarifying the rites of worship and the importance of following Allah’s command with confidence. The verse also encourages doing good willingly, rather than reluctantly, because Allah appreciates and knows all good deeds.",
  "159": "“Those who hide the clear proofs and guidance that We have revealed.”\n\nThis verse condemns the concealment of divine guidance. \"Those who hide the clear proofs and guidance that We have revealed— after We made it clear for humanity in the Book—will be condemned by Allah and ˹all˺ those who condemn.\" The verse describes those who hide the clear proofs and guidance that Allah revealed, after it was made clear in the Book for humanity. Their sin is concealment: they knew the truth and hid it from people. This is a serious offense, because it deprives others of guidance.\n\nThe punishment is stated: \"will be condemned by Allah and ˹all˺ those who condemn.\" Those who hide the guidance are condemned by Allah and by all who condemn wrong. Their concealment brings a curse and disapproval. The condemnation is not only from Allah but from all who know of their wrongdoing.\n\nScholars explain that this verse, and the two that follow, were revealed in connection with those of the People of the Book who hid the descriptions of the final messenger from their scriptures. They concealed what they knew to be true. For the reader, the verse teaches the gravity of hiding religious knowledge. Knowledge is a trust, and concealing it when people need it is a sin. The verse encourages sharing the truth and making it known, rather than hiding it. It also warns that those who conceal divine guidance will be condemned by Allah and by all who stand against wrongdoing. The condemnation mentioned in this verse is not only a future punishment but also a present reality, as those who hide the truth lose the respect and trust of people and are held in low regard by those who know of their wrongdoing. This verse teaches that knowledge carries a heavy responsibility. When a person knows the truth and is in a position to share it, hiding it for selfish reasons is a grave betrayal. The verses that follow show that this sin, like all sins, can be forgiven through sincere repentance, revealing the mercy of Allah alongside His warning against concealing the truth.",
  "160": "“As for those who repent, mend their ways, and let the truth be known.”\n\nThis verse offers hope after the warning of the previous verse. \"As for those who repent, mend their ways, and let the truth be known, they are the ones to whom I will turn ˹in forgiveness˺.\" Those who hid the truth were warned, but now Allah opens the door of forgiveness for those who repent. Repentance means turning back to Allah with regret for the sin. \"Mend their ways\" means to correct their behaviour and stop concealing the truth. \"Let the truth be known\" means to reveal what they had hidden. These three acts, repentance, correction, and revealing the truth, are the path back.\n\nFor such people, Allah says, \"they are the ones to whom I will turn ˹in forgiveness˺, for I am the Accepter of Repentance, Most Merciful.\" Allah will turn to them in forgiveness, because He is the Accepter of Repentance and the Most Merciful. Their sincere return is met with divine mercy.\n\nThis verse teaches that no sin, even the serious sin of hiding divine guidance, is beyond forgiveness for those who sincerely repent. The conditions are clear: repent, correct one’s ways, and reveal the truth that was hidden. This shows that true repentance involves not only regret but also action to undo the harm. For the reader, the verse gives hope that even those who have concealed the truth can be forgiven if they turn back sincerely. It also teaches the importance of repentance and of making known the truth, and it emphasizes the mercy of Allah, the Accepter of Repentance. This verse shows that repentance is not complete with mere words; it must be accompanied by action. The one who hid the truth must not only regret but must reveal the truth he had concealed, undoing the harm he caused. This teaches that true repentance includes correcting one’s wrong and restoring what was lost. The mercy of Allah is vast, and it reaches those who genuinely turn back, even after serious sin. The verse therefore balances the severe warning of the previous verse with the open door of mercy, showing that the path of repentance is always available and that Allah, the Accepter of Repentance, welcomes those who return to Him sincerely.",
  "161": "“Surely those who disbelieve and die as disbelievers are condemned by Allah.”\n\nThis verse states the fate of those who persist in disbelief. \"Surely those who disbelieve and die as disbelievers are condemned by Allah, the angels, and all of humanity.\" The verse describes those who disbelieve and remain in disbelief until they die. For them, condemnation comes from Allah, from the angels, and from all of humanity. Their rejection of the truth, maintained to the end of their lives, brings this universal condemnation.\n\nThe condemnation of the angels and all of humanity shows how grave their state is. They are cursed and rejected, not only by Allah but by the creation that knows of their wrongdoing. This is the opposite of the blessings promised to those who believe and do good. The contrast between the two groups is complete.\n\nThis verse teaches the seriousness of persisting in disbelief until death. It is not the same as a person who disbelieves but later repents and believes; rather, it is the state of those who die in disbelief. For such people, the condemnation is severe and universal. For the reader, the verse serves as a warning against persisting in disbelief and as a call to accept the truth before death. It also teaches that the opportunity for repentance is in this life, and that a person should not delay returning to Allah. The verse contrasts with the mercy promised to those who repent, showing the two paths clearly. The mention of the angels and all of humanity in the condemnation shows the gravity of dying in disbelief. The angels, who are obedient to Allah, stand against those who rejected His message, and humanity itself condemns those who chose falsehood over truth. This universal condemnation is the opposite of the mercy promised to the believers. The verse also shows the importance of the condition \"die as disbelievers.\" It is not simply disbelieving that brings this condemnation but persisting in disbelief until death, without repenting. This leaves the door of repentance open during life and makes clear how serious it is to delay turning to Allah until it is too late.",
  "162": "“They will be in Hell forever.”\n\nThis verse describes the state of those who persist in disbelief, following the condemnation in the previous verse. \"They will be in Hell forever.\" Their stay in Hell is eternal. There is no end to it. This is the consequence of dying in disbelief. The verse then states, \"Their punishment will not be lightened, nor will they be delayed ˹from it˺.\" Their punishment will not be made lighter, and it will not be delayed. They will suffer it fully and permanently.\n\nThe two statements together remove all hope of escape or relief. The punishment is eternal, it is not lightened, and it is not delayed. Those who persist in disbelief until death face this reality. The verse is a clear and sobering statement of the consequences of rejecting the truth to the very end.\n\nThis verse teaches the finality of the punishment for those who die in disbelief. There is no reduction, no delay, and no end. This is why the Quran repeatedly calls people to accept the truth and repent while there is still time, because the Hereafter is eternal and final. For the reader, the verse serves as a serious warning and a motivation to seek faith and righteousness in this life. It also highlights the justice of Allah, who punishes those who persist in rejecting the truth. The verse contrasts with the eternal reward promised to the believers, showing the two eternal destinations that every person will face. The eternity of the punishment and its finality are stated to remove any false hope. Some might imagine that the punishment will end or be lightened, but the verse rules out both possibilities. This is a serious warning that is meant to move people to act in this life, while the opportunity to repent still exists. The clarity of this warning is itself a mercy, because it tells people the truth plainly so they can avoid it. For the reader, the verse serves as a powerful reminder of the reality of the Hereafter and the importance of sincere faith and repentance before death arrives.",
  "163": "“Your God is ˹only˺ One God.”\n\nThis verse is one of the clearest statements of monotheism in the Quran. \"Your God is ˹only˺ One God.\" The word \"only\" emphasizes that there is just one God, and He alone is the object of worship. This is the foundation of Islamic faith. The verse then affirms, \"There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him.\" There is no other god worthy of worship besides Allah. All other things that people worship, whether idols, people, or powers, are false.\n\nThe verse then describes Allah with two of His beautiful names: \"the Most Compassionate, Most Merciful.\" The One God is not a distant, harsh deity; He is the Most Compassionate and Most Merciful. These names, which opened the Quran and appear throughout it, describe the nature of Allah as full of mercy. The One who is worshipped alone is also the One whose mercy encompasses all.\n\nThis verse is a summary of the entire message of the Quran: Allah is One, there is no god worthy of worship except Him, and He is the Most Compassionate and Most Merciful. This is the pure monotheism that all the prophets taught. For the reader, the verse encourages a deep awareness of the oneness of Allah and directs all worship to Him alone. It also fills the heart with hope, because the One God worshipped is merciful. The verse stands as a foundation of faith and a reminder of the nature of Allah, both His oneness and His mercy. The placement of this verse, with its emphasis on the oneness and mercy of Allah, is significant. It comes after the warnings about the punishment of the disbelievers, and it serves to direct the believer’s heart to the true God who is to be worshipped. The oneness of Allah is the foundation of all worship, and His mercy gives hope. These two ideas, oneness and mercy, run throughout the Quran and are essential to a proper understanding of Allah. The reader is encouraged to hold firmly to the belief that Allah is One and Merciful, and to direct all worship and hope to Him alone, free from any partner or equal.",
  "164": "“Indeed, in the creation of the heavens and the earth.”\n\nThis verse points to the signs of Allah in the universe. \"Indeed, in the creation of the heavens and the earth; the alternation of the day and the night; the ships that sail the sea for the benefit of humanity; the rain sent down by Allah from the skies, reviving the earth after its death; the scattering of all kinds of creatures throughout; the shifting of the winds; and the clouds drifting between the heavens and the earth—˹in all of this˺ are surely signs for people of understanding.\"\n\nThe verse lists the great signs of Allah in creation: the heavens and the earth, the alternation of day and night, the ships that sail the sea, the rain that revives the dead earth, the many creatures scattered throughout, the shifting winds, and the clouds between heaven and earth. All of these are signs, pointing to the power, wisdom, and mercy of the Creator. They are not random events but deliberate works of Allah.\n\nThe verse says these are \"signs for people of understanding.\" The signs are clear, but they benefit those who reflect and understand. A person who looks at the universe with understanding sees the hand of Allah in everything. This is the Quran’s way of proving the existence and greatness of Allah: through the visible world around us. For the reader, the verse encourages reflection on creation as a means of strengthening faith. It teaches that the universe is not silent but speaks of its Creator. The signs of Allah are everywhere, and those who understand them are led to worship Allah alone. This verse is an invitation to reflect, and reflection is one of the greatest acts of worship. The signs listed cover the heavens, the earth, the cycles of day and night, the movement of the seas, the rain that brings life, the creatures scattered across the earth, the winds, and the clouds. Each of these is a sign that points to Allah. A person who reflects on them with understanding is led to faith, while a person who ignores them misses the message. The verse therefore encourages the reader to look at the world with the eye of understanding and to see, behind the order and beauty of creation, the power and wisdom of the Creator.",
  "165": "“Still there are some who take others as Allah’s equal.”\n\nThis verse exposes the wrongdoing of those who worship others besides Allah. \"Still there are some who take others as Allah's equal—they love them as they should love Allah.\" Despite all the signs of Allah’s power and mercy, some people take others as equals of Allah, loving them with the love that should be reserved for Allah alone. They devote to these false objects the love and devotion that belong to Allah.\n\nThe verse contrasts them with the believers: \"but the ˹true˺ believers love Allah even more.\" The believers love Allah more than these people love their false gods, because the believers know the true greatness and worth of Allah. Their love for Allah is the greatest love, surpassing all other loves. This is the proper order of love: Allah is loved above everything.\n\nThe verse then says, \"If only the wrongdoers could see the ˹horrible˺ punishment ˹awaiting them˺, they would certainly realize that all power belongs to Allah and that Allah is indeed severe in punishment.\" If the wrongdoers could see the punishment awaiting them, they would realize that all power belongs to Allah and that Allah is severe in punishment. Their false gods would not help them.\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of directing love to Allah alone, and warns against loving anything with the love due to Allah. The believers love Allah more than anything, while the wrongdoers misplace their love on false objects. For the reader, the verse encourages a love of Allah that surpasses all other loves, and it warns that those who take others as Allah’s equal will face a severe punishment, realizing too late that all power belongs to Allah. The contrast between the love of the believers and the love of the wrongdoers is a central point. The wrongdoers love their false gods with a love that should be for Allah, while the believers love Allah above everything else. This shows that the problem of the wrongdoers is not that they have no love but that their love is misplaced, directed to what has no right to it. True faith is not the absence of love but the right ordering of love, placing Allah first and above all. The verse warns that the wrongdoers will realize this too late, when they see the punishment and understand that all power belongs to Allah alone.",
  "166": "“˹Consider the Day˺ when those who misled others will disown their followers.”\n\nThis verse describes a terrible scene on the Day of Judgment. \"˹Consider the Day˺ when those who misled others will disown their followers—when they face the torment.\" On that day, the leaders who misled people will disown their followers. When they face the torment, the bonds that united them in this world will be cut off. The false leaders will deny their followers, and the followers will be left alone.\n\nThe verse continues, \"and the bonds that united them will be cut off.\" The bonds of loyalty and companionship that existed in this world, between the misleaders and their followers, will be completely severed. Those who followed and those who led will turn against each other, each trying to escape responsibility. This is a complete reversal of the relationships of this world.\n\nThis verse teaches that the relationships built on falsehood will not survive the Day of Judgment. Those who misled others will abandon them in their time of need, and the bonds that seemed strong will be cut. For the reader, the verse is a warning against following those who lead others astray, and against building one’s life on relationships that are not based on truth. It also teaches that on the Day of Judgment, every person will be responsible for himself, and no leader will be able to save his followers. The next verse shows the followers’ regret. This verse reveals the emptiness of following false leaders. In this world, those who lead others astray seem powerful and important, and their followers imagine that following them brings benefit. On the Day of Judgment, however, these leaders will abandon their followers, and the bonds of this world will be cut. This shows that no human relationship based on falsehood can provide real security. The verse encourages the reader to examine whom he follows and on what foundation his loyalty is built, because the day will come when every false bond is severed and every person stands alone before Allah.",
  "167": "“The ˹misled˺ followers will cry, ‘If only we could have a second chance.’”\n\nThis verse records the regret of the misled followers on the Day of Judgment. \"The ˹misled˺ followers will cry, 'If only we could have a second chance, we would disown them as they disowned us.'\" When the misled followers see their leaders disowning them, they wish for a second chance in this world, so that they could disown their leaders just as their leaders disowned them. Their regret is bitter, but it comes too late.\n\nThe verse then states, \"And so Allah will make them remorseful of their misdeeds.\" Allah will show them their misdeeds and make them feel remorse for what they did in this world, following the misleaders and turning from the truth. Their remorse is a punishment in itself, as they realize too late what they should have done.\n\nThe verse ends, \"And they will never ˹be able to˺ leave the Fire.\" Their regret and remorse will not help them, because they will never leave the Fire. Their punishment is eternal, and their wish for a second chance will never be granted. This is the tragic end of those who followed misleaders instead of the truth.\n\nThis verse teaches the seriousness of following those who lead astray, and the regret that will come too late. The followers will wish they had not followed their misleaders, but the wish will be useless. For the reader, the verse is a warning to follow the truth and not to be led astray by others, because the regret of the Hereafter is too late to change anything. It also teaches that each person is responsible for his own choice to follow the truth, and that no excuse will save those who turned from it. This verse teaches that regret in the Hereafter is useless, because the opportunity for change is only in this life. The followers wish for a second chance, but none will be given. This is why the Quran emphasizes acting in this world while the door of repentance is open. The remorse that Allah makes them feel is part of their punishment, as they come to see clearly what they did and what they should have done. For the reader, the verse is a powerful call not to delay repentance or to follow misleaders, because the day will come when regret can no longer change anything.",
  "168": "“O humanity! Eat from what is lawful and good on the earth.”\n\nThis verse gives guidance about food. \"O humanity! Eat from what is lawful and good on the earth.\" Allah commands people to eat from what is lawful and good. The word \"lawful\" refers to what is permitted, and \"good\" refers to what is pure and wholesome. This command allows people to enjoy the provisions of Allah while staying within the limits He has set. Eating lawful and good food is part of obedience to Allah.\n\nThe verse then warns, \"and do not follow Satan's footsteps. He is truly your sworn enemy.\" People are warned not to follow the footsteps of Satan, meaning not to follow his suggestions and deceptions. This warning follows the command about food because Satan often leads people to make unlawful things seem lawful, or to overindulge. The verse reminds that Satan is a sworn enemy, whose aim is to lead people astray.\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of eating what is lawful and good, and of guarding against the whispers of Satan. A person should be mindful of where his food comes from and whether it is permitted. The reminder that Satan is a sworn enemy encourages vigilance against his deceptions. For the reader, the verse encourages eating lawful and good provisions with gratitude and awareness, and it warns against following the suggestions of Satan in matters of food and beyond. The next verse explains what Satan specifically incites people to do. The command to eat what is lawful and good is a mercy, because it allows people to enjoy the bounties of Allah while staying within His limits. Islam does not forbid good things but directs people to the lawful and pure among them. The warning against following Satan’s footsteps is essential, because Satan often leads people to confuse the lawful with the unlawful or to indulge beyond the limits. Recognising Satan as a sworn enemy helps the believer remain vigilant and avoid his traps. The verse therefore combines permission and warning, encouraging the enjoyment of Allah’s provisions with awareness and self-control.",
  "169": "“He only incites you to commit evil and indecency.”\n\nThis verse explains what Satan calls people to. \"He only incites you to commit evil and indecency, and to claim against Allah what you do not know.\" Satan’s aim is to lead people to evil and indecency, and to make them say things about Allah without knowledge. The word \"evil\" refers to sin and wrong, and \"indecency\" refers to shameful and immoral acts. Satan works to draw people into these.\n\nThe verse also mentions that Satan incites people \"to claim against Allah what you do not know.\" This refers to making false claims about Allah, such as declaring something lawful or unlawful without knowledge, or attributing false beliefs to Allah. This is a grave sin, because it speaks about Allah without knowledge. Satan leads people to this through their desires and assumptions.\n\nThis verse teaches that Satan’s call is always toward evil and away from good. He does not call people to truth but to sin, indecency, and false claims about Allah. This is why people are warned against following him. For the reader, the verse encourages recognizing Satan’s tactics and resisting them. It also warns against the serious sin of speaking about Allah without knowledge, which is one of the ways Satan misleads people. The verse reinforces the previous command to eat lawful food and to follow Allah’s guidance rather than the footsteps of Satan. The mention of making false claims about Allah is a serious matter. Speaking about Allah without knowledge, whether by declaring things lawful or unlawful without evidence, or by attributing false beliefs to Him, is a grave sin. This is one of the ways Satan misleads people, by making them confident in their own opinions about religion. The verse teaches that a person should speak about Allah only with knowledge and should follow the guidance He has revealed, not personal desires or assumptions. This protects the religion from distortion and keeps the believer grounded in what Allah has actually commanded.",
  "170": "“When it is said to them, ‘Follow what Allah has revealed.’”\n\nThis verse exposes the attitude of those who follow their ancestors instead of Allah’s guidance. \"When it is said to them, 'Follow what Allah has revealed,' they reply, 'No! We ˹only˺ follow what we found our forefathers practicing.'\" When they are called to follow what Allah has revealed, they refuse and say they will follow what their forefathers practiced. They cling to the ways of their ancestors out of habit and loyalty, even when those ways contradict the truth.\n\nThe verse then challenges this attitude: \"˹Would they still do so,˺ even if their forefathers had ˹absolutely˺ no understanding or guidance?\" The question exposes the foolishness of blind imitation. If their forefathers had no understanding and no guidance, what is the value of following them? The point is that truth is not determined by ancestry but by evidence and guidance. Following ancestors without reason is not wisdom; it is blind habit.\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of following the truth, not just tradition. While respecting one’s ancestors and heritage is good, it cannot justify following falsehood. A person must follow what is true and guided by Allah, even if it means differing from the practices of one’s forefathers. For the reader, the verse encourages reflecting on one’s beliefs and practices, asking whether they are based on truth and guidance or merely on inherited tradition. It also warns against blind imitation of ancestors when their ways contradict the guidance of Allah. This verse challenges the authority of tradition when it contradicts truth. While respecting ancestors is good, it cannot justify following falsehood. The question posed by the verse, whether they would follow their forefathers even if they had no understanding or guidance, exposes the emptiness of blind imitation. A person is responsible for his own beliefs and cannot use his ancestors as an excuse for rejecting the truth. The verse encourages the reader to reflect on his practices and to accept the guidance of Allah, even when it means differing from the ways of one’s forefathers, because truth and guidance are more important than inherited habit.",
  "171": "“The example of the disbelievers ˹not responding to the Messenger’s warning˺ is like a flock.”\n\nThis verse gives a striking example of the disbelievers. \"The example of the disbelievers ˹not responding to the Messenger's warning˺ is like a flock not comprehending the calls and cries of the shepherd.\" The disbelievers, when they do not respond to the warning of the Messenger, are compared to a flock of animals that does not understand the calls and cries of the shepherd. The shepherd calls and warns, but the flock, being animals, does not comprehend the meaning. Similarly, the disbelievers hear the warning but do not understand or benefit from it.\n\nThe verse continues, \"˹They are wilfully˺ deaf, dumb and blind so they have no understanding.\" The disbelievers are described as deaf, dumb, and blind, meaning they do not hear the truth truly, do not speak it, and do not see it. Because of this, they have no understanding. This is not a physical condition but a spiritual one, resulting from their wilful rejection of the truth.\n\nThis verse teaches the seriousness of refusing to respond to the warning. The warning is clear, but those who turn away from it become like a flock that cannot understand. The description of being deaf, dumb, and blind points to a spiritual state of being cut off from the truth. For the reader, the verse encourages being responsive to the guidance of the Messenger and not becoming like those who hear but do not understand. It also warns against wilful rejection of the truth, which can leave a person unable to benefit from it. This verse also shows the patience and clarity of the Messenger. The shepherd calls and warns the flock, even though it does not comprehend, and in the same way the Messenger delivered the warning clearly, even to those who would not understand or benefit. The responsibility of the caller is to deliver the message clearly, not to guarantee acceptance. The description of the disbelievers as deaf, dumb, and blind points to their wilful refusal, which cut them off from the truth. The verse encourages the reader not to become like a heedless flock but to receive the warning with understanding and respond to it.",
  "172": "“O believers! Eat from the good things We have provided for you.”\n\nThis verse addresses the believers with a command about food. \"O believers! Eat from the good things We have provided for you.\" The believers are told to eat from the good things that Allah has provided. The word \"good\" refers to what is pure, wholesome, and lawful. This command encourages the believers to enjoy the provisions of Allah while remaining within the limits He has set.\n\nThe verse then connects eating with worship: \"And give thanks to Allah if you ˹truly˺ worship Him ˹alone˺.\" Those who truly worship Allah alone are called to give thanks to Him for His provisions. Thankfulness is a sign of true worship. A person who worships Allah alone recognizes that all provisions come from Him and responds with gratitude. The verse ties the simple act of eating to the greater reality of worship and gratitude.\n\nThis verse teaches that eating is not only a physical act but also an occasion for gratitude and worship. The believer eats what is good and thanks Allah for it. The connection between eating lawful food and worshipping Allah shows that religion covers all aspects of life, including food. For the reader, the verse encourages eating what is lawful and good, and being grateful to Allah, recognizing Him as the provider. It also teaches that true worship of Allah alone is accompanied by thankfulness, and that gratitude is a sign of sincere faith. This verse connects the daily act of eating with the worship of Allah, showing that religion encompasses all of life. Eating is not merely a physical need but an occasion for gratitude and remembrance. The mention of worshipping Allah alone in connection with thankfulness shows that true worship and gratitude belong together. A person who worships Allah alone recognizes Him as the provider and responds with thanks. The verse encourages the believer to be mindful of Allah in the ordinary acts of daily life, turning even the simple act of eating into a moment of worship and gratitude.",
  "173": "“He has only forbidden you ˹to eat˺ carrion, blood, swine, and what is slaughtered in the name of any other than Allah.”\n\nThis verse specifies what is forbidden to eat. \"He has only forbidden you ˹to eat˺ carrion, blood, swine, and what is slaughtered in the name of any other than Allah.\" The verse lists four things: carrion, meaning animals that die without being properly slaughtered; blood; the meat of swine, meaning pigs; and what is slaughtered in the name of anything other than Allah, meaning animals dedicated to other gods. These are the forbidden foods in Islam.\n\nThe verse then gives an exception: \"But if someone is compelled by necessity —neither driven by desire nor exceeding immediate need—they will not be sinful.\" If a person is forced by extreme necessity to eat something forbidden, such as to save his life, and he does so without desire or exceeding what is needed, then he will not be sinful. This shows the mercy of Allah, who does not burden people beyond their capacity.\n\nThe verse ends, \"Surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\" Allah is All-Forgiving and Most Merciful, forgiving those who are compelled by necessity and merciful to His servants. This verse teaches the general rule of what is forbidden and the exception for extreme necessity. For the reader, the verse encourages following Allah’s guidance in food and understanding the mercy of the exception in cases of necessity. It also highlights the care of Allah, who does not make religion a hardship but balances law with mercy. This verse shows the balance of Islam between law and mercy. The forbidden foods are limited and clear, while the general rule is that what is not forbidden is lawful. The exception for necessity shows that the law does not intend hardship but the preservation of life. A person forced by extreme circumstances is not sinful for eating what is normally forbidden, as long as he does not exceed what is needed. This reflects the mercy of Allah, who knows human weakness and has provided for it. The verse encourages following Allah’s guidance in food with understanding, and trusting in His mercy in difficult circumstances.",
  "174": "“Indeed, those who hide Allah’s revelations, trading them for a fleeting gain consume nothing but fire into their bellies.”\n\nThis verse condemns those who hide the revelations of Allah for worldly gain. \"Indeed, those who hide Allah's revelations, trading them for a fleeting gain consume nothing but fire into their bellies.\" The verse uses a powerful image: those who hide the truth for a worldly benefit are described as consuming fire into their bellies. Their worldly gain is pictured as fire, because what they earn through hiding the truth will become a source of punishment for them. The fleeting gain they sought is nothing compared to the fire they are filling themselves with.\n\nThe verse then describes their fate on the Day of Judgment: \"Allah will neither speak to them on the Day of Judgment, nor will He purify them.\" On that day, Allah will not speak to them in a way of mercy, and He will not purify them from their sin. This is a severe deprivation, because the mercy and purification of Allah are withheld. The verse ends, \"And they will suffer a painful punishment.\" Their end is a painful punishment.\n\nThis verse continues the theme of those who conceal the truth, which was discussed in earlier verses. Hiding divine guidance for worldly gain is a grave sin, and its reward is the fire. For the reader, the verse teaches the sanctity of the truth and the seriousness of concealing it for worldly benefit. It also shows the justice of Allah, who will deal with those who hide the truth. The image of consuming fire serves as a vivid warning against the shortsightedness of trading the truth for a fleeting worldly gain. This verse also teaches that wealth gained through concealing the truth brings no real benefit. Whatever a person earns by hiding what Allah revealed is gained at the cost of his own soul, and that cost far outweighs any worldly benefit. The verse connects this to the Day of Judgment, where Allah will neither speak to them in mercy nor purify them, showing how serious the betrayal of the truth is. This is a powerful warning for anyone who has knowledge, reminding them that knowledge is a trust and that concealing it for worldly gain leads to ruin. The reader is encouraged to share the truth sincerely and to value the reward of Allah over any fleeting worldly benefit.",
  "175": "“They are the ones who trade guidance for misguidance.”\n\nThis verse describes those who hide the truth in terms of a bad trade. \"They are the ones who trade guidance for misguidance and forgiveness for punishment.\" They have exchanged guidance for misguidance, giving up the right path for error, and they have exchanged forgiveness for punishment, giving up what would have earned them forgiveness and choosing instead what brings punishment. The image of trade shows that their condition is the result of their own choice. They deliberately made a bad exchange, giving up what was valuable for what was worthless and harmful.\n\nThe verse then expresses amazement at their pursuit: \"How persistent are they in pursuit of the Fire!\" They are persistent in seeking the Fire, meaning they actively pursue what leads to punishment, even though it brings them only harm. Their persistence is astonishing, because they run toward their own destruction. The verse wonders at how a person can be so determined to pursue something that will destroy him.\n\nThis verse teaches the danger of choosing misguidance over guidance and punishment over forgiveness. The trade they made was entirely to their loss, yet they persisted in it. For the reader, the verse is a warning to examine the choices one makes and to recognize the value of guidance and forgiveness, which are far greater than anything that could be gained by rejecting them. It also teaches that a person can become so accustomed to error that he pursues it persistently, even at the cost of his own soul. The persistence mentioned in this verse is remarkable because it shows how deeply a person can become invested in his own error. The one who hides the truth does not merely stumble into misguidance; he pursues it actively, even at the cost of his salvation. This is the tragedy of the one who rejects guidance: he thinks he is gaining something, while in reality he is running toward his own destruction. The verse calls the reader to examine his own direction and to ask whether he is pursuing the Fire or the reward of Allah, and it warns against the stubbornness that makes a person persist in what harms him.",
  "176": "“That is because Allah has revealed the Book in truth.”\n\nThis verse explains the source of the punishment that awaits those who hide the truth. \"That is because Allah has revealed the Book in truth.\" The reason their punishment is just is that Allah revealed the Book in truth, with clear guidance and correct judgment. Because the truth has been revealed clearly, those who reject it or hide it have no excuse. Their punishment is the just consequence of turning away from a truth that was made clear.\n\nThe verse then says, \"And surely those who differ regarding it are totally engrossed in opposition.\" Those who differ about the Book, disputing its truth or rejecting parts of it, are engrossed in opposition. They are deeply caught up in resisting the truth, which is why they suffer. Their opposition is not based on honest seeking but on stubbornness.\n\nThis verse teaches that the truth of the Quran is clear, and that those who oppose or differ from it do so out of obstinacy, not out of genuine uncertainty. Because the Book was revealed in truth, there is no valid reason to reject it. For the reader, the verse encourages accepting the truth of the Quran and avoiding the opposition and disputation that come from stubbornness. It also teaches that the punishment of those who hide or oppose the truth is just, because the truth was revealed clearly and left them without excuse. The verse also teaches that honest difference and honest inquiry are different from stubborn opposition. Those who sincerely seek the truth will find the Book to be clear and will accept it, while those who are determined to oppose it will find reasons to differ even when the truth is plain. The Quran therefore distinguishes between the seeker and the opposer. This encourages the reader to approach the Book with sincerity and openness, rather than with a heart already set on opposition, and to recognise that clarity of the revelation leaves the opposer without excuse.",
  "177": "“Righteousness is not in turning your faces towards the east or the west.”\n\nThis verse gives a comprehensive definition of righteousness. \"Righteousness is not in turning your faces towards the east or the west.\" Facing a particular direction in prayer is not the essence of righteousness. The verse is correcting a narrow view that reduced religion to outward acts of direction or form. Righteousness is something deeper and more complete. The verse then lists what righteousness truly consists of.\n\nFirst, \"the righteous are those who believe in Allah, the Last Day, the angels, the Books, and the prophets.\" Righteousness begins with correct belief. Then the verse lists good actions: \"who give charity out of their cherished wealth to relatives, orphans, the poor, ˹needy˺ travellers, beggars, and for freeing captives.\" This describes giving from one’s cherished wealth to those in need. The verse then adds, \"who establish prayer, pay alms-tax, and keep the pledges they make; and who are patient in times of suffering, adversity, and in ˹the heat of˺ battle.\"\n\nThe verse ends, \"It is they who are true ˹in faith˺, and it is they who are mindful ˹of Allah˺.\" True righteousness combines correct belief, good actions toward others, worship, honesty in keeping promises, and patience in hardship. This is a complete picture of the righteous believer. For the reader, the verse teaches that true religion is not mere outward form but a life of faith, charity, worship, honesty, and patience. It encourages striving for this complete righteousness, which combines belief in the heart with good deeds in action. This verse is one of the most comprehensive descriptions of true faith in the Quran, and it brings together the inner and outer dimensions of religion. It begins with belief in the heart and then moves to charity, prayer, honesty, and patience, showing that faith is not complete without action. The mention of giving from \"cherished wealth\" teaches that true charity comes from what is dear to the giver, not merely from surplus. The verse therefore challenges the reader to examine his life against this complete picture of righteousness and to strive to embody it, combining sincere belief with generous action, consistent worship, faithful promises, and patient endurance.",
  "178": "“O believers! ˹The law of˺ retaliation is set for you in cases of murder.”\n\nThis verse establishes the law of retaliation for murder. \"O believers! ˹The law of˺ retaliation is set for you in cases of murder— a free man for a free man, a slave for a slave, and a female for a female.\" The law of retaliation means that the one who commits murder is to be treated accordingly, establishing justice and protecting life. The verse specifies that a free man is for a free man, a slave for a slave, and a female for a female, ensuring that the punishment fits the crime and that each case is dealt with justly.\n\nThe verse then describes an alternative: \"But if the offender is pardoned by the victim's guardian, then blood-money should be decided fairly and payment should be made courteously.\" If the family of the victim pardons the offender, then blood-money, a compensation paid to the family, should be decided fairly and paid courteously. This allows for mercy alongside justice, as the victim’s family may choose to accept compensation instead of insisting on retaliation. The verse calls this \"a concession and a mercy from your Lord.\" The law of retaliation combined with the option of pardon is a mercy that balances justice with compassion.\n\nThe verse ends with a warning: \"But whoever transgresses after that will suffer a painful punishment.\" Whoever exceeds the limits after this will face a painful punishment. This verse teaches the seriousness of taking life and the justice of the law of retaliation, while also showing the mercy of the option of pardon and compensation. For the reader, the verse teaches the value Islam places on human life and on justice, and it shows how the law balances punishment with mercy, protecting society while allowing forgiveness.",
  "179": "“There is ˹security of˺ life for you in ˹the law of˺ retaliation.”\n\nThis verse explains the wisdom behind the law of retaliation. \"There is ˹security of˺ life for you in ˹the law of˺ retaliation.\" The law of retaliation actually preserves life. This may seem surprising, because the law involves punishment, but it preserves life in a larger sense. When people know that murder will be punished justly, they are deterred from committing it, and so lives are protected. The certainty of justice discourages crime and protects society.\n\nThe verse then addresses those who can understand this: \"O people of reason.\" The wisdom of the law of retaliation is best understood by people of reason, who see beyond the immediate punishment to the greater good of protecting life. The verse ends, \"so that you may become mindful ˹of Allah˺.\" The purpose of this law and its explanation is to make people mindful of Allah, aware that they are accountable to Him and that justice has meaning.\n\nThis verse teaches that just punishment serves a greater purpose than mere vengeance; it preserves life and order in society. The law of retaliation is not about cruelty but about protecting people. For the reader, the verse encourages understanding the wisdom behind Islamic laws and recognizing that they are designed for the benefit of humanity. It also teaches that the purpose of these laws is to cultivate mindfulness of Allah, so that people live justly out of awareness of their accountability to their Lord. This verse also corrects a common misunderstanding about punishment in Islam. Some might think that the law of retaliation is harsh or vengeful, but the verse shows that it is actually a mercy, because it protects life and deters crime. Justice in Islam is not about revenge but about preserving the order and safety of society. The address to \"people of reason\" invites the reader to think deeply about the wisdom behind the law rather than judging it by appearance. This teaches that divine laws have purposes that are understood through reflection, and that the believer is called to use reason in understanding and appreciating the wisdom of Allah’s commands.",
  "180": "“It is prescribed that when death approaches any of you—if they leave something of value—a will should be made.”\n\nThis verse establishes the command to make a will. \"It is prescribed that when death approaches any of you—if they leave something of value—a will should be made in favour of parents and immediate family with fairness.\" When a person approaches death and leaves something of value, he is commanded to make a will in favour of parents and immediate family, with fairness. This ensures that the deceased’s wealth is distributed justly and that the rights of family members are protected.\n\nThe verse then states, \"˹This is˺ an obligation on those who are mindful ˹of Allah˺.\" Making a fair will is an obligation on those who are mindful of Allah. The one who is aware of his accountability to Allah takes care to leave his affairs in order, ensuring that his family is provided for and that his wealth is distributed fairly. This is part of responsible conduct.\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of preparing for death by making a fair will. A person who is mindful of Allah does not neglect this duty. The will protects the rights of parents and immediate family and ensures fairness in the distribution of wealth. For the reader, the verse encourages taking care of one’s responsibilities before death, including making a fair will. It also teaches that mindfulness of Allah leads to responsible behaviour, including the fair treatment of one’s family in the distribution of one’s wealth. This verse also teaches the importance of fairness in dealing with one’s family and wealth. A fair will reflects justice and care for those who are left behind, ensuring that the deceased does not harm his family by neglecting his duties. The mention of mindfulness of Allah as the basis of this obligation shows that responsible behaviour is rooted in one’s awareness of Allah and accountability to Him. The reader is encouraged to think about the end of life and to put one’s affairs in order, treating the making of a will as a duty of justice and care rather than a mere formality.",
  "181": "“But whoever changes the will after hearing it, the blame will only be on those who made the change.”\n\nThis verse addresses the tampering with a will. \"But whoever changes the will after hearing it, the blame will only be on those who made the change.\" If someone changes a will after hearing it, altering what the deceased intended, the blame falls on those who made the change, not on the deceased. Those who alter the will will be held accountable for their alteration. The will of the deceased is to be respected and carried out, and changing it is a wrongdoing.\n\nThe verse then says, \"Indeed, Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\" Allah hears all speech and knows all actions. He knows who has changed the will and the intentions behind the change. Nothing escapes His knowledge. The mention of Allah’s hearing and knowledge serves as a warning to those who might tamper with a will, reminding them that their actions are seen and known by Allah.\n\nThis verse teaches the sanctity of the will and the seriousness of changing it. A will is a trust, and those who alter it wrong the deceased and his family. For the reader, the verse encourages honesty in carrying out the wishes of the deceased and warns against tampering with them. It also teaches that Allah is aware of all actions, including hidden alterations, and that those who change a will will bear the responsibility for their actions. This verse also teaches the importance of honesty and integrity in carrying out trusts. A will is a trust that must be honoured, and altering it is a betrayal of the deceased and of the family. The verse reminds the reader that such betrayal is seen and known by Allah, who is All-Hearing and All-Knowing, and that those who change a will will bear the responsibility. This encourages scrupulous honesty in all dealings with the property and rights of others, and it warns against the temptation to distort what has been entrusted to one’s care for personal gain.",
  "182": "“Whoever suspects an error or an injustice in the will.”\n\nThis verse gives an exception to the rule about changing a will. \"Whoever suspects an error or an injustice in the will and brings about a ˹fair˺ settlement among the parties will not be sinful.\" If a person suspects that the will contains an error or an injustice, and he works to bring about a fair settlement among the parties, he will not be sinful. This means that it is permissible, and even praiseworthy, to correct a will that is unjust, as long as it is done fairly and to bring about justice.\n\nThe verse then says, \"Surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\" Allah is All-Forgiving and Most Merciful. Those who act to correct an injustice in a will, seeking fairness, are not sinful, because they are doing good. The mercy of Allah covers those who act justly.\n\nThis verse balances the command not to change a will with the need to correct injustice. While changing a will wrongfully is blameworthy, correcting an unjust will to achieve fairness is good. The distinction lies in the intention and the result: wrongful change serves self-interest, while fair correction serves justice. For the reader, the verse teaches the importance of fairness in dealing with wills and inheritance, and it shows that Islam encourages justice even when it requires correcting an error. It also highlights the mercy and forgiveness of Allah. This verse shows the balance between respecting the will and pursuing justice. While changing a will wrongfully is forbidden, correcting an injustice in a will is praiseworthy, because the goal is fairness. The reader learns that Islam cares about the spirit of the law, which is justice, not merely its letter. A person who works to settle a matter fairly, even if it means correcting an error, is doing good and is not sinful. This encourages the believer to act justly and to help resolve disputes fairly, trusting that Allah, who is All-Forgiving and Most Merciful, rewards those who seek to establish fairness.",
  "183": "“O believers! Fasting is prescribed for you.”\n\nThis verse establishes the command of fasting. \"O believers! Fasting is prescribed for you—as it was for those before you.\" Fasting was prescribed for the believers, just as it had been prescribed for the communities before them. This shows that fasting is not a new or unfamiliar practice but a part of the worship that Allah has required of His servants throughout history. The command is addressed to all believers, making fasting a universal duty.\n\nThe verse then gives the purpose of fasting: \"so perhaps you will become mindful ˹of Allah˺.\" Fasting is prescribed so that the believer may become mindful of Allah, developing a deep awareness of Allah that shapes his conduct. Through fasting, a person learns self-control, patience, and dependence on Allah. The abstinence from food, drink, and other desires reminds the believer that he is accountable to Allah and strengthens his mindfulness of Him.\n\nThis verse teaches that fasting is not merely abstaining from food but a means of spiritual growth. Its purpose is to cultivate mindfulness of Allah. For the reader, the verse encourages understanding the wisdom behind fasting, which is to develop taqwa, the awareness of Allah that leads to righteous living. Fasting connects the believer to Allah and to the worship that was required of previous communities, uniting them in this act of devotion. The verse also shows that worship has a purpose beyond the act itself, which is the spiritual development of the believer. Fasting is also a way of training the soul and building character. By giving up lawful food, drink, and desires for the sake of Allah, the believer learns self-control and gratitude for the blessings that are usually taken for granted. Fasting also creates empathy for the poor, who feel hunger and need, and it unites the believers in a shared act of worship. The verse connects fasting to the mindfulness of Allah, showing that the purpose of this worship is the development of a deep, guiding awareness of Allah in the heart. The reader is encouraged to fast with understanding, seeking the spiritual benefit that Allah intends through this command.",
  "184": "“˹Fast a˺ prescribed number of days.”\n\nThis verse explains the details of the fast. \"˹Fast a˺ prescribed number of days.\" The fasting was for a prescribed number of days, not a continuous obligation throughout the year. The verse then gives an exception: \"But whoever of you is ill or on a journey, then ˹let them fast˺ an equal number of days ˹after Ramaḍân˺.\" Those who are ill or travelling may break the fast and make up the same number of days later, after the month of Ramadan. This shows the ease and mercy of the law, which takes into account the difficulty of fasting while ill or travelling.\n\nThe verse then gives another provision: \"For those who can only fast with extreme difficulty, compensation can be made by feeding a needy person ˹for every day not fasted˺.\" Those who cannot fast even with difficulty, such as the very old or the chronically ill, may compensate by feeding a needy person for each day not fasted. The verse adds, \"But whoever volunteers to give more, it is better for them.\" Giving more than is required is better. The verse ends, \"And to fast is better for you, if only you knew.\" Despite the allowances, fasting itself is better for the believer, because of its spiritual benefit.\n\nThis verse teaches the mercy and flexibility of the law of fasting. It takes into account the conditions of those who are ill, travelling, or unable to fast, providing clear alternatives. For the reader, the verse encourages understanding the wisdom of these allowances and recognising that fasting is better because of its spiritual benefit. It also shows that Islam does not impose hardship but balances obligation with ease and mercy. The verse also teaches that the allowances in fasting are not a sign of weakness in the law but a sign of its mercy. Allah knows the situations of His servants, and He has provided ways for those who are ill, travelling, or unable to fast to fulfil their obligation in a suitable manner. This reflects the principle that religion is not meant to burden people beyond their capacity. The reader is encouraged to understand these provisions, to use them when necessary without guilt, and to recognise that fasting itself, when one is able, carries a spiritual benefit that makes it better for the believer.",
  "185": "“Ramaḍân is the month in which the Quran was revealed.”\n\nThis verse honours the month of Ramadan. \"Ramaḍân is the month in which the Quran was revealed as a guide for humanity with clear proofs of guidance and the standard ˹to distinguish between right and wrong˺.\" The Quran was revealed in this month, and it is described as a guide for humanity, containing clear proofs of guidance and the standard that distinguishes right from wrong. The month of Ramadan is therefore deeply connected to the revelation of the Quran, which is the greatest blessing for humanity.\n\nThe verse then commands the fast: \"So whoever is present this month, let them fast.\" Those who are present during this month are commanded to fast. It then repeats the exception: \"But whoever is ill or on a journey, then ˹let them fast˺ an equal number of days ˹after Ramaḍân˺.\" Those who are ill or travelling may fast the missed days later. The verse then states the divine intention: \"Allah intends ease for you, not hardship.\" The law of fasting is designed for ease, not hardship.\n\nThe verse continues, \"so that you may complete the prescribed period and proclaim the greatness of Allah for guiding you, and perhaps you will be grateful.\" The purpose of these provisions is that the believer may complete the prescribed fasting, proclaim the greatness of Allah for guiding him, and be grateful. For the reader, the verse teaches the connection between Ramadan and the Quran, and the mercy of the law of fasting. It encourages gratitude for guidance and the completion of the fast with thankfulness to Allah. The connection between Ramadan and the Quran gives the month its special significance. It is the month of the Quran, when the guidance of Allah was revealed to humanity. This encourages the believer to engage with the Quran during this month, reciting, reflecting, and seeking its guidance. The verse also teaches the principle that Allah intends ease, not hardship, which is the spirit of the entire law of fasting. The completion of the fast and the proclamation of Allah’s greatness are acts of gratitude, and the reader is encouraged to complete the fast with thankfulness and to appreciate the guidance for which gratitude is due.",
  "186": "“When My servants ask you ˹O Prophet˺ about Me: I am truly near.”\n\nThis verse is a beautiful statement of the closeness of Allah. \"When My servants ask you ˹O Prophet˺ about Me: I am truly near.\" When people ask the Prophet about Allah, the answer is that Allah is truly near. This nearness is a reality that brings comfort and hope. Allah is not distant from His servants; He is near to them. The verse then states, \"I respond to one's prayer when they call upon Me.\" Allah responds to the prayer of whoever calls upon Him. This is a direct promise that Allah answers those who call on Him.\n\nThe verse then gives the response expected of the servants: \"So let them respond ˹with obedience˺ to Me and believe in Me, perhaps they will be guided ˹to the Right Way˺.\" Because Allah is near and responds to prayer, the servants are to respond to Him with obedience and to believe in Him, so that they may be guided to the right way. The relationship between Allah and His servants is one of mutual response: Allah responds to their prayers, and they are to respond to His commands.\n\nThis verse teaches the nearness of Allah and His promise to respond to prayer. It is a great comfort to know that Allah hears and answers those who call upon Him. For the reader, the verse encourages supplication to Allah, confident that He is near and responds. It also teaches that the proper response to Allah’s nearness is obedience and faith, which lead to guidance. The verse is often connected with the fast, encouraging the believer to call upon Allah in supplication. This verse is a direct and personal reassurance from Allah, and it is often recited during Ramadan because it encourages supplication. The nearness of Allah and His promise to respond to prayer give the believer confidence to call upon Him at all times, and especially in the blessed month of fasting. The verse also teaches that prayer and obedience go together: because Allah responds to His servants, the servants are to respond to Him in obedience and faith. This mutual response is the heart of the relationship between the servant and his Lord, and the reader is encouraged to call upon Allah with sincerity and to respond to His guidance with obedience.",
  "187": "“It has been made permissible for you to be intimate with your wives during the nights preceding the fast.”\n\nThis verse gives rulings about the nights of fasting during Ramadan. \"It has been made permissible for you to be intimate with your wives during the nights preceding the fast.\" In the early period of Islam, there was a restriction that made this difficult, and this verse clarifies that intimacy with one’s wife is permissible during the nights before the fast. The verse then uses a beautiful image: \"Your spouses are a garment for you as you are for them.\" Spouses are like garments for each other, providing closeness, comfort, and mutual support, as a garment covers and protects.\n\nThe verse then mentions a concession: \"Allah knows that you were deceiving yourselves. So He has accepted your repentance and pardoned you.\" Allah knew that the believers were harming themselves through the restriction, so He accepted their repentance and pardoned them, making the matter permissible. The verse continues, \"So now you may be intimate with them and seek what Allah has prescribed for you.\" The believers are permitted to be intimate and to seek what Allah has prescribed.\n\nThe verse then gives the timing of the fast: \"˹You may˺ eat and drink until you see the light of dawn breaking the darkness of night, then complete the fast until nightfall.\" They may eat and drink until dawn, then fast until night. The verse adds, \"Do not be intimate with your spouses while you are meditating in the mosques.\" Those who are staying in the mosques for devotion are not to be intimate. The verse ends, \"These are the limits set by Allah, so do not exceed them. This is how Allah makes His revelations clear to people, so they may become mindful ˹of Him˺.\" This verse teaches the mercy of Allah in easing the restrictions, and it clarifies the rules of the fasting nights.",
  "188": "“Do not consume one another’s wealth unjustly.”\n\nThis verse forbids the unjust consumption of wealth. \"Do not consume one another's wealth unjustly.\" The verse prohibits taking other people’s wealth through unjust means, whether by theft, fraud, deception, or any other unjust method. Wealth is not to be taken from others without right. This is a fundamental principle of justice in Islam, protecting the property of people from being wrongfully consumed.\n\nThe verse then warns against a specific practice: \"nor deliberately bribe authorities in order to devour a portion of others' property, knowing that it is a sin.\" It forbids bribing authorities in order to wrongly take a portion of other people’s property. The bribery is done deliberately, knowing that it is a sin, in order to gain what is not rightfully theirs. This is a severe form of injustice, using corruption to devour the wealth of others.\n\nThis verse teaches the sanctity of wealth and the prohibition of taking it unjustly. It specifically condemns the use of bribery and corruption to seize the property of others. For the reader, the verse encourages honesty in all financial dealings and warns against injustice and corruption. It also teaches that wealth must be acquired and used fairly, and that those who take wealth unjustly, especially through bribery, commit a grave sin. The verse protects the rights of individuals and the justice of society. This verse also teaches the broader principle of justice in society. The wealth of people is protected in Islam, and taking it unjustly is a serious sin. The specific mention of bribery shows that corruption in authority is especially condemned, because it perverts justice and harms many people. The reader is encouraged to be honest in all financial matters, to earn and spend wealth lawfully, and to avoid any form of corruption or injustice. The verse protects both individuals and society, teaching that justice in wealth is a foundation of a righteous community and that those who violate it are accountable to Allah.",
  "189": "“They ask you ˹O Prophet˺ about the phases of the moon.”\n\nThis verse answers a question about the moon. \"They ask you ˹O Prophet˺ about the phases of the moon.\" The Prophet was asked about the changing phases of the moon. The answer is given: \"Say, 'They are a means for people to determine time and pilgrimage.'\" The phases of the moon are a means for people to determine time, to know the months and the days, and to determine the timing of the pilgrimage. The changing moon is therefore a sign of Allah’s wisdom, serving a practical purpose for humanity.\n\nThe verse then corrects a misguided practice: \"Righteousness is not in entering your houses from the back doors.\" In the time of ignorance, some people would enter their houses from the back during certain times, thinking this was a sign of righteousness. The verse corrects this, saying that righteousness is not in such outward acts. \"Rather, righteousness is to be mindful ˹of Allah˺.\" True righteousness is mindfulness of Allah. The verse then says, \"So enter your homes through their ˹proper˺ doors, and be mindful of Allah so you may be successful.\" People should enter their homes normally, through the doors, and be mindful of Allah.\n\nThis verse teaches that the moon is a sign of Allah’s wisdom and a means of determining time. It also corrects the false idea that righteousness lies in certain outward customs. True righteousness is mindfulness of Allah. For the reader, the verse encourages understanding the signs of Allah in creation and focusing on true righteousness, which is the awareness of Allah that guides one’s conduct. This verse also teaches that religion should not be reduced to meaningless customs. The practice of entering houses from the back was a superstition from the time of ignorance, and the Quran corrects it by pointing to true righteousness, which is mindfulness of Allah. This shows that outward acts only have value when they are connected to genuine faith and correct intention. The verse encourages the reader to focus on the substance of religion rather than on empty rituals, and to understand the signs of Allah in creation, such as the phases of the moon, as means of ordering one’s life and worship. True success lies in mindfulness of Allah.",
  "190": "“Fight in the cause of Allah ˹only˺ against those who wage war against you.”\n\nThis verse establishes the rules of fighting. \"Fight in the cause of Allah ˹only˺ against those who wage war against you, but do not exceed the limits.\" The command to fight is limited to those who wage war against the believers, and it is to be in the cause of Allah, not for personal gain or aggression. The verse also commands not to exceed the limits, meaning not to go beyond the bounds of justice and fairness in fighting. Fighting is to be defensive and just.\n\nThe verse then states, \"Surely Allah does not like transgressors.\" Allah does not like those who exceed the limits. This warns against aggression, injustice, and going beyond what is permitted in fighting. The believer is to fight only in self-defence and in the cause of Allah, within the limits of justice.\n\nThis verse establishes the fundamental rules of warfare in Islam: fighting is only against those who wage war against the believers, it must be in the cause of Allah, and it must not exceed the limits. Islam does not permit aggression or injustice in fighting. For the reader, the verse teaches the importance of justice even in conflict, and it shows that fighting in Islam is defensive and governed by strict limits. The verse also teaches that Allah does not like transgressors, encouraging the believers to remain within the bounds of justice. This verse also teaches the ethical limits that govern conflict in Islam. Fighting is not a free-for-all but is subject to strict rules: it is only against those who wage war, it must be in the cause of Allah, and it must not exceed the limits. This shows that even in war, justice and restraint must be maintained. The verse warns against transgression, teaching the reader that Allah does not like those who exceed the bounds. This reflects the high moral standard of Islam, which governs even the most difficult of human activities, and it encourages the believer to act with justice and self-restraint in all circumstances.",
  "191": "“Kill them wherever you come upon them.”\n\nThis verse gives further instructions about fighting. \"Kill them wherever you come upon them and drive them out of the places from which they have driven you out.\" The believers are commanded to fight those who wage war against them wherever they encounter them, and to drive them out of the places from which they were driven. This is in response to the persecution the believers faced. The verse then states a principle: \"For persecution is far worse than killing.\" Persecution, the oppression and driving of people from their homes and faith, is worse than killing in war. This justifies the fighting as a response to severe persecution.\n\nThe verse then gives a restriction: \"And do not fight them at the Sacred Mosque unless they attack you there.\" The believers are not to fight at the Sacred Mosque unless the enemy attacks them there. \"If they do so, then fight them—that is the reward of the disbelievers.\" If the enemy attacks at the Sacred Mosque, the believers may defend themselves.\n\nThis verse teaches the rules of fighting, including responding to persecution and defending oneself. It establishes that persecution is worse than fighting, and it places restrictions on where fighting may occur. For the reader, the verse teaches that the believers are permitted to fight in self-defence against those who wage war and persecute them, while maintaining limits and respecting the sanctity of the Sacred Mosque. The verse also shows that fighting is a response to oppression, not an act of aggression. This verse also shows that the purpose of fighting in Islam is to end persecution and establish justice, not to harm people for its own sake. The principle that persecution is worse than killing provides the moral justification for defensive fighting, while the restriction on fighting at the Sacred Mosque shows that even in conflict, certain limits and sanctities are respected. The reader learns that justice in conflict requires both firmness and restraint, and that the goal is the removal of oppression. This teaches the importance of defending against injustice while remaining within the bounds that Allah has set.",
  "192": "“But if they cease, then surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.”\n\nThis verse sets out the mercy that follows if the enemy stops fighting. \"But if they cease, then surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\" If the enemy ceases their persecution and fighting, then the believers are to stop as well, and Allah is All-Forgiving and Most Merciful. The stopping of the enemy’s aggression is met with the mercy and forgiveness of Allah, and the conflict is to end. This shows that the aim of the fighting is not destruction but the end of persecution.\n\nThe verse teaches that when the enemy stops, the believers stop, and there is no ongoing hostility. The mercy of Allah is connected to the cessation of aggression. This shows the just and merciful nature of the command to fight: it is a response to aggression, and it ends when the aggression ends.\n\nThis verse teaches the principle of proportionate response and the importance of ending conflict when it is over. Fighting is not pursued for its own sake but to end persecution, and when the enemy stops, the fighting stops. For the reader, the verse teaches the merciful nature of Islam’s rules of war, which seek justice and the end of oppression rather than endless conflict. It also encourages forgiveness and mercy when aggression ceases, reflecting the mercy of Allah, who is All-Forgiving and Most Merciful. This verse also teaches the importance of mercy and the end of conflict. When the enemy stops, the fighting stops, and there is no lingering hostility. This reflects the just and merciful nature of Islam’s approach to conflict, which seeks peace once the cause of fighting has ended. The mention of Allah’s forgiveness and mercy shows that the cessation of aggression opens the door to reconciliation. The reader is encouraged to be merciful and to seek peace when conflict is over, following the example of Allah, who is All-Forgiving and Most Merciful. Justice requires firmness against aggression, but mercy requires ending hostility when it ceases.",
  "193": "“Fight against them ˹if they persecute you˺ until there is no more persecution.”\n\nThis verse states the goal of the fighting. \"Fight against them ˹if they persecute you˺ until there is no more persecution, and ˹your˺ devotion will be to Allah ˹alone˺.\" The purpose of fighting is to end persecution and to ensure that devotion is to Allah alone, meaning that people are free to worship Allah without oppression. The goal is not conquest for its own sake but the removal of persecution and the freedom to practice one’s religion.\n\nThe verse then sets the condition for peace: \"If they stop ˹persecuting you˺, let there be no hostility except against the aggressors.\" If the enemy stops persecuting, then there is to be no hostility, except against those who continue to aggress. Hostility is directed only against aggressors, and once persecution ends, peace is to prevail.\n\nThis verse teaches that the ultimate goal of the believers’ fighting is the removal of persecution and the freedom of devotion to Allah. It sets clear limits, ending hostility when the enemy stops. For the reader, the verse teaches the just and defensive nature of fighting in Islam, which aims at peace and freedom of worship rather than aggression. It also teaches that hostility is only against aggressors, and that when oppression ends, peace is restored. The verse reflects the balance of justice and mercy in Islam’s teachings on conflict. This verse also teaches the distinction between fighting for the right cause and fighting out of aggression. The goal of the believers’ fighting is the removal of persecution and the freedom to devote oneself to Allah, not the conquest of people or the pursuit of power. When this goal is achieved and persecution stops, hostility is to end. The verse therefore balances justice with mercy, allowing fighting only against aggressors and restoring peace when oppression ends. The reader is encouraged to understand the just and limited nature of conflict in Islam, and to strive for a world where people are free to worship Allah without persecution, in peace and devotion to their Lord.",
  "194": "“˹There will be retaliation in˺ a sacred month for ˹an offence in˺ a sacred month.”\n\nThis verse sets out the principle of fair retaliation in the context of the sacred months. \"˹There will be retaliation in˺ a sacred month for ˹an offence in˺ a sacred month, and all violations will bring about retaliation.\" During the sacred months, fighting was forbidden, but if an enemy attacked the believers even in a sacred month, the believers were permitted to defend themselves in the same manner. This establishes a principle of equal response: an offence is answered with a like response. The verse makes clear that all violations will bring about retaliation, meaning that the believers are not left helpless against aggression, even at times when fighting is normally forbidden.\n\nThe verse then gives the general rule: \"So, if anyone attacks you, retaliate in the same manner.\" The retaliation must be proportionate, matching the harm done rather than exceeding it. This is a principle of justice that limits retaliation to what is equal to the offence. The verse therefore balances the right to self-defence with the duty of fairness and restraint.\n\nThe verse ends with guidance: \"˹But˺ be mindful of Allah, and know that Allah is with those mindful ˹of Him˺.\" Even in the heat of retaliation, the believers are reminded to be mindful of Allah and not to exceed the limits. The assurance that Allah is with those who are mindful of Him gives the believer confidence that acting justly, even while defending oneself, is the path of Allah. For the reader, this verse teaches the justice of proportionate retaliation, the importance of self-defence, and the constant need for mindfulness of Allah, especially in conflict and in the use of force. The connection to the previous verses, which dealt with fighting and self-defence, is important. This verse continues the theme of justice in conflict, ensuring that the believers are not taken advantage of even during the sacred months. The principle of equal retaliation protects the believers while keeping them within the bounds of fairness. It also teaches that the sacred months, while respected, do not leave the believers defenceless against aggression. The reader is encouraged to understand that justice in Islam is balanced: the believers are commanded to be mindful of Allah, yet they are also given the right to defend themselves proportionately. This balance between mercy and justice runs throughout the teachings of the Quran on conflict.",
  "195": "“Spend in the cause of Allah.”\n\nThis verse commands spending in the cause of Allah. \"Spend in the cause of Allah and do not let your own hands throw you into destruction ˹by withholding˺.\" The command to spend is followed by a warning against withholding, which would throw a person into destruction. Scholars explain that this refers to withholding the spending that is needed, whether for jihad, charity, or the support of what is right. Failing to spend when spending is required can lead to ruin. The verse therefore ties generous spending in the cause of Allah to the preservation of oneself from destruction.\n\nThe verse then adds, \"And do good, for Allah certainly loves the good-doers.\" The command to do good is general, and it is connected to the promise that Allah loves those who do good. Doing good, including spending in the cause of Allah, is beloved to Allah. This gives the believer motivation to do good with sincerity.\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of spending in the cause of Allah and warns against the withholding that leads to destruction. It encourages doing good generally and reminds the believer that Allah loves those who do good. For the reader, the verse encourages generosity in the cause of Allah, an understanding that withholding can be self-destructive, and a commitment to doing good because it is beloved to Allah. The verse balances the call to sacrifice with the assurance of Allah’s love for those who do good. This verse was understood by scholars to connect with the preceding discussion of fighting in the cause of Allah. Spending in the cause of Allah includes supporting what is right, whether through charity or through the means that sustain the community in its struggles. The warning against destruction has been explained in two connected ways: some scholars said it refers to withholding the spending that is obligatory, which brings ruin, while others linked it to the abandonment of the obligations that protect the community. Either way, the verse teaches that generosity and doing good preserve the believer from destruction. The reader is encouraged to spend sincerely, to do good consistently, and to trust that Allah loves those who do good, knowing that both spending and good deeds are seen and rewarded by Allah.",
  "196": "“Complete the pilgrimage and minor pilgrimage for Allah.”\n\nThis verse commands the completion of the pilgrimage and the minor pilgrimage. \"Complete the pilgrimage and minor pilgrimage for Allah.\" The Hajj, the major pilgrimage, and the Umrah, the minor pilgrimage, are to be completed for Allah, meaning they are acts of worship dedicated to Him alone. The command to complete them shows that once a person begins, he should see them through.\n\nThe verse then deals with circumstances that prevent completion: \"But if prevented ˹from proceeding˺, then ˹offer˺ whatever sacrificial animals you can afford. And do not shave your heads until the sacrificial animal reaches its destination.\" If a pilgrim is prevented from proceeding, he is to offer a sacrificial animal and not complete certain rites until the offering reaches its destination. This provides a way for those who are blocked from completing the pilgrimage.\n\nThe verse then addresses those with a genuine need: \"But if any of you is ill or has a scalp ailment ˹requiring shaving˺, then compensate either by fasting, charity, or a sacrificial offering.\" Those who must shave their heads due to illness may compensate by fasting, charity, or an offering. The verse then gives guidance for the normal case: \"In times of peace, you may combine the pilgrimage and minor pilgrimage then make the sacrificial offering you can afford. Whoever cannot afford that ˹offering˺, let them fast three days during pilgrimage and seven after returning ˹home˺—completing ten. These offerings are for those who do not live near the Sacred House.\"\n\nThe verse ends, \"And be mindful of Allah, and know that Allah is severe in punishment.\" The verse teaches the rites of pilgrimage and the mercy of the provisions made for those who are prevented or unable. For the reader, it shows that Allah’s commands are accompanied by allowances that make worship possible in different circumstances.",
  "197": "“˹Commitment to˺ pilgrimage is made in appointed months.”\n\nThis verse speaks of the appointed months of pilgrimage. \"˹Commitment to˺ pilgrimage is made in appointed months.\" The Hajj is performed in specific months that are known and fixed. This gives structure to the pilgrimage and defines when it is performed. The verse then addresses those who commit to the pilgrimage: \"Whoever commits to ˹performing˺ pilgrimage, let them stay away from intimate relations, foul language, and arguments during pilgrimage.\" During the pilgrimage, the pilgrim must avoid intimate relations, foul speech, and quarrelling. These prohibitions keep the pilgrim focused on worship and guard the sacredness of the pilgrimage.\n\nThe verse then says, \"Whatever good you do, Allah ˹fully˺ knows of it.\" Every good deed done during the pilgrimage is known to Allah and will be rewarded. This encourages the pilgrim to do good during the pilgrimage. The verse then advises, \"Take ˹necessary˺ provisions ˹for the journey˺—surely the best provision is righteousness.\" The pilgrim is advised to take provisions for the journey, but the best provision of all is righteousness. This is a profound statement: while provisions for the body are needed, the provision of righteousness is what truly benefits the pilgrim.\n\nThe verse ends, \"And be mindful of Me, O people of reason!\" The verse calls people of reason to be mindful of Allah. For the reader, the verse teaches the sanctity and discipline of the pilgrimage, the importance of avoiding misconduct during it, and the deeper truth that the best provision is righteousness. The emphasis on avoiding intimate relations, foul language, and arguments during the pilgrimage shows how serious the rites are meant to be. The pilgrimage is a time of devotion in which the pilgrim focuses on Allah and restrains his desires and tongue. Arguments especially are warned against, because they can spoil the spirit of the pilgrimage and cause division. The verse’s statement that the best provision is righteousness is profound, because it reminds the pilgrim that while taking supplies for the body is sensible, the true provision that will help him on his journey to Allah is righteousness. The reader is encouraged to prepare for both the journey of the pilgrimage and the greater journey to the Hereafter by carrying the provision of righteousness.",
  "198": "“There is no blame on you for seeking the bounty of your Lord ˹during this journey˺.”\n\nThis verse addresses the practice of trade during the pilgrimage. \"There is no blame on you for seeking the bounty of your Lord ˹during this journey˺.\" Some pilgrims were unsure whether they could engage in trade during the pilgrimage, and this verse removes the blame from them for seeking the bounty of Allah, meaning lawful trade and provision. The pilgrimage does not require abandoning all worldly pursuits; seeking lawful provision is permitted. This shows the practical mercy of Islam, which allows people to combine worship with earning a living.\n\nThe verse then gives guidance about a rite of the pilgrimage: \"When you return from 'Arafât, praise Allah near the sacred place.\" After the pilgrims depart from Arafat, they are to praise Allah near the sacred place, known as Muzdalifah. They are to \"praise Him for having guided you, for surely before this ˹guidance˺ you were astray.\" The pilgrims praise Allah for guiding them to the pilgrimage and to true worship, acknowledging that before this guidance they were astray. This reminds them of the blessing of guidance.\n\nThis verse teaches that the pilgrimage is not meant to isolate people from lawful worldly pursuits, and that seeking provision is permissible. It also teaches the importance of praising Allah and being grateful for His guidance. For the reader, the verse encourages combining worship with lawful livelihood and reminds of the great blessing of being guided to the truth, which is a reason for praise and gratitude. The mention of Arafat and the praise near the sacred place points to one of the central rites of the pilgrimage. Standing at Arafat is the essence of the Hajj, and the verse instructs the pilgrims to move from there and praise Allah, acknowledging His guidance. The reminder that before this guidance they were astray is a powerful lesson in gratitude. It teaches the pilgrim to appreciate the great blessing of being guided to the worship of Allah and to the rites of the pilgrimage, which were a departure from the ignorance that preceded Islam. The reader is encouraged to combine lawful worldly pursuits with worship and to be deeply grateful for the guidance of Allah, which is the greatest blessing.",
  "199": "“Then go forth with the rest of the pilgrims.”\n\nThis verse gives guidance about the departure from Arafat. \"Then go forth with the rest of the pilgrims.\" The pilgrims are to move on with the rest of the pilgrims, in the manner that the majority proceed, rather than departing in a way that showed pride or disunity. Scholars explain that in the time of ignorance, some groups departed from Arafat in a particular manner out of pride, and this verse instructs the pilgrims to proceed together with everyone. The command teaches unity in worship and humility, doing as the rest of the pilgrims do.\n\nThe verse then says, \"And seek Allah's forgiveness. Surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\" After the rites, the pilgrims are to seek the forgiveness of Allah. This shows that the pilgrimage is a time of repentance and seeking mercy. The reminder that Allah is All-Forgiving and Most Merciful gives the pilgrim hope that his repentance will be accepted.\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of unity and humility in the pilgrimage, doing as the rest of the pilgrims do rather than acting out of pride. It also teaches the value of seeking Allah’s forgiveness, which is a central purpose of worship. For the reader, the verse encourages humility and unity in worship, and it reminds that Allah is forgiving and merciful to those who seek His forgiveness with sincerity. The pilgrimage is presented as a journey of repentance and devotion to Allah. The instruction to proceed with the rest of the pilgrims corrects a practice of pride and division. In the time of ignorance, some groups departed from Arafat in a way that showed their supposed superiority, and this verse directs the pilgrims to move on together, in unity and humility. This teaches that worship is a communal act in which believers share, without pride or division. Seeking the forgiveness of Allah after the rites completes the spirit of the pilgrimage, which is a journey of repentance and return to Allah. The reader is encouraged to be humble and united in worship, and to seek Allah’s forgiveness, knowing that Allah is All-Forgiving and Most Merciful to those who turn to Him sincerely.",
  "200": "“When you have fulfilled your sacred rites, praise Allah.”\n\nThis verse instructs the pilgrims to praise Allah after completing the rites. \"When you have fulfilled your sacred rites, praise Allah as you used to praise your forefathers ˹before Islam˺, or even more passionately.\" The pilgrims are to praise Allah after the rites, and the verse refers to the way people used to praise their forefathers before Islam, encouraging them to praise Allah even more passionately. The praise of Allah should be at least as intense as the praise people used to give to their ancestors.\n\nThe verse then contrasts two types of people: \"There are some who say, 'Our Lord! Grant us ˹Your bounties˺ in this world,' but they will have no share in the Hereafter.\" Some people, when they call upon Allah, only ask for worldly things, and they have no share in the Hereafter. Their focus is entirely on this world, and they neglect the Hereafter. This shows the danger of being so attached to the world that one forgets the eternal life.\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of praising Allah after completing acts of worship and of focusing on the Hereafter, not only on this world. It warns against the attitude of those who only seek worldly things and have no concern for the Hereafter. For the reader, the verse encourages balancing worldly concerns with the pursuit of the Hereafter, and it prepares the way for the prayer mentioned in the next verse, which asks for good in both this world and the Hereafter. Praise of Allah and hope for the Hereafter are central to true worship. The contrast between those who seek only this world and those who seek the Hereafter is a central theme. The verse warns that some people, when they pray, ask only for worldly things and neglect the Hereafter entirely, and such people have no share in the Hereafter. This is a serious warning against being consumed by the world. Praise of Allah after completing the rites is a fitting way to end the pilgrimage, directing the heart back to Allah. The reader is encouraged to reflect on what he asks for in his prayers and to ensure that his concern is not limited to this world, but that he also seeks the good of the Hereafter, which is the lasting and true reward.",
  "201": "“Yet there are others who say, ‘Our Lord! Grant us the good of this world and the Hereafter.’”\n\nThis verse presents the prayer of the believers, contrasting with the worldly people described in the previous verse. \"Yet there are others who say, 'Our Lord! Grant us the good of this world and the Hereafter, and protect us from the torment of the Fire.'\" The believers ask Allah for good in both this world and the Hereafter, and for protection from the punishment of the Fire. This prayer is comprehensive, seeking good in both the present life and the eternal life.\n\nThe prayer asks for good in this world, which includes lawful provisions, health, and all that benefits a person in this life. It also asks for good in the Hereafter, which includes the reward of Paradise and Allah’s pleasure. And it asks for protection from the torment of the Fire, which is the greatest concern of the believer. This prayer covers the needs of both worlds and the fear of punishment.\n\nThis verse teaches a balanced supplication, asking for good in both this world and the Hereafter. The believer is not to focus only on the world nor only on the Hereafter, but to seek good in both, while always seeking protection from the Fire. For the reader, the verse encourages making this comprehensive prayer, which is a summary of the believer’s hopes and fears. It also contrasts the attitude of the worldly person with that of the believer, who seeks good in both worlds and protection from punishment. This prayer is beloved and is encouraged in the Quran. This prayer is among the most comprehensive supplications in the Quran, and it is beloved to the believers. It combines the request for good in this world, which includes provision, health, and well-being, with the request for good in the Hereafter, which includes the reward of Paradise and the pleasure of Allah. It also includes the request for protection from the Fire, which shows the believer’s fear of punishment and his hope for mercy. This prayer teaches the balance of Islam: the believer does not reject the world but seeks good in it, while keeping the Hereafter as the ultimate goal. The reader is encouraged to make this prayer regularly, as it covers the needs of both worlds and expresses the true hopes and fears of the believer.",
  "202": "“It is they who will receive a ˹heavenly˺ reward for the good they have done.”\n\nThis verse gives the reward of those who made the prayer described in the previous verse. \"It is they who will receive a ˹heavenly˺ reward for the good they have done.\" The believers who ask for good in both this world and the Hereafter, and who seek protection from the Fire, will receive a heavenly reward for the good they have done. Their prayer is answered, and their good deeds are rewarded. This is the outcome of their faith and their supplication.\n\nThe verse then says, \"Surely Allah is swift in reckoning.\" Allah settles the accounts of His servants quickly and justly. His reckoning is swift, meaning that the reward and the judgement of deeds are carried out with perfect efficiency. This reassures the believer that nothing is delayed or lost.\n\nThis verse teaches that those who combine faith, good deeds, and the comprehensive prayer will be rewarded. It also teaches the swiftness and justice of Allah’s reckoning. For the reader, the verse encourages doing good and making the prayer for both worlds, confident that Allah will reward and reckon swiftly and justly. The reward of the believers is certain, and the reckoning of Allah is perfect. The swiftness of Allah’s reckoning is a source of both reassurance and warning. It reassures the believer that his good deeds will not be lost and that his reward will come, because Allah settles accounts quickly and justly. It also warns that the account is settled, so deeds cannot be delayed or evaded. The verse connects the prayer of the believers, the good they do, and the reward they receive, showing that supplication and action go together. The reader is encouraged to combine sincere prayer with righteous deeds, confident that Allah will reward and reckon swiftly and fairly. Nothing escapes the swift and perfect reckoning of Allah.",
  "203": "“And remember Allah during ˹these˺ appointed days.”\n\nThis verse instructs the remembrance of Allah during the appointed days of the pilgrimage. \"And remember Allah during ˹these˺ appointed days.\" The pilgrims are to remember Allah during the days appointed for this, which scholars explain include the days of Tashreeq following the pilgrimage. The verse then explains the flexibility in the departure: \"Whoever departs swiftly on the second day is not sinful, neither are those who stay behind ˹till the third—seeking additional reward˺, so long as they are mindful ˹of their Lord˺.\" Those who leave after the second day are not sinful, and those who stay until the third, seeking additional reward, are also not sinful, as long as they remain mindful of Allah. This shows the mercy of Allah in allowing flexibility.\n\nThe verse ends, \"And be mindful of Allah, and know that to Him you will ˹all˺ be gathered.\" The believers are reminded to be mindful of Allah and to know that they will all be gathered to Him. The gathering to Allah on the Day of Judgment gives meaning to the rites and reminders of the pilgrimage.\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of remembering Allah during the appointed days and the flexibility allowed in the rites, showing the mercy of Allah. It also teaches that the essential condition is mindfulness of Allah. For the reader, the verse encourages the remembrance of Allah and mindfulness in all acts of worship, and it reminds that everyone will be gathered to Allah. The pilgrimage and its reminders point to the greater gathering before Allah. The flexibility allowed in the departure, permitting the pilgrim to leave after the second day or stay until the third, shows the mercy of Allah in the rites. Both choices are permissible, provided the pilgrim remains mindful of Allah. This teaches that the essence of the pilgrimage is not rigid adherence to a single procedure but sincerity and mindfulness of Allah throughout. The reminder that everyone will be gathered to Allah connects the pilgrimage, which gathers the believers in one place, to the greater gathering on the Day of Judgment. The reader is encouraged to remember Allah during the appointed days and to be mindful of Him, knowing that all people will one day be gathered before Allah.",
  "204": "“There are some ˹hypocrites˺ who impress you with their views regarding worldly affairs.”\n\nThis verse introduces a description of the hypocrites. \"There are some ˹hypocrites˺ who impress you with their views regarding worldly affairs and openly call upon Allah to witness what is in their hearts, yet they are your worst adversaries.\" The verse describes people who are impressive in their speech about worldly matters, and who openly call upon Allah to witness what is in their hearts, claiming sincerity. Yet the verse reveals their true nature: they are the believers’ worst adversaries. Their impressive speech and their claims of sincerity hide their hostility.\n\nThe verse points to a central feature of hypocrisy: beautiful and convincing speech that hides the opposite of what is in the heart. The hypocrites can talk about worldly affairs impressively and can even invoke Allah as a witness, but their inner reality is enmity toward the believers. The verse warns the believers not to be deceived by appearances and by eloquent speech.\n\nThis verse teaches the danger of being deceived by impressive speech and outward claims. The hypocrites say the right things and even invoke Allah, yet they are the worst enemies. For the reader, the verse encourages discernment, judging people by their consistent actions rather than by their words. It also warns that hypocrisy can take the form of eloquent and persuasive speech that hides a hostile heart. The next verses will describe what these people do when they leave the presence of the believers. The verse reveals a key danger of hypocrisy: it is often accompanied by impressive and persuasive speech. The hypocrites described here can speak about worldly affairs in a way that impresses others, and they openly call upon Allah to witness what is in their hearts, pretending to be sincere. Yet the reality is that they are the believers’ worst adversaries. This teaches that eloquent speech is not proof of sincerity, and that invoking Allah as a witness does not make a claim true. The reader is encouraged to judge people by their consistent actions and to be wary of those whose beautiful words are contradicted by their deeds. The warning protects the believers from being deceived by the outward appearance of the hypocrites.",
  "205": "“And when they leave ˹you˺, they strive throughout the land to spread mischief in it.”\n\nThis verse reveals the true behaviour of the hypocrites when they are away from the believers. \"And when they leave ˹you˺, they strive throughout the land to spread mischief in it and destroy crops and cattle.\" When they leave the presence of the believers, they work to spread mischief throughout the land, destroying crops and livestock. Their actions when away from the believers contradict their impressive words when among them. They cause corruption and harm, even though they spoke so convincingly about good.\n\nThe verse then states, \"Allah does not like mischief.\" The corruption and mischief they spread is hated by Allah. The verse makes clear that such corruption, destroying the means of livelihood and harming the land, is detestable to Allah. This confirms that their behaviour is wrong and opposed to what Allah loves.\n\nThis verse teaches the reality of hypocrisy: one face among the believers, and a corrupting presence in the land. The harm they cause, destroying crops and cattle, harms people and society. For the reader, the verse encourages examining actions, not just words, and warns against those who speak well but act corruptly. It also teaches that Allah does not like mischief and corruption. The contrast between the words and the deeds of the hypocrites is stark, and the verse exposes it for the believers to recognize. The behaviour of the hypocrites when they are away from the believers reveals their true nature. Their corruption is not limited to words; they actively strive to spread mischief throughout the land, destroying crops and cattle. This destruction harms the very means of livelihood and the well-being of society. The verse states that Allah does not like mischief, making clear that such corruption is detestable to Him. This teaches that the harm of hypocrisy extends beyond deception, causing real damage to people and the land. The reader is encouraged to examine what people do when no one is watching, because true character is shown in action, and to avoid all forms of corruption and mischief, which Allah does not love.",
  "206": "“When it is said to them, ‘Fear Allah,’ pride carries them off to sin.”\n\nThis verse describes the reaction of the hypocrites when advised. \"When it is said to them, 'Fear Allah,' pride carries them off to sin.\" When they are advised to fear Allah, their pride takes hold of them and leads them to sin. Instead of accepting the advice, their arrogance makes them persist in wrongdoing. The advice to fear Allah, which would benefit them, only provokes their pride and drives them further into sin.\n\nThe verse then states their end: \"Hell will be their proper place. What an evil place to rest!\" Hell is described as their proper place, a fitting and deserved destination, and it is called an evil place to rest. Their pride and persistence in sin lead them to this terrible end.\n\nThis verse teaches the danger of pride and arrogance. When a person is advised to fear Allah and instead responds with pride, that pride leads to sin and ultimately to Hell. The verse warns against the arrogance that rejects sincere advice. For the reader, the verse encourages humility in accepting advice and fearing Allah, and it warns that pride carries a person into sin. The end of those who respond to good advice with pride is Hell, which is indeed an evil place to rest. Humility before Allah and acceptance of sincere counsel are essential to salvation. The response of the hypocrites to advice shows how pride can corrupt a person. When they are told to fear Allah, a sincere person would accept the advice and turn to Allah. Instead, their pride carries them off to sin, meaning their arrogance leads them deeper into wrongdoing. This is a tragic state, because sincere advice, which could have saved them, only fuels their pride and rebellion. The verse then states that Hell will be their proper place, an evil place to rest. This teaches the danger of pride and the importance of accepting sincere advice with humility. The reader is encouraged to respond to good advice with humility and to fear Allah, because pride that rejects advice leads to sin and to a terrible end.",
  "207": "“And there are those who would dedicate their lives to Allah’s pleasure.”\n\nThis verse presents the opposite of the hypocrites. \"And there are those who would dedicate their lives to Allah's pleasure.\" Unlike the hypocrites who seek to spread mischief, there are people who devote their lives to seeking the pleasure of Allah. They are willing to sacrifice, even their own lives, for the sake of Allah’s pleasure. This is the height of sincerity and devotion.\n\nThe verse then states, \"And Allah is Ever Gracious to ˹His˺ servants.\" Allah is gracious to His servants, rewarding their devotion and sincerity. Those who dedicate themselves to Allah’s pleasure are met with Allah’s grace. This gives hope and encouragement to those who strive sincerely.\n\nThis verse contrasts the sincere believer with the hypocrite. While the hypocrite spreads mischief, the sincere believer dedicates his life to Allah’s pleasure. For the reader, the verse encourages sincere devotion to Allah and a willingness to sacrifice for His pleasure. It also teaches that Allah is gracious to His servants, rewarding their sincerity. The contrast between the two types of people shows the two paths: the path of corruption and pride, and the path of devotion and sacrifice for Allah. The believer is called to the latter. The contrast between the hypocrites and the sincere believers is striking. The hypocrites spread mischief and respond to advice with pride, while the sincere believers dedicate their lives to seeking the pleasure of Allah. This dedication may even include sacrificing one’s life in the cause of Allah, as some scholars explain this verse in connection with those who gave everything for Allah’s pleasure. The verse assures that Allah is gracious to His servants, meaning that this sincere devotion is met with Allah’s grace and reward. The reader is encouraged to dedicate his life to seeking Allah’s pleasure, willing to sacrifice for it, and to trust that Allah is gracious to those who serve Him sincerely. True devotion is rewarded with the grace of Allah.",
  "208": "“O believers! Enter into Islam wholeheartedly.”\n\nThis verse calls the believers to complete and sincere Islam. \"O believers! Enter into Islam wholeheartedly.\" The believers are called to enter into Islam completely, embracing it fully in all its aspects, not partially. The word \"wholeheartedly\" points to a complete and sincere surrender to Allah, in which no part of the religion is left unaccepted and no loyalty competes with the worship of Allah.\n\nThe verse then warns, \"and do not follow Satan's footsteps. Surely he is your sworn enemy.\" The believers are warned not to follow the footsteps of Satan, meaning not to follow his suggestions and deceptions. Satan is described as a sworn enemy, and following his footsteps would lead the believer away from complete Islam. This warning is essential because Satan tries to make people accept Islam partially or to stray from its complete path.\n\nThis verse teaches that Islam is to be embraced completely, not selectively. A person enters Islam wholeheartedly, accepting all of it. It also teaches the importance of guarding against Satan, who is the sworn enemy of the believers. For the reader, the verse encourages complete and sincere commitment to Islam, without partiality or compromise, and vigilance against the whispers of Satan. True Islam is total surrender to Allah, and the believer is to enter into it fully. Entering into Islam wholeheartedly means embracing the entire religion, not merely parts of it. A person is called to accept Islam completely, in belief, practice, and loyalty, without holding back any portion or reserving any loyalty for other objects of devotion. This is the complete surrender that Islam requires. The warning against following Satan’s footsteps is essential, because Satan works to make people accept only part of the religion or to stray from its complete path. The reminder that Satan is a sworn enemy teaches vigilance. The reader is encouraged to examine his commitment to Islam, ensuring that it is complete and sincere, and to guard against the whispers of Satan that seek to lead him away from wholehearted submission to Allah.",
  "209": "“If you falter after receiving the clear proofs.”\n\nThis verse warns against turning away from the truth after it has been made clear. \"If you falter after receiving the clear proofs, then know that Allah is indeed Almighty, All-Wise.\" The believers are warned that if they falter, meaning if they slip or turn away from Islam after the clear proofs have come to them, then they should know that Allah is Almighty and All-Wise. The clear proofs refer to the guidance and evidence that Allah has revealed.\n\nThe warning is significant because it emphasizes the responsibility that comes with knowledge. Once the truth has been made clear, turning away from it is a serious matter. The reminder that Allah is Almighty and All-Wise reassures that Allah has the power to deal with those who turn away and that His wisdom is perfect. This both warns and comforts.\n\nThis verse teaches the seriousness of turning away from the truth after it has been made clear. It emphasizes personal responsibility, because faltering after receiving clear proofs is a deliberate choice. For the reader, the verse encourages steadfastness in faith and warns against slipping after knowing the truth. The reminder of Allah’s might and wisdom gives both warning and confidence. The believer who knows the truth is called to hold firmly to it, knowing that Allah is Almighty and All-Wise. The warning to those who falter after receiving clear proofs emphasizes the responsibility that comes with knowledge. Once the truth has been made clear, there is no excuse for turning away. The verse reminds that Allah is Almighty, able to deal with those who turn away, and All-Wise, knowing what is best. This combination of power and wisdom reassures the believer that Allah’s justice is perfect. The reader is encouraged to hold firmly to the faith after knowing the truth, and to be careful not to slip. The clear proofs that Allah has given make the choice a real one, and the believer is called to remain steadfast, trusting in the might and wisdom of Allah.",
  "210": "“Are they waiting for Allah ˹Himself˺ to come to them in the shade of clouds?”\n\nThis verse challenges those who delay accepting the truth. \"Are they waiting for Allah ˹Himself˺ to come to them in the shade of clouds, along with the angels?\" The verse asks whether those who reject the truth are waiting for a dramatic sign, such as Allah coming to them in the shade of clouds with the angels. Such an expectation is unreasonable, because the matter would be settled at once, leaving no opportunity for faith and repentance.\n\nThe verse then states, \"˹If He did˺, then the matter would be settled ˹at once˺.\" If such an overwhelming sign were to come, the matter of judgement would be decided immediately, and there would be no time for belief or for turning back. This shows the foolishness of waiting for such a sign, because it would bring the final judgement rather than guidance.\n\nThe verse ends, \"And to Allah ˹all˺ matters will be returned ˹for judgment˺.\" All matters ultimately return to Allah for judgment. The verse teaches that the truth has been made clear, and waiting for an overwhelming sign is both unreasonable and futile. For the reader, the verse encourages accepting the truth that has already been made clear, rather than waiting for signs that would only bring the final judgement. It also reminds that all matters are returned to Allah, who will judge with perfect justice. This verse challenges the expectation of an overwhelming sign as a reason to delay belief. Waiting for Allah to come with the angels in the shade of clouds is an unreasonable expectation, because such an event would bring the final judgement, not an opportunity to believe. The verse teaches that the truth has already been made clear through the messengers and the scriptures, and that waiting for a dramatic sign is both foolish and futile. The reminder that all matters return to Allah for judgement shows that the final decision belongs to Allah. The reader is encouraged to accept the truth that has been revealed and not to delay, because the signs of guidance are already clear and waiting for a greater sign would only bring the judgement.",
  "211": "“Ask the Children of Israel how many clear signs We have given them.”\n\nThis verse points to the many signs given to the Children of Israel. \"Ask the Children of Israel how many clear signs We have given them.\" The Children of Israel were given numerous clear signs, including miracles and divine guidance, throughout their history. The verse invites them to acknowledge these signs. It also implies a rebuke, because despite these signs, some of them still turned away.\n\nThe verse then warns, \"And whoever trades Allah's favour—after receiving it—˹for disbelief˺ should know that Allah is indeed severe in punishment.\" Whoever exchanges the favour of Allah, after having received it, for disbelief, should know that Allah is severe in punishment. This warns against ingratitude and against turning away from Allah’s favour. The favour of Allah is great, and trading it for disbelief is a grave wrong.\n\nThis verse teaches that Allah’s signs and favours are given to guide people, and that rejecting them is a serious matter. The warning against trading Allah’s favour for disbelief emphasizes the severity of ingratitude and rejection. For the reader, the verse encourages recognizing and being grateful for the favours of Allah, and it warns against turning away from them. It also shows that those who receive clear signs and still reject the truth have no excuse, and they should fear the severity of Allah’s punishment. The many signs given to the Children of Israel are evidence of Allah’s care and of His proof against them. Despite these signs, some of them turned away, which shows how deeply ingratitude can blind people. The warning against trading Allah’s favour for disbelief emphasizes that rejecting the favour of Allah after receiving it is a grave wrong with severe consequences. This teaches the importance of gratitude and faithfulness in response to Allah’s favours. The reader is encouraged to recognize the signs and favours of Allah in his own life, to be grateful for them, and to fear the severity of Allah’s punishment for those who trade His favour for disbelief. Gratitude is the proper response to divine favour.",
  "212": "“The life of this world has been made appealing to the disbelievers.”\n\nThis verse speaks of the appeal of the world to the disbelievers. \"The life of this world has been made appealing to the disbelievers, and they mock the believers.\" The world is made attractive to the disbelievers, who focus on it and are impressed by its temporary pleasures. They mock the believers for their faith and for what the believers value. The disbelievers, absorbed in the world, look down on the believers who prioritise the Hereafter.\n\nThe verse then gives the reality of the Hereafter: \"Those who are mindful ˹of Allah˺ will rank above them on the Day of Judgment.\" On the Day of Judgment, the believers who are mindful of Allah will rank above the disbelievers, despite the world having been made appealing to the disbelievers in this life. The true and lasting superiority belongs to the mindful believers.\n\nThe verse ends, \"And Allah provides for whoever He wills without limit.\" Allah provides for whom He wills without limit, showing that provision in this world is not a measure of true worth. The verse teaches that the appeal of the world is temporary and deceptive, and that the true measure of rank is on the Day of Judgment. For the reader, the verse encourages not being deceived by the world and not being troubled by the mockery of the disbelievers. True rank is with Allah, and the mindful believers will be raised above the disbelievers on the Day of Judgment. The appeal of the world to the disbelievers and their mockery of the believers is a test that believers must face. The disbelievers, absorbed in the world, may seem to be in a better position, and they mock the believers for their faith. But the verse reveals the truth: on the Day of Judgment, the mindful believers will rank above them. This corrects the false impression created by the world. The statement that Allah provides for whom He wills without limit shows that provision in this world is not a measure of worth. The reader is encouraged not to be deceived by the appeal of the world, not to be troubled by the mockery of the disbelievers, and to remember that true and lasting rank is determined on the Day of Judgment, where the mindful believers will be raised above all.",
  "213": "“Humanity had once been one community ˹of believers before they lost faith˺.”\n\nThis verse explains the origin of human communities and the need for messengers. \"Humanity had once been one community ˹of believers before they lost faith˺.\" Humanity was originally one community, united in the worship of Allah. But then they differed and lost faith. \"Then Allah raised prophets as deliverers of good news and as warners, and revealed to them the Scriptures in truth to judge among people regarding their disputes.\" Allah sent prophets to bring good news and warning, and revealed scriptures to judge between people in their disputes.\n\nThe verse then notes the cause of dispute: \"And no one disputed the Scriptures except the very people who received them after clear proofs had come to them—out of jealousy.\" The disputes arose among those who received the scriptures, after clear proofs had come to them, and the cause was jealousy. They knew the truth but differed out of envy and rivalry.\n\nThe verse ends, \"Then Allah, by His grace, has guided the believers to the truth regarding those disputes. And Allah guides whoever He wills to the Straight Path.\" Despite the disputes, Allah guided the believers to the truth by His grace. This verse teaches the unity of humanity’s original faith, the role of prophets and scriptures, and the danger of jealousy that causes disputes. For the reader, the verse encourages holding to the truth that Allah guides His believers to, and it teaches that jealousy and rivalry lead people away from the truth. This verse explains the wisdom behind the sending of prophets and scriptures. Humanity was originally one community, united in the worship of Allah, but differences arose, especially out of jealousy after clear proofs had come. The prophets were sent to bring good news and warning and to judge between people in their disputes, and the scriptures were revealed for this purpose. The verse notes that the disputes came from the very people who received the scriptures, out of jealousy, showing that the problem was not the guidance but the hearts of the people. Despite this, Allah guided the believers to the truth by His grace. The reader is encouraged to hold to the truth that Allah has guided His believers to, and to beware of jealousy, which is a destructive force that causes disputes and turns people away from the truth.",
  "214": "“Do you think you will be admitted into Paradise without being tested like those before you?”\n\nThis verse corrects a false expectation about entering Paradise. \"Do you think you will be admitted into Paradise without being tested like those before you?\" The believers might have imagined that they would enter Paradise without facing trials, but the verse challenges this. Entry into Paradise is not given without testing. The verse then describes the tests that earlier communities faced: \"They were afflicted with suffering and adversity and were so ˹violently˺ shaken that ˹even˺ the Messenger and the believers with him cried out, 'When will Allah's help come?'\" The earlier believers faced severe suffering and were shaken so deeply that even the Messenger and the believers cried out for Allah’s help. This shows the intensity of the trials.\n\nThe verse then gives the reassurance: \"Indeed, Allah's help is ˹always˺ near.\" Despite the severity of the trials, Allah’s help is near. The believers are assured that help comes after the trial, and that the testing is a means of purification and growth. The cry \"When will Allah's help come?\" was itself a sign of the depth of the trial, yet the answer is that help is near.\n\nThis verse teaches that trials are a part of the path to Paradise and that no one is admitted without testing. The trials purify the believers and raise their ranks. For the reader, the verse encourages patience and trust during hardship, knowing that Allah’s help is near and that testing is a necessary part of the journey to Paradise. The verse gives hope that however severe the trial, the help of Allah is close. The example of the previous communities is given to comfort and instruct the believers. If the earlier believers were tested severely, then the believers should expect their own trials and should not be surprised by them. The testing is part of Allah’s justice and mercy: it purifies the believers, distinguishes the sincere from the weak, and prepares them for the reward. The cry of the Messenger and the believers for help, at the peak of their trial, is a human response that Allah understands and answers. The reader is encouraged to see trials not as a sign of Allah’s abandonment but as a sign of the path of faith, and to be certain that Allah’s help is near even when it seems delayed.",
  "215": "“They ask you ˹O Prophet in˺ what ˹way˺ they should donate.”\n\nThis verse answers a question about charity. \"They ask you ˹O Prophet in˺ what ˹way˺ they should donate.\" The believers asked the Prophet about how and where to spend their donations. The answer is given: \"Say, 'Whatever donations you give are for parents, relatives, orphans, the poor, and ˹needy˺ travellers.\"\" The donations should go to those in need: parents, relatives, orphans, the poor, and needy travellers. These are the categories of people who deserve charity, showing the priorities of giving.\n\nThe verse ends, \"Whatever good you do is certainly well known to Allah.\" Every good deed is known to Allah, and nothing is lost. This encourages the believers to give sincerely, knowing that Allah sees and rewards all their good deeds.\n\nThis verse teaches the proper recipients of charity and the importance of giving to those in need. It begins with parents and relatives, then the vulnerable such as orphans and the poor, and includes needy travellers. This shows the order of responsibility in charity, starting with those closest to the giver. For the reader, the verse encourages giving to those in need and trusting that Allah knows all good deeds and will reward them. Charity is an act of worship that benefits both the giver and the receiver, and its value is known to Allah. The order of the recipients of charity in this verse reflects the priorities of giving. The parents come first, then relatives, then orphans, the poor, and needy travellers. This shows that charity begins with those closest to the giver and then extends to the wider community. It also teaches that giving is not limited to one category but includes the various needs of people. The assurance that all good deeds are known to Allah encourages sincerity and removes any concern that one’s giving might be unnoticed or wasted. The reader is encouraged to give according to these priorities, starting with family and extending to the needy, and to trust that Allah knows and rewards every good deed.",
  "216": "“Fighting has been made obligatory upon you ˹believers˺, though you dislike it.”\n\nThis verse makes clear that fighting is obligatory, while acknowledging human reluctance. \"Fighting has been made obligatory upon you ˹believers˺, though you dislike it.\" The command to fight for the cause of Allah is made obligatory, even though people naturally dislike it because of its hardship and danger. The verse then explains the wisdom: \"Perhaps you dislike something which is good for you and like something which is bad for you.\" A person may dislike something that is actually good for him, and may like something that is actually harmful. This is because human judgement is limited and does not always see what is truly best.\n\nThe verse ends, \"Allah knows and you do not know.\" Allah’s knowledge is complete, while human knowledge is limited. This is the reason to trust Allah’s commands even when their wisdom is not immediately clear. What Allah commands is good, even if a person dislikes it, and what He forbids is harmful, even if a person likes it.\n\nThis verse teaches the obligation of fighting in the cause of Allah and the importance of trusting Allah’s wisdom over one’s own feelings. It explains that human likes and dislikes are not reliable guides to what is truly good or bad. For the reader, the verse encourages obedience to Allah’s commands even when they are difficult, and trust in His knowledge. It also teaches that Allah knows what is best, and that the believer should submit to His guidance rather than relying on limited human judgement. This verse addresses a natural human feeling and corrects it with the truth of Allah’s wisdom. People often judge things by their immediate pleasure or pain, but what feels hard may be beneficial, and what feels easy may be harmful. This is why Allah, who knows everything, commands what is truly good even when people dislike it. The verse therefore calls the believer to trust Allah’s judgement over his own feelings. The reader is encouraged to obey the commands of Allah, including those that are difficult, with the confidence that Allah knows what is best. This is a foundational lesson in submission and trust, applying not only to fighting but to all of Allah’s commands.",
  "217": "“They ask you ˹O Prophet˺ about fighting in the sacred months.”\n\nThis verse answers a question about fighting in the sacred months. \"They ask you ˹O Prophet˺ about fighting in the sacred months. Say, 'Fighting during these months is a great sin.\"\" Fighting during the sacred months is a great sin, because these months are sacred and fighting in them is forbidden. The verse then compares this to graver sins: \"But hindering ˹others˺ from the Path of Allah, rejecting Him, and expelling the worshippers from the Sacred Mosque is ˹a˺ greater ˹sin˺ in the sight of Allah.\" Hindering people from the path of Allah, rejecting Allah, and expelling worshippers from the Sacred Mosque are greater sins than fighting in the sacred months. The verse then repeats the principle: \"For persecution is far worse than killing.\" The persecution that the believers faced was worse than fighting.\n\nThe verse then reveals the aim of the enemy: \"And they will not stop fighting you until they turn you away from your faith—if they can.\" The enemies will not stop until they turn the believers away from their faith, if they can. Then the verse warns of the consequence of renouncing faith: \"And whoever among you renounces their own faith and dies a disbeliever, their deeds will become void in this life and in the Hereafter. It is they who will be the residents of the Fire. They will be there forever.\" Renouncing faith voids a person’s deeds and leads to the Fire.\n\nThis verse teaches the gravity of fighting in the sacred months, the even greater gravity of hindering people from the path of Allah, and the seriousness of renouncing faith. For the reader, the verse encourages steadfastness in faith and understanding the priorities of sins. This verse also has a historical context in the early Muslim community, when questions arose about fighting in the sacred months. The answer makes clear that fighting in the sacred months is a great sin, yet it is still lesser than the sins of hindering people from the path of Allah, rejecting Him, and expelling worshippers from the Sacred Mosque, which is what the enemy had done. The verse therefore puts the believers’ conduct in context: their response to persecution was just, while the persecution they faced was far worse. The warning about renouncing faith shows the gravity of apostasy and its severe consequences. The reader is encouraged to understand the priorities of sins and to remain steadfast in faith, knowing that the enemies’ goal is to turn people away from Allah.",
  "218": "“Surely those who have believed, emigrated, and struggled in the Way of Allah.”\n\nThis verse speaks of those who believed, emigrated, and struggled. \"Surely those who have believed, emigrated, and struggled in the Way of Allah—they can hope for Allah's mercy.\" The verse describes three actions: believing, emigrating, and struggling in the way of Allah. The emigrants, who left their homes for the sake of their faith, and who struggled in the cause of Allah, can hope for Allah’s mercy. Their sacrifice and effort are met with hope.\n\nThe verse ends, \"And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\" Allah is All-Forgiving and Most Merciful, and His mercy is vast for those who believe and struggle in His way. This gives hope to the believers who have made sacrifices for their faith.\n\nThis verse teaches the value of faith, emigration, and struggle in the way of Allah, and the hope of Allah’s mercy for those who do these. The emigrants who left their homes and the believers who struggled are promised hope of mercy. For the reader, the verse encourages sincerity in faith and a willingness to sacrifice for Allah, trusting in His mercy. It also shows that Allah’s forgiveness and mercy are available to those who believe and strive in His cause, giving hope and motivation. The actions of believing, emigrating, and struggling are connected, showing that true faith leads to sacrifice and effort. The emigrants who left their homes for the sake of their faith demonstrated their sincerity by giving up their worldly comforts. Their struggle in the way of Allah completed their commitment. The hope of Allah’s mercy is the reward for this sincerity and sacrifice. The verse teaches that faith is not passive but leads to action, even at the cost of hardship. The reader is encouraged to believe sincerely, to be willing to sacrifice for the faith, and to struggle in the way of Allah, trusting in His mercy. Allah’s forgiveness and mercy are vast for those who strive in His cause.",
  "219": "“They ask you ˹O Prophet˺ about intoxicants and gambling.”\n\nThis verse answers a question about intoxicants and gambling. \"They ask you ˹O Prophet˺ about intoxicants and gambling. Say, 'There is great evil in both, as well as some benefit for people—but the evil outweighs the benefit.\"\" The verse acknowledges that there is some benefit in intoxicants and gambling, such as the profit of trade or the enjoyment of games, but it emphasizes that the evil in them is greater than the benefit. This is the principle on which they are forbidden: the harm far outweighs any good. This shows the wisdom of the prohibition.\n\nThe verse then answers another question about charity: \"They ˹also˺ ask you what they should donate. Say, 'Whatever you can spare.'\" The believers are told to donate what they can spare, giving from what is extra beyond their needs. This shows moderation in charity, giving what is surplus.\n\nThe verse ends, \"This is how Allah makes His revelations clear to you ˹believers˺, so perhaps you may reflect.\" The revelations are made clear so that the believers may reflect and understand. For the reader, the verse teaches the wisdom behind the prohibition of intoxicants and gambling, which is that their harm outweighs their benefit, and it teaches moderation in giving charity. The verse encourages reflection on the guidance of Allah, understanding the wisdom behind His commands. The wisdom in the prohibition of intoxicants and gambling lies in the comparison between their benefit and their harm. Though there is some benefit, the harm outweighs it, and so they are forbidden. This shows that the Quran does not forbid things without reason but bases its rulings on the balance of benefit and harm. The gradual approach of the Quran in this matter also reflects its wisdom, as the prohibition was established in stages. The guidance about giving \"whatever you can spare\" shows moderation in charity, giving from what is surplus without harming one’s needs. The reader is encouraged to reflect on the wisdom of Allah’s rulings and to follow them with understanding.",
  "220": "“upon this world and the Hereafter.”\n\nThis verse continues the previous verse and turns to the question of orphans. \"upon this world and the Hereafter. And they ask you concerning orphans. Say, 'Improving their condition is best.\"\" The believers are told that improving the condition of orphans is best. Instead of avoiding any contact with orphans’ wealth, improving their situation is the right course. \"And if you partner with them, they are bonded with you ˹in faith˺.\" If the believers partner with orphans, they are treated as fellow believers and brothers in faith. The verse then states, \"And Allah knows who intends harm and who intends good.\" Allah knows the intentions of people, whether they mean harm or good toward orphans.\n\nThe verse continues, \"Had Allah willed, He could have made it difficult for you. Surely Allah is Almighty, All-Wise.\" If Allah had willed, He could have made the matter difficult, but He made it easy. This shows the mercy of Allah in the guidance He gives. The reminder that Allah is Almighty and All-Wise reassures that His guidance is wise.\n\nThis verse teaches the care that should be shown to orphans and the importance of improving their condition. It teaches that Allah knows the intentions of people regarding orphans, and it shows the mercy of Allah in making the matter easy. For the reader, the verse encourages kindness and fairness toward orphans, sincerity in intentions, and trust in the wisdom of Allah’s guidance. Caring for orphans is a high virtue, and Allah is aware of how people treat them. The care of orphans is a recurring theme in the Quran, and this verse shows the balanced approach Islam takes. The believers are not to avoid orphans or their wealth out of fear, nor to harm them, but to improve their condition. Partnering with orphans is lawful, treating them as fellow believers, while being mindful of their interests. The statement that Allah knows who intends harm and who intends good places a heavy responsibility on those who deal with orphans’ affairs. The reader is encouraged to act with sincerity and fairness toward orphans, to seek to improve their condition, and to remember that Allah knows all intentions. Caring for the vulnerable is a high virtue in Islam.",
  "221": "“Do not marry polytheistic women until they believe.”\n\nThis verse gives guidance about marriage to polytheists. \"Do not marry polytheistic women until they believe; for a believing slave-woman is better than a free polytheist, even though she may look pleasant to you.\" The believers are told not to marry polytheistic women until they believe, and that a believing slave-woman is better than a free polytheist, even if the polytheist appears more attractive. This emphasizes that faith is more important than worldly appearance or status. Similarly, \"And do not marry your women to polytheistic men until they believe, for a believing slave-man is better than a free polytheist, even though he may look pleasant to you.\" The same principle applies to marrying women to polytheistic men.\n\nThe verse then gives the reason: \"They invite ˹you˺ to the Fire while Allah invites ˹you˺ to Paradise and forgiveness by His grace.\" The polytheists call to the Fire, while Allah calls to Paradise and forgiveness. The choice is between two invitations, and the believer is to choose the invitation of Allah. The verse ends, \"He makes His revelations clear to the people so perhaps they will be mindful.\" The revelations are made clear so that people may be mindful.\n\nThis verse teaches that faith is the primary consideration in marriage, more important than appearance, wealth, or status. It emphasizes that marriage with a polytheist risks being drawn to the Fire, while marriage based on faith aligns with the invitation to Paradise. For the reader, the verse encourages prioritizing faith in choosing a spouse and understanding the wisdom behind the guidance. The believer is called to choose the invitation of Allah over the invitation to the Fire. This verse emphasizes that faith is the foundation of marriage, which is more important than appearance, wealth, or social status. A believing partner, even one who is poor or of lower social standing, is better than a polytheist who is attractive and wealthy. This is because marriage is not only a worldly union but also a spiritual partnership, and being joined to a polytheist risks drawing one away from the faith. The verse presents the choice clearly: the polytheist invites to the Fire, while Allah invites to Paradise and forgiveness. The reader is encouraged to prioritize faith in choosing a spouse and to trust in the wisdom of this guidance, which protects the religion and the family.",
  "222": "“They ask you ˹O Prophet˺ about menstruation.”\n\nThis verse answers a question about menstruation and marital intimacy. \"They ask you ˹O Prophet˺ about menstruation. Say, 'Beware of its harm! So keep away, and do not have intercourse with your wives during their monthly cycles until they are purified.\"\" The believers are told to beware of the harm of menstruation, to keep away from intercourse during the monthly cycle, and not to have intercourse until the wife is purified. This guidance regulates marital intimacy with concern for health and purity.\n\nThe verse then says, \"When they purify themselves, then you may approach them in the manner specified by Allah.\" Once the wife is purified, the husband may approach her in the manner specified by Allah, meaning lawful marital relations. The verse ends, \"Surely Allah loves those who always turn to Him in repentance and those who purify themselves.\" Allah loves those who turn to Him in repentance and those who purify themselves, both in terms of physical purity and spiritual purity.\n\nThis verse teaches the guidance regarding menstruation and marital intimacy, regulating it with concern for purity and health. It also teaches the love of Allah for those who repent and purify themselves. For the reader, the verse encourages following the guidance of Allah in intimate matters and striving for both physical and spiritual purity. The love of Allah for those who repent and purify themselves is a great encouragement to seek purity in all aspects of life. This verse shows that Islam provides guidance for all aspects of life, including intimate matters, with concern for health and purity. The guidance about menstruation regulates marital intimacy in a way that respects both the physical well-being of the wife and the spiritual framework of the marriage. The mention of Allah loving those who turn to Him in repentance and those who purify themselves connects physical purity with spiritual purity, showing that the two are related. The reader is encouraged to follow the guidance of Allah in all matters and to seek purity in both body and soul, trusting that Allah loves those who repent and purify themselves. This guidance reflects the comprehensive nature of Islam.",
  "223": "“Your wives are like farmland for you.”\n\nThis verse uses a beautiful and clear image to describe the marital relationship. \"Your wives are like farmland for you, so approach them ˹consensually˺ as you please.\" The wives are like farmland, from which a person reaps a harvest, and the believer is told to approach them consensually as he pleases. This describes lawful marital intimacy, with the image of farmland emphasizing the natural and lawful nature of the relationship.\n\nThe verse then gives an important advice: \"And send forth something good for yourselves.\" This refers to doing good deeds, such as the remembrance of Allah and acts of righteousness, which are sent ahead as provision for the Hereafter. The marital relationship is an opportunity for good, not only for pleasure. The verse then says, \"Be mindful of Allah, and know that you will meet Him.\" The believer is to be mindful of Allah in all matters, knowing that he will meet Him. \"And give good news to the believers.\" Good news is given to the believers.\n\nThis verse teaches the lawful nature of marital intimacy and encourages the believer to combine it with the doing of good deeds. The image of farmland shows the natural and lawful character of the relationship, while the advice to send forth good reminds the believer of the Hereafter. For the reader, the verse encourages lawful enjoyment within marriage combined with mindfulness of Allah and the pursuit of good. The reminder that the believer will meet Allah shapes all behaviour, including within the marital relationship. The image of wives as farmland describes the lawful and natural character of marital intimacy, while the advice to \"send forth something good\" elevates the marital relationship by connecting it to the Hereafter. The believer is not to view marriage only as a means of pleasure but as an opportunity for good deeds and mindfulness of Allah. The reminder that the believer will meet Allah shapes conduct within marriage, encouraging righteousness and consciousness of accountability. The verse ends with good news to the believers, promising reward for their mindfulness. The reader is encouraged to combine lawful enjoyment within marriage with the pursuit of good, always mindful of meeting Allah.",
  "224": "“Do not use Allah’s Name in your oaths as an excuse.”\n\nThis verse warns against the misuse of oaths. \"Do not use Allah's Name in your oaths as an excuse for not doing good, not guarding against evil, or not making peace between people.\" The believers are warned not to use oaths made in Allah’s name as an excuse to avoid doing good, to avoid guarding against evil, or to avoid making peace between people. Some people might swear an oath not to do something good, and then use that oath to justify not doing it. The verse forbids this misuse of Allah’s name.\n\nThe verse ends, \"And Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\" Allah hears all oaths and knows all intentions. The reminder of Allah’s hearing and knowledge warns against abusing oaths and using them for wrong purposes.\n\nThis verse teaches the proper use of oaths and forbids using them as an excuse for avoiding good or persisting in harm. An oath made in Allah’s name should not prevent a person from doing good, guarding against evil, or making peace. For the reader, the verse encourages prioritizing good, righteousness, and peace, and not using oaths to avoid these. It also teaches that Allah is All-Hearing and All-Knowing, aware of oaths and intentions. When a good deed is required, an oath should not stand in the way. This verse warns against a subtle misuse of oaths. A person might swear not to do a good deed, and then hide behind the oath to avoid doing it. The verse forbids using Allah’s name in this way, because good deeds, guarding against evil, and making peace are duties that an oath should not obstruct. This teaches that the higher purposes of religion, such as goodness and reconciliation, take precedence over a careless oath. The reminder that Allah is All-Hearing and All-Knowing warns that such misuse of oaths is seen and known. The reader is encouraged to use oaths responsibly and never to let them become a barrier to doing good or making peace.",
  "225": "“Allah will not hold you accountable for unintentional oaths.”\n\nThis verse distinguishes between intentional and unintentional oaths. \"Allah will not hold you accountable for unintentional oaths, but for what you intended in your hearts.\" Allah does not hold people accountable for oaths they made unintentionally, such as words spoken without a firm intention to swear. But He holds people accountable for what they intended in their hearts, meaning oaths made with deliberate intention. The accountability is based on intention.\n\nThe verse ends, \"And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Forbearing.\" Allah is All-Forgiving and Most Forbearing, forgiving of unintentional slips and forbearing with His servants. This shows the mercy of Allah in dealing with human weakness.\n\nThis verse teaches that accountability for oaths is based on intention. Unintentional oaths, spoken without a firm intention, are not held against a person, while deliberate oaths are. This reflects the mercy of Allah, who does not burden people for slips but holds them accountable for their real intentions. For the reader, the verse encourages sincerity in speech and intention, and it reassures that Allah is forgiving and forbearing. The principle that actions are judged by intention is central to Islamic understanding. This verse shows the mercy of Allah in distinguishing between intentional and unintentional oaths. People speak many words, some without firm intention, and Allah does not hold them accountable for what was not intended. But deliberate oaths, made with a clear intention, are matters of accountability. This reflects the principle that actions are judged by their intentions, a central teaching of Islam. The mention of Allah being All-Forgiving and Most Forbearing reassures the believer that Allah is merciful and patient with human weakness. The reader is encouraged to be sincere in speech and intention, while trusting in the mercy and forbearance of Allah. This understanding of intention also applies beyond oaths to the whole of worship, where the sincerity of the heart is what gives deeds their value. An act done with a sincere intention is rewarded, while the same act done without sincerity is of no benefit. The mercy of Allah in not punishing unintentional oaths is part of His wider mercy toward the weaknesses of His servants, who often speak or act without full deliberation. This reassures the believer that Allah deals with people according to their intentions and forgives their slips, while still holding them accountable for what they truly meant. It is a reminder to purify one’s intentions and to seek Allah’s forgiveness for what one does not fully intend.",
  "226": "“Those who swear not to have intercourse with their wives must wait for four months.”\n\nThis verse deals with a specific marital situation. \"Those who swear not to have intercourse with their wives must wait for four months.\" This refers to a vow some men made to abstain from their wives, and the verse limits this waiting period to four months. The verse then says, \"If they change their mind, then Allah is certainly All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\" If the husband changes his mind and returns to his wife within the four months, he may do so, and Allah is All-Forgiving and Most Merciful.\n\nThis verse regulates the practice of swearing to abstain from one’s wife, limiting it to four months and providing a way back. The mention of Allah’s forgiveness and mercy shows the mercy of Allah for those who return to their wives and abandon the harmful practice.\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of not harming one’s wife through prolonged abstention, limiting it to four months. It also shows the mercy of Allah in providing a way back for those who change their minds. For the reader, the verse encourages fairness and kindness in marriage and understanding that Allah is forgiving and merciful to those who turn back. The regulation protects the rights of wives and encourages reconciliation within marriage. This verse protects the rights of wives by limiting the practice of swearing to abstain from them. The four-month limit prevents a husband from leaving his wife in a state of uncertainty and harm indefinitely. If he changes his mind and returns within the period, reconciliation is possible and Allah is forgiving. This shows the care of Islam for the family and for the dignity of the wife. The reader is encouraged to understand that the family is protected by clear rulings that balance the rights of both spouses, and that Allah’s mercy is available for those who return to fairness and reconcile. The regulation reflects the justice and mercy of Islamic family law.",
  "227": "“But if they settle on divorce, then Allah is indeed All-Hearing, All-Knowing.”\n\nThis verse follows the previous verse about those who swear to abstain from their wives. \"But if they settle on divorce, then Allah is indeed All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\" If, after the four months, the husband settles on divorce instead of returning to his wife, then the divorce takes effect, and Allah is All-Hearing and All-Knowing. Allah hears what is said and knows the intentions behind it.\n\nThe mention of Allah’s hearing and knowledge emphasizes that divorce is a serious matter that is known to Allah. The intentions and actions of the husband, whether returning to his wife or settling on divorce, are heard and known by Allah. This serves as a reminder of accountability in such decisions.\n\nThis verse teaches that when a decision of divorce is settled, it takes effect, and that Allah is aware of all such decisions. The reminder of Allah’s hearing and knowledge brings accountability to the serious matter of divorce. For the reader, the verse encourages taking marriage and its dissolution seriously, knowing that Allah is aware of all intentions and actions. It also completes the guidance on the four-month wait, showing the two possible outcomes: return to the wife or divorce. This verse completes the ruling about the four-month wait by addressing the second possible outcome, which is divorce. If the husband settles on divorce, it takes effect, and Allah, who is All-Hearing and All-Knowing, is aware of the decision. The mention of Allah’s hearing and knowledge brings accountability to the matter, reminding the husband that divorce is not a casual act but a serious decision known to Allah. The reader is encouraged to take marriage and its dissolution with the seriousness they deserve, knowing that Allah hears and knows all. The guidance protects the family and reminds everyone of their accountability before Allah.",
  "228": "“Divorced women must wait three monthly cycles ˹before they can re-marry˺.”\n\nThis verse establishes the waiting period for divorced women. \"Divorced women must wait three monthly cycles ˹before they can re-marry˺.\" A divorced woman must observe a waiting period of three monthly cycles before she can remarry. This waiting period serves several purposes, including giving time to determine if she is pregnant and allowing for possible reconciliation. The verse then says, \"It is not lawful for them to conceal what Allah has created in their wombs, if they ˹truly˺ believe in Allah and the Last Day.\" A woman must not conceal a pregnancy or what is in her womb, as this involves honesty and rights.\n\nThe verse then states, \"And their husbands reserve the right to take them back within that period if they desire reconciliation.\" During the waiting period, the husband may take his wife back if he desires reconciliation. Then the verse states a general principle: \"Women have rights similar to those of men equitably, although men have a degree ˹of responsibility˺ above them.\" Women have rights similar to the rights they owe, and men have a degree of responsibility above them, referring to the responsibility of maintenance and guardianship. The verse ends, \"And Allah is Almighty, All-Wise.\"\n\nThis verse teaches the waiting period for divorced women, the prohibition of concealing a pregnancy, the right of the husband to reconcile, and the equitable balance of rights and responsibilities between spouses. For the reader, the verse encourages fairness and honesty in matters of divorce and family, and it teaches the balance of rights in marriage. The waiting period for divorced women protects several interests: it clarifies any pregnancy, gives time for reconciliation, and preserves the dignity of the woman. The prohibition on concealing what is in the womb emphasizes honesty in matters that affect rights and lineage. The statement that women have rights similar to the rights they owe, with men having a degree of responsibility above them, sets out the balance of justice in marriage: equal rights and responsibilities, with men bearing the duty of maintenance and guardianship. The reader is encouraged to uphold fairness and honesty in family matters and to understand the balanced nature of Islamic family law.",
  "229": "“Divorce may be retracted twice.”\n\nThis verse regulates the practice of divorce. \"Divorce may be retracted twice, then the husband must retain ˹his wife˺ with honour or separate ˹from her˺ with grace.\" A husband may retract his divorce twice, meaning he may divorce and take his wife back twice. After that, he must either retain his wife with honour or separate from her with grace. This prevents a man from endlessly toying with his wife through divorce and reconciliation.\n\nThe verse then says, \"It is not lawful for husbands to take back anything of the dowry given to their wives, unless the couple fears not being able to keep within the limits of Allah.\" A husband may not take back the dowry he gave his wife, except in the case where the couple fears they cannot keep within the limits of Allah. \"So if you fear they will not be able to keep within the limits of Allah, there is no blame if the wife compensates the husband to obtain divorce.\" In such a case, the wife may compensate the husband to obtain a divorce. The verse then says, \"These are the limits set by Allah, so do not transgress them. And whoever transgresses the limits of Allah, they are the ˹true˺ wrongdoers.\"\n\nThis verse teaches the regulation of divorce, the honour in retaining a wife or separating with grace, and the limits set by Allah. For the reader, the verse encourages respecting the limits of Allah in marriage and divorce and treating spouses with honour and fairness. Transgressing the limits of Allah makes one a wrongdoer. The regulation of divorce by allowing only two retractions prevents a husband from endlessly toying with his wife through repeated divorce and reconciliation. After the two retractions, the husband must either keep his wife with honour or release her with grace, leaving no room for games. The protection of the dowry ensures that a husband cannot take back what he gave, except in the exceptional case where the couple cannot keep the limits of Allah. The reader is encouraged to treat marriage and divorce with honour and seriousness and to respect the limits that Allah has set, which protect the dignity of both spouses.",
  "230": "“So if a husband divorces his wife ˹three times˺.”\n\nThis verse deals with divorce pronounced three times. \"So if a husband divorces his wife ˹three times˺, then it is not lawful for him to remarry her until after she has married another man and then is divorced.\" After a third divorce, the husband may not remarry his wife until she has married another man and then been divorced from him. This makes the third divorce final and protects the woman from being toyed with. \"Then it is permissible for them to reunite, as long as they feel they are able to maintain the limits of Allah.\" If the woman marries another man and then is divorced, the original couple may reunite if they believe they can maintain the limits of Allah.\n\nThe verse then says, \"These are the limits set by Allah, which He makes clear for people of knowledge.\" The limits of Allah are made clear for people of knowledge, who understand and respect them.\n\nThis verse teaches the finality of the third divorce and the conditions for remarriage, which protect the dignity of the woman and the seriousness of marriage. For the reader, the verse encourages taking marriage and divorce seriously and respecting the limits that Allah has set. The wisdom behind these rulings is to prevent harm and to protect the sanctity of marriage. The third divorce is final, and the condition that the woman must marry another man before the original couple can reunite serves as a strong deterrent against hasty and repeated divorces. It protects the dignity of the woman and the seriousness of marriage by making the third divorce a decisive break. If a genuine reconciliation occurs through a new marriage and its ending, the couple may reunite if they can maintain the limits of Allah. The reader is encouraged to take the commitment of marriage seriously and to understand that these limits, which Allah makes clear for people of knowledge, are designed to protect the family from harm and abuse.",
  "231": "“When you divorce women and they have ˹almost˺ reached the end of their waiting period.”\n\nThis verse gives guidance about the end of the waiting period. \"When you divorce women and they have ˹almost˺ reached the end of their waiting period, either retain them honourably or let them go honourably.\" As the waiting period comes to an end, the husband must either retain his wife honourably or let her go honourably. The verse then warns, \"But do not retain them ˹only˺ to harm them ˹or˺ to take advantage ˹of them˺. Whoever does that surely wrongs his own soul.\" Retaining a wife only to harm her is a great injustice, and the one who does it wrongs his own soul.\n\nThe verse then says, \"Do not take Allah's revelations lightly. Remember Allah's favours upon you as well as the Book and wisdom He has sent down for your guidance.\" The believers are told not to take Allah’s revelations lightly and to remember His favours and the guidance He sent. The verse ends, \"Be mindful of Allah, and know that Allah has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of all things.\"\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of treating divorced women with honour and fairness, and forbids retaining them only to harm them. It also teaches gratitude for the favours of Allah and mindfulness of His knowledge. For the reader, the verse encourages justice and kindness in family matters and warns against the injustice of harming a spouse. This verse emphasizes honour in dealing with divorce. Whether the husband retains his wife or lets her go, it must be done honourably, without harm or taking advantage. Retaining a wife only to harm her is a great injustice that wrongs the husband’s own soul. The reminder not to take Allah’s revelations lightly and to remember His favours and guidance directs the believer to treat these serious matters with the gravity they deserve. The reader is encouraged to act with justice and kindness in family matters, to avoid harming spouses, and to be mindful that Allah has perfect knowledge of all things, which brings accountability to every decision.",
  "232": "“When you have divorced women and they have reached the end of their waiting period.”\n\nThis verse gives further guidance about divorce and remarriage. \"When you have divorced women and they have reached the end of their waiting period, do not prevent them from re-marrying their ex-husbands if they come to an honourable agreement.\" Once the waiting period ends, the family should not prevent a divorced woman from remarrying her ex-husband if they come to an honourable agreement. This protects the woman’s freedom to remarry and prevents interference that harms her.\n\nThe verse then says, \"This is enjoined on whoever has faith in Allah and the Last Day. This is purer and more dignifying for you. Allah knows and you do not know.\" This guidance is enjoined on those who have faith in Allah and the Last Day, and following it is purer and more dignifying. The verse ends, \"Allah knows and you do not know,\" reminding that Allah’s knowledge is superior.\n\nThis verse teaches that divorced women should not be prevented from remarrying their ex-husbands if they agree honourably, and that this is part of pure and dignified conduct. For the reader, the verse encourages respecting the rights and choices of divorced women and trusting in the wisdom of Allah’s guidance. It teaches the importance of faith in shaping conduct toward others, especially in matters of family and remarriage. This verse protects the freedom of a divorced woman to remarry her ex-husband after the waiting period, preventing relatives from obstructing a reconciliation that the couple desires. Such interference harms the woman and the family. The verse links this guidance to faith in Allah and the Last Day, showing that treating people fairly is part of true faith. The statement that this is purer and more dignifying teaches that following Allah’s guidance elevates moral conduct. The reminder that Allah knows and people do not know encourages humility and trust in divine wisdom. The reader is encouraged to respect the rights and choices of divorced women and to act with purity and dignity.",
  "233": "“˹Divorced˺ mothers will breastfeed their offspring for two whole years.”\n\nThis verse gives guidance about the care of children after divorce. \"˹Divorced˺ mothers will breastfeed their offspring for two whole years, for those who wish to complete the nursing ˹of their child˺.\" Mothers are to breastfeed their children for two years, for those who wish to complete the nursing. This establishes the period of nursing and the right of the child to this care. \"The child's father will provide reasonable maintenance and clothing for the mother ˹during that period˺.\" The father is responsible for providing for the mother during this period. The verse then says, \"No one will be charged with more than they can bear. No mother or father should be made to suffer for their child.\" No one is to be burdened beyond their capacity, and neither parent should be made to suffer because of the child.\n\nThe verse then addresses other details: \"The ˹father's˺ heirs are under the same obligation. But if both sides decide—after mutual consultation and consent—to wean a child, then there is no blame on them. And if you decide to have your children nursed by a wet-nurse, it is permissible as long as you pay fairly.\" The heirs of the father share the obligation, and both parents may agree to wean the child earlier, and a wet-nurse may be used if paid fairly. The verse ends, \"Be mindful of Allah, and know that Allah is All-Seeing of what you do.\"\n\nThis verse teaches the care and provision for children after divorce, including nursing, maintenance, and the rights of the child. For the reader, the verse encourages justice and kindness in caring for children and cooperation between parents, mindful of Allah’s sight over all actions. This verse sets out the rights of the child and the responsibilities of the parents after divorce. The mother nurses the child, and the father provides maintenance, with no one burdened beyond their capacity. The protection against making either parent suffer because of the child shows the care for both parents and the child. The provisions for weaning by mutual consent and for using a wet-nurse with fair payment show the flexibility and practicality of the guidance. The reader is encouraged to put the welfare of the child first, to cooperate fairly between parents, and to be mindful that Allah is All-Seeing of what they do, which brings accountability to their conduct toward their children.",
  "234": "“As for those of you who die and leave widows behind, let them observe a waiting period of four months and ten days.”\n\nThis verse establishes the waiting period for widows. \"As for those of you who die and leave widows behind, let them observe a waiting period of four months and ten days.\" A widow is to observe a waiting period of four months and ten days before she may remarry. This period serves to honor the deceased, to determine any pregnancy, and to give the widow time to grieve. It also protects the rights of the unborn child if she is pregnant.\n\nThe verse then says, \"When they have reached the end of this period, then you are not accountable for what they decide for themselves in a reasonable manner.\" Once the waiting period ends, the family and others are not accountable for what the widow decides for herself in a reasonable manner, meaning she is free to remarry or make her own decisions. The verse ends, \"And Allah is All-Aware of what you do.\" Allah is aware of all actions.\n\nThis verse teaches the waiting period for widows and the freedom of the widow to make her own decisions after it ends. For the reader, the verse encourages respecting the dignity and rights of widows and understanding the wisdom behind the waiting period, which honors the deceased and protects the family. The awareness of Allah over all actions brings accountability and encourages fairness. The waiting period of four months and ten days is longer than the three cycles required of a divorced woman, because it honours the bond of marriage that was ended by death and gives the widow time to mourn and to organise her affairs. If the widow is pregnant, the waiting period continues until she gives birth, as clarified in other verses. The verse also protects the widow’s freedom: once the period ends, she is free to make her own decisions, and no one is accountable for what she reasonably chooses. The reader is encouraged to understand the wisdom behind these rulings, which balance the honour of the deceased, the rights of the widow, and the protection of the family, all under the awareness of Allah, who is All-Aware of every action.",
  "235": "“There is no blame on you for subtly showing interest in ˹divorced or widowed˺ women.”\n\nThis verse addresses the matter of showing interest in marriage during the waiting period. \"There is no blame on you for subtly showing interest in ˹divorced or widowed˺ women or for hiding ˹the intention˺ in your hearts. Allah knows that you are considering them ˹for marriage˺.\" It is permissible to subtly show interest in a woman who is in her waiting period, or to hide such intention in one’s heart, because Allah knows that people naturally consider marriage. This allows for the possibility of future marriage without violating the waiting period.\n\nThe verse then says, \"But do not make a secret commitment with them—you can only show interest in them appropriately. Do not commit to the bond of marriage until the waiting period expires.\" A secret commitment or an actual marriage contract cannot be made until the waiting period expires. The verse then advises, \"Know that Allah is aware of what is in your hearts, so beware of Him. And know that Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Forbearing.\"\n\nThis verse teaches the balance between permitting subtle interest and forbidding actual commitment during the waiting period. It encourages purity of intention and fear of Allah. For the reader, the verse teaches respect for the waiting period and the rights of the woman, while recognizing human intentions and Allah’s awareness of them. Allah’s forgiveness and forbearance give hope for those who sincerely guard their intentions. This verse shows the practical wisdom of Islam in dealing with natural human feelings while upholding the limits of the waiting period. It is natural for a person to consider marriage to a woman who is in her waiting period, and the verse permits subtle expression of interest or keeping such intention hidden, because Allah knows the hearts. But it forbids any secret commitment or actual marriage until the period expires, protecting the integrity of the waiting period and the rights of all parties. The reminder that Allah is aware of what is in the hearts encourages purity of intention. The reader is encouraged to guard the limits of Allah while recognising human nature, and to trust in His forgiveness and forbearance.",
  "236": "“There is no blame if you divorce women before the marriage is consummated or the dowry is settled.”\n\nThis verse addresses divorce in the case where the marriage has not been consummated or the dowry settled. \"There is no blame if you divorce women before the marriage is consummated or the dowry is settled. But give them a ˹suitable˺ compensation.\" If a marriage is ended before consummation and before the dowry is settled, there is no blame, but the husband is to give the woman a suitable compensation. This compensation is a kindness and a recognition of her dignity.\n\nThe verse then says, \"the rich according to his means and the poor according to his. A reasonable compensation is an obligation on the good-doers.\" The compensation is to be given according to the means of the husband, whether rich or poor, and a reasonable compensation is an obligation on those who do good. This ensures that the compensation is fair and within the capacity of the husband.\n\nThis verse teaches the kindness of giving compensation to a divorced woman, even when no dowry was settled, and it ties this to the character of the good-doer. For the reader, the verse encourages generosity and fairness in ending a marriage and treating the woman with dignity and kindness. The compensation reflects the Islamic principle of treating others with fairness and honour, even in difficult circumstances. The compensation given to a woman divorced before consummation and before a dowry was settled is a recognition of her dignity and a kindness from the husband. The verse makes it an obligation on those who do good, showing that generosity in this matter is a mark of righteous character. The compensation is to be according to the means of the husband, rich or poor, ensuring that it is fair and within his capacity. The reader is encouraged to understand that even when a marriage ends without full rights being established, the woman is to be treated with respect and generosity, and that such kindness reflects the mindfulness of Allah. Fair treatment of others, especially in vulnerable positions, is a core value of Islam.",
  "237": "“And if you divorce them before consummating the marriage but after deciding on a dowry, pay half of the dowry.”\n\nThis verse addresses divorce after the dowry has been decided but before consummation. \"And if you divorce them before consummating the marriage but after deciding on a dowry, pay half of the dowry, unless the wife graciously waives it or the husband graciously pays in full.\" In this case, the husband is to pay half of the agreed dowry, unless the wife graciously waives her right to it or the husband graciously pays the full amount. This gives room for generosity from either side.\n\nThe verse then says, \"Graciousness is closer to righteousness. And do not forget kindness among yourselves. Surely Allah is All-Seeing of what you do.\" Graciousness is closer to righteousness, and the verse encourages kindness between the parties. The reminder that Allah is All-Seeing encourages sincerity and fairness.\n\nThis verse teaches the fair division of the dowry in the case of divorce before consummation, while encouraging generosity from either side. For the reader, the verse teaches fairness in financial matters and encourages graciousness and kindness, which are closer to righteousness. It also reminds that Allah sees all actions, which brings accountability and encourages treating others with kindness and dignity. The ruling that half the dowry is due when divorce occurs after the dowry was settled but before consummation shows the fairness of Islamic law, which balances the rights of both parties. The verse also encourages generosity, allowing the wife to waive her right graciously or the husband to pay in full, and it describes graciousness as closer to righteousness. This shows that while the law sets a just minimum, the believer is encouraged to go beyond it in kindness. The reminder that Allah is All-Seeing encourages sincerity and fairness. The reader is encouraged to treat others with graciousness in financial and family matters, knowing that generosity is closer to righteousness and that Allah sees all deeds.",
  "238": "“Observe the ˹five obligatory˺ prayers—especially the middle prayer—and stand in true devotion to Allah.”\n\nThis verse commands the observance of the prayers. \"Observe the ˹five obligatory˺ prayers—especially the middle prayer.\" The believers are commanded to maintain the five obligatory prayers, and special emphasis is placed on the middle prayer, which scholars explain is the afternoon prayer, the Asr. This emphasis shows its importance. The verse then says, \"and stand in true devotion to Allah,\" commanding the believer to stand in prayer with sincere devotion, humility, and focus.\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of the daily prayers and the special emphasis on the middle prayer. It also teaches that prayer must be accompanied by true devotion, not just outward form. For the reader, the verse encourages maintaining the obligatory prayers, paying special attention to the middle prayer, and performing them with sincerity and devotion. Prayer is a pillar of faith and a means of connecting with Allah, and it is to be observed with care and true devotion. The command to observe the prayers, with special emphasis on the middle prayer, shows the central place of prayer in Islam. The middle prayer, which scholars identify as the afternoon prayer, is singled out for special care, though all the obligatory prayers are to be maintained. The command to stand in true devotion emphasizes that prayer is not merely a physical act but requires sincerity, humility, and concentration of the heart upon Allah. This verse teaches the importance of preserving the prayers in their times and performing them with genuine devotion. The reader is encouraged to guard his prayers, to pay special attention to the middle prayer, and to stand before Allah with a humble and devoted heart, for prayer is the foundation of the believer’s connection with his Lord. The emphasis on the middle prayer has been understood by scholars as a special encouragement to guard this particular prayer, which can easily be neglected because it falls in the middle of the day when people are occupied. Guarding it demonstrates a high level of devotion. The command to stand in true devotion teaches that the prayer must be free from distraction and filled with consciousness of Allah. The reader is encouraged to approach prayer with humility and focus, recognising that its value lies in the sincerity of the heart as much as in the outward performance.",
  "239": "“If you are in danger, pray on foot or while riding.”\n\nThis verse provides guidance for praying in danger. \"If you are in danger, pray on foot or while riding.\" When the believer is in danger, such as in battle or in fear of an enemy, he may pray while on foot or while riding, in whatever way he is able, rather than in the normal manner. This shows the flexibility of prayer, which is never abandoned even in the most difficult circumstances, but is adapted to the situation.\n\nThe verse then says, \"But when you are safe, ˹take time to˺ remember Allah for teaching you what you did not know.\" When the believer is safe, he is to remember Allah and thank Him for teaching what he did not know. This includes the guidance of Islam and the knowledge of how to worship. The verse connects the flexibility of prayer in danger with gratitude for the guidance Allah has given.\n\nThis verse teaches the flexibility of prayer and its importance, as it is maintained even in danger. It also teaches gratitude for the guidance of Allah. For the reader, the verse encourages never abandoning prayer, adapting it to circumstances when necessary, and being grateful for the knowledge and guidance that Allah has taught. The remembrance of Allah and thankfulness for His teaching are central to the believer’s worship. The guidance to pray even in danger shows that prayer is never abandoned, regardless of circumstances. When a believer cannot pray in the normal manner due to danger, he prays while on foot or riding, in whatever way he is able. This teaches the importance of maintaining the remembrance of Allah in all situations and the flexibility of the religion, which accommodates the needs of the worshipper. When safety returns, the believer is to remember Allah and thank Him for the guidance He taught, which includes the knowledge of worship and the religion. The reader is encouraged never to neglect prayer, to adapt it to circumstances when necessary, and to be grateful for the teaching and guidance of Allah, which enables him to worship correctly.",
  "240": "“Those of you who die leaving widows should bequeath for them a year’s maintenance without forcing them out.”\n\nThis verse addresses the bequest for widows. \"Those of you who die leaving widows should bequeath for them a year's maintenance without forcing them out.\" A man who dies leaving a widow is to bequeath a year’s maintenance for her and to allow her to remain in the home without being forced out. This provision cares for the widow after the death of her husband. The verse then says, \"But if they choose to leave, you are not accountable for what they reasonably decide for themselves.\" If the widow chooses to leave, the heirs are not accountable for what she reasonably decides. The verse ends, \"And Allah is Almighty, All-Wise.\"\n\nThis verse teaches the provision of maintenance and housing for widows, showing care for their welfare after the death of their husbands. For the reader, the verse encourages kindness and fairness toward widows and understanding of the responsibility to provide for them. The reminder that Allah is Almighty and All-Wise reassures that the guidance is just and wise, protecting the rights of the widow and the family. This verse provides for the maintenance and housing of widows, showing the care of Islam for the welfare of women who have lost their husbands. The bequest of a year’s maintenance and the right to remain in the home without being forced out protect the widow from hardship and give her time to reorganise her life. The verse also respects her freedom, allowing her to leave if she reasonably chooses. This provision reflects the dignity Islam gives to widows and the responsibility of the family and heirs toward them. The reader is encouraged to understand the wisdom of these provisions, which balance the rights of the widow with the obligations of the heirs, and to act with kindness and fairness toward widows, mindful of Allah, who is Almighty and All-Wise.",
  "241": "“Reasonable provisions must be made for divorced women.”\n\nThis verse commands provisions for divorced women. \"Reasonable provisions must be made for divorced women—a duty on those mindful ˹of Allah˺.\" Divorced women are to be given reasonable provisions, and this is described as a duty on those who are mindful of Allah. The provision is a duty that reflects mindfulness of Allah and care for the woman who has been divorced.\n\nThis verse teaches the obligation of providing for divorced women, and it ties this duty to mindfulness of Allah. The provision is to be reasonable, meaning fair and appropriate. For the reader, the verse encourages fulfilling the duty of providing for divorced women with fairness and kindness, understanding that this is part of mindfulness of Allah. Caring for those who are in a vulnerable position, such as divorced women, is a duty that reflects the character of the God-fearing. The verse reinforces the theme of treating spouses fairly and generously throughout the family laws of this chapter. This verse reiterates the duty of providing for divorced women, making it an obligation on those who are mindful of Allah. The provision is to be reasonable, meaning fair and appropriate to the circumstances. By tying this duty to mindfulness of Allah, the verse shows that caring for the divorced woman is not merely a legal formality but a moral and religious obligation that reflects one’s awareness of Allah. The reader is encouraged to fulfil this duty with generosity and fairness, understanding that how one treats a divorced woman is a measure of one’s mindfulness of Allah. The repeated emphasis on provision for divorced women throughout this chapter shows the importance Islam places on treating them with dignity and fairness after the end of a marriage. The emphasis on reasonableness ensures that the provision is just and appropriate, neither excessive nor deficient, and it reflects the dignity of the woman. This duty is described as being on those who are mindful of Allah, showing that the treatment of divorced women is a matter of piety, not merely of law. A person who is truly mindful of Allah will fulfil this duty willingly and generously. The reader is encouraged to see the care of divorced women as an act of worship and to fulfil it with fairness, recognising that such conduct reflects one’s relationship with Allah and one’s awareness of His commands.",
  "242": "“This is how Allah makes His revelations clear to you.”\n\nThis verse explains the purpose of the guidance given in the preceding verses. \"This is how Allah makes His revelations clear to you, so perhaps you will understand.\" Allah makes His revelations clear so that the believers may understand them. The clarity of the guidance is a mercy, enabling people to know and follow what Allah commands.\n\nThe verse teaches that the Quran’s guidance is made clear for the purpose of understanding. The rulings and guidance, such as those about divorce, provision, and the rights of women, are explained so that people may understand and act upon them. For the reader, the verse encourages seeking understanding of the Quran’s guidance and appreciating the clarity with which Allah has explained it. Understanding leads to correct practice. The verse also shows that the guidance of the Quran is not meant to be mysterious but is made clear for people to grasp and apply in their lives, which is a mercy from Allah. The clarity of Allah’s revelations is a recurring theme, and this verse stresses that the guidance is made clear so that people may understand. The rulings about divorce, provision, and the rights of women are explained in detail so that the believers can know what is required and act accordingly. Understanding is the goal, because understanding leads to correct practice. The reader is encouraged to seek understanding of the Quran’s guidance, to appreciate the clarity with which it has been revealed, and to apply it in life. The clarity of the guidance removes confusion and excuse, and it calls the believer to learn and to act. This verse serves as a reminder that the Quran is a book of clear guidance meant to be understood and followed. The verse is a reminder that the Quran’s guidance is not obscure or hidden but is made clear for people to comprehend. This clarity is part of Allah’s mercy, because it enables the believers to know their duties and to act on them. Where understanding is lacking, the believer is encouraged to seek knowledge and reflection. The reader is encouraged to approach the Quran with the intention of understanding, to appreciate the clarity with which its rulings are explained, and to apply them in daily life. Understanding the guidance of Allah leads to correct practice and to a life ordered by His commands, which is the purpose of the clarity of the revelations.",
  "243": "“Have you ˹O Prophet˺ not seen those who fled their homes in the thousands for fear of death?”\n\nThis verse begins a story about those who fled their homes. \"Have you ˹O Prophet˺ not seen those who fled their homes in the thousands for fear of death?\" The verse refers to a group of people who left their homes in great numbers out of fear of death, fleeing from what they dreaded. Their flight was a test, because they sought to escape death, but death is not escaped by running from it.\n\nThe verse then says, \"Allah said to them, 'Die!' then He gave them life.\" When they fled, Allah caused them to die, then brought them back to life. This showed them that death cannot be escaped by fleeing, and that life and death are in the hands of Allah. Their resurrection was a sign of Allah’s power over life and death.\n\nThe verse ends, \"Surely Allah is ever Bountiful to humanity, but most people are ungrateful.\" Allah is bountiful to humanity, but most people are ungrateful. This verse teaches that death cannot be escaped by fleeing, and that life and death are in Allah’s hands. For the reader, the verse encourages trusting in Allah and understanding that no one can escape what Allah has decreed. It also teaches gratitude for the bounties of Allah and warns against ingratitude. The story of those who fled their homes in fear of death teaches a profound lesson about the decree of Allah. They tried to escape death, but death came to them anyway, showing that no one can flee from what Allah has decreed. When Allah caused them to die and then brought them back to life, He demonstrated His absolute power over life and death. Their resurrection was a sign for them and for others. The verse concludes that Allah is ever Bountiful to humanity, but most people are ungrateful, showing that despite His signs and bounties, many people fail to recognise His favours. The reader is encouraged to trust in Allah’s decree, to understand that life and death are in His hands, and to be grateful for His bounties rather than ungrateful.",
  "244": "“Fight in the cause of Allah.”\n\nThis verse commands fighting in the cause of Allah. \"Fight in the cause of Allah, and know that Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\" The believers are commanded to fight in the cause of Allah, meaning to strive and struggle in the path of Allah, including defending the faith and the community. The verse ends with the reminder that Allah is All-Hearing and All-Knowing, aware of all words and deeds.\n\nThis verse reinforces the command to fight in the cause of Allah, which appears several times in this chapter. The reminder that Allah is All-Hearing and All-Knowing brings accountability and encourages sincerity in the struggle. For the reader, the verse encourages striving in the cause of Allah with sincerity, knowing that Allah hears and sees all. The command to fight is in the context of defending the faith and responding to aggression, and it is accompanied by the awareness that Allah knows the intentions and actions of all people. This short verse reinforces the command to fight in the cause of Allah, which appears several times in this chapter. The command is accompanied by the reminder that Allah is All-Hearing and All-Knowing, bringing accountability to the believer’s words and deeds. Fighting in the cause of Allah is not an act of aggression but a struggle in the path of Allah, including defending the faith and the community. The awareness that Allah hears and knows all encourages sincerity in this struggle. The reader is encouraged to strive in the cause of Allah with sincerity and to remember that Allah is aware of all intentions and actions. The reminder of Allah’s hearing and knowledge gives both motivation and accountability to the believer’s efforts in the path of Allah. The brevity of the verse does not reduce its weight, as it reinforces a central duty of the believers. The command is followed immediately by the reminder of Allah’s hearing and knowledge, which encourages the believer to fight with sincerity, knowing that Allah sees the true purpose of the struggle. This verse also connects to the surrounding verses about spending and the cause of Allah, showing that both wealth and effort are to be devoted to the path of Allah. The reader is encouraged to understand that striving in the cause of Allah, whether through spending or fighting, is a means of drawing near to Allah, and that Allah, who hears and knows all, will reward those who strive sincerely.",
  "245": "“Who will lend to Allah a good loan?”\n\nThis verse uses a striking image of lending to Allah. \"Who will lend to Allah a good loan which Allah will multiply many times over?\" Spending in the cause of Allah is described as lending Allah a good loan, which Allah will multiply many times over. This shows that whatever a person spends in the cause of Allah is not lost but is rewarded abundantly, as if repaid with great increase. The image of lending emphasizes that the person will be repaid.\n\nThe verse then says, \"It is Allah ˹alone˺ who decreases and increases ˹wealth˺.\" Allah is the One who controls wealth, increasing and decreasing it as He wills. This reminds that wealth is in Allah’s hands, and that spending in His cause does not truly decrease it. The verse ends, \"And to Him you will ˹all˺ be returned.\" Everyone will return to Allah.\n\nThis verse teaches the reward of spending in the cause of Allah, using the image of a good loan that is multiplied. For the reader, the verse encourages generous spending in the cause of Allah, trusting that it is rewarded abundantly, and recognizing that wealth is controlled by Allah. The return to Allah gives the believer the ultimate perspective on wealth and spending. Spending in the cause of Allah is not a loss but an investment with great reward. The image of lending Allah a good loan is a powerful encouragement to spend in His cause. It presents spending as a loan that will be repaid with multiplication, showing that nothing spent in the cause of Allah is lost. The increase is many times over, emphasising the abundance of the reward. The verse also reminds that it is Allah who decreases and increases wealth, so spending in His cause does not truly diminish one’s wealth but is part of how Allah increases it. The return to Allah gives the believer the ultimate perspective: wealth is temporary, and the reward of spending is eternal. The reader is encouraged to spend generously in the cause of Allah, trusting in His promise of multiplication and recognising that true wealth is in what one sends ahead for the Hereafter.",
  "246": "“Have you not seen those chiefs of the Children of Israel after Moses?”\n\nThis verse begins the story of the chiefs of the Children of Israel after Moses. \"Have you not seen those chiefs of the Children of Israel after Moses? They said to one of their prophets, 'Appoint for us a king, ˹and˺ we will fight in the cause of Allah.'\" The chiefs asked a prophet of theirs to appoint a king for them, promising to fight in the cause of Allah under him. The prophet responded, \"Are you not going to cower if ordered to fight?\" He questioned whether they would really fight when ordered, anticipating their weakness. They replied, \"How could we refuse to fight in the cause of Allah, while we were driven out of our homes and ˹separated from˺ our children?\" They claimed that they would fight because they had been driven out and separated from their children.\n\nBut the verse reveals their reality: \"But when they were ordered to fight, they fled, except for a few of them.\" When the order to fight actually came, they fled, except for a few. Their promise was not kept. The verse ends, \"And Allah has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of the wrongdoers.\" Allah knows the wrongdoers perfectly.\n\nThis verse teaches the difference between claiming willingness and actually fighting, and it shows the weakness of those who promise but flee. For the reader, the verse encourages sincerity and steadfastness in fulfilling one’s commitments, and it warns against promising what one will not deliver. It also teaches that Allah knows the true state of people, including the wrongdoers. The story of the chiefs of the Children of Israel exposes the gap between promising and doing. They asked for a king so they could fight, claiming they would fight because they had been driven out and separated from their children, yet when the order to fight came, they fled except for a few. Their claim of willingness was not matched by their action. The prophet’s questioning of whether they would cower when ordered to fight proved to be justified. The verse teaches the importance of sincerity and steadfastness in fulfilling commitments, and it warns against promising what one will not deliver. It also shows that Allah has perfect knowledge of the wrongdoers, knowing the true state of those who claim strength but flee in action. The reader is encouraged to be truthful in one’s commitments and to follow through on what one promises in the cause of Allah.",
  "247": "“Their prophet told them, ‘Allah has appointed Saul to be your king.’”\n\nThis verse continues the story of the Children of Israel. \"Their prophet told them, 'Allah has appointed Saul to be your king.'\" The prophet informed them that Allah had chosen Saul to be their king. Saul, also known as Talut, was chosen by Allah. The people protested, \"How can he be our king when some of us are more deserving of kingship than he, and he has not been blessed with vast riches?\" They objected because they thought others were more deserving and because Saul was not wealthy. Their objection was based on worldly criteria such as wealth and status.\n\nThe prophet replied, \"Allah has chosen him over you and blessed him with knowledge and stature.\" Allah chose Saul and blessed him with knowledge and stature, qualities more important than wealth. \"Allah grants kingship to whoever He wills. And Allah is All-Bountiful, All-Knowing.\" Allah gives kingship to whom He wills.\n\nThis verse teaches that Allah’s choice of a leader is based on qualities such as knowledge and strength, not on worldly wealth or status. For the reader, the verse encourages valuing the qualities that Allah values and accepting Allah’s choice. It also teaches that Allah grants authority to whom He wills and that His choice is wise. The objection of the people showed their attachment to worldly criteria, while the prophet corrected them by pointing to the true qualities of leadership. The objection of the people to Saul’s appointment was based on worldly criteria, such as wealth and status. They wondered how Saul could be king when others seemed more deserving and he was not rich. The prophet corrected them by pointing to what truly matters: Allah had chosen Saul and blessed him with knowledge and stature, qualities far more important than wealth. This teaches that leadership and honour are not determined by worldly possessions but by the qualities Allah values, such as knowledge and strength. Allah grants authority to whom He wills, and His choice is wise. The reader is encouraged to value the qualities that Allah values and to accept Allah’s decisions with trust, recognising that Allah is All-Bountiful and All-Knowing and chooses leaders according to His wisdom.",
  "248": "“Their prophet further told them, ‘The sign of Saul’s kingship is that the Ark will come to you.’”\n\nThis verse gives the sign of Saul’s kingship. \"Their prophet further told them, 'The sign of Saul's kingship is that the Ark will come to you—containing reassurance from your Lord and relics of the family of Moses and the family of Aaron, which will be carried by the angels.'\" The sign was that the Ark, a sacred chest containing reassurance from Allah and relics of the families of Moses and Aaron, would come to them carried by the angels. This was a miraculous sign confirming the kingship of Saul.\n\nThe verse ends, \"Surely in this is a sign for you, if you ˹truly˺ believe.\" The coming of the Ark was a clear sign for them, if they truly believed. The sign confirmed the choice of Saul and the truth of the prophet’s message.\n\nThis verse teaches that Allah gives signs to confirm His choices and to guide people. The Ark, with its relics and its being carried by angels, was a clear sign. For the reader, the verse encourages recognizing the signs of Allah and accepting His guidance with faith. It also teaches that Allah confirms His decisions with signs that are clear for those who believe. The story continues in the following verses, showing the trial of Saul’s army and the victory granted by Allah. The sign of the Ark confirmed the kingship of Saul and the truth of the prophet’s message. The Ark, containing reassurance from Allah and relics of the families of Moses and Aaron, was carried by angels, a clear and miraculous sign. Such a sign removed any doubt about Allah’s choice. The verse ends by noting that in this is a sign for those who truly believe, showing that the sign benefits those whose hearts are open to faith. The reader is encouraged to recognise the signs of Allah and to accept His guidance with faith, understanding that Allah confirms His decisions with clear signs. The story of the Ark also connects the kingship of Saul to the legacy of the earlier prophets, showing the continuity of Allah’s guidance.",
  "249": "“When Saul marched forth with his army, he cautioned: ‘Allah will test you with a river.’”\n\nThis verse describes the testing of Saul’s army. \"When Saul marched forth with his army, he cautioned: 'Allah will test you with a river. So whoever drinks ˹his fill˺ from it is not with me, and whoever does not taste it—except a sip from the hollow of his hands—is definitely with me.'\" Saul warned his army that Allah would test them with a river. Those who drank their fill from it would not be with him, while those who did not drink, except for a sip from their hands, would be with him. This tested their obedience and self-control.\n\nThe verse says, \"They all drank ˹their fill˺ except for a few!\" Most of the army drank their fill, failing the test, with only a few obeying. This showed the weakness of most of them. \"When he and the ˹remaining˺ faithful with him crossed the river, they said, 'Now we are no match for Goliath and his warriors.'\" Those who crossed, a small number, feared they were no match for Goliath and his warriors. \"But those ˹believers˺ who were certain they would meet Allah reasoned, 'How many times has a small force vanquished a mighty army by the Will of Allah! And Allah is ˹always˺ with the steadfast.'\" The believers reasoned that a small force can defeat a mighty army by Allah’s will.\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of obedience and trust in Allah over numbers and strength. For the reader, the verse encourages obedience to leadership, self-control, and trust that Allah supports the steadfast, regardless of the size of the force. Victory comes from Allah. The test of the river was a test of obedience and self-control. Saul warned his army that those who drank their fill would not be with him, while those who only took a sip would be. Most of the army failed the test by drinking their fill, showing their lack of discipline and trust, while only a few obeyed. The small faithful band then feared they were no match for Goliath, but the believers reasoned that a small force can defeat a mighty army by Allah’s will, because Allah is with the steadfast. This verse teaches the importance of obedience, self-control, and trust in Allah over numbers and strength. The reader is encouraged to obey leadership, to control one’s desires, and to trust that Allah supports the steadfast, for victory comes from Allah, not from the size of one’s force.",
  "250": "“When they advanced to face Goliath and his warriors, they prayed.”\n\nThis verse records the prayer of the believers before facing Goliath. \"When they advanced to face Goliath and his warriors, they prayed, 'Our Lord! Shower us with perseverance, make our steps firm, and give us victory over the disbelieving people.'\" The small band of believers prayed to Allah for perseverance, firmness, and victory. Their prayer shows their dependence on Allah and their trust in Him. They asked for the strength to remain steadfast and for victory over the enemy.\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of prayer and dependence on Allah before facing difficulty. The believers did not rely only on their own strength but turned to Allah, asking for perseverance, firmness, and victory. For the reader, the verse encourages turning to Allah in prayer, especially in times of difficulty and struggle, and asking for the strength to be steadfast. Their prayer is a model for the believer: seek perseverance from Allah, ask for firmness in steps, and seek victory over what is wrong. The story continues in the next verse with the victory granted by Allah. The prayer of the small band of believers before facing Goliath is a model of reliance on Allah. They asked for perseverance, firmness, and victory, showing that they did not depend on their own strength but on Allah’s help. Their prayer combines the request for inner strength, the steadfastness of their steps, and success over the enemy. This teaches the believer to turn to Allah in prayer before facing difficulty, asking for the strength to endure and the firmness to stand. The reader is encouraged to make such prayers in times of struggle, relying on Allah and seeking His help. The believers in the story did not despair despite being few, because they placed their trust in Allah, and their prayer was a key part of their preparation for the battle that Allah would grant them victory in.",
  "251": "“So they defeated them by Allah’s Will, and David killed Goliath.”\n\nThis verse records the victory of the believers. \"So they defeated them by Allah's Will, and David killed Goliath.\" The small band of believers, by Allah’s will, defeated their enemies, and David, who was among them, killed Goliath. The victory was granted by Allah, confirming the faith of the steadfast believers. \"And Allah blessed David with kingship and wisdom and taught him what He willed.\" Allah blessed David with kingship and wisdom, and taught him what He willed. David became a king and a prophet.\n\nThe verse then explains the wisdom behind such events: \"Had Allah not repelled a group of people by ˹the might of˺ another, corruption would have dominated the earth, but Allah is Gracious to all.\" If Allah did not repel some people by means of others, corruption would dominate the earth. This shows the wisdom of struggle and conflict in preventing corruption. Allah is gracious to all.\n\nThis verse teaches that victory comes from Allah and that He raises the righteous. For the reader, the verse encourages trust in Allah and understanding that He protects the earth from corruption through the struggle of the righteous. The story of David and Goliath shows that a small band of faithful believers can triumph with Allah’s help. The victory of the small band of believers by Allah’s will, and the killing of Goliath by David, confirmed the truth of the believers’ faith and trust. Allah blessed David with kingship and wisdom, raising him in rank. The verse then explains a profound wisdom: had Allah not repelled some people by means of others, corruption would have dominated the earth. This shows that struggle against evil is part of Allah’s plan to preserve the earth from corruption, and that the righteous are the means through which Allah protects humanity. The reader is encouraged to trust in Allah’s support of the righteous and to understand the wisdom behind the struggle against corruption, recognising that Allah is gracious to all and that victory comes from Him.",
  "252": "“These are Allah’s revelations which We recite to you ˹O Prophet˺ in truth.”\n\nThis verse confirms the truth of what was recited. \"These are Allah's revelations which We recite to you ˹O Prophet˺ in truth.\" The verses and stories, such as the story of David and Goliath and the earlier messengers, are Allah’s revelations recited to the Prophet in truth. They are not invented tales but true accounts revealed by Allah. The verse then says, \"And you are truly one of the messengers.\" The Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him, is confirmed as one of the messengers of Allah.\n\nThis verse affirms the truthfulness of the Quran and the prophethood of the Prophet. The stories of the past messengers are true revelations, and the Prophet is confirmed as a true messenger. For the reader, the verse strengthens faith in the Quran and in the Prophet, confirming that the guidance is true and from Allah. It also connects the Prophet to the line of messengers, showing the continuity of the divine message. This verse confirms that the stories and verses recited to the Prophet are Allah’s revelations, true and not invented. The accounts of the earlier messengers and communities are part of the Quran, revealed in truth to guide humanity. The verse also confirms the prophethood of Muhammad, peace be upon him, as one of the messengers of Allah. This connects him to the line of prophets and shows the continuity of the divine message. For the reader, this verse strengthens faith in the Quran and in the Prophet, confirming that the guidance is true and from Allah. It encourages the reader to accept the revelations with certainty and to recognise the Prophet as a true messenger, following the guidance he brought. The truthfulness of the Quran is the foundation of the faith.",
  "253": "“We have chosen some of those messengers above others.”\n\nThis verse speaks of the ranks of the messengers. \"We have chosen some of those messengers above others.\" Allah chose some messengers over others, giving them different ranks. \"Allah spoke directly to some, and raised some high in rank.\" Some messengers were spoken to directly by Allah, and others were raised high in rank. \"To Jesus, son of Mary, We gave clear proofs and supported him with the holy spirit.\" Jesus was given clear proofs and supported with the holy spirit, the angel Gabriel.\n\nThe verse then explains the difference and fighting among successors: \"If Allah had willed, succeeding generations would not have fought ˹among themselves˺ after receiving the clear proofs. But they differed—some believed while others disbelieved. Yet if Allah had willed, they would not have fought one another. But Allah does what He wills.\" If Allah had willed, the generations after the messengers would not have fought among themselves, but they differed, with some believing and others disbelieving. This difference and fighting was by Allah’s decree and wisdom.\n\nThis verse teaches the different ranks of the messengers and the reality of human difference after clear proofs. For the reader, the verse encourages belief in all the messengers and understanding that Allah raises whom He wills. It also teaches that human difference and struggle are part of Allah’s decree, and that Allah does what He wills. This verse teaches the different ranks of the messengers, showing that Allah chose some above others in various ways. Some were spoken to directly by Allah, some were raised high in rank, and Jesus was given clear proofs and supported with the holy spirit. Despite these differences in rank, the believers accept all the messengers and believe in all of them. The verse then explains the reality of human difference: if Allah had willed, the succeeding generations would not have fought among themselves, but they differed, some believing and others disbelieving. This difference was part of Allah’s decree. The reader is encouraged to believe in all the messengers, to understand that Allah raises whom He wills, and to accept that human difference and struggle are part of Allah’s wisdom. Allah does what He wills.",
  "254": "“O believers! Donate from what We have provided for you before the arrival of a Day.”\n\nThis verse commands the believers to donate from what Allah has provided. \"O believers! Donate from what We have provided for you before the arrival of a Day when there will be no bargaining, friendship, or intercession.\" The believers are to give charity from the provisions Allah has granted them, before the arrival of the Day of Judgment. On that day, there will be no bargaining, no friendship, and no intercession, meaning that no one will be able to buy their way out or be saved by friends or intercessors. The only thing that will avail on that day is what a person has sent ahead through good deeds.\n\nThe verse ends, \"Those who disbelieve are ˹truly˺ the wrongdoers.\" Those who disbelieve are the real wrongdoers, because they have wronged their own souls by rejecting faith and neglecting good deeds.\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of giving charity before the Day of Judgment, when no bargaining, friendship, or intercession will help. The reader is encouraged to donate generously from what Allah has provided, recognising that the deeds sent ahead are what will benefit on the Day when nothing else can help. The verse also teaches that disbelievers are the true wrongdoers, because they harm themselves by rejecting the truth and neglecting what would benefit them. The urgency in the command to donate \"before the arrival of a Day\" shows that charity is to be done while there is still opportunity, in this life. On the Day of Judgment, no one will be able to bargain for a better outcome, no friend will be able to intercede for another, and no intercession will be accepted without Allah’s permission. This means that the deeds sent ahead in this life are the only provision that will help. The reader is encouraged to be generous with charity now, recognising that the opportunity to give will not last forever, and that those who disbelieve and neglect good deeds are the true wrongdoers, harming their own souls. The verse calls the believer to act while there is still time.",
  "255": "“Allah! There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him, the Ever-Living, All-Sustaining.”\n\nThis verse, known as the Verse of the Throne, is among the greatest verses of the Quran. \"Allah! There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him, the Ever-Living, All-Sustaining.\" It affirms the oneness of Allah and describes Him as the Ever-Living, who never dies, and the All-Sustaining, who sustains and manages all of creation. \"Neither drowsiness nor sleep overtakes Him.\" Allah is never overtaken by drowsiness or sleep, showing His perfect and unceasing watchfulness over creation. \"To Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth.\" Everything belongs to Allah.\n\n\"Who could possibly intercede with Him without His permission?\" No one can intercede with Allah without His permission, showing His absolute authority. \"He ˹fully˺ knows what is ahead of them and what is behind them, but no one can grasp any of His knowledge—except what He wills ˹to reveal˺.\" Allah knows all things, and no one can grasp His knowledge except what He reveals. \"His Seat encompasses the heavens and the earth, and the preservation of both does not tire Him. For He is the Most High, the Greatest.\" His power encompasses everything, and preserving all things does not tire Him.\n\nThis verse teaches the greatness of Allah: His oneness, His eternal life, His sustaining power, His perfect knowledge, and His absolute authority. For the reader, this verse deepens faith in the greatness of Allah and encourages relying on Him, who is All-Sustaining and Most High. It is a verse of immense virtue, praised for its power and meaning. This verse is held in the highest honour among the verses of the Quran, and it is beloved for its comprehensive description of Allah. It teaches that Allah is the only true object of worship, that He is Ever-Living and All-Sustaining, and that neither drowsiness nor sleep overtakes Him, meaning His care and watchfulness never pause. Everything in the heavens and the earth belongs to Him, and no one can intercede except with His permission. His knowledge encompasses all things, and His power and preservation of all creation never tire Him. The reader is encouraged to recite and reflect on this verse, which strengthens faith in the greatness of Allah, deepens reliance on Him, and teaches the believer to place his trust in the Most High, the Greatest, who sustains everything and is never overwhelmed by the care of creation.",
  "256": "“Let there be no compulsion in religion.”\n\nThis verse establishes a fundamental principle of Islam. \"Let there be no compulsion in religion, for the truth stands out clearly from falsehood.\" Religion is not to be forced upon anyone, because the truth has been made clear and distinct from falsehood. A person is to accept the truth willingly and with understanding, not under compulsion. The clarity of the truth removes any excuse for rejecting it, and compulsion is both unnecessary and contrary to the nature of faith.\n\nThe verse then says, \"So whoever renounces false gods and believes in Allah has certainly grasped the firmest, unfailing hand-hold.\" Whoever gives up the worship of false gods and believes in Allah has grasped the firmest and strongest hand-hold, which will not break. This is the path of security and safety, holding firmly to Allah. The verse ends, \"And Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\"\n\nThis verse teaches that faith is to be accepted freely and that the truth is clear enough that no compulsion is needed. For the reader, the verse encourages embracing faith willingly and renouncing false worship, grasping firmly to Allah. It also teaches that the truth is clear and that compulsion is contrary to the nature of religion. Believing in Allah and renouncing false gods is the firmest foundation a person can hold. This verse establishes that faith must be accepted freely and willingly, because the truth has been made perfectly clear and distinct from falsehood. Compulsion is unnecessary, since the truth is evident to those who reflect, and true faith is a matter of the heart. The verse also teaches that whoever renounces false gods and believes in Allah has grasped the firmest hand-hold, meaning a secure and unbreakable support, which is the faith and the path that leads to safety. The reader is encouraged to embrace the truth willingly and to renounce false worship, holding firmly to Allah. The clarity of the truth leaves no excuse, and the firmness of the hand-hold gives confidence to the believer who grasps it with sincerity.",
  "257": "“Allah is the Guardian of the believers.”\n\nThis verse describes the guardianship of Allah over the believers. \"Allah is the Guardian of the believers—He brings them out of darkness and into light.\" Allah protects and guides the believers, bringing them out of darkness and into light. This is the journey from ignorance and misguidance to knowledge and guidance. Allah leads the believers from the darkness of disbelief and error into the light of faith.\n\nThe verse then contrasts the disbelievers: \"As for the disbelievers, their guardians are false gods who lead them out of light and into darkness.\" The disbelievers are under the guardianship of false gods, who lead them out of light and into darkness. While the believers move from darkness to light, the disbelievers are led from light into darkness. \"It is they who will be the residents of the Fire. They will be there forever.\" The disbelievers will be residents of the Fire forever.\n\nThis verse teaches the guidance of Allah for the believers and the misguidance of false gods for the disbelievers. For the reader, the verse encourages trusting in Allah as the Guardian, who leads from darkness to light, and it warns against false guardians who lead to darkness. The contrast between the two paths and their ends is stark: the believers are led to light and salvation, while the disbelievers are led to darkness and the Fire. The imagery of darkness and light is central to understanding this verse. The believers are brought by Allah from darkness to light, meaning from ignorance, misguidance, and sin to knowledge, guidance, and righteousness. This is a continuous process, as Allah leads the believers step by step into greater light. The disbelievers, by contrast, are led by false gods from light into darkness, meaning they are taken away from any good they might have and plunged into error. The end of the disbelievers is the Fire, where they will reside forever. The reader is encouraged to remain under the guardianship of Allah by faith and obedience, so as to be led always toward light, and to beware of false guardians that lead to darkness and destruction.",
  "258": "“Are you ˹O Prophet˺ not aware of the one who argued with Abraham about his Lord?”\n\nThis verse begins the story of Abraham’s argument with a tyrant. \"Are you ˹O Prophet˺ not aware of the one who argued with Abraham about his Lord because Allah had granted him kingship?\" This refers to a ruler who argued with Abraham, feeling proud because Allah had given him kingship. \"˹Remember˺ when Abraham said, 'My Lord is the One Who has power to give life and cause death.'\" Abraham declared that his Lord gives life and causes death. \"He argued, 'I too have the power to give life and cause death.'\" The tyrant claimed the same power, though he could not truly give life or cause death as Allah does.\n\nAbraham challenged him: \"Abraham challenged ˹him˺, 'Allah causes the sun to rise from the east. So make it rise from the west.'\" Abraham presented a proof that the tyrant could not answer: if the tyrant claimed power over life and death, let him make the sun rise from the west. \"And so the disbeliever was dumbstruck.\" The tyrant was left speechless. \"And Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people.\"\n\nThis verse teaches the power of Abraham’s argument and the emptiness of the tyrant’s claim. For the reader, the verse encourages recognising the greatness of Allah and using sound reasoning to respond to false claims. It also teaches that Allah does not guide the wrongdoers, who persist in their arrogance. The argument between Abraham and the tyrant is a powerful example of reasoning in matters of faith. The tyrant, proud of his kingship, claimed power over life and death, but Abraham’s challenge exposed the emptiness of his claim: if the tyrant truly had such power, he should be able to make the sun rise from the west. Unable to do so, the tyrant was left speechless. This story teaches that the truth is supported by clear reasoning and that false claims, no matter how arrogant, can be defeated by sound argument. The reader is encouraged to respond to falsehood with reasoning and clarity, as Abraham did, and to recognise that Allah does not guide those who persist in wrongdoing. Truth prevails over falsehood through sound argument and divine support.",
  "259": "“Or ˹are you not aware of˺ the one who passed by a city which was in ruins.”\n\nThis verse tells the story of a man who passed by a ruined city. \"Or ˹are you not aware of˺ the one who passed by a city which was in ruins. He wondered, 'How could Allah bring this back to life after its destruction?'\" The man wondered how Allah could bring the ruined city back to life. \"So Allah caused him to die for a hundred years then brought him back to life.\" Allah caused him to die for a hundred years and then brought him back to life, to show him the reality of resurrection.\n\nAllah asked him, \"How long have you remained ˹in this state˺?\" He replied, \"Perhaps a day or part of a day.\" He thought he had been dead a short time. \"Allah said, 'No! You have remained here for a hundred years! Just look at your food and drink— they have not spoiled. ˹But now˺ look at ˹the remains of˺ your donkey! And ˹so˺ We have made you into a sign for humanity. And look at the bones ˹of the donkey˺, how We bring them together then clothe them with flesh!'\" Allah showed him that his food and drink had not spoiled, while his donkey had decomposed, and then He brought the bones together and clothed them with flesh, showing His power.\n\nWhen this was made clear to him, he declared, \"˹Now˺ I know that Allah is Most Capable of everything.\" This verse teaches the power of Allah over life and death and the reality of resurrection. For the reader, the verse strengthens faith in the resurrection and in Allah’s power, and it encourages certainty that Allah is capable of all things. The story of the man who passed by the ruined city teaches the reality of resurrection and the power of Allah. When the man wondered how Allah could bring the ruined city back to life, Allah showed him directly by causing him to die for a hundred years and then bringing him back to life. His food and drink had not spoiled, while his donkey had decomposed, and then Allah brought the bones together and clothed them with flesh. This was a clear demonstration of Allah’s power over death and life. The man declared his certainty that Allah is Most Capable of everything. The reader is encouraged to strengthen faith in the resurrection, understanding that Allah, who can bring the dead back to life, is indeed capable of all things.",
  "260": "“And ˹remember˺ when Abraham said, ‘My Lord! Show me how you give life to the dead.’”\n\nThis verse records the prayer of Abraham seeking reassurance. \"And ˹remember˺ when Abraham said, 'My Lord! Show me how you give life to the dead.' Allah responded, 'Do you not believe?'\" Allah asked Abraham whether he did not believe. \"Abraham replied, 'Yes I do, but just so my heart can be reassured.'\" Abraham affirmed his belief but asked for reassurance of the heart. His request was not out of doubt but out of a desire for greater certainty.\n\nAllah said, \"Then bring four birds, train them to come to you, ˹then cut them into pieces,˺ and scatter them on different hilltops. Then call them back, they will fly to you in haste.\" Allah instructed Abraham to take four birds, cut them into pieces, scatter them on hills, and call them back, and they would come to him. This demonstrated Allah’s power to bring the dead to life. The verse ends, \"And ˹so you will˺ know that Allah is Almighty, All-Wise.\"\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of seeking reassurance of faith and the demonstration of Allah’s power. For the reader, the verse encourages certainty in the resurrection and understanding that Allah is Almighty and All-Wise. The story of Abraham shows that even the prophets sought to strengthen their hearts in certainty, and that Allah is able to show His power in the most remarkable ways. Abraham’s request to be shown how Allah gives life to the dead was not born of doubt but of a desire for greater certainty and reassurance of the heart. Allah’s question, \"Do you not believe?\", and Abraham’s answer, \"Yes I do, but just so my heart can be reassured,\" show that even the prophets sought to strengthen their certainty. Allah then instructed Abraham to take four birds, cut them into pieces, scatter them on hills, and call them back, and they came to him, demonstrating Allah’s power. The reader is encouraged to seek certainty in faith and to understand that Allah is able to demonstrate His power in remarkable ways. Certainty of heart is a blessing, and the believer is encouraged to seek reassurance of faith through reflection on Allah’s power.",
  "261": "“The example of those who spend their wealth in the cause of Allah is that of a grain that sprouts into seven ears.”\n\nThis verse gives a beautiful example of the reward of spending in the cause of Allah. \"The example of those who spend their wealth in the cause of Allah is that of a grain that sprouts into seven ears, each bearing one hundred grains.\" A single grain that grows into seven ears, each with a hundred grains, yields seven hundred times the original. This is the example of the reward for spending in the cause of Allah, which is multiplied many times. \"And Allah multiplies ˹the reward even more˺ to whoever He wills.\" Allah increases the reward even more for whom He wills. \"For Allah is All-Bountiful, All-Knowing.\"\n\nThis verse teaches the abundance of the reward for spending in the cause of Allah, using the vivid image of a grain multiplying into hundreds. The reward is not limited but is multiplied, and Allah increases it for whom He wills. For the reader, the verse encourages generous spending in the cause of Allah, trusting in His great reward. The example shows that what is spent in the cause of Allah is not lost but grows and multiplies, and that Allah, who is All-Bountiful and All-Knowing, rewards His servants generously. The example of the grain that sprouts into seven ears, each bearing a hundred grains, illustrates the immense reward for spending in the cause of Allah. One grain yields seven hundred, and Allah multiplies the reward even more for whom He wills. This teaches that spending in the cause of Allah is not a loss but an investment that Allah rewards with abundant multiplication. The reader is encouraged to spend generously in the cause of Allah, trusting in His promise of multiplication and recognising that the reward is far greater than what was given. The example also shows the generosity and bounty of Allah, who rewards the sincere giver far beyond what he spent.",
  "262": "“Those who spend their wealth in the cause of Allah and do not follow their charity with reminders of their generosity.”\n\nThis verse describes those who spend in the cause of Allah with sincerity. \"Those who spend their wealth in the cause of Allah and do not follow their charity with reminders of their generosity or hurtful words—they will get their reward from their Lord.\" Those who give charity but do not spoil it by reminding others of their generosity or by using hurtful words will receive their reward from their Lord. The purity of their giving is preserved by their good conduct.\n\nThe verse ends, \"and there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve.\" Those who give sincerely, without reminders or hurtful words, have no fear for the future and no grief over the past. Their reward is secure with Allah.\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of giving charity with sincerity and good conduct, without spoiling it by reminders of generosity or hurtful words. For the reader, the verse encourages giving purely for Allah, without seeking praise or reminding others, and it teaches that such sincere giving brings reward and peace. The reward of those who give sincerely is with their Lord, and they have no fear or grief. Sincere charity preserves its value and brings security to the giver. Sincere charity is that which is given for Allah and not spoiled by reminders of generosity or hurtful words. Those who give in this way, without seeking praise or reminding others of their favour, will receive their reward from their Lord. The purity of their giving preserves its value, and they have no fear for the future and no grief over the past. The reader is encouraged to give charity sincerely, seeking only the pleasure of Allah, and to guard against spoiling it by reminders or harm. The reward of such sincere giving is secure with Allah, and it brings peace to the giver. This verse teaches that the manner of giving is as important as the giving itself.",
  "263": "“Kind words and forgiveness are better than charity followed by injury.”\n\nThis verse teaches the value of kindness and forgiveness. \"Kind words and forgiveness are better than charity followed by injury.\" Kind words and forgiving others are better than giving charity that is spoiled by hurting someone afterwards. A charity that is followed by reminders, insults, or harm loses its value, while kind words and forgiveness carry great worth in themselves. This shows that the manner of giving and the treatment of people matter as much as the giving itself.\n\nThe verse ends, \"And Allah is Self-Sufficient, Most Forbearing.\" Allah is Self-Sufficient, needing nothing from anyone, and Most Forbearing, patient with His servants. This reassures that Allah values sincere goodness.\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of treating people with kindness and forgiveness, which are better than charity spoiled by injury. For the reader, the verse encourages good manners and forgiving others, understanding that these have great value in the sight of Allah. It also teaches that charity should be given in a way that preserves dignity and does not harm, because harm destroys the value of giving. Allah, who is Self-Sufficient and Most Forbearing, rewards sincere goodness. This verse teaches that kind words and forgiveness carry great value, even greater than charity that is spoiled by injury. When charity is followed by reminders, insults, or harm, its value is destroyed, while kind words and forgiveness, which preserve dignity and mend relationships, are better. This shows that good conduct toward people is a form of good in itself. The reader is encouraged to treat people with kindness and to forgive, understanding that these are better than giving charity that hurts. The verse also reminds that Allah is Self-Sufficient, needing nothing, and Most Forbearing, patient with His servants, which encourages the believer to be forbearing and kind toward others.",
  "264": "“O believers! Do not waste your charity with reminders ˹of your generosity˺ or hurtful words.”\n\nThis verse warns against spoiling charity. \"O believers! Do not waste your charity with reminders ˹of your generosity˺ or hurtful words, like those who donate their wealth just to show off and do not believe in Allah or the Last Day.\" The believers are warned not to spoil their charity by reminding people of their generosity or by hurting them, as those who donate to show off and do not truly believe do. Such people give for appearances, not for Allah. \"Their example is that of a hard barren rock covered with a thin layer of soil hit by a strong rain— leaving it just a bare stone.\" Their charity is like soil on a rock that is washed away by rain, leaving nothing. \"Such people are unable to preserve the reward of their charity. Allah does not guide ˹such˺ disbelieving people.\"\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of sincere charity, free from showing off and harm, and warns that charity spoiled by these loses its reward. For the reader, the verse encourages giving sincerely for Allah, without seeking praise or hurting others. The example of the barren rock shows that charity without sincerity yields nothing. The reader is encouraged to preserve the value of charity through sincerity and good conduct. This verse warns against spoiling charity by reminders and hurtful words, and it gives the example of those who give to show off without true faith. Their charity is like a thin layer of soil on a hard rock that is washed away by rain, leaving nothing. Such people are unable to preserve the reward of their charity, because their intention was not sincere. The reader is encouraged to give charity sincerely for Allah, free from showing off and harm, so that its reward is preserved. The example of the barren rock teaches that charity without sincerity yields nothing, and the verse warns that Allah does not guide those who persist in such insincerity. Sincerity is the foundation that preserves the value of giving.",
  "265": "“And the example of those who donate their wealth, seeking Allah’s pleasure.”\n\nThis verse gives the positive example of sincere giving. \"And the example of those who donate their wealth, seeking Allah's pleasure and believing the reward is certain, is that of a garden on a fertile hill: when heavy rain falls, it yields up twice its normal produce.\" Those who give seeking Allah’s pleasure and believing in the reward are like a garden on fertile soil that yields twice its produce when rain falls. \"If no heavy rain falls, a drizzle is sufficient.\" Even a light rain is enough to make it fruitful. \"And Allah is All-Seeing of what you do.\"\n\nThis verse teaches that sincere giving, done for Allah’s pleasure with faith in the reward, is like a fertile garden that produces abundantly. Even a small amount of sincere giving bears fruit. For the reader, the verse encourages giving with sincerity and trust in Allah, knowing that Allah rewards generously and sees all deeds. The contrast with the previous verse is clear: charity spoiled by showing off yields nothing, while sincere charity, like a fertile garden, produces abundantly and is blessed by Allah. The example of the fertile garden shows the blessedness of sincere charity. Those who give seeking Allah’s pleasure, with certainty of the reward, are like a garden on fertile soil that yields abundant produce, doubling its yield with heavy rain and bearing fruit even with a light drizzle. This teaches that sincere giving, even if small, is blessed and produces abundant reward. The reader is encouraged to give with sincerity and trust in Allah, knowing that Allah rewards generously and sees all deeds. The contrast with the barren rock of the previous verse is clear: sincere charity is fruitful and blessed, while charity done for show is fruitless. Allah, who is All-Seeing, rewards the sincere giver.",
  "266": "“Would any of you wish to have a garden with palm trees, grapevines, and all kinds of fruits.”\n\nThis verse gives a powerful warning about charity spoiled by harm. \"Would any of you wish to have a garden with palm trees, grapevines, and all kinds of fruits with rivers flowing underneath and as they grow very old with dependent children, a fiery whirlwind hits the garden, burning it all up?\" The verse pictures a person who has a beautiful, fruitful garden that provides for him and his family, but then a fiery whirlwind destroys it, leaving him with nothing. This is the example of the person who gives charity but spoils it by reminders and harm, destroying its reward.\n\nThe verse ends, \"This is how Allah makes His revelations clear to you, so perhaps you will reflect.\" The revelations are made clear so that people may reflect on this lesson.\n\nThis verse teaches the tragedy of spoiling one’s good deeds by harm, using the vivid image of a garden destroyed by fire. For the reader, the verse encourages reflecting on the value of good deeds and preserving them through sincerity and good conduct, rather than destroying them by harm and reminders. The loss of the garden at the moment of greatest need is a powerful warning against losing the reward of one’s charity through spoiling it. Reflection on this lesson protects the value of one’s good deeds. This verse uses a vivid and painful image to warn against spoiling good deeds. A person with a beautiful, fruitful garden that sustains him and his family loses it all to a fiery whirlwind at the moment he needs it most. This is the example of the person who gives charity but spoils it by reminders and harm, destroying its reward. The loss is greatest when it is needed most, making the warning especially powerful. The reader is encouraged to preserve the value of good deeds through sincerity and good conduct, reflecting on how easily the reward can be destroyed. The verse calls the believer to reflect on this lesson, understanding that good deeds must be protected from what spoils them.",
  "267": "“O believers! Donate from the best of what you have earned.”\n\nThis verse guides the believers in what to give in charity. \"O believers! Donate from the best of what you have earned and of what We have produced for you from the earth.\" The believers are to give from the best of what they have earned and from the good produce of the earth. \"Do not pick out worthless things for donation, which you yourselves would only accept with closed eyes.\" They are not to give the worthless things, which they themselves would only accept reluctantly. They should give from what is good and valuable.\n\nThe verse ends, \"And know that Allah is Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy.\" Allah is Self-Sufficient, needing nothing, and Praiseworthy. The reader is encouraged to give generously from the best, not the worst.\n\nThis verse teaches that charity should be given from the good and valuable, not from the worthless, reflecting sincerity and generosity. For the reader, the verse encourages giving from the best of one’s wealth, as a reflection of sincere faith, and it teaches that giving worthless things does not honour Allah, who is Self-Sufficient and Praiseworthy. The quality of what one gives reflects the sincerity of one’s intention and one’s respect for the act of giving. This verse teaches that charity should be given from the best of one’s wealth, not from the worthless. A person should give from what he has earned and from the good produce of the earth, and should not give the worthless things that he himself would only accept with closed eyes. Giving the best reflects sincerity and generosity, while giving the worst shows a lack of respect for the act of giving and for the recipient. The reader is encouraged to give from the best of what he has, as a reflection of sincere faith, and to remember that Allah is Self-Sufficient and Praiseworthy. The quality of one’s giving reflects the sincerity of one’s heart.",
  "268": "“The Devil threatens you with ˹the prospect of˺ poverty.”\n\nThis verse exposes the tactic of the Devil regarding charity. \"The Devil threatens you with ˹the prospect of˺ poverty and bids you to the shameful deed ˹of stinginess˺, while Allah promises you forgiveness and ˹great˺ bounties from Him.\" Satan threatens people with poverty to prevent them from giving charity, and he encourages stinginess, while Allah promises forgiveness and great bounties. The contrast is clear: Satan calls to stinginess and fear, while Allah calls to generosity and reward.\n\nThe verse ends, \"And Allah is All-Bountiful, All-Knowing.\" Allah is All-Bountiful and All-Knowing, promising generous reward to those who give.\n\nThis verse teaches that Satan tries to prevent charity by threatening poverty and encouraging stinginess, while Allah promises forgiveness and bounties to those who give. For the reader, the verse encourages resisting Satan’s fear and giving generously, trusting in Allah’s promise of bounties. The contrast between the threat of Satan and the promise of Allah guides the believer to choose generosity and trust in Allah’s reward over fear and stinginess. This verse exposes the devil’s tactic of using fear to prevent charity. The devil threatens poverty and encourages stinginess, making people afraid that giving will leave them in need. Allah, in contrast, promises forgiveness and great bounties to those who give. The believer is to trust Allah’s promise over the devil’s threat. The reader is encouraged to resist the fear of poverty and to give generously, knowing that Allah’s bounties are greater than any loss, and that giving brings forgiveness and reward. The verse teaches that generosity is a means of drawing near to Allah, while stinginess is a shameful deed that the devil promotes. Trust in Allah overcomes the fear that the devil uses. The believer is called to recognise that wealth is not increased or decreased by what is spent in the path of Allah, but by His decree. The devil uses the fear of poverty because it is a powerful fear in the human heart, but Allah’s promise of bounties is far more certain. The reader is encouraged to give despite the fear, trusting that Allah provides, and to recognise that stinginess is a shameful deed that harms the one who clings to it. Generosity, done for Allah, is a means of drawing nearer to Him and of receiving His forgiveness and bounties, while the fear promoted by the devil only holds a person back from good.",
  "269": "“Allah grants wisdom to whoever He wills.”\n\nThis verse speaks of the gift of wisdom. \"Allah grants wisdom to whoever He wills. And whoever is granted wisdom is certainly blessed with a great privilege.\" Wisdom is a gift from Allah, given to whom He wills, and whoever is granted wisdom is blessed with a great privilege. Wisdom includes the ability to understand, to judge correctly, and to use knowledge rightly. It is a great blessing because it guides a person to what is true and good.\n\nThe verse ends, \"But none will be mindful ˹of this˺ except people of reason.\" Only people of reason will be mindful of this gift and seek it.\n\nThis verse teaches the value of wisdom as a great blessing from Allah and encourages seeking it. For the reader, the verse encourages seeking wisdom and understanding, recognising that it is a gift from Allah that brings great benefit. It also teaches that only people of reason appreciate the value of wisdom. Wisdom guides a person in knowledge, judgement, and action, and it is among the greatest privileges Allah grants to His servants. The reader is encouraged to seek wisdom and to be mindful of this great blessing. Wisdom is described as a great privilege granted by Allah to whom He wills. Wisdom includes correct understanding, sound judgement, and the right use of knowledge, and it is a gift that brings great benefit to its possessor and to others. The reader is encouraged to seek wisdom, recognising that it is among the greatest blessings, and to appreciate its value. Only people of reason are mindful of this gift, meaning those who reflect appreciate the worth of wisdom. The verse encourages the pursuit of wisdom through knowledge and reflection, understanding that it guides a person to what is true and good. Wisdom is a privilege that elevates its possessor and benefits the community.",
  "270": "“Whatever charities you give or vows you make are surely known to Allah.”\n\nThis verse teaches that all charities and vows are known to Allah. \"Whatever charities you give or vows you make are surely known to Allah.\" Nothing is hidden from Allah, whether it is a charity given or a vow made. Allah knows all such deeds. The verse ends, \"And the wrongdoers will have no helpers.\" Those who do wrong, including those who give insincerely or neglect their duties, will have no helpers to save them.\n\nThis verse teaches the awareness of Allah over all charities and vows, which brings accountability and sincerity. For the reader, the verse encourages giving charity and fulfilling vows with sincerity, knowing that Allah sees and knows all. It also teaches that the wrongdoers will have no helpers, warning against wrongdoing. The knowledge of Allah over all deeds encourages the believer to act sincerely and to be accountable, while the warning against the wrongdoers serves as a reminder of the consequences of wrongdoing. This verse teaches that all charities and vows are known to Allah, whether given or made openly or in secret. The awareness of Allah over all deeds brings accountability and encourages sincerity, because nothing is hidden from Him. The verse also warns that the wrongdoers will have no helpers, meaning that those who do wrong will find no one to save them on the Day of Judgment. The reader is encouraged to give charity and fulfil vows sincerely, knowing that Allah sees and knows all, and to avoid wrongdoing, which brings the loss of helpers. The knowledge of Allah over all deeds gives the believer confidence that sincere giving is not wasted and warns that wrongdoing is never hidden. The awareness that Allah knows all charities and vows should both encourage sincerity and give confidence. It encourages sincerity because one cannot hide the true intention behind a deed from Allah, and it gives confidence because nothing sincere is lost or overlooked. Vows, like charities, are commitments that should be fulfilled, and they too are known to Allah. The warning that the wrongdoers will have no helpers serves as a strong motivation to avoid wrongdoing, since on the Day of Judgment no one will come to the aid of those who did wrong. The reader is encouraged to give and to vow with sincerity and to fulfil one’s commitments, trusting in the knowledge and justice of Allah.",
  "271": "“To give charity publicly is good, but to give to the poor privately is better for you.”\n\nThis verse addresses the manner of giving charity. \"To give charity publicly is good, but to give to the poor privately is better for you, and will absolve you of your sins.\" Giving charity publicly is good, but giving to the poor privately is better, because it protects the dignity of the recipient and the sincerity of the giver, and it will absolve the giver of sins. The verse ends, \"And Allah is All-Aware of what you do.\"\n\nThis verse teaches that while public charity is good, private charity is better, because it preserves sincerity and the dignity of the poor, and it brings forgiveness of sins. For the reader, the verse encourages giving charity with sincerity, preferring private giving to preserve purity of intention and the dignity of the recipient. It also teaches that charity absolves sins and that Allah is All-Aware of what people do. The guidance balances the good of public charity, which can encourage others, with the greater sincerity of private charity. This verse balances the good of public charity with the greater good of private charity. Public charity is good because it encourages others and benefits the community, but private charity is better because it preserves the sincerity of the giver and the dignity of the recipient, and it absolves the giver of sins. The reader is encouraged to give with sincerity, preferring private giving to protect purity of intention and the honour of the poor, while recognising the value of public charity in encouraging others. The verse also teaches that charity absolves sins and that Allah is All-Aware of what people do, encouraging sincere giving with the intention of purification and seeking the pleasure of Allah. The preference for private charity reflects the value Islam places on sincerity and on protecting the dignity of the poor. When charity is given privately, the giver’s intention is more likely to be pure, and the recipient is not exposed or humiliated. The verse also teaches that charity is a means of absolution from sins, washing away wrongs through sincere giving. The reader is encouraged to give charity in a way that preserves both sincerity and dignity, recognising that while public charity has its place in encouraging others, private charity is better for the soul. Allah, who is All-Aware of what people do, sees the sincerity behind every act of giving and rewards it accordingly.",
  "272": "“You are not responsible for people’s guidance ˹O Prophet˺.”\n\nThis verse clarifies the responsibility of the Prophet. \"You are not responsible for people's guidance ˹O Prophet˺—it is Allah Who guides whoever He wills.\" The Prophet is not responsible for whether people accept guidance; it is Allah who guides whom He wills. The Prophet’s duty is to convey the message clearly, and guidance is in Allah’s hands. This relieves the Prophet and the believers of the burden of others’ choices.\n\nThe verse then says, \"Whatever you ˹believers˺ spend in charity, it is for your own good—as long as you do so seeking the pleasure of Allah.\" Spending in charity is for the giver’s own good, provided it is done seeking Allah’s pleasure. \"Whatever you donate will be paid back to you in full, and you will not be wronged.\" What is donated is repaid in full, without any injustice.\n\nThis verse teaches that guidance is in Allah’s hands and that spending in charity is for the giver’s own benefit. For the reader, the verse encourages sincerity in charity, seeking Allah’s pleasure, and trusting that the reward is full and certain. It also teaches that the believer’s responsibility is to convey the truth sincerely, while leaving guidance to Allah, which relieves the believer of anxiety over the response of others. This verse clarifies that guidance is in the hands of Allah, and that the Prophet is not responsible for whether people accept the message. This relieves the believer of anxiety over the response of others, since the duty is only to convey the truth sincerely. The verse then teaches that spending in charity is for the giver’s own good, provided it is done seeking Allah’s pleasure, and that what is donated is repaid in full without injustice. The reader is encouraged to give charity sincerely, seeking Allah’s pleasure, and to trust that the reward is full and certain. The verse also teaches the believer to leave the matter of guidance to Allah, doing his duty sincerely and trusting in Allah’s wisdom.",
  "273": "“˹Charity is˺ for the needy who are too engaged in the cause of Allah.”\n\nThis verse identifies the deserving recipients of charity. \"˹Charity is˺ for the needy who are too engaged in the cause of Allah to move about in the land ˹for work˺.\" The charity is for the needy who are so engaged in the cause of Allah that they cannot move about seeking work. \"Those unfamiliar with their situation will think they are not in need ˹of charity˺ because they do not beg.\" Because they do not beg, people unfamiliar with them think they are not in need. \"You can recognize them by their appearance.\" They can be recognized by their appearance, showing signs of need. \"They do not beg people persistently.\" They do not beg persistently, making their need less visible.\n\nThe verse ends, \"Whatever you give in charity is certainly well known to Allah.\" All charity is known to Allah.\n\nThis verse teaches the virtue of giving to the needy who are dignified and do not beg, and the awareness of Allah over all charity. For the reader, the verse encourages recognising and helping the hidden needy, those who suffer need without asking, and giving charity sincerely, knowing that Allah knows all giving. It teaches the importance of seeking out those who are in need even when they do not ask, and the reward for doing so is known to Allah. This verse highlights the needs of the hidden poor, those who are too engaged in the cause of Allah to seek work and who do not beg, so that people unfamiliar with them may not realise their need. They can be recognised by their appearance, but they do not ask persistently. The reader is encouraged to seek out and help such dignified needy people, giving charity to those who do not ask, and to understand that all charity is known to Allah. This verse teaches the virtue of recognising hidden need and helping it, and it encourages giving sincerely, knowing that Allah sees and rewards all giving. Charity to the hidden needy is a high virtue that reflects sincere faith and care for others.",
  "274": "“Those who spend their wealth in charity day and night, secretly and openly.”\n\nThis verse describes those who give charity at all times and in all ways. \"Those who spend their wealth in charity day and night, secretly and openly—their reward is with their Lord.\" The believers who give charity both day and night, and both secretly and openly, do so consistently and sincerely. Their giving covers all times and all modes, showing their constant commitment to charity. Their reward is with their Lord.\n\nThe verse ends, \"and there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve.\" Those who give charity in this way have no fear for the future and no grief over the past. Their reward is secure with Allah.\n\nThis verse teaches the virtue of consistent and sincere charity, given at all times and in all ways. For the reader, the verse encourages giving charity regularly, whether in secret or in public, day or night, with sincerity for Allah. The reward of such giving is with the Lord, and it brings peace, with no fear and no grief. The verse shows that sincere and consistent charity is a path to security and reward in the sight of Allah. The description of charity given both secretly and openly shows the balance in Islamic giving. Giving openly can encourage others and strengthen the community, while giving in secret preserves sincerity and the dignity of the recipient. The mention of both day and night shows that charity is not limited to particular times but is a constant practice. The reward of those who give in this comprehensive manner is with their Lord, and they have no fear for the future or grief over the past, because their giving was sincere and their reward is secure. The reader is encouraged to make charity a constant habit, given in a balanced way, with the assurance that such sincere and consistent giving brings peace and the reward of Allah.",
  "275": "“Those who consume interest will stand ˹on Judgment Day˺ like those driven to madness by Satan’s touch.”\n\nThis verse condemns the consumption of interest. \"Those who consume interest will stand ˹on Judgment Day˺ like those driven to madness by Satan's touch.\" On the Day of Judgment, those who consumed interest will rise like people driven to madness by Satan, staggering and disturbed. This vivid image describes the punishment and disgrace of those who dealt in interest. \"That is because they say, 'Trade is no different than interest.'\" They claimed that trade is no different from interest, equating the two. \"But Allah has permitted trading and forbidden interest.\" Allah permitted trade but forbade interest, showing that they are not the same.\n\nThe verse then says, \"Whoever refrains—after having received warning from their Lord—may keep their previous gains, and their case is left to Allah. As for those who persist, it is they who will be the residents of the Fire. They will be there forever.\" Those who stop after the warning may keep their previous gains, but those who persist will be residents of the Fire forever.\n\nThis verse teaches the prohibition of interest and the difference between trade and interest, which Allah permitted and forbade respectively. For the reader, the verse warns against consuming interest and encourages refraining from it, understanding that trade is permitted while interest is forbidden. The punishment for those who persist in interest is the Fire, and the verse serves as a serious warning against this sin. This verse establishes a clear and serious prohibition of interest, and it corrects the false claim that trade and interest are the same. Trade is an exchange of goods and services in which benefit is earned fairly, while interest is a charge on a loan that can burden the borrower and increase injustice. By permitting trade and forbidding interest, Allah sets out a just economic framework. The verse also offers a path of mercy: those who stop after the warning may keep their previous gains, and their case is left to Allah, while those who persist face the Fire. The reader is encouraged to understand the distinction between trade and interest, to refrain from interest, and to recognise the justice and mercy of Allah’s guidance in financial matters.",
  "276": "“Allah has made interest fruitless and charity fruitful.”\n\nThis verse contrasts interest and charity. \"Allah has made interest fruitless and charity fruitful.\" Allah causes interest to be fruitless, meaning it brings no true benefit, while He causes charity to be fruitful, meaning it grows and brings blessing. Even though interest may seem to increase wealth, it is fruitless in reality, while charity, which may seem to decrease wealth, is blessed and fruitful. \"And Allah does not like any ungrateful evildoer.\" Allah does not like the ungrateful evildoer, those who reject His commands and commit wrong.\n\nThis verse teaches that Allah makes interest fruitless and charity fruitful, showing the true value of each. For the reader, the verse encourages charity and warns against interest, understanding that true blessing is in charity even if it appears to reduce wealth, while interest brings no real benefit. The verse also teaches that Allah does not like the ungrateful evildoer, encouraging gratitude and righteousness. The blessing of charity and the fruitlessness of interest reflect the wisdom of Allah’s guidance. The contrast between interest and charity is a lesson in where true blessing lies. Interest may appear to increase wealth, but Allah makes it fruitless, stripping it of real benefit and baraka, while charity, which may appear to decrease wealth, is made fruitful and brings growth and blessing. This corrects the worldly view that sees only outward increase and decrease. The reader is encouraged to place trust in the wisdom of Allah, giving charity and avoiding interest, even when the world sees things differently. The verse also warns that Allah does not like the ungrateful evildoer, encouraging gratitude and righteousness. True wealth and blessing are determined by Allah, not by appearances. The verse teaches the believer to look beyond outward appearances to the true reality that Allah has decreed. What seems to grow through interest is emptied of blessing, while what is given in charity is multiplied in reward, even if it seems to decrease in this world. This is a matter of faith, because it requires trusting Allah’s judgement over human calculation. The reader is encouraged to prefer what Allah has blessed over what the world considers gain, and to recognise that the fruitfulness of charity is a reality known to Allah and rewarded by Him. Gratitude and righteousness are the qualities Allah loves, and they are the path to His blessing.",
  "277": "“Indeed, those who believe, do good, establish prayer, and pay alms-tax.”\n\nThis verse describes those who combine faith with good works. \"Indeed, those who believe, do good, establish prayer, and pay alms-tax will receive their reward from their Lord.\" Those who believe, do good deeds, establish prayer, and pay alms-tax will receive their reward from their Lord. The verse lists the essential acts: belief, good deeds, prayer, and charity. These together form the complete practice of faith. Their reward is with their Lord.\n\nThe verse ends, \"and there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve.\" Those who combine these acts have no fear for the future and no grief over the past.\n\nThis verse teaches that true faith is combined with good deeds, prayer, and charity, and that those who do these will be rewarded. For the reader, the verse encourages combining belief with action, establishing prayer and paying charity, and trusting in the reward of Allah. The assurance of no fear and no grief gives peace to those who practice their faith sincerely. This verse reinforces the importance of both faith and action in receiving the reward of Allah. This verse combines the essential elements of faith and practice: belief, good deeds, prayer, and charity. Belief is the foundation in the heart, good deeds are the action, prayer is the worship of the body, and charity is the worship of wealth. Together they form the complete practice of the believer. Those who combine these receive their reward from their Lord, and they have no fear for the future and no grief over the past. The reader is encouraged to build faith on this complete foundation, combining sincere belief with righteous action, prayer, and charity, and to trust in the secure reward of Allah. This verse reinforces the theme of the chapter that faith is not complete without action.",
  "278": "“O believers! Fear Allah, and give up outstanding interest if you are ˹true˺ believers.”\n\nThis verse commands the believers to give up interest. \"O believers! Fear Allah, and give up outstanding interest if you are ˹true˺ believers.\" The believers are commanded to fear Allah and to give up the interest that is outstanding, meaning to abandon the interest that they are owed or that remains due. This giving up of interest is linked to the sincerity of their belief: if they are true believers, they will obey this command. Faith and obedience are connected.\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of giving up interest as part of true faith and obedience to Allah. For the reader, the verse encourages fearing Allah and abandoning interest, understanding that this is a sign of true belief. The command links faith with action, showing that genuine belief is expressed in obedience to Allah’s commands. Giving up interest, even what is owed, is a test of sincerity and a requirement of true faith. The verse serves as a clear command to the believers to obey Allah in this matter. The command to give up outstanding interest is linked directly to the sincerity of belief. The verse calls the believers to fear Allah and to abandon the interest that remains due, and it makes clear that this is a requirement of true belief. Faith is not merely a claim but is expressed in obedience to Allah’s commands, even when obedience is difficult and involves giving up something owed. The reader is encouraged to understand that true belief demands action, and that giving up interest is a test and proof of sincerity. The command shows the seriousness of interest and the importance of obedience in the life of the believer. Fearing Allah and following His commands are inseparable from true faith.",
  "279": "“If you do not, then beware of a war with Allah and His Messenger!”\n\nThis verse gives a severe warning about persisting in interest. \"If you do not, then beware of a war with Allah and His Messenger!\" If the believers do not give up interest, they are warned of a war with Allah and His Messenger. This is the most severe warning possible, showing the gravity of persisting in interest after the command. \"But if you repent, you may retain your principal—neither inflicting nor suffering harm.\" If they repent, they may keep their principal, neither inflicting harm on others nor suffering harm themselves. Repentance brings a return to justice.\n\nThis verse teaches the severity of persisting in interest and the mercy available through repentance. For the reader, the verse warns against continuing in interest, which brings war with Allah and His Messenger, and it encourages repentance, which allows one to keep the principal without inflicting or suffering harm. The verse shows the gravity of the sin of interest and the justice and mercy of Allah, who warns severely but accepts repentance. The believer is encouraged to repent and return to what is just. The warning of war with Allah and His Messenger is the most severe warning possible, and it shows the gravity of persisting in interest after the command to give it up. It is a warning that those who continue in this sin place themselves in opposition to Allah and His Messenger, and no one can win such a war. Yet the verse immediately opens the door of repentance, telling those who repent that they may keep their principal, neither inflicting nor suffering harm. This shows the mercy of Allah, who accepts repentance and returns the matter to justice. The reader is encouraged to take the warning seriously, to repent from interest, and to understand that repentance restores justice and mercy, while persistence brings the most severe consequence.",
  "280": "“If it is difficult for someone to repay a debt, postpone it until a time of ease.”\n\nThis verse gives guidance about debt. \"If it is difficult for someone to repay a debt, postpone it until a time of ease.\" If a debtor is facing difficulty in repaying a debt, the creditor is to postpone the repayment until a time of ease. This shows mercy and consideration for the debtor’s circumstances. \"And if you waive it as an act of charity, it will be better for you, if only you knew.\" If the creditor waives the debt entirely as an act of charity, it is better for the creditor, if only he knew. Waiving a debt is a form of charity that brings great reward.\n\nThis verse teaches mercy and kindness in dealing with debt. For the reader, the verse encourages postponing debts for those in difficulty and waiving debts as an act of charity, which is better. The verse shows the importance of compassion in financial dealings and the great reward of relieving the burden of others. The reader is encouraged to be considerate toward debtors and to understand that acts of charity, such as waiving a debt, are better and bring great reward from Allah. Justice and mercy are combined in this guidance. This verse shows the mercy of Allah in financial dealings. When a debtor is in difficulty, the creditor is commanded to postpone the repayment until a time of ease, rather than pressing and harming the debtor. This mercy is part of justice, because it recognises the genuine hardship of the debtor. The verse then goes further, encouraging the creditor to waive the debt entirely as an act of charity, and it declares that this is better for the creditor, if only he knew. The reader is encouraged to be compassionate toward those in debt, to postpone repayment for those in difficulty, and to consider waiving debts as an act of charity, which brings great reward. Justice combined with mercy is the way of Islam in financial matters.",
  "281": "“Be mindful of the Day when you will ˹all˺ be returned to Allah.”\n\nThis verse reminds the believers of the Day of Judgment. \"Be mindful of the Day when you will ˹all˺ be returned to Allah, then every soul will be paid in full for what it has done, and none will be wronged.\" The believers are to be mindful of the Day when all people will be returned to Allah. On that day, every soul will be paid in full for what it has done, and none will be wronged. This is a day of perfect justice, where everyone receives exactly what they earned.\n\nThis verse teaches the reality of the Day of Judgment and the perfect justice of Allah. For the reader, the verse encourages mindfulness of the Day of Judgment, understanding that every soul will be repaid for its deeds and none will be wronged. This awareness shapes behaviour, encouraging good deeds and discouraging wrongdoing. The certainty of the return to Allah and the perfect justice of that day give the believer a powerful motivation to live righteously. The verse serves as a reminder that all actions will be accounted for, and that Allah’s justice is complete. This verse serves as a closing reminder to the guidance about financial dealings, and it points to the Day when all accounts will be settled. Every person will be returned to Allah, and every soul will be paid in full for what it has done, with none wronged. This is the ultimate guarantee of justice, correcting any injustice or imbalance that may occur in this world. The awareness of this Day should shape how the believer deals with others, especially in financial matters, because every transaction and every deed will be accounted for. The reader is encouraged to be mindful of this Day, to deal justly and fairly, and to understand that Allah’s justice is complete and none will be wronged. This gives both motivation and hope.",
  "282": "“O believers! When you contract a loan for a fixed period of time, commit it to writing.”\n\nThis is the longest verse in the Quran, and it gives detailed guidance about recording debts. \"O believers! When you contract a loan for a fixed period of time, commit it to writing.\" When a loan is contracted for a fixed period, it should be put in writing. \"Let the scribe maintain justice between the parties. The scribe should not refuse to write as Allah has taught them to write. They will write what the debtor dictates, bearing Allah in mind and not defrauding the debt.\" The scribe is to write justly, without refusing, and to record what the debtor dictates, bearing Allah in mind.\n\nIf the debtor is incompetent or unable to dictate, his guardian is to dictate with justice. The verse then addresses witnesses: \"Call upon two of your men to witness. If two men cannot be found, then one man and two women of your choice will witness—so if one of the women forgets the other may remind her.\" Two men, or one man and two women, are to witness the contract. The verse then says, \"You must not be against writing ˹contracts˺ for a fixed period—whether the sum is small or great. This is more just ˹for you˺ in the sight of Allah, and more convenient to establish evidence and remove doubts.\" Writing the contract is more just and removes doubt.\n\nThe verse then addresses immediate transactions: \"However, if you conduct an immediate transaction among yourselves, then there is no need for you to record it, but call upon witnesses when a deal is finalized.\" Immediate transactions need not be written, but witnesses should be called. The verse ends with a warning about harming the scribe or witnesses and the reminder that Allah teaches and has perfect knowledge.\n\nThis verse teaches the importance of writing contracts and taking witnesses in financial dealings, for the sake of justice and the removal of doubt. For the reader, the verse encourages careful and just dealings in matters of debt.",
  "283": "“If you are on a journey and a scribe cannot be found, then a security can be taken.”\n\nThis verse gives guidance for debts while travelling. \"If you are on a journey and a scribe cannot be found, then a security can be taken.\" When travelling and no scribe is available, a security, such as a pledge or collateral, can be taken for the debt. This provides a means of securing the debt when writing is not possible. \"If you trust one another, then ˹there is no need for a security, but˺ the debtor should honour this trust ˹by repaying the debt˺—and let them fear Allah, their Lord.\" If the parties trust one another, no security is needed, but the debtor should honour the trust by repaying, fearing Allah.\n\nThe verse then says, \"And do not conceal the testimony, for whoever conceals it, their hearts are indeed sinful.\" Concealing testimony is a sin. The verse ends, \"And Allah ˹fully˺ knows what you do.\"\n\nThis verse teaches the flexibility and trust in financial dealings. For the reader, the verse encourages honouring trusts and repaying debts, fearing Allah, and not concealing testimony. The verse shows the balance between taking security when needed and trusting one another, and it emphasises the importance of honesty and the fear of Allah in financial matters. Concealing testimony is a sin, and the believer is encouraged to bear witness truthfully. This verse shows the practical mercy and flexibility of Islamic guidance in financial matters. When travelling and no scribe is available, a security may be taken for the debt, providing a practical means of protection. If the parties trust one another, no security is needed, but the debtor should honour the trust by repaying, fearing Allah. This places the fear of Allah and honesty at the centre of financial dealing, beyond mere legal safeguards. The verse also forbids concealing testimony, warning that the heart of one who conceals it is sinful. The reader is encouraged to honour trusts, repay debts, fear Allah, and bear testimony truthfully. Honesty and the fear of Allah are the true safeguards of justice in all dealings.",
  "284": "“To Allah ˹alone˺ belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth.”\n\nThis verse affirms the ownership of Allah over all creation. \"To Allah ˹alone˺ belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth.\" Everything in the heavens and on the earth belongs to Allah. \"Whether you reveal what is in your hearts or conceal it, Allah will call you to account for it.\" Whether a person reveals what is in his heart or conceals it, Allah will call him to account for it. This shows the accountability of the inner state, not only outward actions. \"He forgives whoever He wills, and punishes whoever He wills. And Allah is Most Capable of everything.\" Allah forgives and punishes as He wills.\n\nThis verse teaches the ownership of Allah over all things and the accountability of the inner state. For the reader, the verse encourages purifying the heart, since Allah will call people to account for what is in their hearts, whether revealed or concealed. It also teaches the mercy and justice of Allah, who forgives and punishes as He wills, and is Most Capable of everything. The awareness of Allah’s ownership and knowledge encourages sincerity and purity of the inner state. This verse affirms the complete ownership and knowledge of Allah, and it extends accountability to the inner state. Allah will call people to account not only for their outward actions but also for what is in their hearts, whether they reveal it or conceal it. This is a profound teaching, because it shows that sincerity and purity of intention are matters of accountability before Allah. The verse also balances justice and mercy, stating that Allah forgives and punishes as He wills, and that He is Most Capable of everything. The reader is encouraged to purify the heart, to be sincere in intentions, and to recognise that Allah, who owns all things and knows all hearts, deals justly and mercifully with His servants. The awareness of this accountability shapes the inner life of the believer.",
  "285": "“The Messenger ˹firmly˺ believes in what has been revealed to him from his Lord, and so do the believers.”\n\nThis verse affirms the belief of the Messenger and the believers. \"The Messenger ˹firmly˺ believes in what has been revealed to him from his Lord, and so do the believers.\" The Messenger and the believers believe firmly in what was revealed to him. \"They ˹all˺ believe in Allah, His angels, His Books, and His messengers. ˹They proclaim,˺ 'We make no distinction between any of His messengers.'\" They believe in Allah, the angels, the Books, and the messengers, making no distinction between the messengers.\n\nThey say, \"We hear and obey. ˹We seek˺ Your forgiveness, our Lord! And to You ˹alone˺ is the final return.\" The believers declare their hearing and obedience, seek forgiveness, and affirm that to Allah is the final return. This is the profession of true faith.\n\nThis verse teaches the comprehensive faith of the believers, who believe in Allah, His angels, His Books, and His messengers, without distinguishing between them. For the reader, the verse encourages this complete and unified faith and the declaration of hearing and obeying, seeking forgiveness, and returning to Allah. The verse shows the sincerity and completeness of the believers’ faith and their submission to Allah. This verse affirms the completeness and sincerity of the faith of the Messenger and the believers. They believe in Allah, His angels, His Books, and His messengers, making no distinction between the messengers, because all of them brought the truth from Allah. Their proclamation, \"We hear and obey,\" expresses their readiness to accept and follow the guidance, and their request for forgiveness and affirmation of the final return to Allah completes their faith. This verse is a beautiful summary of the believer’s faith and submission. The reader is encouraged to hold this complete faith, to accept all the messengers without distinction, and to respond to Allah’s guidance with hearing and obedience, seeking His forgiveness and affirming that to Him is the final return.",
  "286": "“Allah does not require of any soul more than what it can afford.”\n\nThis verse teaches the mercy of Allah in His requirements. \"Allah does not require of any soul more than what it can afford.\" Allah does not burden any soul beyond its capacity. \"All good will be for its own benefit, and all evil will be to its own loss.\" Good deeds benefit the soul, and evil deeds harm it. \"˹The believers pray,˺ 'Our Lord! Do not punish us if we forget or make a mistake.\"\" The believers pray that Allah not punish them for forgetfulness or mistakes. \"Our Lord! Do not place a burden on us like the one you placed on those before us.\" They ask not to be burdened as previous communities were.\n\n\"Our Lord! Do not burden us with what we cannot bear. Pardon us, forgive us, and have mercy on us. You are our ˹only˺ Guardian. So grant us victory over the disbelieving people.\" They ask for pardon, forgiveness, mercy, and victory. This verse ends the chapter with a beautiful prayer.\n\nThis verse teaches the mercy of Allah in not burdening any soul beyond its capacity and the importance of prayer. For the reader, the verse encourages trust in Allah’s mercy and making this prayer, which asks for pardon, forgiveness, mercy, and victory. The verse shows the balance of Allah’s justice and mercy and the believer’s dependence on Allah. This verse teaches the mercy of Allah in His requirements, as He does not burden any soul beyond its capacity. This is a great mercy, because it means that Allah’s commands are within the ability of every person. The verse also states that good benefits the soul and evil harms it, showing the justice of accountability. The verse ends with a beautiful prayer in which the believers ask Allah not to punish them for forgetfulness or mistakes, not to place a burden on them like that placed on previous communities, not to burden them with what they cannot bear, and to pardon, forgive, and have mercy on them, and to grant them victory. This prayer is a model of humility and dependence on Allah, and it ends the chapter on a note of hope and reliance on His mercy and help. The reader is encouraged to trust in Allah’s mercy and to make this prayer."
};
